,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.866933368,"data":"       | Thus any f yields a permutation g where each element moves +/-1. This is essentially a \"signed permutation\" where each edge of the 12-cycle (the underlying undirected cycle) is oriented either forward or backward, and g follows the orientation: if the edge from d to d+1 is oriented forward (i.e., arrow from d to d+1), then g(d)=d+1; if oriented backward (arrow from d+1 to d), then g(d+1)=d (i.e., g(d+1)=d). Wait orientation: For each unordered edge {d,d+1}, we decide direction: either forward (d-\u003ed+1) or backward (d+1-\u003ed). This corresponds to setting f(d)=0 for forward (d-\u003ed+1) and f(d+1)=1 for backward? Let's examine: For edge between d and d+1 (mod 12). If we set f(d)=0, then g(d)=d+1 (forward). The opposite direction would be g(d+1)=d (i.e., f(d+1)=1). Indeed, the condition that each vertex has indegree 1 ensures that for each edge, exactly one of its endpoints points to the other. So the orientation of each edge is determined by f at one endpoint: if f(d)=0 then edge oriented forward; if f(d+1)=1 then edge oriented backward. But note that both cannot be true simultaneously because indegree condition ensures that for each vertex, the incoming edge is from the previous vertex according to orientation. The equality condition A[i][j-1]=A[i-1][j] ensures that orientation is consistent across the torus; it essentially forces the orientation of edges along the \"difference\" axis to be consistent: the orientation of edge between d and d+1 is determined by f(d) (or equivalently f(d+1) = 1 - f(d)? Let's compute: Since f(d) = A[i][j] where i - j = d. The condition A[i][j-1] = A[i-1][j] translates to f(d) = f(d)?? Actually we derived earlier that A[i][j] depends only on i - j, no further constraints. So f can be arbitrary binary sequence of length 12. That means orientation of each edge may not be consistent? Let's examine mapping g: For each d, we have orientation of edge between d and d+1 determined by f(d). Because g(d) = d+1 if f(d)=0 else d-1. So if f(d)=0, then edge from d to d+1 is forward; if f(d)=1, then edge from d to d-1 is forward (i.e., edge from d-1 to d is forward). So orientation of edge between d-1 and d is determined by f(d-1) or f(d)? Let's compute: Edge between d-1 and d: forward direction from d-1 to d corresponds to g(d-1)=d, which occurs when f(d-1)=0. Backward direction from d to d-1 corresponds to g(d)=d-1, which occurs when f(d)=1. So for each undirected edge {d-1,d}, exactly one of f(d-1)=0 or f(d)=1 must hold to have exactly one direction (since indegree/outdegree constraints guarantee exactly one orientation). But can both happen? If f(d-1)=0 and f(d)=1 simultaneously, then we would have both g(d-1)=d (forward) and g(d)=d-1 (backward), which would make both vertices map to each other, forming a 2-cycle. That's allowed; that would mean the edge is bidirectional? Actually that would mean both directions are used, resulting in a 2-cycle (swap). But indegree condition requires each vertex has exactly one incoming edge, which would be satisfied: d-1 receives from d (since f(d)=1) and also from d-2 maybe? Wait d-1's incoming edge could be from d-2 (if f(d-2)=0) or from d (if f(d)=1). If both happen, then indegree would be 2, violating condition. So both cannot happen simultaneously. Let's verify: Suppose f(d-1)=0, then g(d-1)=d. So d receives incoming from d-1. If also f(d)=1, then g(d)=d-1, so d-1 receives incoming from d. This would give each vertex one incoming (d gets from d-1, d-1 gets from d). That's okay: each vertex has exactly one incoming (from the other). So both can happen simultaneously; that would create a 2-cycle between d-1 and d. That's allowed. So orientation of edge {d-1,d} can be either forward (if f(d-1)=0 and f(d) ≠ 1) or backward (if f(d)=1 and f(d-1) ≠ 0) or both (if both conditions hold) leading to a 2-cycle. But can both hold? Let's check indegree for d: incoming edges to d are from d-1 if f(d-1)=0, and from d+1 if f(d+1)=1 (since g(d+1)=d when f(d+1)=1). So indegree of d is (1 - f(d-1)) + f(d+1). The condition that indegree =1 translates to (1 - f(d-1)) + f(d+1) = 1 =\u003e f(d+1) = f(d-1). This is exactly the condition we derived earlier: f(d+1) = f(d-1) for all d. Indeed, because A[i][j-1] = A[i-1][j] leads to f(i - j) = f(i - j)?? Let's compute: The condition A[i][j-1] = A[i-1][j] translates to f(i - (j-1)) = f((i-1) - j) =\u003e f(i - j + 1) = f(i - j - 1). So f(d+1) = f(d-1) for all d. This is a stronger condition than just arbitrary f. Wait earlier we concluded that A[i][j] depends only on i - j, but we didn't impose any further constraints. However the indegree condition gave equality A[i][j-1] = A[i-1][j] which leads to f(d+1) = f(d-1). Let's verify.\n"}
