,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.867035614,"data":"       | If we consider the combined mapping T, after k steps we have (s0 + k mod 12, g^k(d0)). The pair repeats when both components repeat simultaneously. The s component repeats after multiples of 12. The d component repeats after multiples of its cycle length L (the length of the cycle of g containing d0). So the overall period is the least common multiple of 12 and L. Indeed, because we need k such that k ≡ 0 (mod 12) and k ≡ 0 (mod L). So period = lcm(12, L). So maximum possible period is lcm(12, L). Since L divides 12 (because g is a permutation on 12 elements, but its cycles could have lengths that divide 12? Actually cycles of a permutation on 12 elements can have lengths up to 12, not necessarily dividing 12. For example, a 5-cycle is allowed. However g is constrained by the rule that each element moves +/-1, which may restrict possible cycle lengths. Let's explore.\n"}
