,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.867047304,"data":"       | Consider the directed graph on vertices 0..11 with edges oriented either forward or backward along the circle. This defines a functional digraph where each vertex has out-degree 1, indegree 1. This is exactly a permutation composed of cycles that follow the orientation of edges: each vertex points to its neighbor either clockwise (+1) or counterclockwise (-1). This is known as a \"signed permutation\" where each cycle corresponds to a directed cycle around the circle, possibly reversing direction at some edges. Actually if we have a mixture of forward and backward edges, the permutation can have cycles of various lengths. For example, if we have pattern 0-\u003e1 (forward), 1-\u003e0 (backward), that forms a 2-cycle. Similarly, we could have 0-\u003e1 (forward), 1-\u003e2 (forward), 2-\u003e1 (backward) etc. Let's analyze.\n"}
