,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.867053204,"data":"       | The permutation g can be represented as a collection of directed cycles where each step moves to adjacent vertex along the circle (mod 12). So each cycle is a walk that goes around the circle possibly reversing direction at some points, but must be a closed walk that uses each vertex exactly once within that cycle. Since each vertex has out-degree 1 and indegree 1, the cycles are simple cycles (no repeated vertices). Because each vertex appears exactly once in each cycle. Since edges are only between adjacent vertices, each cycle must be a contiguous segment of the circle that goes forward for some edges and backward for others, but to return to start, the net number of forward steps equals backward steps? Let's think.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.867056553,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.86705908,"data":"       | Consider a cycle of length L. Since each step moves to adjacent vertex, the total displacement after L steps must be 0 modulo 12 (since you return to starting vertex). The displacement is sum over steps of +1 for forward edges, -1 for backward edges, modulo 12. So net displacement = (#forward - #backward) ≡ 0 (mod 12). Since L = #forward + #backward. So we need #forward - #backward ≡ 0 (mod 12). Since L ≤ 12 (since there are only 12 vertices). For L less than 12, the only way for net displacement to be 0 mod 12 is if #forward = #backward (since magnitude less than 12). So L must be even, with equal number of forward and backward steps. So cycles of even length are possible. For L=12, net displacement can be 0 mod 12 even if #forward - #backward = 0, 12, -12, etc. Since #forward + #backward =12, #forward - #backward = 12 - 2*#backward. For net displacement 0 mod 12, we need #forward - #backward ≡ 0 (mod 12). This holds for any #forward - #backward = 0, ±12, ±24,... Since #forward - #backward ranges from -12 to 12 in steps of 2. So possibilities: 0, ±12. #forward - #backward = 0 =\u003e #forward = #backward = 6. #forward - #backward = 12 =\u003e #forward =12, #backward=0 (all forward). #forward - #backward = -12 =\u003e all backward. So for L=12, we can have all forward (12-cycle), all backward (12-cycle reverse direction), or 6 forward 6 backward (some mixture) that still net zero displacement.\n"}
