,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.867565241,"data":"       | Our earlier derivation: A[i][j-1] = A[i-1][j] leads to f(i - j + 1) = f(i - j - 1). This holds for all i,j, i.e., for all d, f(d+1) = f(d-1). This recurrence on Z_n (mod n) yields that f is constant on cosets of the subgroup generated by 2 (i.e., the set {d, d+2, d+4,...}). For n odd, the subgroup generated by 2 is the whole group (since 2 is invertible modulo odd n). Therefore f must be constant across all d. So indeed for odd n, f must be constant (2 possibilities). However enumeration for n=3 gave 6 cycles, which suggests there are more possibilities beyond those satisfying indegree condition? Wait maybe our earlier indegree condition is not necessary for the permutation to be a single cycle? Let's revisit the indegree condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.867567686,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.867568887,"data":"       | We derived indegree condition from requirement that each vertex has exactly one incoming edge. But for a permutation (bijection) each vertex must have exactly one incoming edge. However we only ensured that each vertex has exactly one outgoing edge (by definition of mapping). The condition that each vertex has exactly one incoming edge is equivalent to the mapping being a bijection (i.e., the mapping is a permutation). In our enumeration we explicitly checked that the mapping is bijective (len(set(f.values())) == n*n). So indegree condition is automatically satisfied if mapping is bijection. However we derived a necessary condition for bijectivity: A[i][j-1] = A[i-1][j]. Is that condition indeed necessary? Let's test with n=3 enumeration to see if there exist bijective mappings that violate this condition.\n"}
