,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.868047978,"data":"       | Consider mapping each vertex to its \"time\" t = number of moves made so far (starting at 0). At time t, the position (x(t), y(t)) is determined by counts of E and N moves up to t: x(t) = #E moves mod n, y(t) = #N moves mod n. Since each move increments either x or y, the pair (x(t), y(t)) is (e(t) mod n, n(t) mod n) where e(t) = number of E moves up to time t, n(t) = number of N moves up to time t. So the sequence of positions is determined by the sequence of moves (E/N). The condition that each vertex appears exactly once means that the mapping from t to (e(t) mod n, n(t) mod n) is a bijection from {0,...,n^2-1} to Z_n × Z_n. Starting at (0,0) at t=0. After n^2 steps, we return to (0,0) meaning e(n^2) ≡ 0 mod n and n(n^2) ≡ 0 mod n. So total counts of E and N moves each are multiples of n.\n"}
