,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869113348,"data":"       | Let's test n=7 with backtracking with pruning maybe still manageable? 49 vertices, branching 2, worst-case 2^49 huge. But maybe constraints reduce drastically; but still may be too large. Let's try but may take too long. We'll attempt but with early cutoff maybe we can compute counts for n=7 using some smarter combinatorial reasoning.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869116712,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869117837,"data":"       | Observation: The condition that residues are distinct is equivalent to the path being a \"linear extension\" of a partial order defined by (i,j) ≤ (i',j') if i'≥i and j'≥j (componentwise). However due to wrap-around, the path may cross the boundary.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869119537,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869120629,"data":"       | But there is known concept: \"Hamiltonian cycles in the directed torus C_n □ C_n\" correspond to \"decompositions of the group Z_n × Z_n into a single cycle generated by steps (1,0) and (0,1)\". This is similar to \"complete mappings\" of cyclic groups? Let's think.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869122426,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869123531,"data":"       | We can view the sequence of moves as a word w of length n^2 over alphabet {E,N} with exactly n*a' E's and n*(n-a') N's. The condition that residues are distinct is equivalent to the condition that the cumulative sums (e(t), n(t)) modulo n are all distinct. This is equivalent to the condition that the mapping t -\u003e (e(t) mod n, n(t) mod n) is a bijection.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869125322,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869126404,"data":"       | Now consider the function f: Z_{n^2} → Z_n × Z_n given by f(t) = (e(t) mod n, n(t) mod n). Since e(t) and n(t) are cumulative counts, we have e(t+1) = e(t) + 1_{w_{t+1}=E}, n(t+1) = n(t) + 1_{w_{t+1}=N}. So f is a bijection that respects the adjacency: for each t, either f(t+1) = f(t)+(1,0) mod n or f(t+1) = f(t)+(0,1) mod n. So f is a Hamiltonian cycle labeling of the torus.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.86912822,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869129319,"data":"       | Thus we are counting number of Hamiltonian cycles (directed) in the torus with a distinguished starting vertex (0,0) and a distinguished direction for the first step? Actually start at (0,0) after 0 moves, then first move leads to either (1,0) or (0,1). So cycles counted include both possibilities.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869130917,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869132209,"data":"       | Now known result: Number of Hamiltonian cycles in directed torus C_n □ C_n is n * φ(n) maybe? Let's search memory: I recall that number of Hamiltonian cycles in the undirected torus C_n □ C_n (grid with wrap) is something like (n-1)!^2 for directed? Not sure.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869133905,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869135701,"data":"       | Let's attempt to derive combinatorial formula.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869136966,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.86913804,"data":"       | Consider the permutation σ on Z_n × Z_n defined by σ(i,j) = (i+1, j) if (i,j) is designated as E, else (i, j+1). This permutation must be a single cycle. The functional graph of σ is a set of cycles; we need exactly one cycle of length n^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869139664,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.86914096,"data":"       | Observation: The indegree of each vertex is also 1, because σ is a permutation. Since each vertex has exactly two possible preimages: (i-1,j) via E, and (i,j-1) via N. For σ to be a permutation, each vertex must have exactly one incoming edge from either its west neighbor or its south neighbor. So we need to choose for each vertex whether its outgoing edge is east or north, and also ensure each vertex has exactly one incoming edge (i.e., the choice for its west neighbor or south neighbor). This is equivalent to selecting a subset of edges forming a directed 1-regular digraph (each vertex out-degree 1, in-degree 1) that is a subgraph of the torus where each vertex has exactly one of its two outgoing edges selected, and each vertex has exactly one of its two incoming edges selected (from west or south). This is essentially a \"directed 2-factor\" where each vertex has indegree=outdegree=1, which is a collection of directed cycles covering all vertices. We need exactly one cycle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869145574,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869146935,"data":"       | Thus the problem reduces to counting number of \"Eulerian orientations\" of the torus where each vertex has out-degree 1 (choosing one of two outgoing edges) and indegree 1 (automatically satisfied if out-degree 1 for all vertices? Not necessarily; we must ensure indegree condition). But since each vertex has exactly two incoming edges (from west and south), the indegree condition is equivalent to that the selection of outgoing edges forms a permutation (bijection). This is similar to counting \"perfect matchings\" in a bipartite directed graph? Let's model as a 2-regular directed graph.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869149188,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869150373,"data":"       | We can think of assigning to each vertex a direction D(i,j) ∈ {E,N}. Then the indegree condition translates to: for each vertex (i,j), exactly one of its two potential predecessors (i-1,j) and (i,j-1) must have chosen direction pointing to it. That is, D(i-1,j) = E or D(i,j-1) = N, but not both (since indegree must be 1). This gives a constraint system.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869152191,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869153261,"data":"       | Thus we need to count number of assignments D(i,j) ∈ {E,N} such that for each vertex (i,j), exactly one of D(i-1,j) and D(i,j-1) is E/N accordingly. This is reminiscent of counting \"alternating sign matrices\" or \"Eulerian orientations of grid graph\". Might be related to counting \"toroidal Latin squares\" or \"rook tours\".\n"}
