at appear after each movement (i.e., after each movement). So there are 144 movements, and after each movement we have a new configuration. That's 144 configurations (states) observed after each move. They require that each of the 144 possible configurations appears exactly once among those 144 observed states. The initial configuration (before any moves) is not counted among those 144. Then after 144 moves we have visited all 144 states, and the final state is (12,12). So we start at (12,12) but not counted; after 144 moves we end at (12,12) which is counted as one of the 144 visited states. So we have a Hamiltonian cycle of length 144 that starts and ends at (0,0) but the start is not counted as visited; the cycle includes all nodes exactly once, with (0,0) being the final node visited. Equivalent to a Hamiltonian cycle that starts at (0,0) and ends at (0,0) after visiting all nodes exactly once (including start again). However a Hamiltonian cycle of a directed graph of 144 nodes would have length 144 (edges), returning to start after 144 steps, visiting each node exactly once (including start at both beginning and end). That's consistent. So we need number of directed Hamiltonian cycles in this graph that start at (0,0) (or we can count cycles up to rotation? But they fix start at (0,0) and direction of moves (clockwise increments). So we need number of Hamiltonian cycles in the directed graph where each node has out-degree 2 (increment a or b). Starting at (0,0), we must produce a sequence of 144 moves that visits each node exactly once and returns to start.\n"}
