,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869342652,"data":"       | Let’s define a binary matrix A of size 12x12 where A[i][j] = 0 if vertex (i,j) points east, 1 if points north (or vice versa). Then the outgoing arrow from (i,j) goes to (i+1,j) if east, or (i,j+1) if north (mod 12). For each vertex (i,j), the incoming arrows could be from (i-1,j) if that vertex points east, or from (i,j-1) if that vertex points north. So indegree condition: exactly one of A[i-1][j] (east) and A[i][j-1] (north) must be such that they point to (i,j). Let's formalize:\n"}
