,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869345736,"data":"       | Let east = 0, north = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869346869,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869357209,"data":"       | If A[i-1][j] = 0 (east), then (i-1,j) points east to (i,j). If A[i][j-1] = 1 (north), then (i,j-1) points north to (i,j). So indegree of (i,j) = (A[i-1][j]==0) + (A[i][j-1]==1). We need this sum = 1 for all i,j.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869360365,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869361475,"data":"       | Thus condition: For each cell (i,j), exactly one of the two conditions holds: either the left neighbor points east (A[i-1][j]=0) or the below neighbor points north (A[i][j-1]=1), but not both.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.86936293,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869364017,"data":"       | Equivalently, we can write a constraint: (A[i-1][j]==0) XOR (A[i][j-1]==1) = 1 (i.e., they differ). Let's convert to algebraic condition using binary values 0/1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869365415,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869366799,"data":"       | Define B[i][j] = A[i][j] (0 for east, 1 for north). Then condition: (1 - B[i-1][j]) + B[i][j-1] = 1. Because A[i-1][j]==0 corresponds to 1 - B[i-1][j] (since B=0 east). So we have (1 - B[i-1][j]) + B[i][j-1] = 1 =\u003e 1 - B[i-1][j] + B[i][j-1] = 1 =\u003e - B[i-1][j] + B[i][j-1] = 0 =\u003e B[i][j-1] = B[i-1][j].\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869368572,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869369647,"data":"       | Thus the condition simplifies to B[i][j-1] = B[i-1][j] for all i,j (indices modulo 12). That's nice! Let's verify: If B[i][j-1] = B[i-1][j], then indegree condition holds.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869371062,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.86937215,"data":"       | Proof: Suppose B[i][j-1] = B[i-1][j] = 0. Then left neighbor points east (since B=0), and below neighbor points east (since B=0) meaning below neighbor points east to (i+1, j-1) not to (i,j). Wait careful: B[i][j-1] refers to cell (i, j-1) (below neighbor). If B[i][j-1] = 0 (east), then (i, j-1) points east to (i+1, j-1) (mod). That's not incoming to (i,j). So incoming from below neighbor occurs only if B[i][j-1] = 1 (north). Similarly incoming from left neighbor occurs only if B[i-1][j] = 0 (east). So indegree = (1 - B[i-1][j]) + B[i][j-1]. Setting equality B[i][j-1] = B[i-1][j] yields indegree = (1 - x) + x = 1. Indeed works.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869374369,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869375434,"data":"       | Thus the condition reduces to B[i][j-1] = B[i-1][j] for all i,j (mod 12). This is a linear condition on binary matrix with periodic boundary.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869376793,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869377874,"data":"       | Thus the set of valid assignments corresponds to binary matrices B of size 12x12 satisfying B[i][j-1] = B[i-1][j] for all i,j (mod indices). This is reminiscent of a \"constant sum along diagonals\" condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869379445,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869380573,"data":"       | Let's rewrite: B[i][j] = B[i+1][j+1] for all i,j (since shifting indices). Indeed from B[i][j-1] = B[i-1][j], replace i←i+1, j←j+1: B[i+1][j] = B[i][j+1]. Equivalent: B[i][j] = B[i+1][j+1] (mod 12). So B is constant along lines of slope -1 (i.e., along diagonals with wrap-around). So each diagonal (i - j constant modulo 12) has constant value. Since indices modulo 12, there are 12 such diagonals (since i-j mod 12 can be 0..11). So B is determined by its values on one representative per diagonal; i.e., choose values for each of the 12 diagonals, then fill the matrix accordingly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869382753,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.86938402,"data":"       | Thus there are 2^12 possible assignments B that satisfy indegree condition. Each such assignment yields a functional digraph where each vertex has indegree 1 and outdegree 1 (since each vertex has exactly one outgoing direction defined). So each assignment corresponds to a permutation consisting of cycles (possibly multiple cycles). We need those assignments where the permutation is a single 144-cycle (i.e., the digraph is a single cycle). So we need to count among the 2^12 assignments, how many produce a single cycle covering all vertices.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869388559,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869389648,"data":"       | Thus N = number of binary strings of length 12 (values per diagonal) such that the resulting permutation on V is a single cycle. Starting point (0,0) is fixed as start of cycle, but that's automatically satisfied if it's a single cycle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869391173,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869392239,"data":"       | Now we need to analyze the structure of cycles given diagonal pattern.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869393491,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869394609,"data":"       | Let's index vertices by (i,j) where i,j ∈ Z_12. Define B[i][j] = d_{i-j} where d_k ∈ {0,1} for k ∈ Z_12 (since constant on each diagonal). Let's define function f(i,j) = (i+1,j) if d_{i-j}=0 (east) else (i,j+1) if d_{i-j}=1 (north). So the permutation moves east if diagonal value 0, north if 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869396292,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869397348,"data":"       | Now we need to analyze cycles of this permutation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869398531,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869399607,"data":"       | Observation: The permutation is a \"linear\" map? Not exactly linear, but due to diagonal constancy, maybe we can express the effect on coordinates after k steps.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869401058,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869402122,"data":"       | Suppose we start at (i0,j0). After each step, either i increments (east) or j increments (north). The choice depends on current diagonal d_{i - j}. So the difference (i - j) may change after each step. Let's compute how diagonal index evolves.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869403648,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869404964,"data":"       | Let δ = i - j (mod 12). If we move east (i+1,j), new difference δ' = (i+1) - j = δ + 1 (mod 12). If we move north (i, j+1), δ' = i - (j+1) = δ - 1 (mod 12). So moving east increments the diagonal index, moving north decrements it. The direction chosen depends on d_δ: if d_δ = 0, we move east (δ→δ+1). If d_δ = 1, we move north (δ→δ-1). So the process on the diagonal index δ is a deterministic walk on Z_12: at each step, if current δ has d_δ=0, we move to δ+1; else move to δ-1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869407396,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869408471,"data":"       | Thus the permutation's dynamics can be described purely in terms of δ (difference) and also maybe some other coordinate to track position along the cycle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869409881,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869410972,"data":"       | Given that the state (i,j) can be recovered from δ and maybe i (or j). Since i and j are determined up to a common shift: given δ = i - j, and say i (mod 12), then j = i - δ. So the permutation acts as: (i, δ) -\u003e (i+1, δ+1) if d_δ=0, else (i, δ-1) if d_δ=1? Wait need to compute transformation in terms of (i,δ). Starting from (i,j) with δ = i - j. If we move east (d_δ=0): new i' = i+1, j' = j, so δ' = i' - j' = (i+1) - j = δ+1. So (i,δ) -\u003e (i+1, δ+1). If we move north (d_δ=1): new i' = i, j' = j+1, δ' = i - (j+1) = δ-1. So (i,δ) -\u003e (i, δ-1). So transformation depends on d_δ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869416298,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869417433,"data":"       | Thus we have a deterministic automaton on 12x12 states. But maybe cycles correspond to orbits of this transformation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869418785,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869419901,"data":"       | We can try to analyze cycles length given d vector.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.8694211,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869422221,"data":"       | Observation: The transformation preserves the sum i + j? Let's compute: For east move, i+ j increases by 1 (since i+1 + j = i+j+1). For north move, i + j increases by 1 as well (i + j+1 = i+j+1). So each step increments the sum s = i + j (mod 12) by 1 regardless of direction. Indeed because each move increments either i or j by 1, so s increments by 1 mod 12. So s(t) = s0 + t (mod 12). So after 12 steps, s returns to original modulo 12. But we have 144 steps total, which is multiple of 12 (12*12). So after 144 steps, s increments by 144 ≡ 0 mod 12, so s returns to original.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869424399,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.86942551,"data":"       | Thus the permutation can be expressed as: after t steps, (i_t, j_t) = (i0 + #east steps, j0 + #north steps). Since total steps t = #east + #north. So s_t = i_t + j_t = i0 + j0 + t.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869426931,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869428212,"data":"       | Thus the permutation's effect after 144 steps is identity on sum, but we need overall identity on both coordinates.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869429533,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869430645,"data":"       | Now to analyze cycles, we can consider the mapping after one full cycle (i.e., after 144 steps) is identity for any configuration because we require permutation to be a single 144-cycle; but the mapping defined by the assignment B is a permutation of order 144 (i.e., each vertex returns after 144 steps). However not all assignments produce permutation of order 144; some produce multiple cycles of smaller length.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869432509,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869433605,"data":"       | We need to count assignments where the permutation has a single cycle of length 144. Equivalent to the functional digraph being a single cycle (i.e., the permutation is a 144-cycle). Since the graph is regular, the permutation order must divide 144 (since each vertex returns after some number of steps). Actually the order of the permutation is the length of the cycle containing (0,0). Since total vertices 144, order must be a divisor of 144. So we need assignments where order = 144.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869435539,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869436605,"data":"       | Thus we need to count binary strings d[0..11] such that the induced permutation on Z_12×Z_12 has order 144.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869437966,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869439049,"data":"       | Let's attempt to analyze the permutation's structure using group theory.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869440277,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869441356,"data":"       | Define mapping T on Z_12×Z_12: T(i,j) = (i+1, j) if d_{i-j}=0 else (i, j+1) if d_{i-j}=1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.86944264,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869443727,"data":"       | We can think of coordinates modulo 12.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869446504,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869447653,"data":"       | Define function φ: Z_12×Z_12 → Z_12×Z_12 as above.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869448975,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869450056,"data":"       | Observation: The map T can be expressed as composition of two commuting translations? Not directly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869451346,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869452436,"data":"       | But we can analyze using linear algebra over Z (mod 12) perhaps.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.86945364,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869454725,"data":"       | Consider representing state as vector (i,j) in Z_12^2. The map depends on d_{i-j}. This is not linear.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869456003,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869457311,"data":"       | But perhaps we can view the permutation as product of two permutations: one that increments i (east) on some subset of vertices, and one that increments j (north) on the complement. However the map is not a product because each vertex chooses one of the two.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869458885,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869459964,"data":"       | Nevertheless, we can analyze cycles using the δ dynamics.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.86946121,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869462285,"data":"       | Let’s consider the process on δ alone: δ_{t+1} = δ_t + 1 if d_{δ_t}=0, else δ_t - 1 if d_{δ_t}=1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869463678,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869465093,"data":"       | Thus δ evolves according to a deterministic rule defined by d vector. This is a 1D deterministic walk on Z_12, where at each position δ we either step forward (+1) or backward (-1) depending on d_δ (0→+1, 1→-1). This is essentially a signed directed graph on a cycle of length 12: each node has out-degree 1 (to neighbor either clockwise or counterclockwise). This is a functional digraph on Z_12 (a permutation of 12 elements) defined by the rule: f(δ) = δ+1 if d_δ=0 else δ-1 if d_δ=1. Since each node has exactly one outgoing edge, and each node also has exactly one incoming edge? Let's check: The incoming edges to δ come from δ-1 if d_{δ-1}=0 (since that node steps forward) and from δ+1 if d_{δ+1}=1 (since that node steps backward). So indegree condition for δ is also 1? Let's compute: indegree of δ = (d_{δ-1}=0) + (d_{δ+1}=1). For the permutation to be bijective on δ, we need indegree = 1 for each δ. Let's see if that's automatically satisfied given our earlier condition? Let's test: Since we derived B[i][j-1] = B[i-1][j] ensures indegree 1 for each vertex; does that also ensure indegree 1 for each δ? Let's compute using d vector: For each δ, indegree condition is (d_{δ-1}=0) + (d_{δ+1}=1) = 1. This is a constraint on d. Let's verify if our earlier condition B[i][j-1] = B[i-1][j] automatically ensures this. Let's test with arbitrary d vector: Suppose we choose d arbitrarily (any binary string). Does the indegree condition for vertices hold? We derived that indegree condition reduces to B[i][j-1] = B[i-1][j]. Since B[i][j] = d_{i-j}, this condition becomes d_{i-(j-1)} = d_{(i-1)-j} =\u003e d_{i-j+1} = d_{i-j-1}. So for all i,j, we need d_{k+1} = d_{k-1} where k = i - j. So this condition says for all k, d_{k+1} = d_{k-1}. This is a strong condition! Let's verify.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869469723,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869470793,"data":"       | Take B[i][j-1] = d_{i-(j-1)} = d_{i-j+1}. B[i-1][j] = d_{(i-1)-j} = d_{i-j-1}. Equality condition yields d_{i-j+1} = d_{i-j-1} for all i,j, i.e., for all k, d_{k+1} = d_{k-1}. This is a constraint on the d vector: it must be constant on parity classes? Let's analyze.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869474053,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869475174,"data":"       | If d_{k+1} = d_{k-1} for all k mod 12, then the sequence d must be periodic with period 2? Because this recurrence says each term equals the term two steps before. So the sequence is determined by its values at two consecutive positions; indeed d_{k} = d_{k-2} for all k. So the sequence is 2-periodic: d_{k} = d_{k mod 2}. So there are at most 4 possibilities? Actually we need to consider modulo 12; the recurrence leads to d_{k} = d_{k-2} for all k. So the sequence repeats every 2 steps. So d can be either constant (both parity same) or alternating (different). Let's examine possibilities:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869477323,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869478421,"data":"       | Let a = d_0, b = d_1. Then for k even, d_{2m} = a, for k odd, d_{2m+1} = b. So there are 2^2 = 4 possibilities for d: (a,b) ∈ {0,1}^2. However we must also satisfy the indegree condition for δ: (d_{δ-1}=0) + (d_{δ+1}=1) = 1 for each δ. Let's test each of the 4 possibilities.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869480121,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869481199,"data":"       | Case 1: a=0, b=0 (all zeros). Then d_{δ-1}=0 always, d_{δ+1}=0 always. So indegree condition gives 0+0=0 not 1. So invalid.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869482634,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869483737,"data":"       | Case 2: a=1, b=1 (all ones). Then d_{δ-1}=1, d_{δ+1}=1. Indegree condition: (d_{δ-1}=0?) that's false (0), plus (d_{δ+1}=1) = 1, sum =1? Wait (d_{δ-1}=0) is false, so contributes 0, plus (d_{δ+1}=1) true contributes 1 =\u003e sum=1. So indegree condition satisfied? Let's compute: indegree = (d_{δ-1}=0) + (d_{δ+1}=1). Since d_{δ-1}=1, not 0, first term 0. Second term is 1 because d_{δ+1}=1. So indegree=1. So all ones works.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869485807,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869487159,"data":"       | Case 3: a=0, b=1 (alternating 0,1,0,1...). Let's compute indegree for δ even (δ parity 0). Then δ-1 odd =\u003e d_{δ-1}=b=1, not 0. δ+1 odd =\u003e d_{δ+1}=b=1, equals 1. So indegree = (d_{δ-1}=0?) false (0) + (d_{δ+1}=1) true (1) =\u003e 1. For δ odd: δ-1 even =\u003e d_{δ-1}=a=0, which equals 0, contributes 1. δ+1 even =\u003e d_{δ+1}=a=0, not 1, contributes 0. So indegree=1. So alternating works.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869489345,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869490423,"data":"       | Case 4: a=1, b=0 (alternating starting with 1). For δ even: δ-1 odd =\u003e d_{δ-1}=b=0 =\u003e contributes 1 (since equals 0). δ+1 odd =\u003e d_{δ+1}=b=0 =\u003e not 1, contributes 0 =\u003e indegree=1. For δ odd: δ-1 even =\u003e d_{δ-1}=a=1 =\u003e not 0, contributes 0. δ+1 even =\u003e d_{δ+1}=a=1 =\u003e contributes 1 =\u003e indegree=1. So also works.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869492555,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869493681,"data":"       | Thus the indegree condition for δ is automatically satisfied for any d that satisfies the recurrence d_{k+1}=d_{k-1} (i.e., 2-periodic). Let's verify: For any 2-periodic d, indegree condition holds. Let's test with Python for all 4 possibilities.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869495244,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869496339,"data":"       | But we derived earlier that indegree condition for vertices reduces to d_{k+1}=d_{k-1}. So the set of valid assignments B (ensuring indegree 1) is exactly those d vectors that are 2-periodic. Wait earlier we thought there were 2^12 possibilities; but after imposing indegree condition we derived constraint that reduces to 4 possibilities. Let's double-check our derivation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.869499887,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.86950106,"data":"       | We started with indegree condition for each vertex: (1 - B[i-1][j]) + B[i][j-1] = 1, which simplifies to B[i][j-1] = B[i-1][j]. This is a condition on B matrix. Since B[i][j] = d_{i-j}, we substitute: B[i][j-1] = d_{i-(j-1)} = d_{i-j+1}. B[i-1][j] = d_{(i-1)-j} = d_{i-j-1}. Equality yields d_{i-j+1} = d_{i-j-1} for all i,j, i.e., for all k = i - j, d_{k+1} = d_{k-1}. Indeed this condition forces d to be 2-periodic. So the earlier statement that there are 2^12 assignments was incorrect because we missed indegree condition; we only considered outdegree assignment. Indeed we must also ensure each vertex has indegree 1, which imposes constraints. So the number of assignments satisfying both outdegree and indegree constraints is only 4, not 2^12. Let's verify by brute force for small n (say n=3) to see pattern.\n"}
