,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870030795,"data":"       | A de Bruijn sequence of order 2 over alphabet size k is a cyclic sequence of length k^2 where each possible length-2 string appears exactly once as consecutive symbols. That's exactly analogous to our problem if we think of the sequence of hand positions? Let's think: In a de Bruijn sequence of order 2 over alphabet size k, you have a cyclic sequence a_0 a_1 ... a_{k^2-1} such that each ordered pair (a_i, a_{i+1}) (mod k) appears exactly once. That's a Hamiltonian cycle in the de Bruijn graph B(k,2) where vertices are length-1 strings (i.e., symbols) and edges correspond to length-2 strings. Wait B(k,2) has vertices of length-1 (k vertices) and edges of length-2 (k^2 edges). But our graph has vertices of length-2 (ordered pairs) and edges correspond to shifting by one coordinate? Actually our graph vertices are (i,j). Edge from (i,j) to (i+1,j) corresponds to incrementing first coordinate; edge to (i,j+1) increments second coordinate. This is similar to a directed graph where each vertex has two outgoing edges representing adding 1 to first or second coordinate. This is isomorphic to de Bruijn graph B(12,2) if we think of each vertex as a 2-digit base-12 number and each edge corresponds to moving to a vertex where the first digit increments? Not exactly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870034109,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870035263,"data":"       | Let's attempt to map: In de Bruijn graph B(k,n), vertices are strings of length n-1, edges correspond to length n strings: edge from a_1...a_{n-1} to a_2...a_n labeled a_1...a_n. For n=2, vertices are strings of length 1 (i.e., symbols). Edges correspond to ordered pairs (a,b) and go from a to b. So a Hamiltonian cycle in B(k,2) corresponds to a sequence of length k^2 where each ordered pair appears exactly once as consecutive symbols. That's exactly our problem? Let's test: Suppose we have a sequence of symbols s_0,s_1,...,s_{k^2-1} (cyclic) where each ordered pair (s_i,s_{i+1}) appears exactly once. Then the ordered pairs are the edges of the de Bruijn graph. The vertices (symbols) appear many times. Not same as our situation where vertices are ordered pairs and edges correspond to incrementing one coordinate.\n"}
