,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870192249,"data":"       | For n=m=12, we need to count number of functional permutations (i.e., each vertex chooses east or north) that form a single cycle covering all vertices. That's similar to counting \"Hamiltonian cycles\" but we also need to consider the starting point (12,12) as designated start; but cycles are cyclic, starting point is fixed. The number of sequences of moves corresponds to number of directed Hamiltonian cycles starting at (12,12) with a distinguished initial direction? Actually a sequence of moves of length 144 starting at (12,12) and ending at (12,12) uniquely determines a Hamiltonian cycle with a designated start and orientation (since we follow moves). Conversely, any Hamiltonian cycle starting at (12,12) gives a sequence (starting at that vertex and following edges). Since cycles are directed (edges orientation), there is a unique sequence (starting at (12,12)). So N equals number of Hamiltonian cycles (directed) that start at (12,12). Since the graph is vertex-transitive, the number of cycles starting at a given vertex is total number of cycles divided by 144 (if cycles are counted without designated start). However cycles may have symmetry (rotations). But we can just count cycles with designated start; easier to count functional permutations that are a single cycle (since start is arbitrary but we can fix start). The functional permutation approach already counts each cycle once (since each vertex has exactly one outgoing edge). However a given permutation may produce a single cycle covering all vertices; that cycle can be started at any vertex to produce a sequence. But we have designated start (12,12). So each permutation corresponds to exactly one sequence (starting at (12,12) and following edges). So N equals number of permutations with a single cycle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870198109,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870199221,"data":"       | Thus N = number of permutations π of Z_n × Z_n such that for each (i,j), π(i,j) = (i+1,j) or (i,j+1) (mod n). Let's denote n=12.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870200663,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.87020174,"data":"       | Now we need to count such permutations.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870202887,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870204028,"data":"       | Observation: This is similar to counting \"Eulerian tours\" in a 2-regular directed graph? Wait each vertex has outdegree 2, indegree 2. A permutation selects one outgoing edge per vertex such that indegree also 1. This is equivalent to selecting a set of edges that forms a 1-regular directed subgraph (each vertex indegree=outdegree=1). That's a \"directed 1-factor\" (also called a \"perfect matching\" in bipartite representation). Since each vertex has exactly two possible outgoing edges (east or north), we can think of labeling each vertex with a binary variable indicating which edge is selected. The indegree condition imposes constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870206441,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870207563,"data":"       | Let's define binary variable x_{i,j} = 0 if vertex (i,j) maps to east neighbor (i+1,j), 1 if maps to north neighbor (i,j+1). Then the outdegree condition is satisfied automatically. The indegree condition: For each vertex (i,j), exactly one incoming edge must be selected. Which vertices can map to (i,j)? They are the west neighbor (i-1,j) via east move (if that neighbor chooses east) and the south neighbor (i,j-1) via north move (if that neighbor chooses north). So indegree condition: exactly one of x_{i-1,j} = 0 (east from west neighbor) and x_{i,j-1}=1 (north from south neighbor) must be true. In other words, for each (i,j):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870209805,"data":"       | [x_{i-1,j} == 0] + [x_{i,j-1} == 1] = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870211796,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870212925,"data":"       | Where indices modulo n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870214039,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870215111,"data":"       | We can rephrase using binary variables: Let a_{i,j}=0 for east, 1 for north. Then condition:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870216369,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870217445,"data":"       | (1 - a_{i-1,j}) + a_{i,j-1} = 1\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870220205,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870221657,"data":"       | Because a=0 means east (so 1 - a = 1 if east else 0). So we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870222884,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870223991,"data":"       | 1 - a_{i-1,j} + a_{i,j-1} = 1\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870225152,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870226267,"data":"       | =\u003e - a_{i-1,j} + a_{i,j-1} = 0\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870227445,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870228529,"data":"       | =\u003e a_{i,j-1} = a_{i-1,j}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870229705,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870230829,"data":"       | Thus the condition simplifies to equality of certain variables: a_{i,j-1} = a_{i-1,j} for all (i,j). Indices modulo n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870232141,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870233259,"data":"       | Thus we have a system of equations over binary values (0 or 1): a_{i,j-1} = a_{i-1,j}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.8702345,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870235585,"data":"       | This is reminiscent of a \"checkerboard\" condition leading to all a's being equal? Let's analyze.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870236947,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870238033,"data":"       | We can rewrite as a_{i,j} = a_{i+1,j+1} for all i,j (shifting indices). Because replace i-\u003ei+1, j-\u003ej+1: a_{i+1,j} = a_{i,j+1}. Wait original: a_{i,j-1} = a_{i-1,j}. Add 1 to both indices: a_{i+1,j} = a_{i,j+1}. Yes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870239585,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870240667,"data":"       | Thus condition: For all i,j, a_{i+1,j} = a_{i,j+1}. So moving one step east in the first coordinate equals moving one step north in the second coordinate.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870242038,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.87024341,"data":"       | This suggests that the pattern of a's is constant along diagonals (i - j constant). Because equality across shift (i+1,j) vs (i,j+1) says along direction (1,-1) they are equal. Indeed, define d = i - j (mod n). Then a_{i,j} depends only on d. Let's verify: Suppose we have a_{i,j} = f(i - j) for some function f: Z_n -\u003e {0,1}. Then a_{i+1,j} = f(i+1 - j) = f((i - j) + 1). a_{i,j+1} = f(i - (j+1)) = f((i - j) - 1). Wait not equal unless f is constant? Let's compute: Condition a_{i+1,j} = a_{i,j+1}. That would require f(d+1) = f(d-1) for all d. This implies f is periodic with period 2: f(d+2) = f(d). So f can be any 2-periodic function on Z_n. Since n=12 (even), Z_12 has elements 0..11. A 2-periodic function is determined by its values on parity classes: even and odd. So we have two possibilities: assign value 0 to even, 1 to odd; or vice versa; also constant functions (both 0 or both 1) also satisfy? Let's test: constant function: f(d) = c for all d. Then f(d+1) = c = f(d-1) holds. So constant works. Also any function where f(d) = g(d mod 2) works: i.e., depends only on parity. That's 2^2 = 4 possibilities (since two parity classes each can be assigned 0 or 1). But note constant functions are included in that (both parity same). So total possibilities = 4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870246731,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870247848,"data":"       | But we must also consider that a_{i,j} are binary, and the condition a_{i+1,j}=a_{i,j+1} for all i,j yields that the whole matrix is determined by values on a single row and column? Let's solve more systematically.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870251575,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870252743,"data":"       | Take any (i,j). Using equality repeatedly, we can shift along diagonal: a_{i,j} = a_{i+1,j-1} = a_{i+2,j-2} = ... . So along lines of constant (i+j) maybe? Wait (i+1,j) = (i,j+1) equality suggests moving east then north equals north then east? Actually the equality relates a at positions offset by (1,0) vs (0,1). This suggests that a is constant along lines of slope 1 (i - j constant). Let's test: Suppose we have positions (i,j) and (i+1,j+1). Using equality: a_{i+1,j+1} = a_{i+2,j} (by shifting) = a_{i+1,j+2} ... Not straightforward. Let's compute using Python for small n to see solutions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870254911,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870256043,"data":"       | But we derived condition: a_{i,j-1} = a_{i-1,j} for all i,j. Equivalent to a_{i+1,j} = a_{i,j+1}. This is a type of discrete Cauchy equation; solutions are functions of i - j (i.e., along anti-diagonals) that are periodic with period 2? Let's test: Suppose define b_k = a_{i,j} where k = i - j (mod n). Then a_{i+1,j} = b_{(i+1)-j} = b_{k+1}. a_{i,j+1} = b_{i-(j+1)} = b_{k-1}. Condition requires b_{k+1} = b_{k-1} for all k. So indeed b_{k+2}=b_k for all k. So b is 2-periodic. So there are 2 choices for b_0 (even parity) and 2 choices for b_1 (odd parity). So 4 possibilities.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870258148,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870259306,"data":"       | Thus there are exactly 4 assignments of a_{i,j} satisfying indegree condition. Let's verify by brute force for small n (e.g., n=3) enumerating all 2^{n^2} assignments and checking indegree condition; we should get 4 solutions for any n? Let's test for n=3 (odd). The condition b_{k+2}=b_k still holds but because n=3, period 2 may not be compatible with modulo 3. Let's test.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870261098,"data":"       | \n"}
