,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870535238,"data":"       | analysisWe see patterns: each solution seems to be a matrix where each row is a cyclic shift of the previous row? Let's examine first solution all zeros; last all ones; others appear to have pattern where each row is a cyclic shift of previous row by 1 to the right? Let's check second solution:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870536883,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870537963,"data":"       | [0,0,1]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870539082,"data":"       | [0,1,0]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870540476,"data":"       | [1,0,0] Indeed each row is previous row shifted right by 1 (or left). This suggests that the condition a_{i+1,j}=a_{i,j+1} forces each row to be shift of previous row. Indeed a_{i+1,j} = a_{i,j+1} means entry at row i+1 column j equals entry at row i column j+1. So row i+1 is row i shifted left by 1 (since column j corresponds to column j+1 in previous row). So the whole matrix is determined by the first row; then each subsequent row is the previous row shifted left by 1 (or right depending orientation). So the condition reduces to a matrix where each row is a cyclic shift of the previous row.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870542623,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870543729,"data":"       | Thus the degrees of freedom equal number of possible first rows (length n) of bits, which is 2^n. Indeed for any first row r[0..n-1], we can define a_{i,j} = r[(j+i) mod n] maybe. Let's verify: Starting with row0 = r_j. Then row1 (i=1) must be r_{j+1}, etc. So a_{i,j} = r_{j+i} (mod n). Check condition: a_{i+1,j} = r_{j+i+1} ; a_{i,j+1} = r_{j+1+i} = r_{j+i+1}. So holds. So any binary vector r of length n yields a solution.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870545559,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870546646,"data":"       | Thus number of assignments = 2^n. Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870547804,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870548945,"data":"       | Now, each assignment corresponds to a functional permutation (each vertex selects east if a=0 else north). But does every such assignment guarantee indegree=1? We derived condition from indegree=1 and got equality condition a_{i+1,j}=a_{i,j+1}. So any assignment satisfying that yields indegree=1. So all 2^n assignments correspond to permutations where each vertex has indegree=1 (i.e., a bijection). However does each such assignment necessarily produce a single cycle covering all vertices? It could produce multiple disjoint cycles (i.e., permutation with several cycles). The indegree=outdegree=1 condition ensures the functional digraph decomposes into cycles (disjoint). We need the permutation to be a single cycle (Hamiltonian). So among the 2^n assignments, some produce multiple cycles. Need to count those that produce exactly one cycle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870552895,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870554016,"data":"       | Thus N is the number of assignments (binary vectors r of length n) such that the resulting permutation consists of a single cycle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870555363,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870556476,"data":"       | Now we need to analyze the structure of cycles given a pattern r.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870557685,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870559007,"data":"       | Given a_{i,j} = r_{i+j} (mod n) (since row i shift by i). Let's define indices modulo n. So a_{i,j} depends only on s = i+j mod n. Let s = i+j (mod n). Then a_{i,j} = r_s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870560424,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870561533,"data":"       | Now define the permutation mapping: from (i,j) to (i+1,j) if a_{i,j}=0 else to (i,j+1) if a_{i,j}=1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870562804,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870563909,"data":"       | Thus the rule depends on s = i+j. So we can analyze the dynamics.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870565113,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.87056627,"data":"       | Let’s consider the state (i,j). Compute s = i+j (mod n). If r_s = 0, we move east: (i+1,j). Then new sum s' = (i+1)+j = s+1 (mod n). If r_s = 1, we move north: (i,j+1). Then s' = i+(j+1) = s+1 (mod n) as well! Indeed, regardless of which move we take, the sum i+j increases by 1 modulo n. Because moving east increments i, moving north increments j, both increase sum by 1. So the sum modulo n always increments by 1 each step. Therefore after n steps, the sum cycles through all residues. Since we have total of n^2 steps (144 for n=12), the sum will increase by 1 each move, so after n^2 steps, sum increments by n^2 ≡ 0 (mod n). So consistent.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870568571,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870569685,"data":"       | Thus the dynamics of s is deterministic: s(t) = s(0) + t (mod n). Since we start at (0,0) (12 corresponds to 0), s(0)=0. So after t steps, s = t mod n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870571067,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870572175,"data":"       | Now, the path of positions also depends on which hand moves each step, determined by r_{s}. So we can think of the process as: At step t (starting at 0), we have current sum s = t mod n. The decision r_s determines whether we increment i (east) or j (north). So the sequence of moves is predetermined by r vector: for each s = 0,...,n-1 (repeated n times as we cycle through s), we apply the same move each time we encounter that s (since r_s is fixed). Because s repeats every n steps, we will apply each move pattern n times.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870574185,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870575555,"data":"       | Thus the permutation can be described as: Starting at (0,0). For t from 0 to n^2-1, we look at s = t mod n, and if r_s = 0 we increment i, else increment j. So after n steps (one full cycle of s), we have applied each r_s exactly once, resulting in some net change in i and j (call a = number of zeros in r, b = number of ones). After n steps, i increased by a, j increased by b. Since a+b=n. So after n steps, we are at (a,b) modulo n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870577408,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870578502,"data":"       | Now after another n steps (next cycle of s), we will again apply the same sequence of moves, adding again a to i and b to j. So after k cycles (k from 0 to n-1), we will be at (k*a, k*b) modulo n. After n cycles (k=n), we return to (0,0) because n*a ≡ 0 (mod n) and n*b ≡ 0. So indeed the permutation's orbit structure corresponds to the subgroup generated by (a,b) in Z_n × Z_n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870581841,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870583036,"data":"       | Specifically, the mapping π corresponds to moving from a state (i,j) to (i + δ_i, j + δ_j) where δ_i = 1 if r_{i+j}=0 else 0? Wait the mapping depends on current sum s = i+j. So the displacement vector for a given state is either (1,0) or (0,1). So the permutation is not a simple translation; but after n steps, the net displacement is (a,b). So the permutation's cycles correspond to the orbit under repeated application of the mapping. Since after n steps we shift by (a,b). So after k*n steps, shift by k*(a,b). The overall order of the permutation (length of cycle) is n * order of (a,b) in the group Z_n × Z_n, i.e., the smallest positive integer k such that k*(a,b) ≡ (0,0) mod n. That's n / gcd(n, d) where d =? Actually order of (a,b) is n / g where g = gcd(n, a, b)? Let's compute.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.87058561,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870586714,"data":"       | In Z_n × Z_n, the order of element (a,b) is n / g where g = gcd(n, a, b?) Let's recall: The order of (a,b) is the smallest positive k such that k*a ≡ 0 (mod n) and k*b ≡ 0 (mod n). This is equivalent to n dividing k*a and k*b. The minimal such k is n / gcd(n, a, b?) Actually need to satisfy both. Let g1 = gcd(a,n), g2 = gcd(b,n). Then k must be multiple of n/g1 and n/g2, so k must be lcm(n/g1, n/g2) = n / gcd(g1,g2). Since g1 = gcd(a,n), g2 = gcd(b,n). So order = n / gcd(gcd(a,n), gcd(b,n)). But since a+b=n, there may be simplifications. Also a,b are between 0..n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870588862,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870590166,"data":"       | We need the permutation to be a single cycle covering all n^2 vertices. That means its order must be n^2. Since the permutation is a bijection on n^2 elements, the length of its unique cycle equals order of the permutation (since it's a single cycle). The order of the permutation equals the least positive integer L such that applying mapping L times returns to start for all vertices. Since the mapping is deterministic, the order is the length of the cycle containing start, which must be n^2 for Hamiltonian.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870592165,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870593263,"data":"       | We derived that after n steps, the displacement is (a,b). So after n*k steps, displacement is k*(a,b). To return to start, we need k*(a,b) ≡ (0,0) mod n. The minimal such k is the order of (a,b). Then total steps to return is n * order_of_(a,b). For the cycle length to be n^2, we need order_of_(a,b) = n. So we require that (a,b) has order n in Z_n × Z_n. That is, the smallest k such that k*a ≡ 0 (mod n) and k*b ≡ 0 (mod n) is n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870595243,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870596364,"data":"       | Thus we need (a,b) not to have any smaller order. Equivalent to: gcd(a,n) = 1 and gcd(b,n) = 1? Let's analyze.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870597637,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870598757,"data":"       | If k*a ≡ 0 mod n, then n | k*a. Minimal k such that n divides k*a is n / gcd(a,n). Similarly for b. So order_of_(a,b) = lcm( n / gcd(a,n), n / gcd(b,n) ) = n / gcd( gcd(a,n), gcd(b,n) ). To equal n, we need denominator = 1, i.e., gcd( gcd(a,n), gcd(b,n) ) = 1. Since gcd(a,n) and gcd(b,n) are divisors of n, their gcd is also a divisor of n. So we need the gcd of those two gcds to be 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870601877,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870602999,"data":"       | But note that a+b=n. Let's explore possibilities for n=12.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870604183,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870605285,"data":"       | Let a = number of zeros in r (i.e., count of positions where we move east). b = n - a = number of ones (north moves). So a in 0..12.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870606605,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870607997,"data":"       | We need the condition that the permutation is a single cycle (order n^2). That reduces to requiring that the order of (a,b) in Z_n×Z_n is n, i.e., that the pair (a,b) is a generator of the additive group? Actually the additive group Z_n×Z_n is not cyclic unless n is 1. Its exponent is n. The order of an element (a,b) is n / d where d = gcd(a,b,n?) Let's compute more concretely using formula: order = n / g where g = gcd(a,b,n). Let's test: Suppose n=12, a=4,b=8. gcd(4,8,12) = 4? Actually gcd(4,8,12)=4. Then order = 12/4=3. Let's compute using earlier formula: n/gcd(gcd(a,n),gcd(b,n)). gcd(a,n)=4, gcd(b,n)=4, gcd(4,4)=4, order=12/4=3 matches. Suppose a=5,b=7. gcd(5,12)=1, gcd(7,12)=1, gcd(1,1)=1, order=12. Good. Suppose a=6,b=6. gcd(6,12)=6, both, gcd=6, order=12/6=2. So condition for order = n is that gcd(gcd(a,n), gcd(b,n)) = 1. Since each gcd(a,n) divides n, the only way their gcd is 1 is that at least one of them is 1, and the other may have any gcd but must be coprime to that. Actually if one is 1, then gcd(1, anything) = 1, so order = n. So condition reduces to: at least one of a or b is relatively prime to n (i.e., coprime with 12). Because if both have gcd \u003e1, then their gcd will be at least 2, making order less than n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870611333,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870612483,"data":"       | Thus for n=12, we need either a is coprime to 12 or b is coprime to 12 (or both). Since b = 12 - a, condition is: a is coprime to 12 or (12 - a) is coprime to 12. Since coprime to 12 means odd and not divisible by 3, i.e., numbers congruent to 1,5,7,11 mod 12. Among 0..12, the numbers coprime to 12 are: 1,5,7,11. Also maybe 13 etc but not in range.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870614179,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870615276,"data":"       | Thus acceptable a values: a ∈ {1,5,7,11} or 12-a ∈ {1,5,7,11}. Let's compute set.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.870616581,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.87061768,"data":"       | Compute a values 0..12 where either a ∈ {1,5,7,11} or 12-a ∈ {1,5,7,11}. That includes a values: 1,5,7,11 (since they themselves coprime), plus values where 12-a in set: a = 11,7,5,1 again (same). So set is {1,5,7,11}. Also maybe a=0? 12-0=12 not coprime. a=12? 12 coprime? no. So only those four values.\n"}
