,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.871948619,"data":"       | analysisBrute force naive for 144 vertices impossible. But for small m,n we can compute via algorithm using inclusion-exclusion? Or using counting of permutations with constraints. This is like counting \"Latin squares\" of order? Not exactly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.871950284,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.871951721,"data":"       | We can think of representing permutation as a collection of cycles formed by moves east or north. Because each step moves east or north, a cycle must have total displacement zero modulo m and n. Since each move east contributes +1 in i-coordinate, each move north contributes +1 in j-coordinate. For a cycle of length L, the number of east moves equals a multiple of m (since net i displacement 0 mod m) and number of north moves equals multiple of n. So each cycle must have east moves count ≡0 mod m and north moves count ≡0 mod n. Since moves are only +1 each, the net displacement after the cycle is (E mod m, N mod n) = (0,0). So E ≡0 mod m, N≡0 mod n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.871954168,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.871955298,"data":"       | Furthermore, the total number of east moves across all cycles equals total number of vertices with outgoing east (i.e., count of vertices where σ(v) = v + (1,0)). Similarly north moves count equals total vertices with outgoing north. Since each vertex chooses exactly one direction, total east moves + north moves = N (total vertices). Let a = number of east moves. Then a ≡0 mod m (since sum of east moves across each cycle each multiple of m). Also (N-a) ≡0 mod n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.871957415,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.871958503,"data":"       | Thus a must satisfy: a ≡ 0 (mod m) and N-a ≡ 0 (mod n). With N = m n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.871959842,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.871960991,"data":"       | Thus a ≡0 mod m and a ≡ N (mod n). Since N ≡0 mod n, a ≡0 mod n as well? Wait N=mn is divisible by n. So N ≡0 (mod n). Then condition N-a ≡0 (mod n) =\u003e a ≡ N (mod n) ≡0 (mod n). So a must be divisible by both m and n. Hence a must be multiple of lcm(m,n). Since m=n=12, lcm =12. So a must be multiple of 12. Also a between 0 and N (144). So a can be 0,12,24,...,144. So number of east moves could be any multiple of 12.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.871963133,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.871964259,"data":"       | Now, given a, we need to count permutations where exactly a vertices map east (right), and N-a map north (up), and the permutation condition holds.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.871965707,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.871967568,"data":"       | Now think of representing permutation as a bijection f: Z_m×Z_n → Z_m×Z_n, where f(i,j) = (i+δ_i, j+δ_j) with δ_i,δ_j ∈ {0,1} and not both zero; i.e., δ_i+δ_j=1. So δ_i indicates east move; δ_j indicates north. So each vertex assigned a 0/1 variable e_{i,j} where e=1 if move east, else north. Then f(i,j) = (i+e_{i,j}, j+1-e_{i,j}) (mod m,n). That's because if east then j unchanged, else north increments j.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.871969786,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.871971149,"data":"       | Now the permutation condition (bijection) is that mapping is one-to-one. Equivalent to condition that for each (i,j), there is exactly one preimage: there exists unique (i',j') such that f(i',j') = (i,j). The preimage possibilities: either (i-1,j) with e=1 (east from left), or (i,j-1) with e=0 (north from below). So the condition can be expressed as: For all (i,j), exactly one of the following holds: e_{i-1,j}=1 or e_{i,j-1}=0 (where indices modulo). And not both.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.871974697,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.871975833,"data":"       | Thus we have constraints:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.871977027,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.871978156,"data":"       | For each (i,j), (e_{i-1,j} == 1) XOR (e_{i,j-1} == 0). Because exactly one preimage.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.871979485,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.871980591,"data":"       | Equivalently, (e_{i-1,j} == 1) + (e_{i,j-1} == 0) = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.871981829,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.871982922,"data":"       | We can rewrite in binary terms: Let e_{i,j} ∈ {0,1} where 1 = east, 0 = north.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.871984277,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.871985437,"data":"       | Then condition: (e_{i-1,j}) + (1 - e_{i,j-1}) = 1 (mod? Actually need equality over integers not modulo: either left neighbor east (1) and below neighbor north (1)?? Wait compute: e_{i-1,j}=1 gives left neighbor moves east (i.e., to (i,j)). e_{i,j-1}=0 gives below neighbor moves north (to (i,j)). We need exactly one of these to be true. So the sum of truth values must be 1. Since truth values are binary, we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.871987326,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.871988428,"data":"       | e_{i-1,j} + (1 - e_{i,j-1}) = 1\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.871989598,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.8719907,"data":"       | =\u003e e_{i-1,j} - e_{i,j-1} + 1 = 1\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.871991948,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.871993037,"data":"       | =\u003e e_{i-1,j} = e_{i,j-1}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.871994222,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.871995651,"data":"       | Thus condition simplifies to equality: e_{i-1,j} = e_{i,j-1} for all i,j (mod m,n). Wait let's verify: Suppose e_{i-1,j}=1 and e_{i,j-1}=0, then left neighbor east and below neighbor north both true -\u003e sum would be 2 \u003e1, not allowed. Our derived equation gave equality, which would not allow both true. Let's test: If e_{i-1,j}=1, e_{i,j-1}=0, then equality fails (1 ≠ 0). So not allowed. If e_{i-1,j}=0, e_{i,j-1}=1, then left neighbor not east, below neighbor not north (since e=1 means north). Wait below neighbor north condition is e_{i,j-1}=0, but if e=1 then it's east, not north. So condition would be false (no preimage). That also not allowed. So only allowed cases are when exactly one of the conditions holds: either left neighbor east (e_{i-1,j}=1) and below neighbor north false (e_{i,j-1}=1) (since north false) -\u003e then sum =1. Or left neighbor east false (e_{i-1,j}=0) and below neighbor north true (e_{i,j-1}=0) -\u003e sum=1. That gives two possibilities:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.871998677,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.87199981,"data":"       | Case A: e_{i-1,j}=1 and e_{i,j-1}=1 (since e_{i,j-1}=1 means north? Wait e=1 is east, so north condition is e=0. So if e_{i,j-1}=1, then below neighbor moves east, not north, so below neighbor does NOT map to (i,j). So only left neighbor provides preimage. So condition: left neighbor east (1), below neighbor not north (i.e., e=1). So e_{i-1,j}=1, e_{i,j-1}=1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872003306,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872004444,"data":"       | Case B: left neighbor not east (e_{i-1,j}=0) and below neighbor north (e_{i,j-1}=0). So e_{i-1,j}=0, e_{i,j-1}=0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872005833,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872006975,"data":"       | Thus condition is e_{i-1,j} = e_{i,j-1}. Indeed both cases give equality (both 0 or both 1). Good! So equality condition works! Because when both are 1, left neighbor east true, below neighbor north false (since e=1). Wait but we need exactly one preimage. In case both 1, left neighbor east true (preimage), below neighbor north false. So exactly one preimage. In case both 0, left neighbor east false, below neighbor north true (since e=0). So exactly one preimage. So equality condition ensures exactly one preimage. Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872009056,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.87201017,"data":"       | Thus the permutation condition reduces to simple local equality: For all (i,j),\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872011454,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.87201258,"data":"       | e_{i-1,j} = e_{i,j-1} (mod indices).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872013784,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872014933,"data":"       | Thus the binary field values must satisfy that each cell equals the cell to its north-west diagonal? Let's shift indices: define e_{i,j} = e_{i+1,j+1} for all i,j (mod m,n). This indicates that e is constant along lines of slope -1? Let's deduce: The condition e_{i-1,j} = e_{i,j-1} can be rewritten as e_{i,j} = e_{i+1,j+1} (by increment both indices). So indeed e is constant along the direction (1,1) (i.e., diagonal down-right). So the grid's values are constant on each diagonal (mod m,n) with slope 1 (i.e., moving (+1,+1) leaves value same). Therefore the pattern repeats along such diagonal cycles.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872017153,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872018474,"data":"       | Thus the entire 12x12 torus's values are determined by values on a set of representatives of the equivalence classes of (i,j) under relation (i,j) ~ (i+1,j+1). The equivalence classes are the orbits of the vector (1,1). Since we are on torus Z_m×Z_n with m=n=12, the orbit size is the order of (1,1) in the group, which is the smallest k\u003e0 such that k*(1,1) = (0,0) mod (12,12). That is k ≡0 mod12. So orbit size =12. So there are 12 distinct diagonals (since total vertices 144, each orbit size 12 -\u003e 144/12=12 orbits). Indeed the diagonals correspond to sets where i-j (mod 12) is constant? Let's compute: (i,j) and (i+1,j+1) have same difference i-j. So classes are determined by difference d = i - j (mod 12). So there are 12 possible differences, each diagonal of length 12.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872021177,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872022287,"data":"       | Thus the condition reduces to: e_{i,j} depends only on i-j (mod 12). So we can define a function f: Z_12 -\u003e {0,1} where f(d) = e_{i,j} for any (i,j) with i-j = d. So we need to assign 0/1 to each of the 12 diagonals.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.87202384,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872024945,"data":"       | Now we also need the global counts: total east moves a = sum over all vertices of e_{i,j} = sum_{d} (size of diagonal) * f(d). Each diagonal size =12. So a = 12 * (number of diagonals assigned value 1). So a is multiple of 12, as we previously deduced. Let k = number of diagonals with e=1 (east). Then a = 12k, where k ∈ {0,...,12}. Indeed matches earlier condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872028167,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872029362,"data":"       | Thus any assignment of east/north to each diagonal yields a configuration satisfying permutation condition! Let's verify with small example via enumeration for m=n=2 (size 4). There are 2 diagonals (differences 0 and 1). The rule says e constant on each diagonal. So there are 2^2 =4 assignments. Let's brute force compute permutations for 2x2 torus to see if there are indeed 4 permutations.assistantanalysis to=python codedef count_perm_diag(m,n):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.87203136,"data":"       |     # generate all assignments f for each difference class\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872032627,"data":"       |     diff_classes = {}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872033816,"data":"       |     for i in range(m):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872034993,"data":"       |         for j in range(n):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872036202,"data":"       |             d = (i - j) % n  # n==m assumed for torus? We'll use n\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872037468,"data":"       |             diff_classes.setdefault(d, []).append((i,j))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872038893,"data":"       |     # there are n classes if m=n\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872040143,"data":"       |     total = 0\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872041348,"data":"       |     for mask in range(1\u003c\u003cn):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872042557,"data":"       |         e = {}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872043725,"data":"       |         for d in range(n):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.8720449,"data":"       |             val = (mask\u003e\u003ed)\u00261\n"}
