,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872119952,"data":"       | Now for 12x12, number of assignments = 2^{12} = 4096. But we must also ensure that the permutation is a single cycle covering all vertices? The problem states that \"every possible positioning of the hands appears exactly once\" during the sequence of 144 movements, which implies the permutation should be a single cycle (Hamiltonian cycle) not multiple cycles. Because if permutation had multiple cycles, then after some steps we would revisit a state earlier than covering all positions. Actually we need a sequence of moves where each state after a move is distinct until the final state after 144 moves equals initial. That is precisely a Hamiltonian cycle of length 144, i.e., permutation consisting of a single 144-cycle. So we need to count assignments that yield a single directed cycle, not a collection of disjoint cycles.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872122684,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872123897,"data":"       | Thus among the 2^{12} permutations, we need to count those where the functional digraph is a single cycle (a permutation with one orbit). In permutation language, we need permutations of 144 elements where each element maps to either (i+1,j) or (i,j+1) and the permutation is a single cycle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872125548,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872126694,"data":"       | Now we need to count the number of assignments f (diagonal values) that produce a single cycle. Let's analyze structure of cycles in such permutations given the diagonal pattern.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.87212816,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872129322,"data":"       | Given e constant on each diagonal (difference class), we can analyze the dynamics. Let's define coordinates (i,j). The mapping adds vector (1,0) if e=1 else (0,1). Since e depends only on d = i-j (mod 12). So step vector at (i,j) is either east or north based on d.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872130951,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872132335,"data":"       | Thus the walk of the permutation starting from any point will follow a deterministic rule: at each step, if current difference d = i-j, the move direction is determined by f(d). Since moving east increments i, leaving j unchanged, so new difference d' = (i+1) - j = d+1 (mod 12). Moving north increments j, so d' = i - (j+1) = d-1 (mod 12). So each move changes the difference by +1 if move east, -1 if move north (mod 12). Therefore the evolution of d along the cycle follows a walk on Z_12 where each step is either +1 or -1, according to the value of f at current d: if f(d)=1 (east), next d = d+1; if f(d)=0 (north), next d = d-1. So the sequence of differences along a trajectory is a deterministic walk given f.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872134744,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872135894,"data":"       | Now note that the actual coordinates (i,j) also change; but the difference dynamics fully determine the path because the moves also affect the absolute positions. However we also need to know when the walk returns to original (i,j). Since the permutation is a bijection, each orbit corresponds to a cycle. The difference walk is periodic; we can analyze cycles based on the function f.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872137765,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872138879,"data":"       | Observation: Since the mapping is deterministic, each vertex belongs to exactly one cycle. The cycles correspond to orbits of the map on the set of vertices. Since the map only depends on difference d, the structure may be decomposed into cycles that correspond to \"lifts\" of cycles in the difference walk.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872140642,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.87214318,"data":"       | Let's attempt to understand mapping in terms of (i,j) = (starting point) + something. Suppose we start at (i0,j0). After t steps, we have moved east e times and north n times, with e+n = t. The total change in i = e (mod 12), total change in j = n (mod 12). Also difference after t steps: d_t = i_t - j_t = (i0 - j0) + (e - n) mod 12. But we also have rule that each step's direction depends on current d. So the sequence of directions is determined by f evaluated at successive d's.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872145342,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872146808,"data":"       | Thus the walk on differences is a deterministic walk on the cycle graph of 12 nodes where at each node d, you either go to d+1 (if f(d)=1) or to d-1 (if f(d)=0). That is a directed graph where each node has outdegree 1 (since f(d) determines direction). This is exactly a functional digraph on 12 nodes. Since each node has outdegree 1, each component consists of a directed cycle with trees feeding into it. However because indegree also 1? Let's compute indegree: For node d, incoming edges come from d-1 if f(d-1)=1 (since moving east from d-1 leads to d) or from d+1 if f(d+1)=0 (moving north from d+1 leads to d). So indegree may be 0,1,2. For the permutation on vertices to be bijective, we need the full mapping on the 144 vertices to be bijective. That condition is already satisfied by equality condition; it doesn't impose indegree 1 on difference nodes. So difference graph may have nodes with indegree 2 or 0, leading to multiple cycles in original permutation? Let's analyze.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872149671,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872150817,"data":"       | Given a function f, the map on differences is deterministic with outdegree 1 each node. Starting from any difference d0, we follow edges: d_{t+1} = d_t + (1 if f(d_t)=1 else -1). This is a functional digraph on 12 nodes. Since each node has outdegree 1, each component has a unique directed cycle. There may be trees feeding into cycle (if some nodes have indegree 0). However our original permutation on 144 vertices may correspond to multiple cycles that are lifts of cycles in the difference digraph, each of length maybe multiple of 12.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872152911,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872154068,"data":"       | Specifically, consider a cycle C in the difference digraph of length L (i.e., sequence of distinct differences d0,d1,...,d_{L-1} returning to d0). Then for each starting absolute coordinate (i0,j0) such that i0 - j0 = d0 (i.e., any of the 12 vertices on that diagonal), the orbit under the full permutation will follow the same pattern of differences, and after each step also shifts i and j accordingly. The path may return to original vertex after some number of steps equal to L * 12? Let's analyze.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872156133,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872157441,"data":"       | If we have a difference cycle of length L, then after L steps, the difference returns to starting d0, but the absolute coordinates may have shifted by some net vector (Δi, Δj). Let's compute net displacement after traversing the L-step cycle: each step either east (+1,0) or north (0,+1). So net displacement (Δi,Δj) = (E, N) where E = number of east steps = number of times we visited nodes with f=1, N = L - E (north steps). Since the difference change per east is +1, per north is -1, net change in difference over L steps is E - N = (E - (L-E)) = 2E - L, which must be 0 mod 12 because after L steps we return to same difference d0 (mod 12). Indeed, since the difference cycle length L returns to same node, the cumulative difference change (E - N) ≡ 0 (mod 12). So 2E - L ≡ 0 (mod 12). That's a constraint linking L and number of east steps.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872161728,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872162928,"data":"       | Now, after L steps, the absolute position changes by (E,N) modulo (12,12). So the permutation may map a vertex to another vertex with same difference (since difference unchanged) but offset by (E,N). Since both coordinates are modulo 12, after some multiple of L steps we might return to original vertex.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872164637,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872166126,"data":"       | Specifically, starting from a vertex v on diagonal d0, after L steps we end at v' = v + (E,N) (mod 12). Since v and v' have same difference (i - j), they lie on same diagonal. There are 12 vertices on that diagonal. The map restricted to that diagonal essentially acts as a cyclic shift by (E,N). Since moving along diagonal changes both i and j by same amount: moving one step along diagonal (i+1,j+1) adds (1,1). Our shift (E,N) may not be a multiple of (1,1). But note that on a given diagonal, moving along the diagonal corresponds to adding (1,1). So shift (E,N) can be expressed as k*(1,1) + maybe something else? However because we stay on same diagonal, the difference i-j is unchanged, so E - N ≡ 0 (mod 12). Indeed we have that condition. So E - N = 12t for some integer t. Then (E,N) = (N+12t, N) = N*(1,1) + 12t*(1,0). But modulo 12, (E,N) ≡ (N, N) (since 12t ≡0). Thus modulo 12, shift is (N,N) which is a multiple of (1,1). So indeed after L steps, we move along the diagonal by N steps (mod12). So v' = v + (N,N) modulo 12. Since N may be coprime to 12, repeated application of this shift will eventually cycle through all 12 vertices on that diagonal. The order of shift is 12 / gcd(N,12). Therefore the orbit size of a vertex is L * (12 / gcd(N,12)). The total number of vertices in that orbit equals L times the length of the shift cycle on the diagonal.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872169755,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872170913,"data":"       | If we want the whole permutation to be a single cycle covering all 144 vertices, we need that the functional digraph on differences consist of a single cycle (so L = 12) and that the shift along diagonal after one full cycle (i.e., after L steps) moves by N steps where N is relatively prime to 12 (i.e., gcd(N,12)=1) so that the orbit traverses all 12 vertices on each diagonal, leading to total size 12*12 =144.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872172776,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.87217388,"data":"       | Thus conditions:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872175026,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3925.872176235,"data":"       | 1) The difference graph (size 12) must be a single directed cycle covering all 12 nodes (i.e., permutation of Z_12 with outdegree 1 defined by step +1 or -1 depending on f). That means the functional digraph must be a single cycle (no trees). Since each node has outdegree 1, that means indegree also must be 1 for each node to avoid trees (i.e., each node has exactly one predecessor). So the mapping must be a permutation of the 12 differences, i.e., a bijection. Since each node has outdegree 1, it's a permutation if indegree 1 each. That corresponds to the condition that the mapping d → d+1 if f(d)=1 else d-1 is a bijection (i.e., a permutation). This is true iff the map is invertible: each node has exactly one predecessor, which will be either d-1 if f(d-1)=1 (since from d-1 east leads to d) or d+1 if f(d+1)=0 (north from d+1 leads to d). For indegree to be exactly 1, we need that for each d, exactly one of those two conditions holds. That is exactly the same condition as before but on difference graph: f(d-1) = 1 XOR f(d+1) = 0? Wait compute: For node d, its possible predecessors are d-1 (if f(d-1)=1) and d+1 (if f(d+1)=0). We need exactly one of these to be true. So condition: f(d-1) = 1 - f(d+1). In binary, f(d-1) + f(d+1) = 1. That's similar to a constraint.\n"}
