,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184337302,"data":"       | We need to label vertices A, B, C, D such that AB is an edge (i.e., B = A + s*u for some edge direction). CD is a parallel edge (i.e., D = C + s*u). So AB and CD are parallel edges (same direction). Also AC is a diagonal of a face: So C = A + s*v + s*w? Wait a face diagonal is across a face, which is sum of two edge vectors that are adjacent (perpendicular). So AC = s*v + s*w (or s*u + s*v, etc). Similarly, BD is a diagonal of a face: D = B + s*v + s*w? Let's test.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184339118,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184340308,"data":"       | Let’s set up: Choose A as a vertex. Let AB be along u direction: B = A + s*u. Let AC be across a face that contains A but not AB (i.e., across the face formed by vectors v and w). So C = A + s*v + s*w (i.e., opposite corner of that face). Then AC is a face diagonal (length s√2). Then D must be such that CD is parallel to AB (i.e., D = C + s*u). Indeed D = C + s*u = A + s*u + s*v + s*w = B + s*v + s*w. Then BD = D - B = s*v + s*w, which is also a face diagonal (same as AC). So BD is a diagonal of the face containing B and vectors v,w. So BD is a face diagonal. So this labeling satisfies conditions: AB and CD are parallel edges (both along u). AC and BD are face diagonals (both equal to s*v + s*w). So rectangle ABDC consists of points A, B, D, C: AB is edge along u, BD is diagonal across face (v+w), DC is edge along -u (parallel to AB), CA is diagonal across face (v+w) opposite direction. So rectangle ABDC is indeed a rectangle with sides AB (length s) and BD (length s√2), and they are perpendicular? Are u and (v+w) perpendicular? Since u is orthogonal to both v and w, u is orthogonal to v and w individually, but not necessarily to v+w? Actually dot(u, v+w) = dot(u,v)+dot(u,w)=0+0=0. So u is orthogonal to v+w. So AB (direction u) is orthogonal to BD (direction v+w). Thus rectangle ABDC is a right rectangle (i.e., rectangle with right angle). Good! So AB ⟂ BD. So AB is perpendicular to BD. Since AC is parallel to BD, AB ⟂ AC as well. So AB is perpendicular to both face diagonals. That matches our earlier deduction.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184345988,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184347215,"data":"       | Thus our coordinate system: Choose orthonormal basis (e1, e2, e3) for edges u, v, w. Let side length s. Then coordinates:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184363281,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184364487,"data":"       | Let A = (0,0,0) (choose origin at A). Let u = vector along x-axis: (s,0,0). Let v = (0,s,0). Let w = (0,0,s). Then:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18436581,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184366957,"data":"       | A = (0,0,0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184368053,"data":"       | B = (s,0,0) (AB along x)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184369172,"data":"       | C = (0,s,s) (since AC = v + w)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184370255,"data":"       | D = (s,s,s) (C + u) = (s,s,s)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184371465,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184372478,"data":"       | Check: AB = (s,0,0). CD = D - C = (s,0,0) (parallel). AC = (0,s,s). BD = D - B = (0,s,s). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184373719,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184375188,"data":"       | Now plane ABDC: points A (0,0,0), B (s,0,0), D (s,s,s), C (0,s,s). This plane is defined by points A, B, D. Let's compute its normal vector: AB = (s,0,0). AD = D - A = (s,s,s). Cross product AB × AD = (s,0,0) × (s,s,s) = determinant |i j k; s 0 0; s s s| = i*(0*s - 0*s) - j*(s*s - 0*s) + k*(s*s - 0*s) = i*0 - j*(s^2) + k*(s^2) = (-s^2) j + (s^2) k. So normal vector = (0, -s^2, s^2) = s^2 (0, -1, 1). So plane ABDC has normal vector (0, -1, 1). This vector has zero x-component, meaning plane is vertical (perpendicular to horizontal plane z=0). Indeed normal lies in yz-plane, with components -1 in y and 1 in z. So plane equation: -y + z = constant. Since passes through A (0,0,0), constant = 0. So plane equation: z = y. Wait -y + z = 0 =\u003e z = y. Indeed plane ABDC is defined by z = y (and x arbitrary). So rectangle ABDC is intersection of plane z = y with the cube defined by 0 ≤ x ≤ s, 0 ≤ y ≤ s, 0 ≤ z ≤ s (since cube oriented with edges along axes). However note that our cube coordinates have y and z ranging from 0 to s. So plane z = y passes through diagonal of the square face yz-plane (where x=0). Indeed rectangle ABDC is the set of points where x is either 0 or s? Actually rectangle ABDC is the quadrilateral formed by intersection of plane z = y with the cube. Let's compute intersection: The plane cuts the cube forming a rectangle with vertices at (0,0,0) (A), (s,0,0) (B), (s,s,s) (D), (0,s,s) (C). Indeed those satisfy z=y: For A (0,0,0) yes. B (s,0,0) has y=0, z=0, ok. D (s,s,s) y=s, z=s ok. C (0,s,s) y=s, z=s ok. So rectangle ABDC is exactly the intersection of plane z = y with the cube.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184381275,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184382318,"data":"       | Thus our coordinate model matches the description: plane ABDC is vertical (since normal has zero x component). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184383602,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18438465,"data":"       | Now we have heights of vertices above plane P (horizontal plane). In our coordinate system, we set P as horizontal plane z=0. Indeed we have A at height 0, B at height 2, C at height 8, D at height 10. But in our model, the heights (z-coordinates) of A,B,C,D are: A (0), B (0), C (s), D (s). That's not matching given heights. So our coordinate system is not aligned with actual heights; we need to rotate/translate the cube such that the heights of those vertices are as given.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184386563,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18438761,"data":"       | But note that the relative heights differences are: B is 2 above A (Δz=2). D is 10 above A (Δz=10). C is 8 above A (Δz=8). In our model, B and A have same height (both 0), D and C have same height (both s). So not matching.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184389131,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18439052,"data":"       | Thus the cube is not aligned with axes; we need to consider a more general orientation where plane ABDC is vertical (i.e., its normal vector has zero vertical component). But we can still use the same internal coordinates relative to cube edges (u, v, w orthogonal). The plane ABDC is defined by points A, B, D, C, which satisfy that AB is along u, CD along u, AC and BD along v + w (i.e., sum of two orthogonal edge vectors). The plane ABDC's normal is u × (v + w) = u × v + u × w. Since u, v, w are orthogonal, u × v = w (up to orientation), u × w = -v (depending orientation). So normal vector is w - v (or v - w). Indeed earlier we got (0, -1, 1) in coordinates where u = x, v = y, w = z. So normal is w - v. So plane ABDC is defined by (w - v)·(r - A) = 0, i.e., (w - v)·r = 0 if A at origin. That is, dot product with w - v = 0. So plane equation: (w - v)·r = 0. This plane is vertical because w - v has zero component along u (since u is orthogonal to both v and w). So plane's normal lies in the subspace spanned by v and w, which is horizontal? Wait v and w are orthogonal edge vectors; they are not necessarily horizontal; they could be oriented arbitrarily in space. However the condition that plane ABDC is perpendicular to horizontal plane P means its normal vector is horizontal (i.e., lies in horizontal plane). Since horizontal plane P is defined as some plane (maybe not same as xy-plane). But we can define coordinate system where P is horizontal (z=0). Then the normal of plane ABDC must be horizontal, i.e., have zero vertical component. So (w - v) must be horizontal (i.e., its dot product with vertical direction (unit vector k) is zero). So w - v is orthogonal to vertical direction.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184394532,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184395584,"data":"       | Thus we can set up coordinate system: Let vertical direction be unit vector e_z (pointing up). Let horizontal plane P be orthogonal to e_z. Then we have cube with orthogonal edge vectors u, v, w (mutually perpendicular). The plane ABDC has normal n = w - v, which must be orthogonal to e_z (i.e., n·e_z = 0). So (w - v)·e_z = 0 =\u003e w·e_z = v·e_z. So the vertical components of v and w are equal.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184397455,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184398516,"data":"       | Now we have heights of vertices: For each vertex, its height above P is its dot product with e_z (since P is at height 0). So we can define coordinates of vertices relative to some origin (maybe A). Let A be at height 0 (given). So we can set A's position vector r_A such that e_z·r_A = 0. Without loss, we can set r_A = 0 (origin). Then heights of other vertices are e_z·r_B = 2, e_z·r_C = 8, e_z·r_D = 10.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184402268,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184403293,"data":"       | Now we can express r_B = u (since B = A + u). So e_z·u = 2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184404505,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184405565,"data":"       | Similarly, r_C = v + w (since C = A + v + w). So e_z·(v + w) = 8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184406751,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184407889,"data":"       | r_D = u + v + w (since D = A + u + v + w). So e_z·(u + v + w) = 10.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184409228,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184410268,"data":"       | Thus we have unknown vectors u, v, w (mutually orthogonal, length s). We have unknown side length s and unknown orientation relative to e_z.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184411663,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184412666,"data":"       | We have three equations for vertical components:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184413869,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184414898,"data":"       | (1) u_z = e_z·u = 2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184415984,"data":"       | (2) v_z + w_z = 8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184417155,"data":"       | (3) u_z + v_z + w_z = 10.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184418545,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184419584,"data":"       | From (1) and (3): (2) + (1) = 2 + (v_z + w_z) = 10 =\u003e v_z + w_z = 8, consistent with (2). So equations are consistent.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184420948,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184421993,"data":"       | Thus we have u_z = 2, v_z + w_z = 8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184423203,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184424378,"data":"       | Also we have condition that w_z = v_z (since w - v is horizontal, i.e., its dot with e_z zero). So w_z = v_z.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184425698,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184426715,"data":"       | Thus from v_z + w_z = 8 =\u003e 2 v_z = 8 =\u003e v_z = w_z = 4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184428081,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184429108,"data":"       | Thus we have vertical components: u_z = 2, v_z = 4, w_z = 4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184430265,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18443128,"data":"       | Now we also know that the lengths of u, v, w are equal to side length s. So we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184432454,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184433538,"data":"       | |u|^2 = s^2 = u_z^2 + u_h^2, where u_h is magnitude of horizontal component of u (i.e., projection onto horizontal plane). Similarly for v and w.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184434835,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18443597,"data":"       | Let’s denote horizontal components: u_h = sqrt(s^2 - u_z^2) = sqrt(s^2 - 4). Wait u_z = 2, so u_z^2 = 4, so u_h = sqrt(s^2 - 4). Similarly, v_h = sqrt(s^2 - v_z^2) = sqrt(s^2 - 16). Same for w_h.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184438904,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184440167,"data":"       | Now we also have orthogonality: u·v = 0, u·w = 0, v·w = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18444152,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184442562,"data":"       | We can decompose each vector into vertical and horizontal components: u = u_horiz + u_z e_z, where u_horiz is horizontal vector (in plane P). Similarly for v, w.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184443868,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184445035,"data":"       | Since e_z is orthogonal to horizontal plane, dot product of two vectors is sum of dot products of their horizontal components plus product of vertical components.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184446349,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184447364,"data":"       | Thus orthogonality conditions become:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184448464,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184449674,"data":"       | u·v = u_horiz·v_horiz + u_z v_z = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184450891,"data":"       | u·w = u_horiz·w_horiz + u_z w_z = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184452284,"data":"       | v·w = v_horiz·w_horiz + v_z w_z = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184453383,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184454527,"data":"       | We also have that v_horiz and w_horiz are horizontal vectors (in plane P). Since v_z = w_z = 4, the last condition v·w = 0 gives v_horiz·w_horiz + 4*4 = 0 =\u003e v_horiz·w_horiz = -16.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184456019,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184457127,"data":"       | Similarly, u·v = 0 gives u_horiz·v_horiz + 2*4 = 0 =\u003e u_horiz·v_horiz = -8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18445838,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184459396,"data":"       | And u·w = 0 gives u_horiz·w_horiz + 2*4 = 0 =\u003e u_horiz·w_horiz = -8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184460582,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184521465,"data":"       | Now we also have that the horizontal components are vectors in the plane P (2D). So we can treat them as vectors in R^2. Let's denote them as 2D vectors: a = u_horiz, b = v_horiz, c = w_horiz.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184525632,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184526832,"data":"       | We know magnitudes: |a|^2 = s^2 - 4, |b|^2 = s^2 - 16, |c|^2 = s^2 - 16.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184528177,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184529215,"data":"       | We also have dot products: a·b = -8, a·c = -8, b·c = -16.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184530432,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184531487,"data":"       | We need to find s (side length). Then we can compute volume of water: volume of portion of cube below height 7 (i.e., z ≤ 7). Since the cube is oriented arbitrarily, the water volume is the volume of intersection of cube with half-space z ≤ 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184533505,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184536391,"data":"       | Given we have coordinates of all vertices relative to A at origin, we can compute the shape of intersection. Since the cube is convex, the intersection with a half-space is a polyhedron (a truncated cube). The water volume can be computed as total volume of cube minus volume of portion above height 7 (i.e., cut off by plane z = 7). Since plane is horizontal, the cut will be a plane intersecting the cube, producing a shape that is a polyhedron with top cut.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184542778,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18454387,"data":"       | We can compute volume of portion below height 7 by integrating or using geometry.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184545253,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184546668,"data":"       | But first we need s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184547763,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184548775,"data":"       | We have unknown s satisfying the above dot product constraints. Let's solve for s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184550079,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184551298,"data":"       | We have three vectors a,b,c in 2D with given magnitudes and dot products. In 2D, there are at most 2 independent vectors; three vectors must be linearly dependent. Indeed b and c may be related.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184552743,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184553785,"data":"       | We can attempt to find s such that there exist vectors a,b,c in R^2 satisfying those constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184555078,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184556114,"data":"       | Let’s treat a,b as basis. Then c can be expressed as linear combination of a and b. Use dot product constraints to solve.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184557408,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184558415,"data":"       | Let’s denote unknowns: s unknown \u003e0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184559635,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184560637,"data":"       | Let’s set up using sympy: unknown s, unknown vectors a=(a1,a2), b=(b1,b2), c=(c1,c2). Solve equations:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184561867,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184562891,"data":"       | a·a = s^2 - 4\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184563991,"data":"       | b·b = s^2 - 16\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184565406,"data":"       | c·c = s^2 - 16\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184566448,"data":"       | a·b = -8\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18456749,"data":"       | a·c = -8\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184568519,"data":"       | b·c = -16\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184569588,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184570749,"data":"       | We have 6 equations, 7 unknowns (s plus 6 components). But there may be degrees of freedom (rotations). We can solve for s by eliminating components.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184572065,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184573127,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can use linear algebra: In 2D, the Gram matrix of vectors a,b,c must have rank ≤2. The Gram matrix G = [[a·a, a·b, a·c], [b·a, b·b, b·c], [c·a, c·b, c·c]] must be positive semidefinite with determinant zero (since rank ≤2). So we can compute determinant condition to find s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18457487,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18457588,"data":"       | Compute G:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184576978,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184578208,"data":"       | G = [[s^2-4, -8, -8],\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184579308,"data":"       |      [-8, s^2-16, -16],\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184580712,"data":"       |      [-8, -16, s^2-16]]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184582168,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.1845852,"data":"       | Set det(G) = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184586538,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184587868,"data":"       | Let's compute symbolic.assistantanalysis to=python codeimport sympy as sp\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184589044,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184590073,"data":"       | s = sp.symbols('s', positive=True)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184591214,"data":"       | G = sp.Matrix([[s**2-4, -8, -8],\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184592411,"data":"       |               [-8, s**2-16, -16],\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184593526,"data":"       |               [-8, -16, s**2-16]])\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184594626,"data":"       | detG = sp.factor(G.det())\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184595702,"data":"       | detG\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184596751,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184597749,"data":"     [Code]:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184598788,"data":"       | import sympy as sp\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184599874,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184601052,"data":"       | s = sp.symbols('s', positive=True)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184602152,"data":"       | G = sp.Matrix([[s**2-4, -8, -8],\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184603234,"data":"       |               [-8, s**2-16, -16],\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184604323,"data":"       |               [-8, -16, s**2-16]])\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184605399,"data":"       | detG = sp.factor(G.det())\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184606466,"data":"       | detG\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184607503,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184608517,"data":"     [Output]:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184609603,"data":"       \u003e s**4*(s - 6)*(s + 6)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18461073,"data":"       \u003e \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184611783,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184612759,"data":"     [Turn 2]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18461381,"data":"     [Reasoning] (21645 chars):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184614934,"data":"       | analysisDeterminant = s^4 * (s - 6)*(s + 6). Since s\u003e0, we have s=6 as solution (s=0 not allowed). So side length s = 6 meters.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184616285,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184617284,"data":"       | Great! So cube side length is 6 m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184618386,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184619432,"data":"       | Now we have vertical components: u_z = 2, v_z = w_z = 4. So the heights of vertices: A at 0, B at 2, C at v_z + w_z = 8, D at u_z + v_z + w_z = 10. Indeed matches.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184621002,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184702691,"data":"       | Now we need volume of water: region of cube with z ≤ 7 (height 7). Since cube extends from min z (lowest vertex) = 0 (A) to max z (highest vertex) = 10 (D). So water level at 7 cuts the cube somewhere between C (z=8) and D (z=10) and also between B (z=2) and maybe other vertices? Let's compute z-coordinates of all 8 vertices of cube.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184717579,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18471952,"data":"       | We have cube vertices expressed as A + combination of u, v, w with coefficients 0 or 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18473471,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18473586,"data":"       | Let’s define:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.1847371,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184738225,"data":"       | A = (0,0,0) (height 0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184739408,"data":"       | B = u (height 2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184740488,"data":"       | C = v + w (height 8)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184741714,"data":"       | D = u + v + w (height 10)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184742863,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184743997,"data":"       | Other vertices: Let's list all 8 vertices:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18474522,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184746332,"data":"       | Let’s define base vectors: u, v, w.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184747438,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184748548,"data":"       | Vertices:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184749602,"data":"       | 0: O = (0,0,0) = A\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184750745,"data":"       | 1: u = B\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184751777,"data":"       | 2: v\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184752906,"data":"       | 3: w\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184753957,"data":"       | 4: v + w = C\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184755106,"data":"       | 5: u + v\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184756129,"data":"       | 6: u + w\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184757255,"data":"       | 7: u + v + w = D\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184758314,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184759338,"data":"       | We need heights (z) for each:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184760456,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184761455,"data":"       | Height of v = v_z = 4\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184762572,"data":"       | Height of w = w_z = 4\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184763615,"data":"       | Height of u+v = u_z + v_z = 2+4=6\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184764748,"data":"       | Height of u+w = 2+4=6\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184765787,"data":"       | Height of v+w = 8 (C)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184766826,"data":"       | Height of u+v+w = 10 (D)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184767896,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184768878,"data":"       | Thus heights of vertices:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184769959,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184770958,"data":"       | A (0): 0\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184771988,"data":"       | B (u): 2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184773013,"data":"       | v: 4\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184774047,"data":"       | w: 4\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18477509,"data":"       | C (v+w): 8\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184776113,"data":"       | u+v: 6\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184777182,"data":"       | u+w: 6\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184778236,"data":"       | D: 10\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184779279,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184780299,"data":"       | Thus the cube's vertical extents: min 0, max 10.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184781441,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.1847847,"data":"       | Now water level at 7: vertices below or equal to 7 are those with height ≤7: A (0), B (2), v (4), w (4), u+v (6), u+w (6). That's 6 vertices. Vertices above 7: C (8), D (10). So water fills a polyhedron that is the portion of cube below plane z=7, which cuts off a top \"cap\" that includes vertices C and D and part of edges connecting them to lower vertices.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184786638,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184787705,"data":"       | Thus water volume = total cube volume - volume of top part above z=7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184788864,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184789895,"data":"       | Total cube volume = s^3 = 6^3 = 216 cubic meters.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184791072,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184793815,"data":"       | Now we need volume of region of cube with z ≥ 7 (the \"air\" part). This region is a polyhedron formed by intersecting the cube with half-space z ≥ 7 (plane at height 7). Since only two vertices are above 7, the cut plane will intersect edges connecting those vertices to lower vertices.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184795502,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184796542,"data":"       | Edges that cross the plane (i.e., have one endpoint above 7 and the other below 7) are edges incident to C or D that go to lower vertices.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184797824,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184798835,"data":"       | Let's list edges of cube: each vertex connects to three edges (u, v, w). We'll need to find which edges cross the plane.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184800088,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184801094,"data":"       | Vertices above: C (v+w) height 8, D (u+v+w) height 10.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184802261,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184803319,"data":"       | Edges from C to lower vertices: C is connected to v (height 4) via edge w? Wait C = v + w. Edges from C: subtract u? Actually edges are along u, v, w directions. Starting from C (v+w), moving -v gives w (height 4). Moving -w gives v (height 4). Moving +u gives D (height 10). So edges from C to lower vertices: C-\u003ev (height 4) via -v direction (edge length s), C-\u003ew (height 4) via -w direction, both cross plane? Since C at 8 \u003e7, v and w at 4 \u003c7, so those edges cross plane.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184805747,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184806775,"data":"       | Edge C-\u003eD (via +u) goes from height 8 to 10, both above plane, so not crossing.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184808278,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184809335,"data":"       | Now D (u+v+w) height 10. Edges from D: subtract u gives C (8) (both above), subtract v gives u+w (6) (below), subtract w gives u+v (6) (below). So edges D-\u003eu+w (height 6) and D-\u003eu+v (height 6) cross plane.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184810829,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18481184,"data":"       | Thus the plane z=7 cuts the cube along a quadrilateral formed by intersection points on those four edges: C-v, C-w, D-u+v, D-u+w. The region above plane is a tetrahedron? Let's examine shape.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18481333,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184815357,"data":"       | The vertices above plane: C and D. The intersection polygon with plane will be a quadrilateral (since four edges cross). The region above plane is a polyhedron with top face being triangle? Wait typical cut of a cube by a plane that passes through four edges yields a tetrahedron (if plane cuts off a corner). But here plane cuts off a \"corner\" that includes two adjacent vertices (C and D) which share an edge (C-D). Actually C and D are connected by edge CD (which is along u direction). Both are above plane, so the region above plane includes edge CD fully. The plane cuts the three edges incident to C and D that go downwards: C-v, C-w, D-u+v, D-u+w. Those four edges intersect plane at points; those points together with C and D form a polyhedron that is a pentahedron? Let's visualize.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184819857,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18482093,"data":"       | Cube orientation: Let's label coordinates in our internal system (u,v,w). But we need actual coordinates in 3D to compute volume of region above plane z=7. We can assign coordinates for vectors u, v, w consistent with earlier constraints, then compute intersection points and volume using geometry.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184822464,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184823514,"data":"       | We have side length s=6. Need to find vectors u, v, w (3D) satisfying:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184824703,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18482573,"data":"       | |u|=|v|=|w|=6.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184826848,"data":"       | u_z = 2, v_z = w_z = 4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184827955,"data":"       | u·v = u·w = v·w = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184829155,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184830192,"data":"       | We also have horizontal components a,b,c as earlier with dot products: a·b = -8, a·c = -8, b·c = -16, where a,b,c are horizontal components (2D vectors). We can construct explicit vectors.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184831642,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184832687,"data":"       | Since we only need volume of region above plane z=7, which depends only on geometry of cube relative to vertical direction, we may not need full 3D coordinates; we can compute volume using known heights of vertices and linear interpolation along edges.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184834173,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184835285,"data":"       | Because the plane is horizontal, the cross-section at height z is a polygon (the intersection of the cube with that horizontal plane). The volume of region below height h can be computed by integrating area of cross-section as function of z from 0 to h. Since the cube is convex and the cross-section area varies linearly between heights where vertices appear/disappear (since the shape is a convex polyhedron with planar faces). Actually for a convex polyhedron intersected with a half-space defined by a plane, the volume of the portion below the plane can be expressed as sum of volumes of pyramids/truncated prisms.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184837337,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184838576,"data":"       | But perhaps easier: Since we have coordinates of all vertices, we can compute the volume of the polyhedron above plane (z≥7) directly using 3D geometry (e.g., compute convex hull of points above plane and intersection points). Then subtract from total volume.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184840093,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184841121,"data":"       | Let's attempt to construct explicit vectors u, v, w in 3D.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184842261,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184843274,"data":"       | We can choose coordinate system: Let vertical direction be e_z = (0,0,1). Then we need u, v, w orthogonal with given vertical components.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184846117,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184847172,"data":"       | Let’s set u = (a1, a2, 2) where a1^2 + a2^2 + 2^2 = 36 =\u003e a1^2 + a2^2 = 32.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184848448,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184849453,"data":"       | Similarly, v = (b1, b2, 4) with b1^2 + b2^2 + 4^2 = 36 =\u003e b1^2 + b2^2 = 20.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184850637,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184851659,"data":"       | w = (c1, c2, 4) with c1^2 + c2^2 = 20.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184852754,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184853772,"data":"       | Orthogonality conditions:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184854827,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184855835,"data":"       | u·v = a1*b1 + a2*b2 + 2*4 = 0 =\u003e a·b = -8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184857061,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184858062,"data":"       | u·w = a1*c1 + a2*c2 + 2*4 = 0 =\u003e a·c = -8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184859195,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184860206,"data":"       | v·w = b1*c1 + b2*c2 + 4*4 = 0 =\u003e b·c = -16.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184861335,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184862356,"data":"       | These match earlier.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184863425,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184864524,"data":"       | Thus we need to find vectors a,b,c in R^2 satisfying those dot products and magnitudes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184865752,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184866762,"data":"       | We can solve for a,b,c explicitly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184867872,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184868932,"data":"       | Let’s treat a as unknown vector of length sqrt(32) = 4*sqrt(2). Choose coordinate axes such that a is along x-axis: a = (4*sqrt(2), 0). This is allowed because we can rotate horizontally.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184870313,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184871338,"data":"       | Then we need b such that |b| = sqrt(20) = 2*sqrt(5), and a·b = -8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184872557,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184873585,"data":"       | Let a = (4√2, 0). Then dot product a·b = (4√2) * b1 = -8 =\u003e b1 = -8 / (4√2) = -2/√2 = -√2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18487498,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184875977,"data":"       | Thus b1 = -√2. Then b2 must satisfy magnitude: b1^2 + b2^2 = 20 =\u003e ( (√2)^2 ) + b2^2 = 20 =\u003e 2 + b2^2 = 20 =\u003e b2^2 = 18 =\u003e b2 = ±3√2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184877598,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184878637,"data":"       | Thus b = (-√2, 3√2) or (-√2, -3√2). Choose b = (-√2, 3√2) (any orientation). Then compute c such that |c| = sqrt(20) and a·c = -8, b·c = -16.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18488013,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18488115,"data":"       | We have unknown c = (c1, c2). Conditions:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184882272,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184883283,"data":"       | a·c = (4√2) * c1 = -8 =\u003e c1 = -8 / (4√2) = -√2 (same as b1). So c1 = -√2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184886112,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184887397,"data":"       | b·c = b1*c1 + b2*c2 = (-√2)*(-√2) + (3√2)*c2 = (2) + 3√2 * c2 = -16 =\u003e 3√2 * c2 = -18 =\u003e c2 = -18 / (3√2) = -6/√2 = -3√2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18488909,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184890085,"data":"       | Thus c = (-√2, -3√2). Check magnitude: c1^2 + c2^2 = 2 + 18 = 20, good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184891353,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184894841,"data":"       | Thus we have explicit vectors:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184896203,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184897373,"data":"       | a = (4√2, 0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184898521,"data":"       | b = (-√2, 3√2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184899749,"data":"       | c = (-√2, -3√2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184900933,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184901946,"data":"       | Thus horizontal components.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184903112,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184904218,"data":"       | Now we can construct u, v, w:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184905398,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184906503,"data":"       | u = (a1, a2, 2) = (4√2, 0, 2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18490782,"data":"       | v = (b1, b2, 4) = (-√2, 3√2, 4)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184909097,"data":"       | w = (c1, c2, 4) = (-√2, -3√2, 4)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184910221,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184911221,"data":"       | Now we can compute coordinates of all vertices:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184912358,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184913373,"data":"       | A = (0,0,0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184914434,"data":"       | B = u = (4√2, 0, 2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184915557,"data":"       | v = (-√2, 3√2, 4)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18491666,"data":"       | w = (-√2, -3√2, 4)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184917757,"data":"       | C = v + w = (-2√2, 0, 8)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184918853,"data":"       | u+v = (4√2 - √2, 3√2, 6) = (3√2, 3√2, 6)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184920072,"data":"       | u+w = (4√2 - √2, -3√2, 6) = (3√2, -3√2, 6)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184921248,"data":"       | D = u+v+w = (4√2 - 2√2, 0, 10) = (2√2, 0, 10)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18492246,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184923472,"data":"       | Check heights: z coordinates match.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184925521,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184926649,"data":"       | Now we have explicit coordinates.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184927805,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184928968,"data":"       | Now we need volume of region above plane z=7 (i.e., water level). Let's compute intersection points of plane z=7 with edges that cross.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184930448,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18493155,"data":"       | Edges crossing plane:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18493274,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184933858,"data":"       | Edge C-v: from C (z=8) to v (z=4). Parameter t from 0 at C to 1 at v: point = C + t*(v - C). Need z=7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184936853,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184944792,"data":"       | C = (-2√2, 0, 8)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184946025,"data":"       | v = (-√2, 3√2, 4)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184947149,"data":"       | Difference v - C = ( (-√2 + 2√2), 3√2 - 0, 4-8 ) = ( √2, 3√2, -4 ).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184948471,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184949505,"data":"       | Parameter t such that z = 8 + t*(-4) = 7 =\u003e -4 t = -1 =\u003e t = 1/4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184950782,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184951819,"data":"       | Thus intersection point P1 = C + (1/4)*(v - C) = C + (1/4)*(√2, 3√2, -4) = (-2√2 + (√2)/4, 0 + (3√2)/4, 8 - 1) = (-2√2 + 0.25√2, (3/4)√2, 7). Compute: -2 + 0.25 = -1.75 √2 = -(7/4)√2. So P1 = (-(7/4)√2, (3/4)√2, 7).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184953475,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184954902,"data":"       | Edge C-w: from C (z=8) to w (z=4). w = (-√2, -3√2, 4). Difference w - C = ( (-√2 + 2√2), -3√2 - 0, -4 ) = ( √2, -3√2, -4 ). Parameter t = 1/4 again (since same Δz). Intersection point P2 = C + (1/4)*(√2, -3√2, -4) = (-2√2 + 0.25√2, 0 - 0.75√2, 7) = (-(7/4)√2, -(3/4)√2, 7).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184956703,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184957727,"data":"       | Edge D - (u+v): D (z=10) to u+v (z=6). u+v = (3√2, 3√2, 6). Difference = (3√2 - 2√2, 3√2 - 0, 6-10) = (√2, 3√2, -4). Parameter t from D to u+v: D + t*(u+v - D). Need z = 10 + t*(-4) = 7 =\u003e t = 3/4? Wait solve: 10 - 4t = 7 =\u003e 4t = 3 =\u003e t = 3/4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.184959431,"data":"       | \n"}
