,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.185033443,"data":"       | But we need orientation outward to compute signed volume correctly. We can compute volume as absolute value of sum of signed volumes of tetrahedra formed by each face triangle and a reference point (e.g., origin). The signed volume of tetrahedron (p0, p1, p2, p3) is (1/6) * ((p1-p0) × (p2-p0)) · (p3-p0). If we take reference point as origin (0,0,0) which is inside cube but not necessarily inside region above plane (since origin is at A (0,0,0) which is below plane). Using origin may give signed volumes that cancel? Actually we can compute volume of convex hull directly using formula: V = (1/6) * Σ over all oriented faces ( (v_i × v_j) · v_k ), where each face is a triangle oriented outward and v_i, v_j, v_k are its vertices. This formula assumes origin is at (0,0,0). It works for any polyhedron that does not contain origin? Actually the formula gives signed volume of polyhedron formed by triangles with origin as reference point; if origin is inside polyhedron, sum of signed volumes of tetrahedra formed by each triangle and origin equals volume. If origin is outside, you need to ensure orientation signs produce correct volume (some tetrahedra will have negative volume). But the sum of signed volumes still equals volume if orientation is consistent (outward normals). However easier: we can compute volume of convex hull using scipy.spatial.ConvexHull (if available). Not sure if SciPy is available. Might not be.\n"}
