,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.185491717,"data":"       | Let's compute exact volume of region above plane using integration or compute polyhedron defined by intersection of cube with half-space z \u003e= 7. Let's compute using linear programming approach: The region above plane is convex polyhedron defined by inequalities: 0 ≤ a,b,c ≤ 1 (coeffs for u,v,w) and z = (a*u_z + b*v_z + c*w_z) \u003e= 7? Actually point coordinates: P = a*u + b*v + c*w where a,b,c ∈ [0,1]. The height z = a*2 + b*4 + c*4 = 2a + 4b + 4c. Condition z \u003e= 7 =\u003e 2a + 4b + 4c \u003e= 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.185493888,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.185495003,"data":"       | Thus region above plane is set of (a,b,c) in unit cube [0,1]^3 satisfying 2a + 4b + 4c \u003e= 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18549623,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18549722,"data":"       | Similarly water region is 2a + 4b + 4c ≤ 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.185498355,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.185499636,"data":"       | Thus we can compute volume in (a,b,c) space (parameter space) and then multiply by Jacobian (determinant of transformation from (a,b,c) to actual coordinates). Since transformation is linear: point = a*u + b*v + c*w. The volume scaling factor is absolute value of determinant of matrix [u v w] (columns). That determinant equals volume of parallelepiped spanned by u,v,w = s^3 = 6^3 = 216. Indeed because u,v,w are orthogonal with lengths 6, so determinant magnitude = 6*6*6 = 216. So mapping from unit cube to actual cube is volume-preserving scaling factor 216. So volume of region above plane = 216 * volume of region in (a,b,c) space defined by inequality.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.185501732,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.185502758,"data":"       | Thus we can compute volume in (a,b,c) space analytically: region in unit cube where 2a + 4b + 4c \u003e= 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.185503992,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18550507,"data":"       | Since a,b,c ∈ [0,1], we can treat inequality.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.185506775,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.185507798,"data":"       | Let's compute volume of region where 2a + 4b + 4c ≥ 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.185509134,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.185510187,"data":"       | Define variables a,b,c ∈ [0,1]. The inequality defines a half-space. The region is a polyhedron (a truncated tetrahedron). Compute its volume via integration.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.185511615,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18551436,"data":"       | We can compute using sympy integration or geometry.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.185515588,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.185516661,"data":"       | Let's compute analytically using integration: For each a, the condition on b,c: 4b + 4c ≥ 7 - 2a =\u003e b + c ≥ (7 - 2a)/4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.185518108,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.185519173,"data":"       | Given a ∈ [0,1], the RHS ranges: when a=0, RHS = 7/4 = 1.75 \u003e 2 (max b+c is 2). So for a small, condition may be impossible. Actually b,c ≤1 each, so max b+c = 2. So need RHS ≤ 2 for feasible. So need (7 - 2a)/4 ≤ 2 =\u003e 7 - 2a ≤ 8 =\u003e -2a ≤ 1 =\u003e a ≥ -0.5, always true. So feasible for all a? Wait but also need RHS ≥ 0? Not needed; b+c must be at least RHS, but if RHS negative, condition automatically satisfied. So we need to find range of a where RHS ≤ 2 (always) and RHS ≤ 2? Already satisfied. However also need RHS ≤ 2, yes always. But also need RHS ≤ 2 and RHS ≤ 2? That's fine.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.185521703,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.185522722,"data":"       | But also need RHS ≤ 2, but also need RHS ≤ 2? Already.\n"}
