,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18601609,"data":"       | We can treat the rectangle ABDC as a planar rectangle in 3D, vertical plane. Its vertices have coordinates (in some coordinate system) that we can assign using rectangle geometry: Let’s define rectangle ABDC in its own plane with coordinates (x', y') where x' along AB direction (edge), y' along AC direction (face diagonal). Since rectangle ABDC is vertical, its plane is perpendicular to horizontal plane P (i.e., its normal vector is horizontal). So we can set up a coordinate system: Let the horizontal plane P be xy-plane (z=0). Then vertical direction is z-axis. The rectangle ABDC lies in a plane that is vertical, i.e., contains the z-axis direction? Not necessarily; any vertical plane contains a direction parallel to z (vertical). So the rectangle's plane includes a vertical direction (some line parallel to z). Since AB is an edge of cube (horizontal? Not necessarily). Edge AB could be oriented arbitrarily within that vertical plane. However AB is an edge of the cube, which is of length s, and is not necessarily horizontal. Similarly CD is parallel to AB.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186020656,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186021859,"data":"       | But we know heights of vertices: z_A=0, z_B=2, z_C=8, z_D=10. So AB is a segment from (x_A, y_A, 0) to (x_B, y_B, 2). So AB has vertical component 2. Similarly CD from (x_C, y_C, 8) to (x_D, y_D, 10) also vertical component 2 (since heights differ by 2). Indeed both AB and CD have same vertical rise (2). That matches AB and CD being parallel edges: they have same direction vector, thus same vertical component.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186023643,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186024728,"data":"       | Similarly AC is diagonal of a face: from A (0) to C (8) vertical rise 8. BD from B (2) to D (10) vertical rise 8. So both have same vertical component 8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186026026,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186027056,"data":"       | Thus rectangle ABDC is vertical plane; its side vectors AB and AC have vertical components 2 and 8 respectively, and they are perpendicular (since rectangle). Indeed AB is parallel to CD, AC is parallel to BD, and AB ⟂ AC (since rectangle). So we have two vectors in the plane: v1 = AB (length s) with vertical component 2; v2 = AC (length s√2) (since AC is diagonal of a face of cube, which is a square face of side s, so its length is s√2). Wait AC is a diagonal of a face of the cube, not necessarily of the rectangle. The face of the cube containing A and C is a square of side s. Since AC is diagonal of that square, its length is s√2. Similarly BD is also diagonal of a face, length s√2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186029382,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186030417,"data":"       | Thus we have vector AB of length s, vector AC of length s√2, and they are perpendicular (since rectangle). Also AB and AC share vertex A. So we can set up coordinate system.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186031773,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186032889,"data":"       | Let’s denote:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186033996,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186035037,"data":"       | Let s be side length of cube.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18603611,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186037177,"data":"       | Let vector u = AB (edge), length |u| = s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186038263,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186039282,"data":"       | Let vector v = AC (face diagonal), length |v| = s√2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186040441,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186041488,"data":"       | We know u·v = 0 (since rectangle ABDC). Also the vertical components (z components) of u and v are given: u_z = 2 (since B is 2 higher than A). v_z = 8 (since C is 8 higher than A). So we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186042902,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186043908,"data":"       | Let u = (u_x, u_y, 2). Its length squared: u_x^2 + u_y^2 + 2^2 = s^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186045057,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186046097,"data":"       | Similarly v = (v_x, v_y, 8). Its length squared: v_x^2 + v_y^2 + 8^2 = (s√2)^2 = 2 s^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186049202,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186050257,"data":"       | Also orthogonality: u·v = u_x v_x + u_y v_y + 2*8 = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186051429,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186052462,"data":"       | We have unknowns u_x, u_y, v_x, v_y, s. That's 5 unknowns, with three equations above (lengths and dot product). However there may be additional constraints from cube geometry: The vectors u and v correspond to edge and face diagonal that are not arbitrary; they must correspond to a cube orientation. However the relations above may be sufficient to solve s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186054114,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186055204,"data":"       | We also know that the vector w = AD (other side of rectangle) is parallel to v? Wait rectangle ABDC: vertices order A (0), B (AB), D (AB + v?), C (v). Actually rectangle ABDC: A to B is u, B to D is v (since BD is diagonal of face, parallel to AC). Let's verify: In rectangle ABDC, sides are AB (edge), BD (face diagonal), DC (edge parallel to AB), CA (face diagonal). So vector from B to D equals v (since BD is parallel to AC). Indeed BD = v (or maybe -v). Let's compute: B to D = D - B. Since D is opposite vertex of rectangle, D = A + u + v (since rectangle). Indeed rectangle with adjacent sides u and v: A at origin, B = u, C = v, D = u + v. Wait order A-B-D-C? Let's check: Starting at A, go along u to B, then along v to D (since BD is v). Then from D go back along -u to C? Actually C = v (since A to C is v). So D = u + v. Then C = v. So rectangle vertices: A (0), B (u), D (u+v), C (v). Indeed AB = u, BD = v, DC = -u, CA = -v. So rectangle ABDC is defined by vectors u and v.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186057842,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186058855,"data":"       | Thus D = u + v. Let's verify heights: D_z = u_z + v_z = 2 + 8 = 10, matches given (10). Good! So consistency holds.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18606008,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186061081,"data":"       | Thus we have vector u (AB) length s, vertical component 2; vector v (AC) length s√2, vertical component 8; orthogonal.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18606245,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186063468,"data":"       | Now we can solve for s using these equations.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186064581,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186065597,"data":"       | Let’s denote horizontal components (i.e., projection onto plane P) as vectors u_h = (u_x, u_y) and v_h = (v_x, v_y). Then:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186066856,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186067882,"data":"       | |u_h|^2 + 2^2 = s^2 =\u003e |u_h|^2 = s^2 - 4.   (1)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186069039,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186070238,"data":"       | |v_h|^2 + 8^2 = 2 s^2 =\u003e |v_h|^2 = 2 s^2 - 64.   (2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186071403,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186072435,"data":"       | Orthogonality: u_h·v_h + 2*8 = 0 =\u003e u_h·v_h = -16.   (3)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186073683,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186074731,"data":"       | We need to find s that allows such vectors to exist. This is a problem of constructing two vectors in 2D with given lengths and dot product.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186075999,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18607703,"data":"       | Let’s denote a = |u_h|, b = |v_h|, and let θ be angle between u_h and v_h. Then u_h·v_h = a b cosθ = -16.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186082245,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186083374,"data":"       | We have a^2 = s^2 - 4, b^2 = 2 s^2 - 64.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186084585,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186085684,"data":"       | Thus (ab cosθ)^2 = 256.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18608683,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186087935,"data":"       | But we also have that cosθ can be any value between -1 and 1. So we need existence of a,b,θ satisfying a b cosθ = -16.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18608927,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186090368,"data":"       | Thus we need |ab| \u003e= 16 (since |cosθ| ≤ 1). So ab ≥ 16.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186091639,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.1860928,"data":"       | Compute ab = sqrt((s^2 - 4)(2 s^2 - 64)).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.1860939,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186094991,"data":"       | Thus condition: sqrt((s^2 - 4)(2 s^2 - 64)) ≥ 16.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186096222,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186097351,"data":"       | Square both sides: (s^2 - 4)(2 s^2 - 64) ≥ 256.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186098487,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186099636,"data":"       | Simplify: (s^2 - 4)(2 s^2 - 64) = 2 s^4 - 64 s^2 - 8 s^2 + 256 = 2 s^4 - 72 s^2 + 256.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186100833,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186101834,"data":"       | Set \u003e= 256: 2 s^4 - 72 s^2 + 256 ≥ 256 =\u003e 2 s^4 - 72 s^2 ≥ 0 =\u003e 2 s^2 (s^2 - 36) ≥ 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18610322,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186104217,"data":"       | Since s \u003e 0, we have s^2 - 36 ≥ 0 =\u003e s ≥ 6.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18610546,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186106545,"data":"       | Thus side length must be at least 6. But there may be a unique s that satisfies the exact dot product condition (not just inequality). Because we need existence of angle such that u_h·v_h = -16, not just magnitude condition. Since we can adjust angle to achieve any dot product between -ab and ab. So as long as ab \u003e= 16, we can set cosθ = -16/(ab). So there is a continuum of solutions for s≥6.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186108356,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186109384,"data":"       | Thus s is not uniquely determined by heights alone. However perhaps the cube side length is determined by the fact that AB and CD are edges of the cube (length s) and AC and BD are diagonals of faces (length s√2). Those constraints already used. So s can be any value \u003e=6? Let's verify if any s≥6 yields a consistent orientation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.1861111,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186112403,"data":"       | But maybe there is additional constraint: The rectangle ABDC is a rectangle, meaning that AB is perpendicular to AC (i.e., u·v = 0). We used that condition to get orthogonality equation u·v = 0, which gave u_h·v_h = -16. That's already used. So we have three equations (1)-(3). For given s, we can solve for a,b, and cosθ = -16/(ab). This is feasible if |cosθ| ≤ 1, i.e., ab ≥ 16. So s≥6 ensures feasibility.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186114331,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186115337,"data":"       | Thus side length s is not uniquely determined; any s≥6 works. However the water volume may be independent of s? Let's see.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186118275,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186119329,"data":"       | Water volume = area of region of rectangle ABDC below height 7 times depth (distance between front and back faces). Wait earlier we thought depth equals side length s, but that was when rectangle ABDC is a face. However now rectangle ABDC is not a face; it's a rectangle inside the cube, not necessarily the front face. The cube is oriented arbitrarily; the water volume is portion of cube below height 7. Since the cube is a solid, not a prism extruded from rectangle ABDC. So we need to compute volume of part of cube below plane z=7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186121246,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186122279,"data":"       | But maybe we can use symmetry: The cube is a regular solid; the water plane cuts it at height 7. The volume below is a function of the heights of vertices. Since the cube is convex, the volume below a plane can be expressed as sum of tetrahedra formed by plane and faces? Perhaps the volume depends only on the heights of the vertices, not on side length? Let's examine.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186123923,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186124955,"data":"       | Let the cube have vertices with coordinates unknown but we know heights of A,B,C,D. The other four vertices (call them A',B',C',D') are opposite to these across the cube (i.e., each is the vertex opposite to the given one along the space diagonal). In a cube, each vertex has opposite vertex (the farthest). For vertex A, opposite is the vertex opposite across the cube (call it A*). Similarly for B, opposite is B*. However the problem may not need those.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186126741,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186127945,"data":"       | But we can perhaps compute volume of portion below height 7 using the fact that the cube is a convex polyhedron with known vertex heights at four vertices of a rectangle; the other four vertices have heights offset by same amount as these? Wait earlier we argued that opposite face is parallel to rectangle ABDC, but not necessarily horizontal shift; but the direction from rectangle ABDC to opposite rectangle (the other set of four vertices) is perpendicular to rectangle ABDC, which is horizontal (since rectangle ABDC is vertical). So the opposite rectangle is shifted horizontally (parallel to plane P). Therefore the heights of opposite vertices are same as corresponding ones (since shift is horizontal). Indeed, if we move from a point in rectangle ABDC along a vector perpendicular to its plane (i.e., horizontal direction), the vertical coordinate does not change. So the other four vertices (forming the opposite rectangle) have same heights as A,B,C,D respectively. Let's verify using cube geometry.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186130629,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186131971,"data":"       | Take rectangle ABDC as defined by vectors u (AB) and v (AC). The cube extends in the third direction w, which is orthogonal to both u and v (since cube edges are orthogonal). In a cube, we have three mutually perpendicular edge vectors of equal length s: let's denote e1 = u (edge AB). e2 = some edge from A to another vertex (call it A to A' maybe). e3 = some edge from A to another vertex (call it A to something else). However in our rectangle ABDC, we have u = AB (edge), v = AC (face diagonal). The face diagonal AC is sum of two orthogonal edge vectors: e1 + e2 (where e2 is another edge from A orthogonal to e1). Indeed in a cube, face diagonal AC = e1 + e2 (if we choose e1 = AB, e2 = AD where AD is edge orthogonal to AB lying in same face). However AC is diagonal of a face, not necessarily sum of AB and AD? Let's examine: In our earlier coordinate example where A (0,0,0), B (s,0,0), D (0,s,s)??? Wait that example had AC diagonal across left face (x=0). But in general, AC diagonal of a face that contains A and is not the same as AB's face? Actually AB is an edge; there are two faces containing AB. AC is a diagonal of one of those faces. So AC = AB + something? Let's pick the face containing AB and A, maybe the bottom front face (z=0). In that case AC would be diagonal across that face, which would be AB + AD where AD is edge perpendicular to AB within that face. So AC = AB + AD (where AD is edge orthogonal to AB). So AC is sum of two orthogonal edge vectors of length s each, giving length s√2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186137105,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186138168,"data":"       | Thus we can think of cube edges as three orthogonal vectors: e1 = AB (length s), e2 = AD (edge orthogonal to AB, lying in same face as AB and AC), e3 = something perpendicular to both e1 and e2 (the third dimension). Then AC = e1 + e2. BD = e1 + e2 + e3? Wait B to D is diagonal of opposite face? Let's compute: B = A + e1. D = A + e2 + e3? Actually D is opposite vertex of rectangle ABDC, which is A + e1 + e2 + e3? Let's analyze.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186139913,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186140956,"data":"       | Our rectangle ABDC: vertices: A (origin), B = A + e1, C = A + e1 + e2 (since AC = e1 + e2). D = A + e2 (since AD = e2). Wait earlier we had rectangle ABDC with vertices A, B, D, C where D = A + e2, C = A + e1 + e2. Indeed that matches rectangle formed by edges e1 and e2 (adjacent edges). However AB and CD are parallel edges (e1). AC and BD are diagonals of faces: AC = e1 + e2 (face diagonal). BD = e2 + e3? Wait B to D = D - B = (A + e2) - (A + e1) = e2 - e1. That's not a diagonal of a face. Hmm.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186142805,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186144006,"data":"       | Let's step back: The rectangle ABDC defined earlier as A, B, D, C where AB and CD are parallel edges, AC and BD are diagonals of faces. In our earlier coordinate example (A (0,0,0), B (s,0,0), D (s,s,s), C (0,s,s)), AB = (s,0,0), CD = ( -s,0,0) (parallel). AC = (0,s,s) (diagonal of left face x=0). BD = (0,s,s) (diagonal of right face x=s). Indeed AC = BD = (0,s,s). So rectangle ABDC is formed by vectors u = AB (edge) and v = (0,s,s) (face diagonal). However v is not a sum of two orthogonal edges; it's a diagonal of a face that is orthogonal to AB? Wait the left face (x=0) has edges AD (0,s,0) and AE (0,0,s) maybe; its diagonal is (0,s,s). Indeed that's sum of two orthogonal edges of length s each (AD and AE). So v = e2 + e3 where e2 and e3 are orthogonal edges perpendicular to e1 (AB). So e1, e2, e3 are orthogonal edges of cube.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186146415,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186147408,"data":"       | Thus we can define orthogonal basis vectors:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186148525,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186149535,"data":"       | e1: direction of AB (edge) length s, vertical component 2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18615066,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186151667,"data":"       | e2: direction of an edge orthogonal to e1, lying in a face that includes A but not B? In our example, e2 could be edge from A to some vertex (call it A' maybe) that is orthogonal to e1 and lies in the plane of rectangle ABDC? Wait rectangle ABDC plane includes e1 and v = e2 + e3. So e2 and e3 are both orthogonal to e1, but not necessarily in rectangle plane individually; only their sum lies in rectangle plane.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186153372,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186156421,"data":"       | Thus the cube's third edge direction e3 is orthogonal to both e1 and e2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186157633,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186158657,"data":"       | Now the heights of vertices: Since e1 has vertical component 2, e2 and e3 have vertical components unknown, but we know that v = e2 + e3 has vertical component 8. So (e2_z + e3_z) = 8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186160005,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186161298,"data":"       | Also the opposite rectangle (the other set of four vertices) is obtained by adding e2 + e3 to each of A,B,C,D? Wait the cube's opposite vertices relative to rectangle ABDC are obtained by moving each vertex along the direction orthogonal to rectangle ABDC (i.e., along a vector w that is orthogonal to both e1 and v). Since rectangle ABDC plane is spanned by e1 and v = e2 + e3, the orthogonal direction to that plane within the cube is some vector w that is orthogonal to both e1 and v, and of length s (since cube edges are equal). This w must be a linear combination of e2 and e3 that is orthogonal to v = e2 + e3 and also orthogonal to e1 (already orthogonal). Since e1 is orthogonal to both e2 and e3, any combination of e2 and e3 is orthogonal to e1. So w is orthogonal to v if w·(e2+e3)=0. Let w = e2 - e3 (or some scalar multiple). Indeed e2 - e3 is orthogonal to e2 + e3 (dot product = |e2|^2 - |e3|^2 = s^2 - s^2 = 0). So w can be e2 - e3 (or its negative). Its length is sqrt(|e2|^2 + |e3|^2) = sqrt(s^2 + s^2) = s√2. Wait length of e2 - e3 is sqrt(s^2 + s^2) = s√2, not s. But the edge length of cube must be s. So w cannot be e2 - e3. Hmm.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186164354,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186165408,"data":"       | But the third edge direction of the cube (the one orthogonal to e1 and v) must be of length s, and orthogonal to both e1 and v. Since e1 is orthogonal to e2 and e3, any vector orthogonal to e1 is in span{e2, e3}. So we need a unit vector in span{e2, e3} orthogonal to v = e2 + e3, with magnitude s. In the 2D subspace spanned by e2 and e3, there are two orthogonal directions: v (along (1,1) in basis of e2,e3) and w (along (1,-1)). The vector w = e2 - e3 has length sqrt(2) s, not s. However we can scale w to have length s: w' = (e2 - e3)/√2 (since |e2 - e3| = s√2, dividing by √2 yields length s). Indeed w' = (e2 - e3)/√2 is orthogonal to v (since dot product zero) and has length s. So the third edge direction of cube is w' (or its negative). So the cube's three orthogonal edge vectors are:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18616803,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186169253,"data":"       | e1 (AB) length s,\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186170551,"data":"       | e2 (some edge) length s,\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186171725,"data":"       | e3 (another edge) length s,\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186173086,"data":"       | with e1 orthogonal to e2 and e3, and e2 orthogonal to e3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186174233,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186175332,"data":"       | But we don't have e2 and e3 individually; we only know their sum v = e2 + e3 (face diagonal) and their difference w' = (e2 - e3)/√2 (edge direction). So we can reconstruct e2 and e3 as:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186176705,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186177841,"data":"       | Let v = e2 + e3 (known vector of length s√2, vertical component 8).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186179095,"data":"       | Let w' = (e2 - e3)/√2 (unknown direction, length s). Its vertical component unknown; call w'_z.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186180342,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186183013,"data":"       | We can solve for e2_z and e3_z:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186184137,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186185243,"data":"       | From v_z = e2_z + e3_z = 8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186186525,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186187567,"data":"       | From w'_z = (e2_z - e3_z)/√2 =\u003e e2_z - e3_z = w'_z * √2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186188794,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186189837,"data":"       | Thus we can solve e2_z and e3_z in terms of w'_z.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186190967,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186191973,"data":"       | But we also know that e2 and e3 are orthogonal and each have length s. So we have constraints on their horizontal components.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186193222,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186194684,"data":"       | Nevertheless, perhaps we don't need explicit orientation; we just need volume below height 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186195935,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18619696,"data":"       | The water plane z=7 cuts the cube. Since the cube is a convex polyhedron with vertices at heights: A (0), B (2), C (8), D (10), and the opposite vertices (call them A', B', C', D') have heights equal to those of A,B,C,D respectively (since they are obtained by adding w', which is horizontal? Wait w' is orthogonal to rectangle ABDC, which is vertical plane. Since w' is orthogonal to that plane, w' is horizontal (lies in plane P). Indeed rectangle ABDC is vertical, so its normal is horizontal. The vector w' is along that normal, thus horizontal, thus has zero vertical component. Therefore the opposite vertices have same heights as original ones. Let's verify: w' is orthogonal to rectangle ABDC, which is vertical; thus w' is horizontal (parallel to plane P). So its vertical component is 0. Therefore the opposite rectangle (the other face of cube) has same heights as the front rectangle. Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186199428,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186200454,"data":"       | Thus the cube consists of two parallel rectangles (front and back) with same heights at corresponding vertices, connected by horizontal edges (parallel to plane P). So the cube is like a \"prism\" extruded horizontally from the rectangle ABDC. So the cube is a right prism with base rectangle ABDC (vertical) and depth s (horizontal). Indeed the depth direction is w', which is horizontal, length s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186202247,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186203564,"data":"       | Thus the cube is a right prism with base rectangle ABDC (vertical) and depth s (horizontal). This matches earlier reasoning where we thought extruding rectangle ABDC horizontally yields cube. Indeed the base rectangle ABDC is vertical, extruded horizontally yields a cube (since the base rectangle is a square of side s? Wait rectangle ABDC may not be a square; but the cube's cross-section extruded horizontally must be a square? Let's compute dimensions: The rectangle ABDC has sides lengths: AB = s (edge), AC = s√2 (face diagonal). So rectangle ABDC is not a square; it's a rectangle with side lengths s and s√2. However the cube's other dimension (depth) is also s (edge length). So the cube is a rectangular prism with dimensions s (depth) × s (width) × s (height?) Not exactly. Wait the cube is a cube, all edges length s. The rectangle ABDC is formed by two edges (AB length s) and a face diagonal (AC length s√2). So rectangle ABDC is not a face of cube; it's a rectangle with side lengths s and s√2. The depth (distance between front and back rectangles) is also s, which is the third edge direction w' of length s. So the shape is a rectangular prism with dimensions s (edge direction e1), s (edge direction w'), and s√2 (direction v). But that's not a cube (since one dimension is s√2). However the shape is still a cube because the three vectors e1, w', and e2 (or e3) are orthogonal and each length s. The rectangle ABDC is spanned by e1 and v = e2 + e3, which is not orthogonal to e1 (actually they are orthogonal because rectangle ABDC is a rectangle). Indeed e1·v = 0 (since rectangle). So v is orthogonal to e1, but its length is s√2. So rectangle ABDC is a rectangle with sides e1 (length s) and v (length s√2). So it's a rectangle with side lengths s and s√2, not a square. That's fine.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186209367,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186210426,"data":"       | Thus the cube can be thought of as a right prism with base rectangle ABDC (vertical) and depth s (horizontal). So the water volume below height 7 is area of portion of rectangle ABDC below height 7 multiplied by depth s (since extrusion direction is horizontal). Indeed because extrusion direction is horizontal, the water plane (horizontal) cuts the prism in a region that is extruded from the intersection of rectangle ABDC with half-space below height 7. Since extrusion direction is horizontal (parallel to water plane), the cross-section shape is same across depth. So volume = (area of region of rectangle ABDC below height 7) * s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186212487,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186213673,"data":"       | Thus we need to compute area of region of rectangle ABDC (vertical rectangle) that lies below height 7. This depends only on rectangle geometry (heights at its four vertices) and not on s (except through scaling). However the rectangle ABDC's dimensions involve s, so area will be proportional to s^2 (since rectangle area = s * s√2 = s^2√2). The region below height 7 will be some fraction of that area depending on heights 0,2,8,10.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186215494,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186216533,"data":"       | Thus volume = s * (area fraction * total area) = s * (fraction * s^2√2) = s^3 * √2 * fraction.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186217769,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186218777,"data":"       | But we also have condition s ≥ 6, but volume may depend on s^3. However perhaps the fraction times √2 yields a rational number independent of s? Let's compute.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186220115,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186221129,"data":"       | Let’s set up rectangle ABDC coordinates in its own plane: Use 2D coordinates (x,y) within rectangle: Let origin at A. Let u vector = AB of length s, direction horizontal within rectangle plane (but not horizontal globally). Let v vector = AC of length s√2, orthogonal to u within rectangle plane. Represent points in rectangle as p = a*u + b*v where a,b ∈ [0,1] (since rectangle). Height function z(p) = a*z_u + b*z_v, where z_u = 2 (vertical rise of u) and z_v = 8 (vertical rise of v). Because height is linear across rectangle (plane). Indeed at A (0,0) height 0; at B (1,0) height 2; at C (0,1) height 8? Wait C corresponds to v direction (since AC = v). So at (0,1) height 8. At D = u+v (since D = A + u + v) height = 2+8=10. Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186223402,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186224405,"data":"       | Thus rectangle coordinates: (a,b) with a,b in [0,1]. Height z = 2a + 8b.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186225581,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186226583,"data":"       | We need area of region where z ≤ 7. So inequality: 2a + 8b ≤ 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186229417,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186230453,"data":"       | Since a,b ∈ [0,1], region is a subset of unit square in (a,b) coordinates. The mapping from (a,b) to actual area is linear scaling: area element dA = |u × v| da db = |u| |v| (since orthogonal) da db = s * (s√2) da db = s^2 √2 da db.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186231978,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186232993,"data":"       | Thus area of region below height 7 = s^2 √2 * (area in (a,b) parameter space satisfying 2a + 8b ≤ 7, with 0≤a,b≤1).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186234351,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186235357,"data":"       | Thus we need to compute area of region in unit square defined by 2a + 8b ≤ 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186236577,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186237597,"data":"       | Let's compute that area.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186238933,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186239925,"data":"       | The line 2a + 8b = 7 =\u003e a = (7 - 8b)/2 = 3.5 - 4b.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186241115,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186242122,"data":"       | Intercepts: When b=0, a=3.5 (outside unit square, \u003e1). When a=0, b=7/8 = 0.875. So line passes through (a=0,b=0.875) and (a=1, b = (7 - 2)/8 = 5/8 = 0.625). Actually plug a=1: 2*1 + 8b = 7 =\u003e 8b = 5 =\u003e b = 5/8 = 0.625. So line intersects right side of square at (1,0.625). Since a intercept \u003e1, the region below line within square includes all points with a up to 1 for b less than something? Let's visualize.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186244164,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186245191,"data":"       | Inequality 2a + 8b ≤ 7 defines region below line (since coefficients positive). In unit square, the line cuts from left side at (0,0.875) to right side at (1,0.625). The region below line includes points with smaller a,b values (toward origin). So region is a polygon: a quadrilateral? Actually it's the region bounded by axes a=0, b=0, and the line segment between those intersection points, plus the top boundary? Let's examine.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186246937,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186247957,"data":"       | The line intersects left side (a=0) at b=0.875 (within [0,1]). Intersects bottom side (b=0) at a=3.5 (outside). So within the unit square, the line segment from (0,0.875) to (1,0.625) forms the upper boundary of region. The region below includes the triangle near origin plus a trapezoid? Let's compute area of region: It's the set of points (a,b) with 0 ≤ a ≤ 1, 0 ≤ b ≤ min( (7 - 2a)/8, 1). Since (7 - 2a)/8 is decreasing in a, and for a from 0 to 1, (7 - 2a)/8 ranges from 7/8=0.875 down to (7-2)/8 =5/8=0.625. So always ≤1, so the upper bound is the line. So region is simply the area under the line within the unit square: area = ∫_{a=0}^{1} ( (7 - 2a)/8 ) da = (1/8) ∫_{0}^{1} (7 - 2a) da = (1/8) [7a - a^2]_{0}^{1} = (1/8) (7 - 1) = (1/8) * 6 = 6/8 = 3/4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186250415,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186251415,"data":"       | Thus area in (a,b) space = 3/4. So area of region in rectangle ABDC below height 7 = s^2 √2 * 3/4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186252648,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18625364,"data":"       | Thus water volume = depth (horizontal extrusion) * area = s * (s^2 √2 * 3/4) = (3/4) * s^3 * √2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186254878,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186258442,"data":"       | Now we need to find s. However we only have condition s ≥ 6, not a specific value. But maybe s is determined by the fact that the cube is a cube: the three orthogonal edge vectors must have equal length s, which imposes additional constraints linking vertical components of e2 and e3. Let's examine.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186260073,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186261112,"data":"       | We have e1 (AB) with vertical component 2, horizontal component magnitude sqrt(s^2 - 4). e2 and e3 are orthogonal vectors each of length s, with unknown vertical components p and q respectively, such that p + q = 8 (since v = e2 + e3 has vertical component 8). Also e2 and e3 are orthogonal: e2·e3 = 0. Their horizontal components also must satisfy orthogonality.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186262813,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18626382,"data":"       | Let e2 = (x2, y2, p), e3 = (x3, y3, q). Then:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186264922,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186265931,"data":"       | |e2|^2 = x2^2 + y2^2 + p^2 = s^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186267015,"data":"       | |e3|^2 = x3^2 + y3^2 + q^2 = s^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186268092,"data":"       | e2·e3 = x2 x3 + y2 y3 + p q = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186269238,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186270305,"data":"       | Also e1 is orthogonal to both e2 and e3: e1·e2 = u_x x2 + u_y y2 + 2 p = 0; e1·e3 = u_x x3 + u_y y3 + 2 q = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186271613,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186272628,"data":"       | But we may not need to solve all; perhaps s is determined by the condition that e2 and e3 are orthogonal and have same length s, given that their sum v has length s√2 and vertical component 8. Let's use these constraints to solve for s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186274051,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186275062,"data":"       | We have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18627612,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186277136,"data":"       | v = e2 + e3, with length |v| = s√2, vertical component v_z = p + q = 8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186278324,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186279344,"data":"       | Compute |v|^2 = |e2|^2 + |e3|^2 + 2 e2·e3 = s^2 + s^2 + 2*0 = 2 s^2. So indeed |v| = s√2 automatically given orthogonal e2, e3 and equal lengths s. So that condition is automatically satisfied; no extra constraint on s from v length.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186280823,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186281831,"data":"       | Thus the only constraints left are orthogonality of e1 with e2 and e3, and lengths of e1 (vertical component 2). This may impose a specific s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186283118,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186284128,"data":"       | Let's attempt to solve for s using vector algebra.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186285248,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186286258,"data":"       | Let’s denote u = e1 = (u_h, 2) where u_h is a 2D vector (horizontal) of magnitude sqrt(s^2 - 4). Let e2 = (a_h, p), e3 = (b_h, q). Orthogonality:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186288065,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186289408,"data":"       | u_h·a_h + 2 p = 0   (eq4)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186290559,"data":"       | u_h·b_h + 2 q = 0   (eq5)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186293102,"data":"       | a_h·b_h + p q = 0   (eq6)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186294213,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186295278,"data":"       | Lengths:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186296342,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186297343,"data":"       | |a_h|^2 + p^2 = s^2   (eq7)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186298423,"data":"       | |b_h|^2 + q^2 = s^2   (eq8)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186299482,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186300545,"data":"       | We also have p + q = 8 (eq9).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186301618,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186302633,"data":"       | We have unknowns: u_h (2 components), a_h (2 components), b_h (2 components), p, q, s. That's many variables; but we can perhaps find s by using dot product relationships.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186303937,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186304953,"data":"       | We can treat u_h as a vector of length sqrt(s^2 - 4). Without loss of generality, we can orient coordinate axes such that u_h lies along x-axis: u_h = (sqrt(s^2 - 4), 0). Since we can rotate the horizontal plane arbitrarily (the cube orientation relative to horizontal plane is not fixed). This simplifies.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18630652,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186307524,"data":"       | Thus u = (sqrt(s^2 - 4), 0, 2).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18630861,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186309655,"data":"       | Now we need to find a_h = (a1, a2), b_h = (b1, b2) satisfying above.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186310814,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186311858,"data":"       | From eq4: u_h·a_h + 2p = sqrt(s^2 - 4) * a1 + 0*a2 + 2p = 0 =\u003e a1 = -2p / sqrt(s^2 - 4).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18631312,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18631413,"data":"       | Similarly eq5: b1 = -2q / sqrt(s^2 - 4).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186315211,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186316208,"data":"       | Now lengths:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18631727,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186318503,"data":"       | |a_h|^2 = a1^2 + a2^2 = s^2 - p^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18631958,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186320597,"data":"       | Thus a2^2 = s^2 - p^2 - a1^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186321662,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186322687,"data":"       | Compute a1^2 = (4 p^2) / (s^2 - 4). So a2^2 = s^2 - p^2 - (4 p^2)/(s^2 - 4).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18632384,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18632486,"data":"       | Similarly for b_h: b2^2 = s^2 - q^2 - (4 q^2)/(s^2 - 4).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186326015,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186327058,"data":"       | Now orthogonality between a_h and b_h: a_h·b_h + p q = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186328225,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186329245,"data":"       | Compute a_h·b_h = a1 b1 + a2 b2 = ( -2p / sqrt(s^2 - 4) ) * ( -2q / sqrt(s^2 - 4) ) + a2 b2 = (4 p q) / (s^2 - 4) + a2 b2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186330525,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186331542,"data":"       | Thus condition: (4 p q)/(s^2 - 4) + a2 b2 + p q = 0 =\u003e a2 b2 = -p q (1 + 4/(s^2 - 4)) = -p q * ( (s^2 - 4 + 4) / (s^2 - 4) ) = -p q * ( s^2 / (s^2 - 4) ).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186334332,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186335367,"data":"       | Thus a2 b2 = - p q * s^2 / (s^2 - 4).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186336452,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186337488,"data":"       | Now we also have expressions for a2^2 and b2^2. So we can treat a2 and b2 as possibly positive/negative; we can square the equation for a2 b2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186338767,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186339985,"data":"       | Thus (a2 b2)^2 = a2^2 * b2^2 = ( - p q * s^2 / (s^2 - 4) )^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186341132,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186342152,"data":"       | Compute a2^2 * b2^2 using formulas:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186343225,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186344267,"data":"       | a2^2 = s^2 - p^2 - (4 p^2)/(s^2 - 4) = s^2 - p^2 * (1 + 4/(s^2 - 4)) = s^2 - p^2 * ( (s^2 - 4 + 4) / (s^2 - 4) ) = s^2 - p^2 * ( s^2 / (s^2 - 4) ) = ( s^2 (s^2 - 4) - p^2 s^2 ) / (s^2 - 4) = s^2 ( (s^2 - 4) - p^2 ) / (s^2 - 4).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186345702,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186346735,"data":"       | Thus a2^2 = s^2 ( s^2 - 4 - p^2 ) / (s^2 - 4).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186360555,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186361623,"data":"       | Similarly b2^2 = s^2 ( s^2 - 4 - q^2 ) / (s^2 - 4).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186362759,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186363774,"data":"       | Thus product:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186364874,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186365942,"data":"       | a2^2 * b2^2 = s^4 ( (s^2 - 4 - p^2)(s^2 - 4 - q^2) ) / (s^2 - 4)^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186367087,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186368183,"data":"       | Now set equal to (p q s^2 / (s^2 - 4))^2 = p^2 q^2 s^4 / (s^2 - 4)^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186369324,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186370345,"data":"       | Thus we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186371397,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186372427,"data":"       | s^4 ( (s^2 - 4 - p^2)(s^2 - 4 - q^2) ) / (s^2 - 4)^2 = p^2 q^2 s^4 / (s^2 - 4)^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186373598,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186374613,"data":"       | Cancel s^4/(s^2 - 4)^2 (nonzero). We get:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186375699,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186376742,"data":"       | (s^2 - 4 - p^2)(s^2 - 4 - q^2) = p^2 q^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18637784,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186378855,"data":"       | Expand: Let A = s^2 - 4. Then equation becomes:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186379952,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186380955,"data":"       | (A - p^2)(A - q^2) = p^2 q^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18638203,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186383042,"data":"       | Compute left: A^2 - A(p^2 + q^2) + p^2 q^2 = p^2 q^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186384165,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186385194,"data":"       | Cancel p^2 q^2 both sides, we get:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186387765,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186388795,"data":"       | A^2 - A(p^2 + q^2) = 0 =\u003e A (A - (p^2 + q^2)) = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186389945,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186390979,"data":"       | Since A = s^2 - 4 \u003e 0 (s\u003e2), we have A - (p^2 + q^2) = 0 =\u003e A = p^2 + q^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186392247,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186393262,"data":"       | Thus s^2 - 4 = p^2 + q^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186394327,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186395368,"data":"       | Recall p + q = 8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186396409,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186397405,"data":"       | Thus we have system:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186398463,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18639946,"data":"       | p + q = 8\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186400489,"data":"       | p^2 + q^2 = s^2 - 4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186401532,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186402558,"data":"       | We can solve for p,q in terms of s, or eliminate p,q to find s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186403742,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186404786,"data":"       | We know (p + q)^2 = p^2 + q^2 + 2pq =\u003e 64 = (s^2 - 4) + 2pq =\u003e 2pq = 64 - (s^2 - 4) = 68 - s^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18640604,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186407062,"data":"       | Thus pq = (68 - s^2)/2 = 34 - s^2/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186408138,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186409483,"data":"       | Now p and q are real numbers, representing vertical components of edges e2 and e3. Since edges length s, we must have p^2 ≤ s^2, q^2 ≤ s^2 (since vertical component cannot exceed length). Also p and q could be positive (since heights increase upward). In our configuration, A is lowest (0), B is 2, C is 8, D is 10. So e2 and e3 likely have positive vertical components (since moving from A to C (via e2+e3) raises height 8). So p,q positive.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186411285,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186412298,"data":"       | Now we have p+q=8, p^2+q^2 = s^2 - 4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186413393,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186414425,"data":"       | We can solve for s by using identity: (p+q)^2 = p^2+q^2+2pq =\u003e 64 = (s^2 - 4) + 2pq =\u003e 2pq = 68 - s^2 =\u003e pq = 34 - s^2/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18641578,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186416877,"data":"       | But also we have p,q are roots of quadratic t^2 - (p+q) t + pq = 0 =\u003e t^2 - 8 t + (34 - s^2/2) = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186418133,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186419151,"data":"       | For real solutions, discriminant must be nonnegative: Δ = 64 - 4*(34 - s^2/2) = 64 - 136 + 2 s^2 = 2 s^2 - 72 = 2(s^2 - 36). So we need s^2 ≥ 36, i.e., s ≥ 6, consistent with earlier.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186420571,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186421593,"data":"       | Thus any s ≥ 6 yields real p,q. So still not unique.\n"}
