,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186681447,"data":"       | Thus our assumption that AB and CD are opposite edges of same face fails to match heights.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186685974,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186687123,"data":"       | Thus AB and CD must be opposite edges that are not in same face, i.e., skew edges. Then the rectangle ABDC is not a face of the cube but a cross-section rectangle that passes through interior.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186688585,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186689735,"data":"       | Let's analyze more generally.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186690901,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.1866922,"data":"       | Let’s consider cube with side length s. Choose coordinate system aligned with cube edges: vertices at (0,0,0) to (s,s,s). Let’s denote vertices as usual.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186693668,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186694905,"data":"       | We need to select A, B, C, D such that AB and CD are parallel edges (i.e., edges along same axis direction), but not sharing a face (i.e., they are opposite edges of the cube). AC and BD are diagonals of faces. Which faces? Likely each diagonal lies in a face that contains the respective edge endpoints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186696568,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186697685,"data":"       | Let's attempt to find a labeling that satisfies these conditions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18669891,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186700042,"data":"       | Let’s denote edges along x-direction: edges are (0,0,0)-(s,0,0), (0,s,0)-(s,s,0), (0,0,s)-(s,0,s), (0,s,s)-(s,s,s). Choose AB as (0,0,0)-(s,0,0) (bottom front edge). Choose CD as opposite edge (0,s,s)-(s,s,s) (top back edge). These are parallel (x-direction) and not in same face (skew). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186701662,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186702738,"data":"       | Now AC: vertices A (0,0,0) and C (0,s,s) ??? Wait C is (0,s,s) (since CD edge endpoints are (0,s,s) and (s,s,s)). Which one is C? In the rectangle ABDC, vertices are A, B, D, C (order?). The rectangle ABDC includes vertices A, B, D, C. If AB is edge (0,0,0)-(s,0,0), then D must be opposite vertex to B? Actually rectangle ABDC includes A and B (adjacent), then D (adjacent to B) and C (adjacent to D) and back to A. So D is the vertex adjacent to B along rectangle side, which is parallel to CD? Let's compute.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186704752,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186705908,"data":"       | Given AB edge along x-direction at y=0, z=0. CD edge parallel along x-direction at y=s, z=s. So rectangle ABDC would have vertices: A (0,0,0), B (s,0,0), D (s,s,s) (since D is endpoint of CD with same x as B?), and C (0,s,s). Indeed that matches earlier Option 2 (but with different labeling). In that configuration, AB and CD are opposite skew edges, and AC and BD are diagonals of faces? Let's verify.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186707778,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186709137,"data":"       | AC: A (0,0,0) to C (0,s,s) differ in y and z: they lie in face x=0 (left side). Indeed AC is diagonal of face x=0 (vertical square). BD: B (s,0,0) to D (s,s,s) differ in y and z: diagonal of face x=s (right side). So AC and BD are diagonals of faces (different faces). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186710738,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186711915,"data":"       | Now rectangle ABDC is formed by points A (0,0,0), B (s,0,0), D (s,s,s), C (0,s,s). This rectangle lies in plane defined by points (0,0,0), (s,0,0), (s,s,s). Its normal we computed earlier (0,-1,1). This plane is not vertical (not perpendicular to horizontal). But maybe we can rotate the cube relative to horizontal plane P such that this rectangle becomes vertical. In other words, we can tilt the cube so that the plane ABDC is vertical. The coordinates we used are relative to cube axes; the horizontal plane P is some plane in space. We can rotate the whole cube such that plane ABDC becomes vertical (perpendicular to P). Then the heights of vertices above P will be given by projection onto vertical direction (i.e., distance along normal to P). So we need to find a coordinate system where plane ABDC is vertical, and the heights of vertices are given as 0,2,8,10. This is essentially a problem of a cube oriented arbitrarily with respect to horizontal plane, and we know the heights of four vertices that lie on a rectangle (which is a cross-section plane). We need to compute volume of water up to height 7 (i.e., water fills region of cube below plane z=7). Since cube is tilted, water surface is horizontal (parallel to P). So water occupies part of cube below height 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186716852,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186717995,"data":"       | Thus we need to compute volume of portion of cube below a horizontal plane at height 7, given heights of four vertices of a rectangle cross-section. This is a classic problem: a cube is tilted, water fills up to a certain height; volume depends linearly on heights of vertices.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18671959,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186721007,"data":"       | We can treat the cube as a solid with known coordinates of all 8 vertices after orientation. We know heights of four vertices (A,B,C,D) that lie on a rectangle plane. The other four vertices (the opposite ones) will have heights that we can deduce because they are opposite points in the cube (i.e., each is offset by the cube's edge vectors). Since we know side length s, and orientation, we can compute heights of all vertices as linear functions of s and orientation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.1867229,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186724039,"data":"       | But we don't know s or orientation. However we have enough constraints: The heights of A,B,C,D are given. The rectangle ABDC is vertical (plane perpendicular to horizontal). That gives condition that normal of plane ABDC is horizontal (i.e., its vertical component is zero). This yields a relation among coordinates of A,B,C,D.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186725685,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186726728,"data":"       | We can set up coordinate system: Let horizontal plane P be z=0. Let vertical direction be unit vector k = (0,0,1). Let the cube have side length s. Choose three orthogonal edge direction unit vectors u, v, w (mutually perpendicular) each of length s? Actually edges are vectors of length s along directions u, v, w (mutually orthogonal). The cube vertices can be expressed as base point O plus combinations of these vectors.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186728472,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186729634,"data":"       | We can place vertex A at origin O (height 0). So A = O = (0,0,0). Edge AB is along one of the edge vectors, say vector a = s * e1 (where e1 is unit direction). Then B = a.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186731084,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186732271,"data":"       | Edge AC is not an edge but a face diagonal: AC is diagonal of a face, meaning that C = O + s*e2 + s*e3? Wait diagonal of a face uses two edge directions that are perpendicular and belong to same face. Which face? Since AC is diagonal of a face, the vector from A to C must be sum of two edge vectors (perpendicular). So C = O + s*e2 + s*e3 where e2, e3 are two orthogonal edge directions (different from e1). Similarly BD is diagonal of a face: vector from B to D must be sum of two edge vectors (maybe same pair as for C?). Since rectangle ABDC includes opposite edges AB and CD parallel, CD is opposite edge to AB, i.e., CD = O + something? Let's derive.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18673651,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186737558,"data":"       | Let the three orthogonal edge vectors be u, v, w (each length s). Choose orientation such that AB = u (since AB is an edge). Then B = u.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186740461,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186741786,"data":"       | Now we need CD to be a parallel edge to AB, i.e., CD = u as well (same vector) but starting from C to D? Actually CD is an edge, so D = C + u (or C = D + u). Since CD is parallel to AB, the vector from C to D equals u (or -u). We'll determine orientation later.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186743262,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186744313,"data":"       | Now AC is a diagonal of a face. Which face contains A and C? It must be a face formed by two of the edge vectors, say v and w. So AC = v + w (or -v - w). Since A at origin, C = v + w (or -v - w). Since we will have heights positive, choose C = v + w (makes sense). Similarly, BD is diagonal of a face. Since B = u, D must be B + (some combination of two edge vectors) that form a face diagonal. The face that contains B and D must be parallel to the face containing A and C (since they are opposite faces). Likely D = u + v + w (i.e., opposite vertex of the cube). Wait D is the vertex opposite to B along the same face diagonal? Let's compute.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186746382,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18674747,"data":"       | If C = v + w, then D = C + u = u + v + w (since CD = u). That's consistent: D = u + v + w. Then BD = D - B = (u + v + w) - u = v + w = same as AC. But BD is supposed to be diagonal of a face, not same as AC? Actually BD would be same vector as AC, meaning BD is also diagonal of a face (the opposite face). That's fine: BD is diagonal of face opposite to the one containing AC. So BD = v + w as well. So indeed BD is diagonal of a face (the face opposite to the one containing AC). So condition satisfied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186749311,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186750326,"data":"       | Thus we have a consistent labeling: Let u, v, w be orthogonal edge vectors of length s. Then:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186751517,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186752543,"data":"       | A = 0\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186753598,"data":"       | B = u\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186754659,"data":"       | C = v + w\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.1867557,"data":"       | D = u + v + w\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186756752,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18675777,"data":"       | Check AB = u (edge). CD = D - C = u (parallel). AC = v + w (face diagonal). BD = D - B = v + w (face diagonal). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18675898,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186760016,"data":"       | Thus rectangle ABDC consists of points A (0), B (u), D (u+v+w), C (v+w). This rectangle is formed by two parallel edges u and u (AB and CD) and two parallel vectors v+w (AC and BD). Indeed it's a rectangle with sides u and v+w.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186761447,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.1867627,"data":"       | Now plane ABDC is spanned by vectors u and v+w. Its normal is cross product n = u × (v + w). Since u, v, w are orthogonal, we can compute n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186765388,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18676652,"data":"       | Let’s define orthonormal basis: Let u, v, w be orthogonal vectors of equal length s. So we can treat them as s times orthonormal unit vectors e1, e2, e3. Then u = s e1, v = s e2, w = s e3. Then v + w = s(e2 + e3). So n = u × (v + w) = s e1 × s(e2 + e3) = s^2 (e1 × e2 + e1 × e3) = s^2 (e3 - e2) (since e1×e2 = e3, e1×e3 = -e2). So n = s^2 (e3 - e2). This normal has components only in e2 and e3 directions, i.e., lies in the plane spanned by e2 and e3 (which is vertical? Actually e1, e2, e3 are orthogonal directions of cube edges. The horizontal plane P is defined as plane z=0 (global). The condition that plane ABDC is perpendicular to P means its normal n must be horizontal (i.e., have zero component in vertical direction). But we haven't defined which direction is vertical relative to cube. The vertical direction is the direction of gravity, which is the normal to plane P. Let's denote vertical unit vector k (pointing up). The condition that plane ABDC is perpendicular to P means n ⋅ k = 0 (normal of rectangle is orthogonal to vertical direction). So n must be horizontal (i.e., perpendicular to k). So we need to choose orientation of cube such that e3 - e2 is orthogonal to k.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186769675,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186770717,"data":"       | But we can choose coordinate system where k is some linear combination of e1, e2, e3. Since we can rotate cube arbitrarily, we can set k to be unit vector orthogonal to n (i.e., k parallel to e2 + e3). Indeed if n = e3 - e2, then any vector perpendicular to n lies in plane spanned by e1 and (e2+e3). The vertical direction must be perpendicular to n, but also we have heights of vertices defined as dot product with k.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186772435,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186773715,"data":"       | Thus we can set vertical unit vector k = (e2 + e3) / √2 (or any scalar multiple). This ensures n ⋅ k = (e3 - e2) ⋅ (e2 + e3) = e3⋅e2 + e3⋅e3 - e2⋅e2 - e2⋅e3 = 0 + 1 - 1 - 0 = 0. Good. So we can define vertical direction as along e2+e3 (i.e., diagonal in the plane spanned by v and w). This makes sense: the rectangle plane ABDC is vertical (perpendicular to horizontal), with its normal n = e3 - e2 horizontal (lies in plane spanned by e2 and e3). The vertical direction is orthogonal to n, i.e., along e2+e3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18677574,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186776756,"data":"       | Thus we can set up coordinates: Let e1, e2, e3 be orthonormal basis. Let vertical direction k = (e2 + e3)/√2. Then heights of points are given by dot product with k times s? Actually heights are distances along k direction from plane P. Since plane P is horizontal, we can take plane P as the set of points with zero dot product with k (i.e., k·x = 0). Then height of point X is k·X (assuming k is unit). So we can compute heights of vertices using coordinates.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186778648,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186779678,"data":"       | Now we need to compute heights of A, B, C, D in terms of s and orientation (i.e., the components of e1, e2, e3 relative to k). But we have defined k as (e2+e3)/√2, which is unit if e2, e3 orthonormal. So k is unit. Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186781123,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186782139,"data":"       | Now compute heights:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186783187,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186784208,"data":"       | A = 0 → height h_A = k·0 = 0 (matches given).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18678541,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186787753,"data":"       | B = u = s e1. Height h_B = k·(s e1) = s (k·e1). Since e1 is orthogonal to e2 and e3, and k is combination of e2 and e3, k·e1 = 0. That would give height 0, but we need B height = 2. So our assumption that k = (e2+e3)/√2 leads to B height zero, which contradicts given heights. Therefore our choice of orientation for vertical direction must be different; we need B to have height 2, not zero.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186789595,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186790635,"data":"       | Thus we need to allow vertical direction k to have some component along e1 as well. Since plane ABDC is vertical, its normal n = e3 - e2 must be perpendicular to k. So k must be orthogonal to n, i.e., satisfy (e3 - e2)·k = 0. This gives condition that k's components along e2 and e3 must be equal: k·e3 = k·e2. No restriction on component along e1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186792337,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186793362,"data":"       | Thus we can write k = a e1 + b e2 + b e3 (since e2 and e3 coefficients equal). Normalize later.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18679459,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186795601,"data":"       | Let k = a e1 + b e2 + b e3. Since k is unit: a^2 + 2b^2 = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186796751,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186797749,"data":"       | Now compute heights:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186798801,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186800021,"data":"       | h_A = k·0 = 0 (good).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186801121,"data":"       | h_B = k·(s e1) = s a.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18680222,"data":"       | Given h_B = 2 → s a = 2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186803337,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186804344,"data":"       | h_C = k·(v + w) = k·(s e2 + s e3) = s (b + b) = 2 s b.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186805601,"data":"       | Given h_C = 8 → 2 s b = 8 → s b = 4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186806747,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186807763,"data":"       | h_D = k·(u + v + w) = k·(s e1 + s e2 + s e3) = s (a + b + b) = s (a + 2b).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186809052,"data":"       | Given h_D = 10 → s (a + 2b) = 10.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186810311,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186811316,"data":"       | Now we have unknowns s, a, b satisfying:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186812397,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186813408,"data":"       | (1) s a = 2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186814547,"data":"       | (2) s b = 4\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186815599,"data":"       | (3) s (a + 2b) = 10\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18681665,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186817696,"data":"       | Check consistency: From (1) a = 2/s. From (2) b = 4/s. Then compute a + 2b = 2/s + 8/s = 10/s. Then s (a + 2b) = s * (10/s) = 10, which matches (3). So consistent. Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18681901,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186820053,"data":"       | Thus we can solve for s: Use b = 4/s. But also we have unit condition: a^2 + 2 b^2 = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186821262,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186822268,"data":"       | Plug a = 2/s, b = 4/s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186823388,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186825834,"data":"       | Compute: (2/s)^2 + 2 (4/s)^2 = 1 → 4/s^2 + 2 * 16 / s^2 = 1 → (4 + 32)/s^2 = 1 → 36 / s^2 = 1 → s^2 = 36 → s = 6 (positive). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186827251,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186828276,"data":"       | Thus side length of cube is 6 meters.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186829375,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186830385,"data":"       | Then a = 2/s = 2/6 = 1/3. b = 4/s = 4/6 = 2/3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186831507,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186832539,"data":"       | Check unit: a^2 + 2 b^2 = (1/9) + 2*(4/9) = 1/9 + 8/9 = 1, good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186833698,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186834721,"data":"       | Thus we have orientation: k = (1/3) e1 + (2/3) e2 + (2/3) e3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186835871,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186836906,"data":"       | Now we have full geometry: Cube side length 6. Edge vectors: u = 6 e1, v = 6 e2, w = 6 e3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186838082,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18683911,"data":"       | Now we can compute heights of all 8 vertices.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186840229,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186841243,"data":"       | Vertices (expressed as combination of u,v,w):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186842361,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186843365,"data":"       | 0: A = (0,0,0) =\u003e height 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186844503,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186845522,"data":"       | B = u = 6 e1 =\u003e height = k·u = 6 * a = 6 * (1/3) = 2 (matches).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186846762,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186847797,"data":"       | C = v + w = 6 e2 + 6 e3 =\u003e height = k·(v+w) = 6 b + 6 b = 12 b = 12 * (2/3) = 8 (matches).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186849065,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186850117,"data":"       | D = u + v + w = 6 e1 + 6 e2 + 6 e3 =\u003e height = k·(u+v+w) = 6 a + 12 b = 6*(1/3) + 12*(2/3) = 2 + 8 = 10 (matches). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18685142,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186852422,"data":"       | Now the other four vertices (the remaining ones of the cube) are:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18685355,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186854731,"data":"       | E = v (i.e., vertex at (0,6,0) in our basis) — actually we need to list all vertices: The cube vertices are combinations of 0, u, v, w, u+v, u+w, v+w, u+v+w. We have already A=0, B=u, C=v+w, D=u+v+w. The other four are:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186856145,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186857145,"data":"       | E = v (i.e., vertex opposite to B along v direction) – call it maybe vertex? Let's denote:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186858363,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186859373,"data":"       | V1 = 0 (A)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186860444,"data":"       | V2 = u (B)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186861525,"data":"       | V3 = v (call it maybe something)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186862603,"data":"       | V4 = w (call it something)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186863718,"data":"       | V5 = u+v (call)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186866516,"data":"       | V6 = u+w\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186867597,"data":"       | V7 = v+w (C)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186868629,"data":"       | V8 = u+v+w (D)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186869672,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186870742,"data":"       | We need heights of all vertices to compute volume of water up to height 7. Since water surface is horizontal plane at height 7 (parallel to P). The water fills region of cube where height ≤ 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186872135,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186873137,"data":"       | Thus we need to compute volume of portion of cube below that plane.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186874265,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186875316,"data":"       | Given orientation, we can compute heights of each vertex:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186876432,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18687747,"data":"       | Height of vertex corresponding to vector combination α u + β v + γ w where α,β,γ ∈ {0,1}. Height = k·(α u + β v + γ w) = α s a + β s b + γ s b = s (α a + (β+γ) b).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186879157,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186880211,"data":"       | Since s=6, a=1/3, b=2/3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186881284,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186882365,"data":"       | Thus height = 6 (α * 1/3 + (β+γ) * 2/3) = 2α + 4 (β+γ). Wait compute: 6*(α/3) = 2α. 6*(2/3)*(β+γ) = 4 (β+γ). So height = 2α + 4(β+γ).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186884061,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186885168,"data":"       | Let's verify for known vertices:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186886237,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186887237,"data":"       | A: α=0, β=0, γ=0 =\u003e height = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186888425,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186889522,"data":"       | B: α=1, β=0, γ=0 =\u003e height = 2*1 + 0 = 2 (good).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186890706,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.1868917,"data":"       | C = v+w: α=0, β=1, γ=1 =\u003e height = 0 + 4*(2) = 8 (good).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186892961,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186893961,"data":"       | D = u+v+w: α=1, β=1, γ=1 =\u003e height = 2 + 4*2 = 10 (good).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186895205,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186896243,"data":"       | Now compute heights for remaining vertices:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186897536,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186898563,"data":"       | E = v: α=0, β=1, γ=0 =\u003e height = 0 + 4*1 = 4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186899715,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186900738,"data":"       | F = w: α=0, β=0, γ=1 =\u003e height = 0 + 4*1 = 4 (same as v). Indeed v and w have same height.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186901943,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186902955,"data":"       | G = u+v: α=1, β=1, γ=0 =\u003e height = 2 + 4*1 = 6.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186904111,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186905143,"data":"       | H = u+w: α=1, β=0, γ=1 =\u003e height = 2 + 4*1 = 6.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186907577,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186908838,"data":"       | Thus heights of vertices:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186909935,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186910928,"data":"       | 0: 0\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186911962,"data":"       | B: 2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186913004,"data":"       | v: 4\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186914037,"data":"       | w: 4\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186915048,"data":"       | u+v: 6\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186916092,"data":"       | u+w: 6\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186917162,"data":"       | C (v+w): 8\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186918203,"data":"       | D (u+v+w): 10\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186919251,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186920295,"data":"       | All heights are multiples of 2, consistent.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186921383,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186922437,"data":"       | Now water level at height 7. So vertices below 7: those with heights ≤7 are: heights 0,2,4,4,6,6 (six vertices). Those above 7: heights 8,10 (two vertices). So water fills part of cube, cutting off a tetrahedral region near top? Actually plane at height 7 intersects the cube, cutting off a smaller polyhedron comprising the part of cube with heights \u003e7 (the \"dry\" part). The water volume = total cube volume - volume of region above height 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186924343,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18692539,"data":"       | Since heights are linear along direction k, the cross-section of cube by a plane perpendicular to k (i.e., horizontal) will be a polygon whose shape changes linearly with height. The region above height 7 is a smaller similar shape to region below? Not exactly because the cube is not aligned with k.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186926972,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186928002,"data":"       | But we can compute volume of portion of cube below height h using integration or using decomposition into tetrahedra.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186929231,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186930267,"data":"       | Since heights of vertices are linear function of coordinates, the plane at height h cuts edges at points where height interpolates linearly. The region of cube above height h is a polyhedron (a truncated corner). Since only two vertices are above h (C=8, D=10), the intersection plane will cut edges incident to those vertices.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186931831,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186932861,"data":"       | Specifically, vertices above 7: C (height 8) and D (height 10). All other vertices are below 7. So the region above the plane is a tetrahedron with vertices at C, D, and the intersection points of plane with edges CD? Wait CD is edge between C (8) and D (10) (height both above). Edge CD is parallel to u (edge direction). Since both endpoints above, the entire edge CD is above plane, so part of region includes whole edge CD.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186934585,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18693579,"data":"       | But the region above plane is not just a tetrahedron; it could be a polyhedron with base being a quadrilateral formed by intersecting plane with edges connecting high vertices to low vertices.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186937156,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186940969,"data":"       | Let's examine cube edges: The cube has 12 edges. The plane at height 7 will intersect edges that connect a vertex below 7 to a vertex above 7. Those edges are:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186943728,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18694477,"data":"       | Edges from C (8) to its three neighboring vertices: C is v+w. Its neighbors are:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186945945,"data":"       | - v (height 4) via edge w? Wait C = v + w. Edges from C: to v (by subtract w), to w (by subtract v), to u+v+w (D) (by adding u). So edges:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186947248,"data":"       | C - v: heights 8 to 4 (crosses plane)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186948347,"data":"       | C - w: heights 8 to 4 (crosses)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186949435,"data":"       | C - D: heights 8 to 10 (both above, no crossing)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186950633,"data":"       | Similarly D = u+v+w has neighbors:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186951717,"data":"       | - u+v (height 6) via subtract w\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186952801,"data":"       | - u+w (height 6) via subtract v\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18695388,"data":"       | - C (via subtract u) (both above)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18695496,"data":"       | Thus edges crossing plane are:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186956057,"data":"       | C-v, C-w, D-u+v, D-u+w. Also possibly edge between C and D? No both above.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186957237,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186958303,"data":"       | Thus plane cuts four edges, producing intersection points on each. The region above plane will be a polyhedron with vertices: C, D, and the four intersection points on those edges? Wait need to consider shape: The set of points in cube with height \u003e= 7 is a convex polyhedron (since intersection of cube with half-space). Its vertices are the original vertices with height \u003e=7 (C and D) plus intersection points of plane with edges crossing it. So we have C, D, plus 4 points (call P1 on C-v, P2 on C-w, P3 on D-(u+v), P4 on D-(u+w)). That's 6 vertices. The polyhedron is a truncated tetrahedron? Actually shape might be a hexahedron (prism) but we can compute its volume.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18696053,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18696156,"data":"       | Alternatively, compute water volume directly as volume of region where height \u003c= 7. Since we have heights linear, we can compute volume using formula for volume of a convex polyhedron cut by a plane: Volume below plane = total volume - volume of region above plane.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186963647,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186965363,"data":"       | Total cube volume = s^3 = 6^3 = 216 cubic meters.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186967113,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186969075,"data":"       | Now compute volume of region above plane (height \u003e 7). Since heights of vertices are linear, we can compute using integration or using the method of \"average height\" times base area? Not straightforward.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186971467,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186973037,"data":"       | But we can compute volume of region above plane by decomposing cube into tetrahedra from a reference point (e.g., A) and using linear interpolation of height? Another way: Since height function is linear (affine) over the cube (since k·x is linear), the set of points where height ≤ h corresponds to a sublevel set of an affine function intersected with a cube. The volume of sublevel set as function of h is a piecewise cubic polynomial? But we can compute using formula for volume of a region of a convex polyhedron cut by a plane: For a convex polyhedron with vertices having known heights, the volume of portion above plane can be expressed as sum over tetrahedra formed by intersecting plane with each edge? Might be easier to compute using integration: Since height function is linear, we can change coordinates to align k as one axis.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186978018,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186979578,"data":"       | Let’s define coordinate system where k is the z' axis (vertical). In that coordinate system, the cube is some oriented parallelepiped (actually a rectangular box) because the edge vectors u, v, w are orthogonal but not aligned with k. However in k-coordinate, the cube's projection onto the k-axis (height) ranges from 0 to 10 (min to max). The cross-section at height t is the intersection of the cube with plane k·x = t, which is a parallelogram (since intersection of a plane with a rectangular parallelepiped oriented arbitrarily yields a parallelogram). The area of this cross-section varies linearly with t (since the shape is a parallelogram whose side lengths are linear functions of t). Actually for a rectangular box oriented arbitrarily, cross-section area of a plane perpendicular to a fixed direction (here k) is a quadratic function? Let's think.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18698322,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186984837,"data":"       | But we can compute volume above height 7 by integrating area of cross-section from t=7 to t=10. Since height function is linear along each edge, the cross-section at height t is a polygon (maybe a quadrilateral) whose area varies linearly with t because the intersection of a convex polyhedron with a family of parallel planes yields area that is a piecewise quadratic? Not sure.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18698711,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186988808,"data":"       | Simpler: Since we have coordinates of all vertices, we can compute the polyhedron above plane using computational geometry (convex hull of points with height \u003e=7 plus intersection points). Then compute its volume via decomposition into tetrahedra (e.g., using convex hull algorithm). Use Python to compute volume exactly rationally (since coordinates are rational multiples of s and orientation). Let's attempt.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186991415,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18699379,"data":"       | We have coordinates in terms of orthonormal basis e1, e2, e3. But we can choose actual numeric coordinates for e1, e2, e3 in 3D Euclidean space such that they are orthonormal. For simplicity, let e1 = (1,0,0), e2 = (0,1,0), e3 = (0,0,1). Then u = 6 e1 = (6,0,0), v = (0,6,0), w = (0,0,6). However this orientation would make k = (a,b,b) = (1/3, 2/3, 2/3). But this k is not unit (its magnitude sqrt((1/3)^2 + 2*(2/3)^2) = sqrt(1/9 + 8/9) = sqrt(1) =1, good). So we can use that.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186996252,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186997806,"data":"       | Thus we have actual coordinates of vertices in standard Cartesian coordinates:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.186999715,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187001125,"data":"       | A = (0,0,0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187002786,"data":"       | B = (6,0,0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187004467,"data":"       | v = (0,6,0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187005934,"data":"       | w = (0,0,6)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187007569,"data":"       | u+v = (6,6,0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187009177,"data":"       | u+w = (6,0,6)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187012935,"data":"       | v+w = (0,6,6) (C)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187014436,"data":"       | u+v+w = (6,6,6) (D)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187015945,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187017557,"data":"       | Now compute heights using dot product with k = (1/3, 2/3, 2/3). Let's verify:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18701947,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187021092,"data":"       | k·B = (1/3)*6 = 2 (good)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187023027,"data":"       | k·v = (2/3)*6 = 4 (good)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187024784,"data":"       | k·w = (2/3)*6 = 4 (good)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187026516,"data":"       | k·(u+v) = 2 + 4 = 6 (good)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187028339,"data":"       | k·(u+w) = 2 + 4 = 6\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18703,"data":"       | k·(v+w) = 8\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187031705,"data":"       | k·(u+v+w) = 10\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187033298,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18703489,"data":"       | Thus coordinates are simple.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187036696,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187038327,"data":"       | Now we can compute intersection points of plane h=7 with edges crossing.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187040044,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187041286,"data":"       | Edges crossing plane: list edges of cube where one endpoint height \u003c7 and other \u003e7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187042597,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187043635,"data":"       | We have heights:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18704469,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187045731,"data":"       | A 0\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187046777,"data":"       | B 2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187047844,"data":"       | v 4\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187048895,"data":"       | w 4\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187049915,"data":"       | u+v 6\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187051128,"data":"       | u+w 6\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187052147,"data":"       | C 8\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187053165,"data":"       | D 10\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187054206,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187055249,"data":"       | Edges of cube (12 edges) connect vertices that differ by a single edge vector u, v, w.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18705646,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18705747,"data":"       | Edge list (pairs):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187058534,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187059563,"data":"       | A-B (0-2) both below -\u003e no crossing.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187060739,"data":"       | A-v (0-4) below.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187061791,"data":"       | A-w (0-4) below.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187062845,"data":"       | B-u+v (2-6) below.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187063887,"data":"       | B-u+w (2-6) below.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18706495,"data":"       | v-u+v (4-6) below.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187066039,"data":"       | v-v+w (4-8) crossing (v to C). Indeed edge v to C (v to v+w) is w direction.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187068377,"data":"       | w-v+w (4-8) crossing (w to C) (w to v+w) is v direction.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187069532,"data":"       | u+v - D (6-10) crossing (edge u+v to D) via w direction.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187070655,"data":"       | u+w - D (6-10) crossing (edge u+w to D) via v direction.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187071795,"data":"       | C - D (8-10) both above.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187072873,"data":"       | A - ??? also edges to u? Actually A to u (which is B) already considered. A to v, A to w done.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187074082,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187075126,"data":"       | Thus crossing edges are:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187076188,"data":"       | 1) v (0,6,0) to C (0,6,6) (edge along w)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187077291,"data":"       | 2) w (0,0,6) to C (0,6,6) (edge along v)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187078394,"data":"       | 3) u+v (6,6,0) to D (6,6,6) (edge along w)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187079541,"data":"       | 4) u+w (6,0,6) to D (6,6,6) (edge along v)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187080642,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18708169,"data":"       | Also maybe edge between B (6,0,0) and ? B to ? Not crossing because B height 2, neighbor u+v (6) not crossing (both below). B to u+w (6) also below. B to ? B to ? Actually B's neighbors are A, u+v, u+w. All below.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187083747,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187084783,"data":"       | Thus we have 4 crossing edges.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187085855,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187086864,"data":"       | Now we can compute intersection points P1-P4 where height = 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187088326,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187090227,"data":"       | Parametric: For edge from low point L (height hL) to high point H (height hH), intersection point = L + t*(H - L) where t = (7 - hL)/(hH - hL).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187092298,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187093946,"data":"       | Compute for each edge.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187095815,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187097452,"data":"       | Edge v (height 4) to C (height 8): t = (7-4)/(8-4) = 3/4 = 0.75. So point P1 = v + 0.75*(C - v) = v + 0.75*w (since C = v + w). So coordinates: v = (0,6,0). w = (0,0,6). So P1 = (0,6,0) + 0.75*(0,0,6) = (0,6,4.5).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18709971,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187101754,"data":"       | Edge w (height 4) to C (height 8): t = (7-4)/(8-4) = 3/4 as well. Edge direction v. So P2 = w + 0.75*v = (0,0,6) + 0.75*(0,6,0) = (0,4.5,6).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187103893,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187105652,"data":"       | Edge u+v (height 6) to D (height 10): t = (7-6)/(10-6) = 1/4 = 0.25. Edge direction w. So P3 = (6,6,0) + 0.25*(0,0,6) = (6,6,1.5).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187107767,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187109576,"data":"       | Edge u+w (height 6) to D (height 10): t = (7-6)/(10-6) = 1/4. Edge direction v. So P4 = (6,0,6) + 0.25*(0,6,0) = (6,1.5,6).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187111615,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187113312,"data":"       | Thus the region above plane is a polyhedron with vertices: C (0,6,6), D (6,6,6), and points P1 (0,6,4.5), P2 (0,4.5,6), P3 (6,6,1.5), P4 (6,1.5,6). Wait note that C and D are also part of region above plane. However note that edges C-D both above, so region includes that top edge.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187117311,"data":"       | \n"}
