,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187795098,"data":"       | analysisWe need to parse problem: Cube-shaped container (i.e., a cube). Vertices A,B,C,D are four vertices of the cube such that AB and CD are parallel edges, and AC and BD are diagonals of faces. So they form a rectangle ABDC? Wait rectangle ABDC is plane containing A,B,D,C? Actually rectangle ABDC is a rectangle formed by those four vertices: AB edge, CD edge parallel to AB, AC diagonal of a face, BD diagonal of opposite face? Let's think.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187799563,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18780131,"data":"       | In a cube, there are 8 vertices. Choose a pair of opposite edges that are parallel and not adjacent? AB and CD are parallel edges. So they could be opposite edges of a face? Actually opposite edges of a face are parallel but not adjacent; they are across the square. However AB and CD being parallel edges could be edges of the same face but not adjacent (i.e., opposite sides of a face). But then AC would be a diagonal of that face (connecting opposite vertices of the face). But BD would also be diagonal of that same face? Wait if AB and CD are opposite sides of a face, then the vertices are A, B, C, D forming a rectangle (actually a square) that is a face of the cube. In that case AC and BD are both diagonals of the same face, intersecting at its center. However the problem says AC and BD are diagonals of faces of the cube (maybe different faces). Hmm.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187805367,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187807366,"data":"       | Let's examine typical labeling: In a cube, there are 4 space diagonals connecting opposite vertices. There are also face diagonals. If we have vertices A,B,C,D such that AB and CD are parallel edges (not necessarily same face). AC and BD are diagonals of faces (maybe different faces). The rectangle ABDC (order A-B-D-C?) Wait rectangle ABDC includes vertices A, B, D, C (maybe not in that order). The plane of rectangle ABDC is perpendicular to plane P (horizontal plane). So rectangle ABDC is vertical plane (perpendicular to horizontal). So AB and CD are vertical edges? Actually rectangle ABDC is vertical plane; its plane is perpendicular to horizontal plane P. So rectangle ABDC is like a vertical rectangle inside the cube (like a cross-section). So AB and CD are edges of the cube that are parallel and vertical? Let's analyze.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187811376,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187813076,"data":"       | We have cube placed such that vertex A is on plane P (horizontal). The plane of rectangle ABDC is perpendicular to P (i.e., vertical). So rectangle ABDC is a vertical rectangle formed by vertices A, B, D, C (maybe in that order). So AB is an edge of the cube, CD is opposite edge of rectangle ABDC, parallel to AB. So AB and CD are vertical edges (since rectangle is vertical). But the cube is oriented arbitrarily relative to horizontal plane P, not necessarily axis-aligned. So edges AB and CD are not necessarily vertical (i.e., perpendicular to P) but they lie in a vertical plane (plane perpendicular to P). So the rectangle ABDC is vertical, meaning its plane is perpendicular to horizontal plane. So the rectangle's normal vector is horizontal. So the rectangle's edges AB and CD are not necessarily vertical; they could be slanted but lying in vertical plane.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187817098,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187818743,"data":"       | Given heights above plane P: B is 2 m above P, C is 8 m above P, D is 10 m above P, and A is on P (height 0). So we have coordinates for heights (z-values) of vertices: z_A = 0, z_B = 2, z_C = 8, z_D = 10.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187821002,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187822651,"data":"       | Cube side length unknown s. The cube is oriented such that these four vertices correspond to some vertices of the cube. Need to determine s and orientation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187824705,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187826576,"data":"       | Goal: water surface is horizontal (parallel to P) at height 7 m above P. Cube contains water up to that level. So we need volume of portion of cube below height 7 (i.e., water fills region of cube with z \u003c= 7). Since cube may be partially above water level. We need volume of intersection of cube with half-space z \u003c= 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187830794,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187832452,"data":"       | Given heights of vertices, we can compute the cube's orientation and side length, then compute volume of part below plane z = 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187834427,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.187836098,"data":"       | We have coordinates of four vertices (A,B,C,D) unknown x,y positions but known z coordinates. However we also know they are vertices of a cube with edges of equal length s and orthogonal edges.\n"}
