,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189093662,"data":"       | Goal: compute volume of intersection of cube with half-space z \u003c= 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18909496,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18909612,"data":"       | Given coordinates of A,B,C,D relative to P? We can set up coordinate system: Let plane P be z=0 horizontal. Let rectangle ABDC plane be vertical; we can define coordinates such that A at (0,0,0). Since AB is an edge of cube (edge length s). B is at height 2. Since AB is an edge, its length is s. So distance between A and B is s. B is at height 2, A at 0, so the edge AB is not horizontal; it has vertical component 2. So AB is some vector with length s and vertical component 2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189098101,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189099259,"data":"       | Similarly CD is opposite edge, parallel to AB, with same length s, and its endpoints C (height 8) and D (height 10). So vector from C to D is same as from A to B (parallel and same magnitude). Indeed CD is edge of cube, parallel to AB, length s, vertical component (10-8)=2 as well. So consistent.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18910092,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189102085,"data":"       | Now AC is a diagonal of a face of the cube. Which face? Since AC is a diagonal, the face containing A and C is a square face of the cube (since diagonal of a face). The face includes vertices A, something, something, C. Edge length s, diagonal length s*sqrt(2). So distance between A and C is s*sqrt(2). Similarly B and D distance is also s*sqrt(2). Let's compute distances using heights: vertical difference between A (0) and C (8) is 8. So the squared distance between A and C is s^2 * 2 = (horizontal component)^2 + (vertical diff)^2. But we don't know horizontal components.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18910587,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189107326,"data":"       | We can treat unknown orientation: Let's assign coordinates: Let A = (0,0,0). Let vector v = AB = (x1, y1, 2) where length s: x1^2 + y1^2 + 2^2 = s^2. Let w = AC = (x2, y2, 8) (since C height 8). Since AC is a face diagonal, w is perpendicular to v? Wait in a square face, the diagonal is not perpendicular to an edge; but the edge of the face adjacent to A is either AB or another edge (say AD?). Actually the face containing A and C also contains another vertex (call E) such that A-E is an edge, and C-E is an edge. The diagonal AC is across the face; AB is an edge of the cube but may not be on that same face. Since AB is an edge; the face containing A and C could be the face formed by A, some vertex (call X), some vertex (call Y), and C. AB may not be part of that face.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189109852,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189110994,"data":"       | But we know AC is diagonal of a face, meaning AC vector is sum of two orthogonal edge vectors of the cube (edges meeting at A). The cube has three orthogonal edge vectors: let's denote e1, e2, e3 (mutually perpendicular, length s). The vertices of cube can be expressed as A + linear combinations of e1, e2, e3 (0 or 1). Suppose we set e1 = AB (edge). Then AB = e1. Then other edges from A are e2 and e3 (orthogonal to e1 and each other). Then the opposite vertex (across space diagonal) is A + e1 + e2 + e3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18911306,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189114475,"data":"       | Now AC is diagonal of a face, so AC = e_i + e_j for some i != j. Since AB = e1, AC could be e1 + e2 (or e1 + e3, or e2 + e3). But AC must be a diagonal of a face that includes A, so it's sum of two edges from A. Which pair? Could be e1 + e2 or e1 + e3 or e2 + e3. However AB is e1, so AC could be e1 + e2 (face formed by edges e1 and e2) or e1 + e3 (face formed by e1 and e3). But we also have BD is diagonal of a face, and B = A + e1. BD = diagonal of a face containing B. If AC = e1 + e2, then the face is spanned by e1 and e2. Then the opposite vertex of that face is A + e1 + e2 = C. So B is A+e1, D would be? BD diagonal of a face: which face includes B? Could be the face spanned by e1 and e3 (i.e., B + e3 + ...?). Wait B is A+e1. The faces containing B are: (e1,e2) face (same as A's face), (e1,e3) face, and (e2,e3) face offset. But BD diagonal of a face, and D is opposite vertex of that face. We need D such that BD = sum of two orthogonal edge vectors from B. Could be B + e2 + e3? Actually B + e2 + e3 would be opposite vertex of face spanned by e2 and e3 that passes through B? Let's think.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189117581,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189118718,"data":"       | But we also have CD is parallel to AB (i.e., e1). Since CD = e1 (parallel). Indeed CD vector = D - C = e1 (or -e1). Since CD is parallel to AB and same direction (maybe same orientation). So D = C + e1 = (A + e_i + e_j) + e1. If e1 = AB, then D = A + e_i + e_j + e1. But note we already have B = A + e1. So D = B + e_i + e_j? Wait D = C + e1 = A + (some sum) + e1.\n"}
