,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189122974,"data":"       | Now we also have BD is a diagonal of a face. Let's compute BD = D - B = (C + e1) - (A + e1) = C - A = AC. So BD = AC! Indeed if D = C + e1, then BD = C - A = AC. So BD is same vector as AC, meaning BD is also a diagonal of a face (same length). That matches condition: AC and BD are diagonals of faces. So we have consistency.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189124761,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18912591,"data":"       | Thus the configuration: Choose orthogonal edge vectors e1, e2, e3. Let AB = e1. Let AC = e2 + e3 (or e1 + e2?). Wait we derived that D = C + e1, which implies CD = e1 (parallel to AB). Then BD = C - A = AC. So AC = BD. Since both are diagonals of faces, they must be equal length and direction? Not necessarily direction but they are same vector (since BD = AC). That would mean AC and BD are parallel and equal magnitude. Are they supposed to be? The problem says \"AC and BD are diagonals of faces of the cube\". Not necessarily equal. But they could be equal due to geometry.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189128153,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189129318,"data":"       | Let's test: Suppose AC = e2 + e3 (i.e., diagonal of face spanned by e2, e3). Then D = C + e1 = A + e2 + e3 + e1. Then BD = D - B = (A + e1 + e2 + e3) - (A + e1) = e2 + e3 = AC. So indeed BD = AC. So both are same diagonal of opposite parallel faces (faces spanned by e2 and e3). That matches condition: AC and BD are diagonals of faces (maybe opposite faces). So it's consistent.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18913119,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189132329,"data":"       | Thus we can set up coordinate system: Let e1 be vector AB = (x, y, 2). Its length s: x^2 + y^2 + 4 = s^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189133657,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189135016,"data":"       | Let e2 and e3 be orthogonal to each other and to e1 (mutually perpendicular). They lie in plane perpendicular to e1? Wait edges of cube are orthogonal, so e2 and e3 are orthogonal to e1 and to each other.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189136515,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189137638,"data":"       | Now AC = e2 + e3 = (some vector) with vertical component 8 (since C height 8). So (e2 + e3)_z = 8.\n"}
