,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189191303,"data":"       | Thus (r + t)^2 + (s2 + v)^2 = 2 L^2 - 64.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189192956,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189194128,"data":"       | Also, we have AB height 2, CD height 10 (consistent). So we have enough unknowns.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189195427,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189196531,"data":"       | We can perhaps solve for L using dot products.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18919775,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189198908,"data":"       | Since e2 and e3 are orthogonal and each orthogonal to e1, they lie in the plane perpendicular to e1. That plane is vertical (since e1 has vertical component 2). The sum e2+e3 has vertical component 8. So each of e2, e3 may have vertical components that sum to 8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189201954,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189203167,"data":"       | We can approach by using orthonormal basis: Let e1 be known direction (unit vector). Then e2 and e3 are any orthonormal vectors perpendicular to e1, scaled by L. So we can think of coordinate system where e1 is along z' axis (some direction). In that coordinate system, e2 and e3 are orthogonal axes in the perpendicular plane.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189204913,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189206017,"data":"       | Let’s define unit vectors:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189207269,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189208411,"data":"       | u1 = e1 / L (direction of edge AB). Then we need two unit vectors u2, u3 orthonormal to u1 and each other. Then e2 = L * u2, e3 = L * u3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18920981,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18921092,"data":"       | Thus e2 + e3 = L (u2 + u3). Its vertical component (z) is 8. The vertical component of u2 + u3 is (u2_z + u3_z). Since u2 and u3 are orthonormal and both orthogonal to u1, they lie in plane perpendicular to u1. We know u1 has components (p/L, q/L, 2/L). So u1_z = 2/L.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189212532,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18921368,"data":"       | Now we need to find L such that there exist orthonormal vectors u2, u3 perpendicular to u1 with sum vertical component 8/L.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189215069,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189216212,"data":"       | Let’s denote the vertical axis as the standard z-axis (global). We have known u1 (unit). We need to find orthonormal basis of the plane perpendicular to u1. For any orthonormal pair (u2, u3) in that plane, the vector sum s = u2 + u3 will have length sqrt(2 + 2 (u2·u3)) = sqrt(2) because u2·u3 =0. Wait magnitude of sum of orthogonal unit vectors is sqrt(1^2 + 1^2) = sqrt(2). So |u2 + u3| = sqrt(2). Indeed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189218189,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18921951,"data":"       | Thus e2 + e3 = L (u2 + u3) has magnitude L * sqrt(2) (consistent with diagonal length). Its direction is some unit vector in that plane at 45 degrees between u2 and u3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189220966,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189222096,"data":"       | We know its vertical component (z) is 8. So (u2 + u3)_z * L = 8. So (u2_z + u3_z) * L = 8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189223398,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189224528,"data":"       | Let’s denote a = u2_z, b = u3_z. Then a + b = 8/L.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189225815,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189226991,"data":"       | We also have constraints: u2 and u3 are unit vectors orthogonal to each other and to u1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189228294,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189229448,"data":"       | Thus we need to find L such that there exist orthonormal vectors u2, u3 with given sum of z-components a+b = 8/L.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189230788,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189231931,"data":"       | But we also know u1_z = 2/L (since e1_z=2). Since u2 and u3 are orthogonal to u1, they satisfy dot product zero: u2·u1 = 0 =\u003e u2_x * u1_x + u2_y * u1_y + u2_z * u1_z = 0. Similarly for u3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189233538,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189234665,"data":"       | Thus each of u2, u3 have z components limited by u1_z.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189237255,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18923844,"data":"       | We can approach by constructing orthonormal basis: Choose any unit vector u2 orthogonal to u1. Then define u3 = u1 × u2 (or some orthogonal vector) to ensure orthonormal. The z-components of u2 and u3 will depend on the choice.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189240042,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189241187,"data":"       | Goal: find maximum possible sum of z-components of u2 and u3 given u1_z fixed. Since we can rotate within plane perpendicular to u1, the sum of z components may vary. We need to find L such that sum equals 8/L.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189242732,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189243902,"data":"       | Thus we need to compute possible values of a + b = (u2_z + u3_z) given u1_z = 2/L.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18924535,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189246473,"data":"       | Let’s treat u1 fixed. Then we can param param.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189247733,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189248871,"data":"       | Let’s denote coordinate system where u1 is some vector with components (α, β, γ) where γ = 2/L, α^2 + β^2 + γ^2 = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189250418,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189251891,"data":"       | We can choose orthonormal vectors u2, u3 spanning the plane orthogonal to u1. The sum of their z-components is the projection of the vector (0,0,1) (the global z unit) onto the subspace spanned by u2 and u3, expressed as dot product with (u2+u3). Since u2+u3 is not unit; but we can think of vector w = u2 + u3. Its z-component is a + b. Its squared length is 2. So w is a vector of length sqrt(2) lying in the plane orthogonal to u1. Its z-component is the projection of (0,0,1) onto that plane, scaled appropriately? Let's compute.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189253987,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189255134,"data":"       | Given any vector w in the plane orthogonal to u1, we can write w = projection of (0,0,1) onto that plane multiplied by some factor plus orthogonal component within plane. However, to maximize z-component for given length sqrt(2), we would align w with the component of the global z-axis within the plane.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189256847,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189258005,"data":"       | The maximum possible z-component of a vector w of length sqrt(2) lying in plane orthogonal to u1 is sqrt(2) * (length of projection of unit z onto that plane). Because we can align w with the direction of the projection.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189259537,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189260672,"data":"       | Thus the maximum possible a+b = w_z_max = sqrt(2) * |proj_{plane}(e_z)|, where e_z = (0,0,1). The projection onto plane orthogonal to u1 is e_z - (e_z·u1) u1 = e_z - γ u1. Its magnitude squared = 1 - γ^2 (since e_z is unit and component along u1 is γ). So |proj| = sqrt(1 - γ^2).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189262438,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189263563,"data":"       | Thus maximum possible a+b = sqrt(2) * sqrt(1 - γ^2). Minimum possible is negative of that (by opposite direction). Since we can choose orientation to get any value between -max and max (by rotating w within plane). So the possible values of a+b are any real number with absolute value ≤ sqrt(2) * sqrt(1 - γ^2).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189265371,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189267882,"data":"       | Thus we have constraint:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.1892691,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189270249,"data":"       | |a + b| ≤ sqrt(2) * sqrt(1 - γ^2).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189271619,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189272724,"data":"       | But a+b = 8/L.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189273918,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18927502,"data":"       | Thus:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189276175,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189277294,"data":"       | |8/L| ≤ sqrt(2) * sqrt(1 - γ^2).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189278541,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189279699,"data":"       | But γ = u1_z = 2/L.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189280901,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189281993,"data":"       | Thus:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189283102,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189284211,"data":"       | 8/L ≤ sqrt(2) * sqrt(1 - (2/L)^2).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18928545,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189286555,"data":"       | Since L positive, we can drop absolute.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189287767,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189288906,"data":"       | Square both sides:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189290049,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189291235,"data":"       | (64)/(L^2) ≤ 2 * (1 - 4/L^2) = 2 - 8/L^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189292478,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18929357,"data":"       | Multiply by L^2:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189294723,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18929583,"data":"       | 64 ≤ 2 L^2 - 8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189297003,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189298146,"data":"       | =\u003e 2 L^2 - 8 - 64 ≥ 0? Wait inequality direction: 64 ≤ 2 L^2 - 8 =\u003e 2 L^2 - 8 - 64 ≥ 0 =\u003e 2 L^2 - 72 ≥ 0 =\u003e L^2 ≥ 36 =\u003e L ≥ 6.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189299893,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189301354,"data":"       | Thus edge length L must be at least 6. Also there is possibly upper bound? Since we need existence, the condition is L \u003e=6. But also L must be such that 8/L ≤ sqrt(2) * sqrt(1 - 4/L^2). For L \u003e=6, is inequality satisfied? Let's test L=6: compute left 8/6 = 4/3 ≈1.333. Right sqrt(2)*sqrt(1 - 4/36) = sqrt(2)*sqrt(1 - 1/9) = sqrt(2)*sqrt(8/9) = sqrt(2)* (2*sqrt(2)/3) = (2*2)/3 = 4/3. Indeed equality at L=6. For larger L, left decreases, right also changes: sqrt(1 - 4/L^2) decreases as L increases? Actually 4/L^2 decreases, so (1 - 4/L^2) increases? Wait L larger =\u003e 4/L^2 smaller =\u003e 1 - smaller = larger. So sqrt term increases (approaches 1). So right side increases (approaches sqrt(2)). So inequality holds for L \u003e=6. So minimal L is 6, but any larger L also works. However the geometry is determined by given heights of vertices; L cannot be arbitrarily large because AB length must be L, and AB vertical component is 2. So L must satisfy that the horizontal component length sqrt(L^2 - 4) is some value; not constrained further.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189304602,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189305745,"data":"       | But we also have that AC vertical component is 8, which is sum of u and w (vertical components of e2 and e3). The maximum possible sum of vertical components is sqrt(2) * sqrt(L^2 - (vertical component of u1)^2?) Wait we derived condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189309582,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189310742,"data":"       | Thus L could be any value \u003e=6. But does the given heights of B=2, C=8, D=10 uniquely determine L? Let's compute C height = 8 = (e2+e3)_z = L*(a+b). So a+b = 8/L.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189312233,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189313395,"data":"       | But we also have that a+b can be any value between -sqrt(2)*sqrt(1 - γ^2) and +..., where γ = 2/L.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189314807,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189315961,"data":"       | Since we found that the maximal possible a+b equals sqrt(2)*sqrt(1 - (2/L)^2). This is exactly the bound we used. For given L, we can achieve any a+b up to that bound by rotating w appropriately. Since we need a+b = 8/L, we must have 8/L ≤ sqrt(2)*sqrt(1 - (2/L)^2). That gives L \u003e=6. So any L \u003e=6 works; we can adjust orientation to achieve required sum.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189317873,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18931925,"data":"       | Thus L not uniquely determined; but water volume may be independent of L? Possibly volume depends only on heights of vertices relative to water level. Since water level is at 7, which is between B=2 and C=8, D=10. So water fills part of cube up to plane z=7. The shape of intersection maybe a polyhedron whose volume depends on L but perhaps cancels out? Let's analyze.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189321066,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18932224,"data":"       | We have cube with vertices: A (0), B (2), C (8), D (10). The other four vertices (the opposite face) will have heights: let's compute heights of the vertices opposite A,B,C,D (i.e., A' = A + e2 + e3 = C? Wait A + e2 + e3 = C (since AC = e2+e3). Actually A + e2 + e3 = C. So C is opposite of A across the face spanned by e2 and e3. Similarly B' = B + e2 + e3 = D? Let's verify: B = A + e1. Then B + e2 + e3 = A + e1 + e2 + e3 = opposite vertex of cube (the farthest). That vertex would be the one opposite A across space diagonal. Let's call it G. Its height would be (z of A) + (z of e1) + (z of e2) + (z of e3) = 0 + 2 + (u + w) = 2 + 8 = 10? Wait u + w = 8, so height = 2 + 8 = 10. That's D? Actually D is C + e1, height 8+2=10. So D = G? Let's check: D = C + e1 = A + e2 + e3 + e1 = G. So D is opposite vertex of cube (space diagonal from A). Indeed D is the vertex opposite A across space diagonal. So the cube's vertices are: A (0), B (2), C (8), D (10). The other three vertices? Let's list all 8 vertices of cube based on A and edge vectors e1,e2,e3:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189325251,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18932638,"data":"       | - A = (0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18932751,"data":"       | - A+e1 = B (height 2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189328684,"data":"       | - A+e2 = ? call E (height u)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189329897,"data":"       | - A+e3 = ? call F (height w)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189331097,"data":"       | - A+e1+e2 = B+e2 = ? call G (height 2+u)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189332302,"data":"       | - A+e1+e3 = B+e3 = ? call H (height 2+w)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189333522,"data":"       | - A+e2+e3 = C (height 8)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189334689,"data":"       | - A+e1+e2+e3 = D (height 10)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189335916,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189338469,"data":"       | Thus we have heights for each vertex expressed in terms of u and w (vertical components of e2,e3). We know u + w = 8. Unknown individual u,w.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189339901,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189341018,"data":"       | Thus we have heights:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189342203,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189343311,"data":"       | A: 0\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18934446,"data":"       | B: 2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189345586,"data":"       | E: u\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189346757,"data":"       | F: w\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189359276,"data":"       | G: 2+u\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189361231,"data":"       | H: 2+w\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189363064,"data":"       | C: 8\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189364967,"data":"       | D: 10\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189366867,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189369227,"data":"       | Now water level at height 7. So vertices below water: those with height ≤ 7. Let's compute categories based on u,w.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189370768,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189372001,"data":"       | Since u + w = 8, and both u,w are between? Since e2 and e3 have length L, and vertical component magnitude cannot exceed L. So u,w ∈ [-L, L]. But also we know u + w = 8 \u003e0, so both likely positive. Since L \u003e=6, and u,w may be up to L, but sum 8. So possible values: u between 0 and 8 (if w positive). Actually could be negative but then other positive \u003e8. But w must be ≤ L, etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189374023,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189375187,"data":"       | We don't know u individually, which determines heights of E,F,G,H.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189376458,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189377584,"data":"       | But water volume maybe independent of u (i.e., only depends on heights of B,C,D). Let's test by reasoning.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18937893,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189380085,"data":"       | The water region is the intersection of cube with half-space z ≤ 7. This is a convex polyhedron (a truncated cube). Its volume can be computed as total cube volume minus volume of part above water (z\u003e7). Since water level is above some vertices and below others.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189381816,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189383,"data":"       | Vertices above water (height \u003e7): those with height \u003e7: C (8), D (10), maybe G (2+u) if u\u003e5, maybe H (2+w) if w\u003e5, maybe E (u) if u\u003e7 (unlikely), F (w) if w\u003e7 (unlikely). Since u + w = 8, max of u or w is at most 8 (if other 0). So could be \u003e7. So there may be multiple vertices above water.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189384952,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189386104,"data":"       | But perhaps water volume is independent of u,w because the shape of region above water is a tetrahedron of fixed volume? Let's examine geometry.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189387534,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189388681,"data":"       | Since plane z=7 cuts the cube; the intersection polygon on each edge is linear interpolation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189390022,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189391184,"data":"       | We can compute volume of part below water as integral of area of cross-section at height z from 0 to 7. Since cube is a convex polyhedron defined by linear inequalities from vertices.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189394782,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189396164,"data":"       | But perhaps easier: Since cube is defined by linear transformation of unit cube: coordinates = A + M * (x,y,z) where (x,y,z) in [0,1]^3 in edge coordinate basis (e1,e2,e3). Indeed the cube can be represented as { A + s1 e1 + s2 e2 + s3 e3 | 0 ≤ s1,s2,s3 ≤ 1 }.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189397872,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189399009,"data":"       | The height (global z-coordinate) of a point is z = s1 * e1_z + s2 * e2_z + s3 * e3_z (since A_z=0). So z = 2 s1 + u s2 + w s3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189400382,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189401522,"data":"       | We need volume of set where 0 ≤ s_i ≤ 1 and 2 s1 + u s2 + w s3 ≤ 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189402865,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189404005,"data":"       | Since the transformation from (s1,s2,s3) to physical space is linear with determinant |det[e1 e2 e3]| = L^3 (since orthogonal basis scaled by L). Actually e1, e2, e3 are orthogonal and each length L, so volume of cube = L^3. Indeed determinant = L^3 (since orthogonal matrix times L scaling). So volume of region under water = L^3 * (volume in (s1,s2,s3) space of region defined by inequality). Since transformation is linear scaling by L in each orthogonal direction, volume factor L^3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189405998,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189407143,"data":"       | Thus water volume V = L^3 * V_s, where V_s is volume of region in unit cube coordinates (s1,s2,s3) where 2 s1 + u s2 + w s3 ≤ 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189408575,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189409698,"data":"       | But note that s_i in [0,1]. Since u,w positive maybe less than L but not needed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189410978,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189412135,"data":"       | We can compute V_s independent of L? Because u and w scale with L? Since u + w = 8, but u,w themselves are components of e2,e3 vertical components. However e2 and e3 have length L, so u ≤ L, w ≤ L, and also u^2 + (horizontal)^2 = L^2. So u = L * cosθ2 where θ2 is angle between e2 and vertical axis. Similarly w = L * cosθ3. So u = L * a, w = L * b, where a,b are cosines of angles, satisfying a^2 + (horizontal component)^2 =1. But we also have e2·e3 =0, which imposes relation between a,b and other components.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189414364,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189415552,"data":"       | But perhaps u and w are not independent of L; they satisfy u^2 + w^2 + ...? Let's derive constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189416859,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189418009,"data":"       | Since e2 and e3 are orthogonal unit vectors scaled by L: u = L * (u_hat_z), w = L * (w_hat_z), where u_hat and w_hat are unit vectors orthogonal to each other and to u1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18941946,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.1894206,"data":"       | Thus the vertical components a = u_hat_z, b = w_hat_z satisfy:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189421839,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189423136,"data":"       | - a^2 + b^2 + (2/L)^2 = 1? Wait each unit vector's squared components sum to 1: a^2 + (horizontal component)^2 =1. Similarly for b.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189426298,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189427511,"data":"       | - Orthogonality: u_hat·w_hat = 0 =\u003e a*b + (horizontal components dot) = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18942886,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189430015,"data":"       | But we can use property that the sum of squares of vertical components of an orthonormal set equals 1 (since sum of squares of components of each orthonormal vector along a fixed axis equals 1). Indeed for orthonormal vectors u1, u2, u3 (unit), we have u1_z^2 + u2_z^2 + u3_z^2 = 1 (since each vector's z-component squared, sum across orthonormal basis equals squared length of projection of standard basis onto the subspace). Because the matrix of orthonormal vectors is orthogonal, columns are orthonormal; each row also orthonormal. So the sum of squares of entries in any row is 1. Therefore:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189432217,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189433357,"data":"       | (u1_z)^2 + (u2_z)^2 + (u3_z)^2 = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189434568,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189435711,"data":"       | We have u1_z = 2/L, u2_z = a, u3_z = b. So:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189436911,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189438057,"data":"       | (2/L)^2 + a^2 + b^2 = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189439989,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189441129,"data":"       | Thus a^2 + b^2 = 1 - 4/L^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189442313,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189443436,"data":"       | Also, orthogonality between u2 and u3 gives a*b + (horizontal components dot) = 0. Not needed maybe.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189444747,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189445862,"data":"       | But we also have a + b = 8/L (since u + w = L(a + b) = 8).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189447096,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189448215,"data":"       | Thus we have two equations for a,b:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189449413,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189450531,"data":"       | (1) a^2 + b^2 = 1 - 4/L^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189451694,"data":"       | (2) a + b = 8/L.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189452855,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189453984,"data":"       | We can solve for a,b in terms of L. Let's do algebra.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189455256,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189456358,"data":"       | Let S = a + b = 8/L.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189457505,"data":"       | Let P = a b.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189458656,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189459791,"data":"       | We have a^2 + b^2 = (a+b)^2 - 2ab = S^2 - 2P = 1 - 4/L^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189461016,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189462135,"data":"       | Thus P = (S^2 - (1 - 4/L^2))/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189463308,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189464456,"data":"       | Compute S^2 = 64 / L^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189465592,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189466738,"data":"       | Thus P = (64/L^2 - (1 - 4/L^2))/2 = (64/L^2 - 1 + 4/L^2)/2 = ((68/L^2) - 1)/2 = (68/L^2 - 1)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189468036,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189470654,"data":"       | Now we also have condition that a,b are real numbers, which requires discriminant (a-b)^2 = (a+b)^2 - 4ab = S^2 - 4P ≥ 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189472115,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189473271,"data":"       | Compute S^2 - 4P = 64/L^2 - 4 * ((68/L^2 - 1)/2) = 64/L^2 - 2*(68/L^2 - 1) = 64/L^2 - (136/L^2) + 2 = (64 - 136)/L^2 + 2 = (-72)/L^2 + 2 = 2 - 72/L^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189474738,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189476041,"data":"       | Thus discriminant nonnegative gives 2 - 72/L^2 ≥ 0 =\u003e L^2 ≥ 36 =\u003e L ≥ 6, consistent.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189477517,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189478631,"data":"       | Now we can compute a,b explicitly: a,b = (S ± sqrt(discriminant))/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189479934,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189481068,"data":"       | Thus a = (S + sqrt(2 - 72/L^2))/2, b = (S - sqrt(...))/2 (or vice versa). So they depend on L.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189482421,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189483557,"data":"       | Now we need water volume: V = L^3 * V_s, where region defined by inequality 2 s1 + L a s2 + L b s3 ≤ 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189484901,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189486018,"data":"       | Simplify: inequality: 2 s1 + L a s2 + L b s3 ≤ 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189487277,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189488381,"data":"       | Since s_i ∈ [0,1].\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189489551,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189490714,"data":"       | We can factor L maybe.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189491873,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18949298,"data":"       | Define t = 7/L. Then inequality becomes (2/L) s1 + a s2 + b s3 ≤ t.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189494231,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18949535,"data":"       | Let α = 2/L, β = a, γ = b.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189496645,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189497745,"data":"       | Thus α s1 + β s2 + γ s3 ≤ t, with 0≤s_i≤1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189499091,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189500244,"data":"       | We know α = 2/L, β+γ = S = 8/L = 4α (since α = 2/L, then 4α = 8/L = S). Indeed β+γ = 4α. Also α^2 + β^2 + γ^2 = 1/L^2 * (4 + L^2 a^2 + L^2 b^2?) Wait we have earlier: (2/L)^2 + a^2 + b^2 = 1, i.e., α^2 + β^2 + γ^2 = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189502126,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189503254,"data":"       | Thus (α, β, γ) is a unit vector in ℝ^3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189504564,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189505688,"data":"       | Inequality α s1 + β s2 + γ s3 ≤ t.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189506973,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189508072,"data":"       | Since (α,β,γ) is unit, t = 7/L.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18950936,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189510484,"data":"       | Now we need volume of region in unit cube where dot product with unit vector ≤ t.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18951181,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189512951,"data":"       | This is a standard problem: volume of portion of unit cube cut by a plane with normal vector (α,β,γ) passing through point where dot = t.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189515596,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189517045,"data":"       | Since the cube is axis-aligned in s-space (0-1). The region is a tetrahedron-like polyhedron. The volume depends only on t and orientation? Actually for a unit cube, the volume of region {x∈[0,1]^3: u·x ≤ t} where u is a unit vector with positive components? Are α,β,γ positive? Let's see: α=2/L positive. a,b positive? Since u and w positive? Likely a,b positive because sum is 8/L positive. Since L≥6, each a,b could be positive. Indeed from earlier discriminant, a,b are positive? Let's verify with L=6: α=2/6=1/3, S=8/6=4/3, discriminant sqrt(2 - 72/36)= sqrt(2-2)=0 =\u003e a=b=S/2=2/3. So a=b=2/3 positive.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189519662,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18952088,"data":"       | For larger L, discriminant positive, a and b may be different but still positive because S is positive and sqrt less than S? Let's check L large: L→∞, α→0, S→0, discriminant sqrt(2). Then a≈ (0+sqrt2)/2 ≈0.707, b≈ (0 - sqrt2)/2 ≈ -0.707 negative. So for large L, one of a,b becomes negative. But is that allowed? e2 and e3 vertical components could be negative (pointing downwards) while still sum 8? If one is negative, the other must be \u003e8 to make sum 8, which may exceed L for large L? Wait for L large, a,b are cosines; they cannot exceed 1 in magnitude. To have sum 0 (since S→0), they must be opposite signs of equal magnitude. That would give u + w = L(a+b) = 0, contradict sum 8. So large L not allowed because sum is 8/L which tends to 0, but we need sum = 8/L. Wait we defined a+b = 8/L = S. As L→∞, S→0, so a+b→0, consistent. But we need u + w = L(a+b) = 8 (fixed). Indeed u + w = L S = 8, independent of L. So as L grows, a+b decreases, but multiplied by L gives 8 constant. So a and b can be of opposite signs for large L, but still sum small; then each magnitude may be up to 1, but product may be negative. That may be possible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18952444,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189525574,"data":"       | But the heights of vertices E (u) = L a, and F (w) = L b. Since u + w = 8, if a positive, b may be negative but then w could be less than 0 (height negative), which would place vertex below ground (plane P). However problem says vertex A is set on plane P, but other vertices could be below? Not specified, but likely the cube is positioned such that only A touches plane, others above. Since B=2, C=8, D=10 all above. If w negative enough to make F below plane, that would be odd; but maybe allowed? However typical container would have all vertices above plane? Not necessarily.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189527766,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18952915,"data":"       | Given typical geometry of cube with one vertex on ground and plane of rectangle ABDC perpendicular to ground, the other vertices likely all above ground (since the cube is leaning). So likely all heights are nonnegative. So we need u = L a ≥ 0 and w = L b ≥ 0. This imposes a,b ≥ 0. So we need both a,b nonnegative. That restricts L to range where solution yields nonnegative a,b.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189531038,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189532181,"data":"       | Thus we need a,b ≥ 0. Since a+b = 8/L \u003e0, both nonnegative implies each ≤ 8/L. Also from earlier we have a^2 + b^2 = 1 - 4/L^2. For nonnegative a,b, we also need discriminant sqrt(2 - 72/L^2) ≤ S (since a,b = (S ± sqrt)/2, both nonnegative requires S ≥ sqrt). Because the smaller one is (S - sqrt)/2 ≥ 0 =\u003e S ≥ sqrt. So condition: S ≥ sqrt(discriminant). That is (8/L) ≥ sqrt(2 - 72/L^2). Square both sides: 64/L^2 ≥ 2 - 72/L^2 =\u003e 64/L^2 + 72/L^2 ≥ 2 =\u003e 136/L^2 ≥ 2 =\u003e L^2 ≤ 68 =\u003e L ≤ sqrt(68) ≈ 8.246. Combined with L ≥ 6. So L in [6, sqrt(68)].\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189537257,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189538429,"data":"       | Thus L is bounded between 6 and sqrt(68). In this range both a,b nonnegative. So cube orientation such that all vertices are above ground.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189539841,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189540982,"data":"       | Thus L is not uniquely determined, still range.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189542206,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189543325,"data":"       | Now water level at 7: we need volume below height 7. Since all vertices heights are nonnegative, water plane cuts through cube possibly intersecting some vertices above water and some below.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189544813,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189545918,"data":"       | Heights:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189547067,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189548194,"data":"       | A 0 (below)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189549344,"data":"       | B 2 (below)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189550532,"data":"       | E = u = L a\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189551673,"data":"       | F = w = L b\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189552805,"data":"       | G = 2 + u = 2 + L a\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189553987,"data":"       | H = 2 + w = 2 + L b\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189555144,"data":"       | C = 8 (above)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189556315,"data":"       | D = 10 (above)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18955746,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189558578,"data":"       | Since L a + L b = 8, we have E+F = 8. So one of E, F may be below 7, maybe both, depending.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189559909,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189561023,"data":"       | Similarly G = 2 + u = 2 + L a, H = 2 + L b. Since u+ w = 8, G+H = 2+u + 2+w = 4 + 8 =12.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189562335,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189563456,"data":"       | Now water height 7: vertices below water are those with height ≤7. Let's analyze depending on L a and L b.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18956486,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189565976,"data":"       | Because u = L a, w = L b, with a,b nonnegative and sum 8/L.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189567206,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189569033,"data":"       | Let’s compute thresholds:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189571173,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189573464,"data":"       | - E (u) ≤ 7? Since u ≤ 8 (max when b=0). So could be ≤7 or \u003e7 depending on L a.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189575631,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189577372,"data":"       | - F (w) ≤ 7 similarly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189579343,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189581273,"data":"       | - G = 2 + u ≤ 7 =\u003e u ≤ 5. So if u ≤5 then G below water, else above.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189583523,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189585321,"data":"       | - H = 2 + w ≤ 7 =\u003e w ≤5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189589139,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189590964,"data":"       | Now we know u + w = 8. So at most one of u,w can be ≤5? Actually if u ≤5 then w = 8 - u ≥3. So w could also be ≤5 if u≥3. So both could be ≤5 if u between 3 and 5 (inclusive). Then both G and H below water.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189593105,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189594244,"data":"       | If u ≤3, then w ≥5, maybe w \u003e5? w could be 5 to 8. So G below, H maybe above if w\u003e5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189595666,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189596771,"data":"       | Similarly if u \u003e5, then G above.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189598018,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189599113,"data":"       | Thus water volume depends on u (or a) value.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189600368,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189601482,"data":"       | But perhaps water level 7 is such that exactly half of cube volume is below? Let's test with L=6 (minimum). Then compute a=b=2/3. Then u = L a = 6*(2/3)=4. So w=4 as well. So heights: E=4, F=4, G=6, H=6, C=8, D=10. Water level 7: vertices below: A0, B2, E4, F4, G6, H6 (all ≤7). C8, D10 above. So water plane cuts off top region containing C and D only. The region above water is a tetrahedron with vertices C, D, and intersection points on edges from C to other lower vertices? Actually plane z=7 intersects edges from C (height 8) to each of the lower vertices (A,B,E,F,G,H) maybe.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189603691,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189604806,"data":"       | But note edges from C to each of the other vertices: C is connected to A via diagonal e2+e3 (face diagonal) not an edge; but in cube edges are only along e1, e2, e3 directions. C is adjacent to E (A+e2) via e3? Wait C = A+e2+e3. So edges from C are to A+e2 (E) via e3, to A+e3 (F) via e2, to B+e2 (G) via e3, to B+e3 (H) via e2. So C is connected to E, F, G, H via edges. Similarly D = C + e1 is connected to G, H, etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18960672,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189607828,"data":"       | Thus the region above water includes vertices C and D, plus portions of edges from C to lower vertices where height crosses 7, and from D to lower vertices. The shape is a polyhedron possibly a truncated tetrahedron.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189609375,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.1896109,"data":"       | We can compute volume of water region as total cube volume minus volume of part above water (height \u003e7). The part above water is a polyhedron with vertices at C (8), D (10), and intersection points on edges where z=7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189612507,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189613618,"data":"       | Given linear interpolation along edges, we can compute the volume above water as integral over region where z\u003e7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189614998,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189616111,"data":"       | Alternatively compute water volume directly by integrating over s-space: region α s1 + β s2 + γ s3 ≤ t (where t = 7/L). Since α,β,γ are positive for L in [6, sqrt68] (both a,b positive). So we have all coefficients positive.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189617833,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189618947,"data":"       | Thus region is a simplex (tetrahedron) defined by plane intersecting the unit cube. Since all coefficients positive, the region where dot ≤ t is a convex polyhedron that includes origin (0,0,0). The complement (dot ≥ t) is another polyhedron near the opposite corner (1,1,1). The volume of region where dot ≤ t can be expressed piecewise depending on t relative to 0,1, etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189622419,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189623713,"data":"       | Specifically, for a unit cube with plane α x + β y + γ z = t, with α,β,γ positive and α+β+γ = 1 (since they form a unit vector). Then the volume of region where α x + β y + γ z ≤ t is:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189625489,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189626718,"data":"       | - If t ≤ 0: volume 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18962792,"data":"       | - If 0 ≤ t ≤ min(α,β,γ): region is a tetrahedron with vertices at origin and intercepts on axes: (t/α,0,0), (0,t/β,0), (0,0,t/γ). Volume = (1/6)*(t/α)*(t/β)*(t/γ) = t^3/(6 α β γ).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189629684,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189630817,"data":"       | - If t larger, region shape becomes more complex (truncated tetrahedron). Generally volume is piecewise polynomial.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189632293,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189633582,"data":"       | But in our case t = 7/L. Since L between 6 and sqrt68 (~8.246), t ranges from 7/6 ≈1.1667 down to 7/8.246≈0.848. So t is between ~0.85 and 1.1667. Since α+β+γ =1, and each coefficient ≤1. For L=6, α=1/3, β=γ=2/3? Wait α=2/L=1/3, β=γ=2/3. Indeed α+β+γ = 1/3+2/3+2/3=5/3 \u003e1. Wait not unit? Let's compute: For L=6, a=b=2/3. Then α=2/6=1/3, β=2/3, γ=2/3. Sum = 1/3+2/3+2/3 = 5/3 \u003e1. That's not correct because earlier we derived α^2+β^2+γ^2=1, not sum. Indeed (α,β,γ) is unit vector, not necessarily sum=1. So coefficients not sum to 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189636258,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189637442,"data":"       | Thus we cannot use that formula.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189638733,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189639856,"data":"       | But we can still compute volume V_s = measure of {x∈[0,1]^3: α x + β y + γ z ≤ t}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189641264,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189642406,"data":"       | Since α,β,γ positive, the region is a simplex truncated by the cube boundaries. For t between 0 and α+β+γ? Actually maximum of α x + β y + γ z over the cube is α+β+γ (when x=y=z=1). Since α+β+γ = sqrt(α^2+β^2+γ^2) * sqrt(3)??? Not simple. For L=6, α+β+γ = 1/3+2/3+2/3 = 5/3 \u003e1. The maximum dot product at (1,1,1) is 5/3. t = 7/6 ≈ 1.1667 which is less than 5/3 ≈1.6667, so plane cuts interior.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18964478,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189645901,"data":"       | Thus region is defined by linear inequality with positive coefficients. The volume can be computed via integration: V_s = ∫_{x=0}^{1} ∫_{y=0}^{1} ∫_{z=0}^{1} 1_{α x + β y + γ z ≤ t} dz dy dx.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189647577,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18964872,"data":"       | Since coefficients positive, for given x,y, the condition on z is z ≤ (t - α x - β y)/γ. So integrate over region where RHS positive.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18965019,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189651307,"data":"       | Thus V_s = ∫_{x=0}^{1} ∫_{y=0}^{1} max(0, (t - α x - β y)/γ) dy dx, with the constraint that t - α x - β y ≤ γ (since z max 1). Actually if RHS \u003e1, then whole z interval [0,1] included, but we cap at 1. So we need to handle min(1, (t - α x - β y)/γ). However since t ≤ α+β+γ maybe less than 1? Not sure.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189654944,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189656115,"data":"       | But we can compute volume piecewise.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189657315,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189658681,"data":"       | Alternative: Since region is convex and plane cuts the cube, the volume of region below plane equals total cube volume minus volume of tetrahedron formed by the part of cube where α x + β y + γ z ≥ t. This complement region is a tetrahedron with vertices at intersection points of plane with three of the cube's faces near (1,1,1). However because coefficients are positive, the region where dot ≥ t is a tetrahedron (or possibly a truncated tetrahedron) with vertices at points where each coordinate is 1 or the plane intersect. Actually the set {x∈[0,1]^3 | α x + β y + γ z ≥ t} is a convex polyhedron that includes (1,1,1). Since plane is linear, the intersection of plane with cube yields a polygon (triangle or quadrilateral). For positive coefficients, the region above plane is a tetrahedron with vertices at (1,1,1) and the three points where the plane meets the coordinate planes x=0, y=0, z=0? Wait those points may not be within cube if t is large. Let's think.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189661769,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189662979,"data":"       | Plane equation α x + β y + γ z = t. At x=0,y=0 =\u003e z = t/γ. If t/γ ≤ 1, then intersection point on z-axis inside cube. Similarly for other axes. Since t maybe \u003eγ? Let's compute for L=6: α=1/3, β=γ=2/3. Then γ=2/3, t=7/6≈1.1667. t/γ = (7/6)/(2/3) = (7/6)*(3/2)= (7*3)/(12)=21/12=7/4=1.75 \u003e1. So intersection with z-axis lies outside cube. So plane does not intersect the z-axis within cube. Similarly for x-axis: t/α = (7/6)/(1/3)= (7/6)*3=7/2=3.5 \u003e1. So not inside. So plane does not intersect any axis within cube; instead it intersects three faces x=1, y=1, z=1 maybe. Let's check intersection with x=1 plane: α*1 + β y + γ z = t =\u003e β y + γ z = t - α. Since t - α = 7/6 - 1/3 = 7/6 - 2/6 =5/6 ≈0.8333. This is less than β+γ = 4/3, so there will be a line segment inside the face x=1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189666263,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189667413,"data":"       | Thus region above plane is a tetrahedron with vertices at (1,1,1) and three points where the plane intersects the three edges from (1,1,1) to the other vertices? Actually edges from (1,1,1) to (0,1,1), (1,0,1), (1,1,0). Because those edges have two coordinates 1 and one coordinate variable. For each edge, we can find point where plane equals t.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189669193,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189670322,"data":"       | Let's param: Edge from (1,1,1) to (0,1,1): varying x from 1 to 0, y=1, z=1. Plug in: α x + β*1 + γ*1 = α x + (β+γ). At x=1, value = α+β+γ \u003e t. At x=0, value = β+γ. If β+γ ≥ t, then the plane intersects this edge between. Since β+γ = (β+γ). For L=6, β+γ=4/3≈1.333 \u003e t=1.1667, so yes intersection occurs.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189672462,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189673741,"data":"       | Similarly for edges with y varying: α+γ vs t; α+γ = 1/3+2/3=1 \u003e t? Actually α+γ = 1/3+2/3=1 \u003c t (1.1667). So at y=0, value = α+γ =1 \u003c t, meaning the plane is above that edge? Wait we need to check.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189675515,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189676689,"data":"       | Edge (1,0,1): x=1, y variable, z=1 =\u003e α*1 + β*y + γ*1 = α+γ + β*y. At y=0 value = α+γ =1 \u003c t. At y=1 value = α+β+γ = 5/3 \u003e t. So plane intersects this edge.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189679984,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189681177,"data":"       | Edge (1,1,0): x=1,y=1,z variable =\u003e α+β + γ*z. At z=0 value = α+β = 1/3+2/3=1 \u003c t. At z=1 value = α+β+γ =5/3 \u003e t. So intersect.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189682829,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18968397,"data":"       | Thus three intersect points on those three edges, forming a triangle. Together with (1,1,1) they form a tetrahedron (a corner cut off). So the volume above water is volume of tetrahedron with vertex at (1,1,1) and base triangle formed by those three intersection points.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189685597,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189686741,"data":"       | Thus the volume below water is total cube volume (1) minus tetrahedron volume.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189688029,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189689214,"data":"       | Thus water volume in physical space = L^3 * (1 - V_tet), where V_tet is volume of tetrahedron in unit cube coordinates.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189690581,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189691726,"data":"       | Now V_tet can be computed using distances along edges: For each intersect point, we can compute its coordinate along the edge from (1,1,1) to (0,1,1) etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189693166,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189694268,"data":"       | Let's param.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189695455,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189696594,"data":"       | Edge e_x: from (1,1,1) to (0,1,1). Let parameter λ where point = (1-λ,1,1). At this point, dot = α (1-λ) + β*1 + γ*1 = α + β + γ - α λ = (α+β+γ) - α λ. Set equal to t =\u003e λ = ((α+β+γ) - t)/α.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189698505,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189699671,"data":"       | Similarly edge e_y: from (1,1,1) to (1,0,1): point (1,1-μ,1). Dot = α*1 + β (1-μ) + γ*1 = α+β+γ - β μ = t =\u003e μ = ((α+β+γ) - t)/β.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189701337,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18970246,"data":"       | Edge e_z: from (1,1,1) to (1,1,0): point (1,1,1-ν). Dot = α+β+γ - γ ν = t =\u003e ν = ((α+β+γ) - t)/γ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189703937,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189705375,"data":"       | Thus the three intersection points are at distances λ, μ, ν from the vertex (1,1,1) along each edge. The tetrahedron formed by (1,1,1) and these three points has volume = (1/6) * λ * μ * ν * (since edges are orthogonal? Actually the three edges from (1,1,1) to the other vertices are orthogonal (they are along coordinate axes directions). The tetrahedron with right-angle corner at (1,1,1) where three edges are orthogonal, its volume = (1/6) * (length along x) * (length along y) * (length along z). Indeed because it's a right tetrahedron.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18970767,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189708804,"data":"       | Thus V_tet = (λ * μ * ν) / 6.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189710099,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189711248,"data":"       | Now λ = (S_sum - t)/α, where S_sum = α+β+γ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189712559,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189713678,"data":"       | Similarly μ = (S_sum - t)/β, ν = (S_sum - t)/γ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189716467,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18971762,"data":"       | Thus product λ μ ν = (S_sum - t)^3 / (α β γ).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189718964,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189720191,"data":"       | Thus V_tet = (S_sum - t)^3 / (6 α β γ).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189721438,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189722602,"data":"       | Thus V_s = 1 - (S_sum - t)^3 / (6 α β γ).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189723837,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189724967,"data":"       | Now we need to compute α,β,γ, t = 7/L, S_sum = α+β+γ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189726309,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18972743,"data":"       | Recall α = 2/L, β = a, γ = b.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189728702,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189729819,"data":"       | We have a+b = 8/L = S.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189730966,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189732109,"data":"       | Thus S_sum = α + a + b = 2/L + 8/L = 10/L.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189733367,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189734474,"data":"       | Thus S_sum = 10/L.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189735667,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189736768,"data":"       | Interesting! So α+β+γ = 10/L.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189738003,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189739133,"data":"       | Check for L=6: 10/6 = 5/3 = 1.666..., matches earlier sum 5/3. Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189740396,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18974154,"data":"       | Now t = 7/L.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189742704,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189743835,"data":"       | Thus S_sum - t = (10/L) - (7/L) = 3/L.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189745058,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189746181,"data":"       | Thus S_sum - t = 3/L.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189747332,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189748445,"data":"       | Thus λ = (3/L) / α = (3/L) / (2/L) = 3/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189749712,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189750846,"data":"       | Similarly μ = (3/L) / a = (3/L) / a = 3/(L a) = 3 / u (since u = L a). Similarly ν = 3 / w.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189752242,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189753382,"data":"       | Thus product λ μ ν = (3/2) * (3/u) * (3/w) = (27) / (2 u w). Since u w = (L a)(L b) = L^2 a b = L^2 P, where P = a b.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189754783,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189755887,"data":"       | But we can compute directly using known relationships.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189757129,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189758251,"data":"       | Recall u + w = 8, and we have u w = ?\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189759468,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189760591,"data":"       | We can compute u w from earlier equations.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189761795,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189762898,"data":"       | We have a b = P = (68/L^2 - 1)/2 from earlier.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189765728,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189766885,"data":"       | Thus u w = L^2 * P = L^2 * ((68/L^2 - 1)/2) = (68 - L^2)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189768153,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18976927,"data":"       | Thus u w = (68 - L^2)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189770444,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189771621,"data":"       | Check for L=6: u w = (68 - 36)/2 = 32/2 = 16. Indeed u=w=4 =\u003e product 16 matches.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189772955,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189774102,"data":"       | Thus product λ μ ν = 27 / (2 * u w) = 27 / (2 * ((68 - L^2)/2)) = 27 / (68 - L^2).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189775671,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189776773,"data":"       | Because 2 * ( (68 - L^2)/2 ) = 68 - L^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189777978,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189779146,"data":"       | Thus V_tet = (λ μ ν)/6 = (27) / (6 (68 - L^2)) = (27) / (6(68 - L^2)) = simplify: divide numerator and denominator by 3: 9 / (2(68 - L^2)) = 9 / (136 - 2 L^2).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189780632,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189781798,"data":"       | Thus V_tet = 9 / (136 - 2 L^2).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189782971,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189784088,"data":"       | Now V_s = 1 - V_tet.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189785284,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189786462,"data":"       | Thus V_s = 1 - 9 / (136 - 2 L^2) = (136 - 2 L^2 - 9) / (136 - 2 L^2) = (127 - 2 L^2) / (136 - 2 L^2).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189787827,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189788951,"data":"       | Thus water volume V = L^3 * V_s = L^3 * (127 - 2 L^2) / (136 - 2 L^2).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189790213,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189791361,"data":"       | Simplify: V = L^3 * (127 - 2 L^2) / (136 - 2 L^2).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189792598,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189793738,"data":"       | Now L is unknown but must satisfy constraints: L between 6 and sqrt(68) ≈ 8.246, and also a,b nonnegative. However the expression for V may be independent of L? Let's test with L=6: compute V.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189795259,"data":"       | \n"}
