,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189884421,"data":"       | Yes! That's 9 / (136 - 2 L^2). Good matches earlier. So denominator zero when L^2 =68, giving infinite V_tet, which can't be. Wait but if L^2=68, then a b = (68/L^2 -1)/2 = (1 -1)/2 = 0. So a b =0, meaning either a=0 or b=0, i.e., one of the vertical components zero (edge parallel to horizontal). Then α β γ product becomes zero (since β or γ zero), causing V_tet formula (which involves division by α β γ) to blow up, meaning the plane intersects the cube in a different way (the tetrahedron becomes degenerate, maybe region above water becomes a prism?). At that limit, the water plane passes through a vertex? Let's examine: If a=0, then u = L a =0, so vertex E (u) height 0 (on ground). Then water plane at 7 would be above many vertices. But anyway.\n"}
