,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189895871,"data":"       | We used only those constraints but maybe there is additional constraint: The rectangle ABDC plane is perpendicular to plane P (ground). That imposes that the normal vector of rectangle ABDC is horizontal (i.e., has zero z component). The rectangle ABDC plane is defined by points A,B,D,C. Its normal is cross product of vectors AB and AD? Wait rectangle ABDC includes vertices A,B,D,C (in that order). Sides AB and CD are parallel edges, AD and BC are also edges? Actually rectangle ABDC is a rectangle with vertices A,B,D,C (maybe order A-B-D-C). Since AB is edge, BD is diagonal of a face, DC is edge parallel to AB, and AC is diagonal of a face. So rectangle ABDC has sides AB (edge) and AD? Wait AD is not given as edge; but rectangle ABDC's sides are AB, BD, DC, CA? That would be a parallelogram? Let's compute: Quadrilateral A-B-D-C: edges AB, BD, DC, CA. AB is edge, BD is diagonal of a face, DC is edge parallel to AB, CA is diagonal. This quadrilateral is a rectangle? The problem states rectangle ABDC (maybe order A-B-D-C). Since AB and DC are parallel edges, and AC and BD are diagonals of faces, indeed A-B-D-C forms a rectangle (since opposite sides are parallel and equal). So rectangle ABDC's plane is determined by vectors AB and AD? Wait AD is not a side of rectangle; the sides are AB and BD? Let's examine geometry: In rectangle ABDC, vertices in order A→B→D→C→A. So sides: AB (edge), BD (face diagonal), DC (edge parallel to AB), CA (face diagonal). Since AB and DC are parallel edges, and BD and CA are also parallel (since opposite sides of rectangle). Indeed BD and AC are diagonals of faces and they are parallel? Are they parallel? In a rectangle, opposite sides are parallel. So BD must be parallel to AC. Indeed BD and AC are both face diagonals; they could be parallel.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189900295,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189901976,"data":"       | Thus rectangle ABDC is formed by two vectors: AB and AC (or AB and BD). Actually AB and AC are not adjacent sides; they are diagonal and edge. But rectangle's sides are AB and BD (or AB and DC). Let's compute: Vector AB = e1. Vector BD = e2 + e3 (since BD = AC). Vector DC = e1 (same as AB). Vector CA = -(e2+e3). So rectangle ABDC is a parallelogram with adjacent sides AB and BD (or AB and AC?). But AB and BD are not orthogonal; rectangle requires adjacent sides perpendicular. So AB must be perpendicular to BD. Indeed rectangle means AB ⟂ BD. Let's verify: In a rectangle, adjacent sides are perpendicular. So AB must be perpendicular to BD. Since AB is edge e1, BD is diagonal e2+e3. Are e1 and (e2+e3) orthogonal? Since e1 is orthogonal to both e2 and e3 individually, it's also orthogonal to any linear combination, so yes e1·(e2+e3)=0. So AB ⟂ BD. Good. So rectangle ABDC has sides AB and BD (perpendicular). So plane of rectangle is spanned by e1 and (e2+e3). Its normal vector is cross product e1 × (e2+e3). Since e1 is orthogonal to e2 and e3, cross product will be along direction of e2×e3 (or something). Let's compute: e1 × (e2+e3) = e1 × e2 + e1 × e3. Since e1 orthogonal to e2 and e3, cross product magnitude = L^2 (since each pair orthogonal). The resulting vector is orthogonal to both e1 and (e2+e3). Notably, e2×e3 is along direction of e1 (since orthogonal basis). Actually e2×e3 is parallel to e1 (or -e1). Indeed in right-handed orthonormal basis, e2 × e3 = ± e1. Since e1, e2, e3 are orthogonal, cross product of e2 and e3 is ± (L^2) * unit vector along e1 direction. So e1 × (e2+e3) = e1 × e2 + e1 × e3 = (L^2) * (some unit vectors) maybe? Let's compute using orthonormal basis: Let unit vectors u1, u2, u3 orthonormal with u1 = e1/L, u2 = e2/L, u3 = e3/L. Then e1 × e2 = L^2 (u1 × u2) = L^2 u3 (or -u3 depending orientation). Similarly e1 × e3 = -L^2 u2 (depending orientation). So sum is L^2 (u3 - u2). This vector is perpendicular to u1? Let's dot with u1: u1·(u3 - u2) = 0. So normal lies in plane spanned by u2 and u3, i.e., horizontal? Wait we need its z-component (global vertical) to be zero because rectangle plane is perpendicular to ground plane P (horizontal). So the normal vector must be horizontal (i.e., have zero z-component). Since u2 and u3 may have non-zero z components (a,b). The combination u3 - u2 may have zero vertical component if a = b? Let's compute vertical component of u3 - u2: (b - a). So for normal to have zero vertical component, we need b - a = 0 =\u003e a = b. Thus condition for rectangle plane to be perpendicular to horizontal plane is that a = b, i.e., the vertical components of e2 and e3 are equal.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18991586,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189917033,"data":"       | Thus we have additional constraint a = b (since the normal must be horizontal). Good! That will uniquely determine L.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189918406,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189919541,"data":"       | Thus a = b = (S)/2 = (8/L)/2 = 4/L.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18992075,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189921905,"data":"       | Thus a = b = 4/L.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189923098,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189924253,"data":"       | Check that this satisfies earlier equations: a^2 + b^2 = 2 * (4/L)^2 = 32 / L^2. This should equal 1 - 4/L^2 (from earlier). So:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189925676,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189926802,"data":"       | 32 / L^2 = 1 - 4/L^2 =\u003e multiply L^2: 32 = L^2 - 4 =\u003e L^2 = 36 =\u003e L = 6.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189928242,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189929371,"data":"       | Thus indeed L is uniquely determined as 6. Good! So the condition that rectangle plane is perpendicular to P forces L=6.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189930747,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189931904,"data":"       | Thus the cube side length is 6 meters. Then we can compute water volume.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189933206,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189934302,"data":"       | We already computed water volume for L=6 earlier: V = 185.625 cubic meters.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18993557,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189936669,"data":"       | But we need rational fraction m/n. Let's compute exact fraction.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189940969,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189943731,"data":"       | For L=6, compute V exactly using formula V = L^3 * (127 - 2 L^2) / (136 - 2 L^2). Plug L=6.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189945062,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189946182,"data":"       | Compute numerator: L^3 = 216. L^2 = 36.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189947409,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189948533,"data":"       | 127 - 2*36 = 127 - 72 = 55.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18994974,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189950853,"data":"       | Denominator: 136 - 2*36 = 136 - 72 = 64.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189952073,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189953217,"data":"       | Thus V = 216 * 55 / 64 = (216/64) * 55 = (27/8) * 55 (since 216/8=27, 64/8=8). Actually 216/64 simplifies: divide by 8 =\u003e 27/8. So V = (27/8) * 55 = (27*55)/8 = (1485)/8? Let's compute: 27*55 = 1485. So V = 1485/8 = 185.625. Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189954822,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189955939,"data":"       | Thus m=1485, n=8, relatively prime? gcd(1485,8)=1 (since 8 is power of 2, 1485 odd). So m+n = 1485+8 = 1493.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189957294,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189958412,"data":"       | Thus answer should be 1493.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189959624,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18996104,"data":"       | Let's double-check reasoning.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189962235,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18996337,"data":"       | We derived that rectangle ABDC plane perpendicular to ground implies a=b. Let's verify more rigorously.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189964734,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189965902,"data":"       | Plane ABDC contains points A, B, D, C. Normal vector n = (B-A) × (C-A) (or cross of two non-parallel edges). Choose vectors AB = e1, AC = e2+e3. Since AB ⟂ AC (as e1 orthogonal to e2 and e3 individually, thus orthogonal to sum). So rectangle ABDC is spanned by e1 and e2+e3, as we said. Its normal n = e1 × (e2+e3) = e1 × e2 + e1 × e3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189967779,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189968901,"data":"       | Now e1 × e2 is parallel to e3 (since orthogonal right-handed). e1 × e3 is parallel to -e2. So n is combination of e3 and e2. In terms of unit vectors: n ∝ L^2 (u3 - u2) (or plus depending orientation). So n's vertical component = L^2 (b - a). For n to be horizontal (perpendicular to vertical axis), we need b - a = 0. So a=b.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189970729,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18997184,"data":"       | Thus condition holds.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189973015,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189974254,"data":"       | Now with a=b, we derived L=6.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189975446,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189976564,"data":"       | Now compute water volume: We can also compute directly using geometry: With L=6, we have cube side 6. Coordinates: Let's set basis.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189977964,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189979207,"data":"       | Let e1 = (x,y,2) with length 6. Since a=b=4/L = 4/6 = 2/3. So u = L a = 6*(2/3)=4, w = also 4. So vertical components of e2 and e3 are both 4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189980605,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189983116,"data":"       | Thus e2 and e3 have length 6 and vertical component 4, so their horizontal component magnitude sqrt(6^2 - 4^2) = sqrt(36-16)= sqrt20 = 2*sqrt5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189984549,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189985831,"data":"       | Thus we can set e2 = (2√5,0,4) and e3 = (0,2√5,4) perhaps after rotating coordinate axes to align. Since they must be orthogonal and have same vertical component 4. Indeed dot product: (2√5,0,4)·(0,2√5,4)=0+0+16=16, not zero! Wait dot product includes vertical components: 4*4=16, not zero. So they are not orthogonal. Need orthogonal vectors with same vertical component and length 6.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189987862,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189989258,"data":"       | We need two orthogonal vectors with same vertical component 4. Let's construct orthogonal vectors: Let e2 = (a1, a2, 4), e3 = (b1, b2, 4). Orthogonality: a1 b1 + a2 b2 + 16 = 0 =\u003e dot of horizontal components = -16.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189990863,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189992131,"data":"       | Also each length squared: a1^2 + a2^2 + 16 = 36 =\u003e a1^2 + a2^2 = 20. Similarly b1^2 + b2^2 = 20.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189993534,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189994759,"data":"       | So we need two vectors in horizontal plane (R^2) of length sqrt(20) = 2√5 whose dot product is -16. Let horizontal components be vectors u and v in R^2 with lengths sqrt(20) and dot product -16. Since dot = |u||v| cosθ = 20 cosθ = -16 =\u003e cosθ = -16/20 = -4/5. So angle between u and v is arccos(-4/5) \u003e 90°, which is okay.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.18999666,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.189997796,"data":"       | Thus we can pick u = (2√5,0). Then v must have length sqrt(20) and dot = -16: (2√5,0)·(v1, v2) = 2√5 * v1 = -16 =\u003e v1 = -16/(2√5) = -8/√5 = -(8√5)/5 ≈ -3.577. Then v2 determined by length: v1^2 + v2^2 = 20 =\u003e v2^2 = 20 - v1^2. Compute: v1^2 = (64*5)/25 = 320/25 = 12.8. So v2^2 = 20 - 12.8 = 7.2 = 36/5. So v2 = ± sqrt(36/5) = ± (6/√5) = ± (6√5)/5 ≈ 2.683. Choose positive.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190000122,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190001329,"data":"       | Thus e2 = (2√5, 0, 4). e3 = ( -8/√5, 6/√5, 4 ). These are orthogonal and length 6.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190002724,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190003848,"data":"       | Thus coordinates of vertices:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190005069,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190006311,"data":"       | A = (0,0,0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190007478,"data":"       | B = e1 = (p,q,2). Need to find p,q such that e1 orthogonal to e2 and e3. Since e2 and e3 have known components, we can find e1 as cross product normalized.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190008955,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190010064,"data":"       | But we don't need explicit coordinates for volume.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190011426,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190012545,"data":"       | Now water plane at z=7 cuts off top tetrahedron with vertices C (height 8) and D (10) etc. Our earlier volume calculation gave 1485/8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190013927,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190015103,"data":"       | Let's double-check by performing Monte Carlo integration numeric to verify.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190016389,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190018829,"data":"       | assistantanalysis to=python codeimport random, math, itertools, numpy as np, sys, math, itertools, random, time, collections, fractions, decimal, itertools, math\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190020286,"data":"       | L=6\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190021446,"data":"       | a=b=4/L # vertical component per unit length\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190022715,"data":"       | # define orthogonal vectors as earlier\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19002391,"data":"       | e2 = np.array([2*math.sqrt(5), 0.0, 4.0])\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190025113,"data":"       | e3 = np.array([-8/math.sqrt(5), 6/math.sqrt(5), 4.0])\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190026671,"data":"       | # compute e1 as unit vector orthogonal to e2,e3 with length 6 and vertical component 2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190028026,"data":"       | # e1 direction is cross product of e2 and e3 (or opposite)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190029285,"data":"       | e1_dir = np.cross(e2, e3)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19003045,"data":"       | e1_dir_norm = np.linalg.norm(e1_dir)\n"}
