,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190309327,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190310429,"data":"       | Thus the heights of vertices are given: Height (z-coordinate) of A = 0, B = 2, C = 8, D = 10.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190311721,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190312835,"data":"       | Let’s denote the vertical direction (perpendicular to plane P) as unit vector k (0,0,1). Then the height of any point X is its dot product with k: h(X) = X · k.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190314346,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190315455,"data":"       | Thus we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190316596,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190317708,"data":"       | h(A) = 0 = 0·k = 0 (since A at origin). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19031901,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190320103,"data":"       | h(B) = u·k = 2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190321298,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.1903224,"data":"       | h(C) = (v + w)·k = 8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190323615,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190324751,"data":"       | h(D) = (u + v + w)·k = 10.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190325972,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190327072,"data":"       | Thus we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190328221,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19032934,"data":"       | u·k = 2. (1)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190330518,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190331619,"data":"       | (v + w)·k = 8. (2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190334266,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190335402,"data":"       | (u + v + w)·k = 10. (3)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190336632,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19033777,"data":"       | From (3) = u·k + (v+w)·k = 2 + 8 = 10, which matches. So equations consistent. So we have two independent equations: u·k = 2, (v+w)·k = 8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190339276,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190340413,"data":"       | We also know that u, v, w are orthogonal vectors of equal length s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190341687,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190343044,"data":"       | Thus we have unknowns: u, v, w (3 vectors in R^3) each length s, orthogonal. Also unknown s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190344358,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190345491,"data":"       | We also have that k is unit vector (0,0,1) (since we set coordinates). So we can treat k as known vertical direction.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190346829,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190358404,"data":"       | Thus we need to find s given dot products.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190360416,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190362328,"data":"       | Let’s denote components of u, v, w: u = (u_x, u_y, u_z), etc. Orthogonal: u·v = 0, u·w = 0, v·w = 0. Lengths: |u| = |v| = |w| = s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190364613,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190366496,"data":"       | Given u·k = u_z = 2. So u_z = 2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190368644,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190370427,"data":"       | Given (v + w)·k = v_z + w_z = 8. So v_z + w_z = 8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190373191,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190374439,"data":"       | Since v and w are orthogonal to u? Actually u·v = 0 =\u003e u_x v_x + u_y v_y + u_z v_z = 0. Similarly for w.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190375848,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190376964,"data":"       | Given u_z = 2, unknown u_x, u_y.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19037817,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190379271,"data":"       | Similarly unknown v_z, w_z.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19038048,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190381592,"data":"       | But we also know that |u|^2 = s^2 = u_x^2 + u_y^2 + u_z^2 = u_x^2 + u_y^2 + 4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190382895,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190383996,"data":"       | Thus s^2 = u_x^2 + u_y^2 + 4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190385177,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190386277,"data":"       | Similarly, for v: s^2 = v_x^2 + v_y^2 + v_z^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190387473,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190388598,"data":"       | For w: s^2 = w_x^2 + w_y^2 + w_z^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190389801,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190390888,"data":"       | Also orthogonality conditions:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190392071,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190393179,"data":"       | u·v = u_x v_x + u_y v_y + 2 v_z = 0. (since u_z = 2).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19039444,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190396936,"data":"       | u·w = u_x w_x + u_y w_y + 2 w_z = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190398195,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190399299,"data":"       | v·w = v_x w_x + v_y w_y + v_z w_z = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190400538,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190401661,"data":"       | Goal: Find s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190402793,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19040393,"data":"       | We have many unknowns; perhaps we can solve using linear algebra.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190405186,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190406376,"data":"       | Observation: Since v and w are orthogonal to each other and each have same length, they can be considered as forming an orthogonal basis for the plane orthogonal to u? Not necessarily; they are orthogonal to each other, but not necessarily orthogonal to u. Actually they must be orthogonal to u as well, because all three edge vectors are mutually orthogonal (cube edges). So indeed u·v = 0 and u·w = 0, meaning v and w lie in plane perpendicular to u (i.e., orthogonal complement). So v and w are both orthogonal to u. So they both lie in the plane perpendicular to u.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19040865,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190410097,"data":"       | Thus we have u orthogonal to both v and w. So the set {u, v, w} forms an orthogonal basis.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190411424,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19041256,"data":"       | Thus we can treat u as one axis, and v, w as orthogonal axes within plane orthogonal to u.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190413858,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190414958,"data":"       | Given that v and w are orthogonal to u, we have constraints above.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190416217,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190417329,"data":"       | Now, we also have sum of their vertical components: v_z + w_z = 8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190418588,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190419682,"data":"       | Given they are orthogonal to u, we can perhaps compute s using dot products with k.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190420972,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190422104,"data":"       | Let’s decompose k (vertical unit vector) into components parallel and perpendicular to u. Since u is not vertical (since u_z=2, but u also has horizontal components). Write k = α û + β n, where û = u / s (unit vector along u), and n is a unit vector perpendicular to u (i.e., in plane orthogonal to u). Since we have two orthogonal directions v and w within that plane, n can be expressed as combination of v̂ and ŵ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190424204,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190425338,"data":"       | But perhaps we can use known dot products: u·k = 2 =\u003e s * cosθ = 2, where θ is angle between u and vertical direction. So cosθ = 2 / s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190426871,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190428052,"data":"       | Similarly, for vector v + w, its dot with k is 8. Since v and w are orthogonal to u, they lie in plane perpendicular to u, but not necessarily both vertical.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190429506,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190430629,"data":"       | Let’s denote v̂ = v / s, ŵ = w / s. Then v̂ and ŵ are orthonormal and both orthogonal to û.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190434598,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190435738,"data":"       | Thus we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190436907,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190438015,"data":"       | k·û = 2 / s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190439225,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190440331,"data":"       | k·v̂ + k·ŵ = (v·k + w·k)/s = 8 / s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190441704,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190442841,"data":"       | Let’s denote a = k·v̂, b = k·ŵ. Then a + b = 8 / s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190444211,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190445349,"data":"       | We also have that v̂ and ŵ are orthonormal and orthogonal to û. So they form an orthonormal basis for the plane perpendicular to û.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190446805,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190447927,"data":"       | Thus k can be expressed in this orthonormal basis: k = (2/s) û + a v̂ + b ŵ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190449273,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19045038,"data":"       | Since k is a unit vector, we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190451567,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19045268,"data":"       | |k|^2 = 1 = (2/s)^2 + a^2 + b^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.1904539,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190454996,"data":"       | Thus a^2 + b^2 = 1 - (4 / s^2).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190456173,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190457304,"data":"       | But also a + b = 8 / s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190458533,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190459947,"data":"       | We need to find s such that there exist real numbers a,b satisfying these equations, with the additional condition that v̂ and ŵ are orthonormal (which is already used). No further constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190461509,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190462631,"data":"       | Thus we need to find s such that there exists a,b satisfying:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190463885,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190464994,"data":"       | a + b = 8 / s   (eq1)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190466185,"data":"       | a^2 + b^2 = 1 - 4 / s^2   (eq2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190467372,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19046848,"data":"       | We can solve for a,b.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190469636,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190470734,"data":"       | Recall that (a+b)^2 = a^2 + b^2 + 2ab.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190471949,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190473096,"data":"       | Thus (8 / s)^2 = (1 - 4 / s^2) + 2ab.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190474275,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190475372,"data":"       | Thus 64 / s^2 = 1 - 4 / s^2 + 2ab.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190476536,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190477676,"data":"       | Thus 2ab = 64 / s^2 - 1 + 4 / s^2 = (64 + 4) / s^2 - 1 = 68 / s^2 - 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190478932,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190480033,"data":"       | Thus ab = (68 / s^2 - 1)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190482439,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190483652,"data":"       | Now, we also have that a and b are dot products of unit vectors with k, so each must satisfy |a| ≤ 1, |b| ≤ 1, and also because v̂,ŵ are orthogonal to û, but not needed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190485235,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190486437,"data":"       | Moreover, a and b can be any real numbers satisfying these constraints; there is no further restriction besides that they correspond to some orthonormal basis orientation. However there may be additional condition: Since v̂ and ŵ are orthonormal, we can rotate them within the plane perpendicular to û; this rotation can adjust a and b while preserving a^2 + b^2. In fact, given k's components in the plane perpendicular to û, we can choose the orientation of v̂, ŵ such that a = component along v̂, b = component along ŵ. So as long as the vector (a,b) (i.e., the projection of k onto that plane) has magnitude sqrt(a^2 + b^2) = sqrt(1 - 4 / s^2) (which is the length of projection), we can choose orthonormal basis such that its components along v̂ and ŵ are any pair (a,b) that satisfy a^2 + b^2 = projection length squared. So there is no further constraint; any pair (a,b) with that magnitude works, and we can rotate basis to achieve any distribution between a and b. So the condition reduces to existence of a,b satisfying both eq1 and eq2. That imposes a constraint on s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190489693,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190491064,"data":"       | Thus we need s such that there exist real numbers a,b satisfying those two equations.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190492368,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190493529,"data":"       | We can solve for a,b explicitly using system; but we can derive condition for s by using that a and b satisfy (a+b)^2 ≤ 2(a^2 + b^2) (by Cauchy-Schwarz or inequality). Indeed (a+b)^2 ≤ 2(a^2 + b^2) with equality when a=b.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190495144,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190496335,"data":"       | Let's apply: (8/s)^2 ≤ 2 (1 - 4/s^2). This yields inequality that must hold for any real a,b. Because for given sum and sum of squares, there is condition: For real numbers a,b, we have (a+b)^2 ≤ 2(a^2 + b^2). Indeed by Cauchy: (1^2 + 1^2)(a^2 + b^2) ≥ (a+b)^2. So yes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190498079,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190499169,"data":"       | Thus we require:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190500337,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.1905015,"data":"       | 64 / s^2 ≤ 2 (1 - 4 / s^2).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190502735,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190503844,"data":"       | Simplify:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190505015,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190506171,"data":"       | 64 / s^2 ≤ 2 - 8 / s^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190507381,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190508482,"data":"       | Bring terms:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190509627,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190510723,"data":"       | 64 / s^2 + 8 / s^2 ≤ 2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190511964,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190513088,"data":"       | =\u003e 72 / s^2 ≤ 2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190514438,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190515565,"data":"       | =\u003e 72 ≤ 2 s^2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190518076,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.1905192,"data":"       | =\u003e s^2 ≥ 36\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190520408,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190521568,"data":"       | =\u003e s ≥ 6 (since side length positive). So side length at least 6.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190522899,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190524084,"data":"       | Also there is a lower bound maybe from other inequality: (a+b)^2 ≥ (a^2 + b^2) ? Not always; we have (a+b)^2 can be as low as 0 (if a = -b). So not constraint.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190526418,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190527559,"data":"       | Thus s must be at least 6.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190528736,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19052986,"data":"       | Also we have that a and b must be ≤1 in magnitude individually. That may impose an upper bound on s. Let's examine.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190531218,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190532358,"data":"       | Given a+b = 8/s. Since |a| ≤ 1, |b| ≤ 1, then |a+b| ≤ |a|+|b| ≤ 2. So we need 8/s ≤ 2 =\u003e s ≥ 4. Already satisfied by s≥6.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190533911,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190535015,"data":"       | Also we need each of a,b to be within [-1,1] given projection magnitude sqrt(1 - 4/s^2). This magnitude must be ≤ sqrt(2) (since each component ≤1). But anyway.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190536488,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19053767,"data":"       | Thus side length s can be any value ≥6 that also satisfies that there exist a,b real with given sum and sum of squares. For a given s≥6, we can solve for a,b. The discriminant condition ensures existence.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190539217,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190540533,"data":"       | From earlier equations, we can compute a,b as roots of quadratic t^2 - (8/s) t + ab = 0 where ab is given by (68/s^2 - 1)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190541897,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190543039,"data":"       | The discriminant Δ = (8/s)^2 - 4ab must be non-negative.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190544358,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190545453,"data":"       | Compute Δ:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190546649,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190547784,"data":"       | Δ = (64 / s^2) - 4 * ((68 / s^2 - 1)/2) = (64 / s^2) - 2 * (68 / s^2 - 1) = (64 / s^2) - (136 / s^2) + 2 = 2 - (72 / s^2).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190549192,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190550347,"data":"       | Thus Δ = 2 - 72 / s^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190551564,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190552689,"data":"       | For real a,b, need Δ ≥ 0 =\u003e 2 - 72 / s^2 ≥ 0 =\u003e s^2 ≥ 36 =\u003e s ≥ 6. Same condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190554205,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19055532,"data":"       | Thus any s≥6 yields real a,b.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190556555,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190557693,"data":"       | Thus side length s is not uniquely determined by heights? Wait maybe side length is uniquely determined because we also have that AB is an edge of cube (length s) and its height difference between A and B is 2. So u_z = 2, but u length s must satisfy s ≥ 2 (obviously). But many s can produce u_z=2 for some orientation (by adjusting horizontal components). So side length not uniquely determined by given heights alone; but water volume may be independent of s? Let's test.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190560884,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190562074,"data":"       | We need volume of water within cube up to height 7 (horizontal plane). This volume will depend on the orientation and side length s. However maybe the volume is independent of s given the heights? Let's analyze.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19056362,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190564799,"data":"       | We have cube oriented arbitrarily with side length s unknown. But we have heights of four vertices (0,2,8,10). That gives us some constraints that may uniquely determine s after all. Because the heights of all eight vertices are not given, but maybe the cube's orientation and side length are determined by these four heights plus orthogonal condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190566574,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190567725,"data":"       | But we only used heights of A,B,C,D (four vertices). Yet there are other vertices (E,F,G,H) unknown; their heights would be determined by orientation and side length. However we only need water volume; perhaps it depends only on heights of these four vertices and not on s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190569347,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190570684,"data":"       | But we need to compute volume of intersection of cube with half-space z≤7. This volume is the portion of cube below a horizontal plane at height 7. Since we know height of each vertex relative to ground, we can treat cube as a convex polyhedron (a parallelepiped). The water plane cuts the cube, possibly forming a polyhedron whose volume can be computed via known formula for volume of a sliced cube based on heights at vertices. There is a known theorem: For any convex polyhedron, volume of region below a plane can be expressed as average of heights of vertices times base area? Not exactly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190572957,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190574091,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can compute volume via integration: Represent cube as set of points x = a u + b v + c w where a,b,c in [0,1] (since we anchored at A). Then position vector is P = a u + b v + c w. Height (z-coordinate) is h = a u_z + b v_z + c w_z = a*2 + b v_z + c w_z.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190575708,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190576844,"data":"       | Given u_z=2, v_z + w_z = 8. But we don't know individual v_z, w_z.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190578137,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190579245,"data":"       | Let’s denote p = v_z, q = w_z. Then p + q = 8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190580456,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190581589,"data":"       | We also have orthogonality constraints: u·v = 0 =\u003e u_x p_x + u_y p_y + 2 p = 0 (where p_x, p_y are horizontal components). Similarly for w.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190583047,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190584172,"data":"       | But perhaps we can param in terms of heights only: Since we only need volume under plane z=7, we can integrate over region of (a,b,c) where height ≤ 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190585596,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190586706,"data":"       | Thus we need to know the distribution of heights within the cube: h(a,b,c) = 2a + p b + q c.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190589338,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190590485,"data":"       | But note that v and w are orthogonal and both orthogonal to u; but their horizontal components may affect shape but not the height function which depends only on vertical components.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190591958,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190593117,"data":"       | Thus the height function is linear in a,b,c with coefficients 2, p, q. So the region below height 7 is defined by inequality:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190594467,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190595591,"data":"       | 2a + p b + q c ≤ 7, with a,b,c ∈ [0,1].\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190596858,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190598189,"data":"       | Thus the volume of water (in terms of unit cube coordinates) is the volume of the set of (a,b,c) in unit cube where this inequality holds, multiplied by the actual volume of cube, which is s^3 (since mapping (a,b,c) → P is linear with determinant s^3 (since u,v,w orthogonal and length s each). Indeed the transformation from unit cube to actual cube is a linear map with determinant |u·(v×w)| = s^3 (since orthogonal basis). So volume of water = s^3 * V0, where V0 is fraction of unit cube satisfying inequality.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190600373,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190601502,"data":"       | Thus we need to compute V0 = volume of region in unit cube {0≤a,b,c≤1} where 2a + p b + q c ≤ 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190602877,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190604017,"data":"       | But we also know p + q = 8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190605227,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190606352,"data":"       | Since s unknown, p and q are vertical components of v and w, which are each ≤ s (since length s). Since v_z = p, w_z = q.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190607745,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190608858,"data":"       | We also have lengths: v_z^2 + (horizontal component)^2 = s^2 =\u003e horizontal component magnitude sqrt(s^2 - p^2). Similarly for w.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190610269,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190611431,"data":"       | But for inequality only p and q matter.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190612623,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190613762,"data":"       | Now, note that a,b,c range 0..1, so max of 2a + p b + q c occurs at a=1,b=1,c=1: value = 2 + p + q = 2 + 8 = 10, which matches D height 10. Minimum occurs at a=b=c=0: 0. So the height function ranges from 0 to 10 linearly across cube, consistent.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190615324,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190616444,"data":"       | Thus plane at height 7 corresponds to inequality 2a + p b + q c ≤ 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19061774,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190618867,"data":"       | We need to find volume of region in unit cube satisfying that inequality.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190620114,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190621227,"data":"       | This is a linear inequality dividing the unit cube; the region is a polyhedron (a truncated tetrahedron maybe). Its volume depends on coefficients 2, p, q.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190622642,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190624151,"data":"       | We have p + q = 8, p,q positive? Since v_z and w_z may be positive; but could be negative? However heights of C (v+w) is 8, which is positive. So sum positive. Since they are vertical components of edges from A, which is ground at 0, maybe both positive (since cube likely oriented upward). But they could be one positive larger than 8 and other negative? That would still give sum 8 but then some vertices would have heights beyond 0? Let's check: Vertex at v (i.e., A + v) would have height p; if p negative, that vertex would be below ground (negative height). Not impossible but maybe not allowed? Problem didn't specify about other vertices; but likely the cube is placed such that A is on ground, maybe other vertices are above ground? Not necessarily; could be below.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190628124,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190629308,"data":"       | But we have B height 2, C height 8, D height 10. So the heights of those four vertices are increasing; suggests orientation upward. It's plausible that p and q are both positive (since they contribute to upward tilt). Let's assume p,q positive.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190630884,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190632043,"data":"       | Thus we have p,q ∈ (0, s] maybe.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190633242,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190634361,"data":"       | Now we need to compute V0 = volume of region defined by linear inequality.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190635618,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190636764,"data":"       | General formula: For unit cube with variables a,b,c ∈ [0,1], inequality α a + β b + γ c ≤ h, where α,β,γ ≥0, h between 0 and α+β+γ (max). Volume can be computed via integration.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190638449,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190639575,"data":"       | We can treat a as variable: For each (b,c) we have condition a ≤ (h - β b - γ c)/α. Provided α \u003e0 (here α=2). So for given (b,c), the allowed a range is [0, min(1, (h - β b - γ c)/α )] if the RHS positive else none.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190641344,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190642496,"data":"       | Thus V0 = ∫_{b=0}^{1} ∫_{c=0}^{1} max(0, min(1, (h - β b - γ c)/α )) dc db.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190643881,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190644979,"data":"       | Given h=7, α=2, β=p, γ=q.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190646233,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190647351,"data":"       | Since h is relatively large (7) compared to coefficients, the RHS may exceed 1 for many (b,c). Let's compute threshold where RHS≥1: (h - p b - q c)/2 ≥ 1 =\u003e h - p b - q c ≥ 2 =\u003e p b + q c ≤ h - 2 = 5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190649062,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190650184,"data":"       | Thus for (b,c) satisfying p b + q c ≤ 5, a can be up to 1 (full). For points where 5 \u003c p b + q c ≤ h (i.e., ≤7) the a range is limited to (h - p b - q c)/2. For points where p b + q c \u003e h (7), no a allowed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190651884,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190653028,"data":"       | Thus region in b,c plane: define region R1 = {(b,c)∈[0,1]^2: p b + q c ≤ 5} where a full height 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190654367,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190655469,"data":"       | Region R2 = {(b,c)∈[0,1]^2: 5 \u003c p b + q c ≤ 7} where a limit = (7 - p b - q c)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190656813,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190660083,"data":"       | Region R3 = rest where \u003e7, no contribution.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190661395,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190662536,"data":"       | Thus V0 = area(R1)*1 + ∫_{R2} (7 - p b - q c)/2 db dc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190663851,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190665276,"data":"       | Thus we need to compute area of region defined by linear inequality in unit square.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190666562,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190667683,"data":"       | Given p,q positive with p+q=8. Let's denote p and q unknown.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19066892,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190670055,"data":"       | But we may suspect that volume V0 is independent of p,q given the sum constraint. Let's test.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19067135,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19067257,"data":"       | Compute area(R1) = area of set {p b + q c ≤ 5} intersect unit square.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190673873,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190674982,"data":"       | Similarly integrate over region where 5\u003c p b + q c ≤ 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190676281,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190677406,"data":"       | We can compute these using geometry of lines.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190678594,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190679756,"data":"       | Line p b + q c = constant is a line with intercepts: when b=0, c = constant/q; when c=0, b = constant/p.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190681049,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190682164,"data":"       | Thus region of inequality ≤ constant is a polygon (triangle) within square.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190683489,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.1906846,"data":"       | Given that p,q \u003e0, the line slopes negative.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190685877,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190687002,"data":"       | We can compute area for constant K: area of region where p b + q c ≤ K within unit square is:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190688339,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190689488,"data":"       | If K ≤ 0 =\u003e area 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190690796,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190691933,"data":"       | If K ≥ p+q (i.e., 8) =\u003e area 1 (full square). Since max of p b + q c over [0,1]^2 is p*1 + q*1 = p+q =8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190693319,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19069448,"data":"       | Our constants: K=5 and K=7, both less than 8, greater than 0. So region is a right triangle with vertices at (0,0), (K/p,0) if K/p ≤1, and (0, K/q) if K/q ≤1. But if K/p \u003e1, then line intersects top edge before reaching b=1; similarly for c.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190696113,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190697271,"data":"       | Thus need to consider whether K/p ≤1 and K/q ≤1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190698717,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190699833,"data":"       | Since p and q could be up to 8 (if other is 0). But typical values unknown.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190701086,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190703643,"data":"       | We need to see if K=5 is less than both p and q? Actually K/p ≤1 when p ≥ K. So if p ≥5, then K/p ≤1. Similarly for q.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190705103,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190706269,"data":"       | Given p+q=8, at least one of p,q is ≥4. But could be less than 5. Example p=3, q=5 =\u003e p\u003c5, q\u003e5. Then K/p = 5/3 \u003e1, so line would intersect b=1 edge before reaching c=0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190707949,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190709062,"data":"       | Thus region shape depends on relative sizes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190710293,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190711427,"data":"       | Thus V0 may depend on p and q individually, not just sum.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190712647,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190713987,"data":"       | But maybe the condition that v and w are orthogonal and length s imposes additional constraints linking p and q to s, which may make V0 independent of s (maybe constant). Let's explore.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190715443,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190716591,"data":"       | We have p^2 + (horizontal component)^2 = s^2, q^2 + (horizontal component)^2 = s^2. Since both have same magnitude, their horizontal components magnitude squared are s^2 - p^2 and s^2 - q^2 respectively.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190718077,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190719205,"data":"       | But orthogonality between v and w: v·w = 0 =\u003e (horizontal components vector) dot (horizontal components vector) + p q = 0. Let’s denote v_h, w_h are horizontal vectors (2D) in plane perpendicular to vertical direction k (i.e., the ground plane). Then v·w = v_h·w_h + p q = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190720973,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190722133,"data":"       | Also note that v_h and w_h are orthogonal to u (which has some horizontal component). But not needed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190723461,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190724563,"data":"       | Thus we have condition: v_h·w_h = - p q.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190725823,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190726956,"data":"       | But also lengths: |v_h|^2 = s^2 - p^2, |w_h|^2 = s^2 - q^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190728189,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190729298,"data":"       | Thus we have dot product of two vectors with given magnitudes and dot product -pq.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190730588,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190731779,"data":"       | Thus by Cauchy-Schwarz, |v_h·w_h| ≤ |v_h||w_h| =\u003e |pq| ≤ sqrt((s^2 - p^2)(s^2 - q^2)). Since p,q positive, we have\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190733263,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190734371,"data":"       | pq ≤ sqrt((s^2 - p^2)(s^2 - q^2)).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190735589,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190736693,"data":"       | Square both sides: p^2 q^2 ≤ (s^2 - p^2)(s^2 - q^2) = s^4 - s^2(p^2+q^2) + p^2 q^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190738,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190739109,"data":"       | Cancel p^2 q^2 from both sides, we get:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190740288,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190742721,"data":"       | 0 ≤ s^4 - s^2(p^2 + q^2) =\u003e s^2 (s^2 - (p^2+q^2)) ≥ 0 =\u003e s^2 ≥ p^2 + q^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190744179,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190745314,"data":"       | Thus s^2 ≥ p^2 + q^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190746498,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190747613,"data":"       | Recall also p+q = 8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.1907488,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190749904,"data":"       | Thus s^2 ≥ p^2 + q^2 = (p+q)^2 - 2pq = 64 - 2pq.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190751169,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190752322,"data":"       | Thus s^2 ≥ 64 - 2pq.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190753524,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19075466,"data":"       | Also we have earlier from vertical component of u: u_z =2, and s^2 = u_x^2 + u_y^2 + 4 =\u003e s^2 ≥ 4, trivial.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190756061,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190757199,"data":"       | Additionally, we have earlier constraint s ≥ 6.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190758485,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190759863,"data":"       | Now, also from orthogonality, we have equality condition? Not necessarily; the dot product condition v_h·w_h = -pq may not be maximal magnitude. In general, we can have any dot product between -|v_h||w_h| and +|v_h||w_h|. Here dot product is negative. So we have -|v_h||w_h| ≤ -pq ≤ |v_h||w_h|? Wait -pq is negative; we have v_h·w_h = -pq. The magnitude |v_h·w_h| = pq. Since |v_h·w_h| ≤ |v_h||w_h|, we get condition pq ≤ sqrt((s^2 - p^2)(s^2 - q^2). That's what we derived. So inequality must hold.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190762111,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190763246,"data":"       | Now, we also have orthogonality of u with v and w: u·v = 0 and u·w = 0. That yields constraints linking horizontal components of u with those of v,w and vertical components.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190764775,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190765899,"data":"       | Let u = (u_h, 2), where u_h is horizontal vector (2D). v = (v_h, p), w = (w_h, q). Orthogonality: u·v = u_h·v_h + 2p = 0. Similarly u_h·w_h + 2q = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.1907674,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190768522,"data":"       | Thus we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190769667,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190770773,"data":"       | u_h·v_h = -2p,\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190771975,"data":"       | u_h·w_h = -2q.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19077322,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190774333,"data":"       | Also u_h·u_h = s^2 - 4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190775538,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190776662,"data":"       | Now we can try to solve for possible p,q,s that satisfy these constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190777933,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190779064,"data":"       | Given we have many degrees of freedom (vectors in 2D). However we may be able to deduce p,q uniquely.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19078041,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190781519,"data":"       | Perhaps we can use the fact that v_h·w_h = -pq, and also we can compute (u_h·v_h) + (u_h·w_h) maybe.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190784139,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190785346,"data":"       | But we have 2D vectors; there are 2 components each: u_h (2), v_h (2), w_h (2) =\u003e 6 unknowns. Equations:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190786709,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19078782,"data":"       | 1) |u_h|^2 = s^2 - 4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190789018,"data":"       | 2) |v_h|^2 = s^2 - p^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190790196,"data":"       | 3) |w_h|^2 = s^2 - q^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190791346,"data":"       | 4) u_h·v_h = -2p.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190792551,"data":"       | 5) u_h·w_h = -2q.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190793801,"data":"       | 6) v_h·w_h = -pq.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19079494,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190796062,"data":"       | That's six equations for six unknowns (u_h components, v_h components, w_h components). So there may be a unique solution up to rotations (which is irrelevant). So p,q and s may be uniquely determined.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190797573,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190798683,"data":"       | We can attempt to solve these equations algebraically.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190799902,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190801014,"data":"       | Let’s treat u_h, v_h, w_h as vectors in R^2. We can consider them as column vectors.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190802339,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19080362,"data":"       | We can think of forming Gram matrix of vectors u_h, v_h, w_h. The Gram matrix G = [[u·u, u·v, u·w], [v·u, v·v, v·w], [w·u, w·v, w·w]] where dot product is standard inner product in R^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190805248,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190806352,"data":"       | Given we have values:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190807486,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190808588,"data":"       | u·u = s^2 - 4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190809798,"data":"       | v·v = s^2 - p^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190810973,"data":"       | w·w = s^2 - q^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19081214,"data":"       | u·v = -2p.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190813322,"data":"       | u·w = -2q.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190814464,"data":"       | v·w = -pq.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19081562,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190816729,"data":"       | Thus Gram matrix:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190817894,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190819027,"data":"       | G = [[s^2-4, -2p, -2q],\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190820189,"data":"       |      [-2p, s^2-p^2, -pq],\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190821384,"data":"       |      [-2q, -pq, s^2 - q^2]]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19082261,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190823727,"data":"       | Since these vectors lie in 2-dimensional space, the rank of G is at most 2, meaning its determinant must be zero. That's key! Because three vectors in 2D are linearly dependent. So determinant of G = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190825192,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190826298,"data":"       | Thus we can set determinant to zero and obtain relationship among s, p, q.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190828905,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190830076,"data":"       | Let's compute determinant symbolic.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190831308,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190832465,"data":"       | assistantanalysisLet's compute using sympy.assistantanalysis to=python codeimport sympy as sp\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190833782,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19083489,"data":"       | s,p,q=sp.symbols('s p q', positive=True, real=True)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190836109,"data":"       | G=sp.Matrix([[s**2-4, -2*p, -2*q],\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190837305,"data":"       |              [-2*p, s**2-p**2, -p*q],\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190838484,"data":"       |              [-2*q, -p*q, s**2-q**2]])\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190839693,"data":"       | detG=sp.simplify(G.det())\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190840856,"data":"       | detG\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190841981,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190843115,"data":"     [Code]:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190844249,"data":"       | import sympy as sp\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190845401,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190846547,"data":"       | s,p,q=sp.symbols('s p q', positive=True, real=True)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190847772,"data":"       | G=sp.Matrix([[s**2-4, -2*p, -2*q],\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190848938,"data":"       |              [-2*p, s**2-p**2, -p*q],\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190850125,"data":"       |              [-2*q, -p*q, s**2-q**2]])\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.190851313,"data":"       | detG=sp.simplify(G.det())\n"}
