,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191071964,"data":"       | Let's read statement: \"Vertex A of the cube is set on a horizontal plane P so that the plane of the rectangle ABDC is perpendicular to P, vertex B is 2 meters above P, vertex C is 8 meters above P, and vertex D is 10 meters above P.\" It does not say anything about other vertices. So they could be anywhere relative to plane. So p or q could be negative. But we must ensure the cube is a rigid body; orientation may cause some vertices below ground.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19107404,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191075184,"data":"       | Thus p and q can be any real numbers satisfying sum 8, and relation s^2 = p^2 + q^2 + 4. This uniquely determines s given p and q. But we have infinite possibilities (choose any p, then q = 8-p, compute s^2 = p^2 + (8-p)^2 + 4 = 2p^2 -16p + 68). So s^2 = 2p^2 -16p + 68. That's a quadratic in p. For each p, s determined.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191076947,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191078094,"data":"       | But we also have that s must be positive and also must satisfy that s^2 = p^2 + q^2 + 4 (already satisfied). So any p yields a valid s. However p must also satisfy that s^2 \u003e= 0 obviously. This is always positive.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191079668,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191080847,"data":"       | Thus there is a continuum of possible orientations (different tilt) that satisfy heights of A,B,C,D. Indeed we can tilt cube around AB axis maybe, adjusting p and q.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191082262,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191083418,"data":"       | Thus water volume might depend on p (and hence orientation). But perhaps the volume of water up to height 7 is independent of p (and thus s). Let's test numerically.\n"}
