,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191139294,"data":"       | Given linear inequality region in unit square, we can compute area of region where p b + q c ≤ K for any K.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19114068,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191141793,"data":"       | General formula: For positive p,q, area A(K) =\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19114302,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191144147,"data":"       | - If K ≤ 0: 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191145304,"data":"       | - If 0 \u003c K \u003c min(p,q): area = K^2/(2 p q). Wait not exactly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191146653,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191147771,"data":"       | Let's derive: region defined by p b + q c ≤ K, with b,c∈[0,1].\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191149062,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191150154,"data":"       | We can consider solving for c ≤ (K - p b)/q for each b where RHS \u003e= 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19115151,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191152641,"data":"       | Thus for b from 0 to K/p, c_max = (K - p b)/q (which is ≤ 1 if K - p b ≤ q). But we need also limit b to ≤1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191154084,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191155231,"data":"       | Case 1: K ≤ p and K ≤ q: then K/p ≤1 and K/q ≤1. Then region is triangle with vertices (0,0), (K/p,0), (0,K/q). Area = (1/2)*(K/p)*(K/q) = K^2/(2 p q).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191156712,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191157829,"data":"       | Case 2: K between p and q (assuming p\u003cq). Then K/p \u003e 1? Actually if K\u003ep, then K/p \u003e1, but b max is 1. So for b from 0 to 1, c_max = min( (K - p b)/q, 1). Since at b=0, c_max = K/q ≤1 if K ≤ q; else \u003e1. So need to handle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191159604,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191160713,"data":"       | In general, area can be expressed piecewise based on K relative to p and q.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191161973,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191163108,"data":"       | Since p+q=8, p and q could be \u003e5 or \u003c5 etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191164427,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191165518,"data":"       | Our K values: 5 and 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191166658,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191167936,"data":"       | Let's assume w.l.o.g. p ≥ q? Not necessarily. But we can consider p≥q (i.e., p≥4). Since sum 8, p≥4 implies q≤4. So p≥4, q≤4. Could also have p≤4. But due to symmetry, we can treat p≥q.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191169553,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191170707,"data":"       | Because the rectangle ABDC orientation might have p≥q, but water volume depends symmetrically on p,q (since swapping v and w corresponds to swapping b and c axes). The inequality 2a + p b + q c ≤ 7 is symmetric if we rename b,c accordingly. So volume fraction V0 depends only on unordered pair {p,q}. So we can assume p≥q.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191172484,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191173613,"data":"       | Thus p ∈ [4,8] (since p≥q and sum 8). q = 8-p ∈ [0,4].\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19117494,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191176057,"data":"       | Now consider K=5. Since q ≤4, K=5 \u003e q. Also p ≥4, possibly \u003e5? p could be \u003e5 if p\u003e5. So need piecewise.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191179424,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191180586,"data":"       | Similarly K=7 \u003e q (since q≤4). Also K=7 may be \u003e p depending on p.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191181966,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191183091,"data":"       | Thus for K=5, we have K\u003eq always, and K may be \u003ep or ≤p.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191184464,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191185605,"data":"       | For K=7, also \u003eq, and \u003ep if p\u003c7? Actually p could be up to 8, so could be \u003e7 or \u003c7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191187107,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191188587,"data":"       | Thus region shape will be a trapezoid/triangle plus rectangle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191190664,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191192277,"data":"       | Let's compute area A(K) for given p≥q.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191193537,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191194696,"data":"       | General method: For each b in [0,1], c_max = min( (K - p b)/q, 1 ) if K - p b \u003e= 0 else 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191196029,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191197129,"data":"       | Define b0 = K/p (where RHS zero). For b \u003e b0, c_max negative =\u003e zero.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191198512,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191199612,"data":"       | So integration over b from 0 to min(b0,1). For each b, c_max = min( (K - p b)/q, 1 ).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191200887,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191201978,"data":"       | Now we need to find b* where (K - p b)/q = 1 =\u003e K - p b = q =\u003e b = (K - q)/p.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191203338,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191204482,"data":"       | Thus if (K - q)/p \u003c 0, then at b=0 already (K/q) \u003e 1, meaning c_max capped at 1 for small b. If (K - q)/p \u003e 1, then the line never reaches 1 within b∈[0,1]; then c_max always less than 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19120614,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191207864,"data":"       | Thus region consists of rectangle part where c=1 for b up to some limit, then triangular part.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191209204,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19121033,"data":"       | Thus compute area:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19121148,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191212615,"data":"       | Let b1 = min( (K - q)/p , 1 ) . Actually need to consider if K - q positive; since K\u003eq, K - q \u003e0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191214036,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191215127,"data":"       | If (K - q)/p ≥ 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191216311,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19121744,"data":"       | Now there are two regimes:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19121862,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191220049,"data":"       | - If (K - q)/p ≥ 1, i.e., K - q ≥ p, then for all b ∈ [0,1], (K - p b)/q ≥ (K - p)/q ≥ (q)/q? Wait compute: If K - q ≥ p =\u003e K ≥ p + q = 8. But K =5 or 7 \u003c8, so this never holds. So (K - q)/p \u003c 1 for K \u003c p+q. Since p+q=8 \u003e K, we have (K - q)/p \u003c 1 always. So the line reaches c=1 at b = (K - q)/p which is less than 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191223507,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191224688,"data":"       | Thus for b ∈ [0, (K - q)/p], c_max = 1 (since (K - p b)/q \u003e= 1). For b ∈ [(K - q)/p, K/p], c_max = (K - p b)/q (which decreases from 1 to 0). For b \u003e K/p, c_max = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191226256,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191227385,"data":"       | Now need to ensure that (K - q)/p ≥ 0. Since K\u003eq, it's positive. Also need to ensure that (K - q)/p ≤ K/p (obviously because subtracting q from numerator reduces). So region shape is a right trapezoid plus triangle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191229033,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191230142,"data":"       | Thus area A(K) = rectangle area: width (K - q)/p * height 1 = (K - q)/p.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191231429,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191232541,"data":"       | Plus triangle area: base = K/p - (K - q)/p = q/p, height decreases from 1 to 0 linearly, area = (1/2)*(base)*(height) = (1/2)*(q/p)*1 = q/(2p).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191233923,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191235045,"data":"       | Thus total area A(K) = (K - q)/p + q/(2p) = (K - q + q/2)/p = (K - q/2)/p.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191236309,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191237411,"data":"       | Check: A(K) = (K - q/2) / p.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19123859,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191239714,"data":"       | Let's test for K small? For K less than q, this formula would give negative? Not applicable; but for K\u003eq, this works.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19124112,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191242219,"data":"       | Now for K=5: area A5 = (5 - q/2)/p.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191243392,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191244498,"data":"       | For K=7: area A7 = (7 - q/2)/p.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191245687,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191246802,"data":"       | But we must also ensure that K/p ≤ 1; otherwise b limit is 1. Since p could be \u003c K, then K/p \u003e1, then region saturates at b=1. Let's check.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191248286,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191249376,"data":"       | If p ≤ K, then K/p ≥1. Since b is limited to [0,1], the line may intersect at b=1 before hitting zero? Let's analyze.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191250781,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191251926,"data":"       | When p ≤ K, then K/p ≥ 1. The zero point b0 = K/p may be \u003e1, meaning line does not reach zero within unit square; thus region includes whole square for b up to 1? Actually for b=1, c_max = (K - p)/q which may be positive if K \u003e p. So region includes all b ∈ [0,1] with c_max = min( (K - p b)/q, 1). Since at b=1, we have (K - p)/q which may be \u003e0. So region is full width.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191253906,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191255421,"data":"       | Thus the earlier piecewise formula changes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19125661,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191257723,"data":"       | Thus we need to consider whether p \u003e K or not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191259008,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191261631,"data":"       | Since K=5 and 7. For p in [4,8] (since p≥q). So p may be less than 5 (i.e., p ∈ [4,5)) or greater than 5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191263058,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191264154,"data":"       | Similarly for 7: p may be less than 7 (most) or greater (p ∈ (7,8]).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191265473,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191266587,"data":"       | Thus we need to treat cases:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191267752,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191268847,"data":"       | Case A: p ≥ K (i.e., p≥5 for K=5). Then K/p ≤1, formula above holds.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191270167,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191271322,"data":"       | Case B: p \u003c K. Then K/p \u003e 1, region extends to b=1. The inequality p b + q c ≤ K for b up to 1 gives c ≤ (K - p b)/q; but for b=0, c_max = K/q (which may be \u003e1). Since q ≤4, K/q ≥ K/4 \u003e 1 for K=5 (5/4=1.25) and K=7 (7/4=1.75). So at b=0, c_max \u003e 1, but capped at 1. So there will be rectangle region where c=1 for some range of b, then triangular region where c less than 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191273433,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191274549,"data":"       | Let's derive general formula for area when p \u003c K.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191275828,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191276949,"data":"       | In this case, the line p b + q c = K intersects the top boundary c=1 at b = (K - q)/p. Since K\u003eq, this b is positive; but because p\u003cK, (K - q)/p may be \u003e1? Let's test: Since p \u003c K, denominator smaller, so (K - q)/p \u003e (K - q)/K = 1 - q/K \u003c1 (since q\u003e0). Actually (K - q)/p could be \u003e1 if p is small enough. Let's compute maximum: p minimum is q (since p≥q). But p could be as low as 4 (when q=4). For K=5, (K - q)/p = (5 - 4)/4 = 0.25 \u003c1. So still \u003c1. For K=7, (7-4)/4 = 0.75 \u003c1. So still \u003c1. So the intersection with c=1 occurs before b=1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191279539,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191280666,"data":"       | Thus region: For b from 0 up to b0 = (K - q)/p, c_max = 1 (since line gives \u003e1). Then for b from b0 to 1, c_max = (K - p b)/q (since now line less than 1). However note that at b=1, c_max = (K - p)/q which is positive because K \u003e p? Wait if p \u003c K, then K - p \u003e0, so c_max positive. So region includes all b up to 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19128252,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191283622,"data":"       | Thus area = rectangle width b0 * height 1 + integral from b0 to 1 of (K - p b)/q db.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19128492,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191286026,"data":"       | Compute:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191287169,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191288273,"data":"       | Area = b0 + (1/q) * [K b - (p/2) b^2]_{b0}^{1}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191289488,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19129082,"data":"       | Let's compute symbolic.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191292025,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.191293127,"data":"       | assistantanalysis to=python codeb0 = (sp.Symbol('K') - q)/p\n"}
