,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192171551,"data":"       | analysisVolume varies with p, not constant. So we need more constraints to uniquely determine p (i.e., orientation). Perhaps we missed condition: The plane of rectangle ABDC is perpendicular to plane P (ground). We used that to set rectangle plane vertical (perpendicular to ground). Did we fully incorporate? Yes we used that rectangle plane is vertical, which gave that the normal of rectangle ABDC is horizontal (i.e., its dot with k = 0). Let's verify we used that condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192173558,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192174784,"data":"       | Our earlier derivation of determinant condition from Gram matrix (vectors u_h,v_h,w_h in horizontal plane) implicitly used the fact that u_h, v_h, w_h are horizontal components (i.e., orthogonal to k). Indeed we defined u_h as horizontal component of u (i.e., projection onto ground). That is consistent with rectangle ABDC being vertical: Because rectangle ABDC's plane includes u (edge AB) and v+w (face diagonal). The normal to rectangle ABDC is perpendicular to both u and (v+w). Since u and v,w are orthogonal, the normal is u × (v + w). This normal's vertical component may be zero if u and v,w have appropriate components. Let's test: Since u_h, v_h, w_h are horizontal components, the vertical component of u is u_z = 2. The vertical component of v+w is p+q = 8. The cross product of u and (v+w) could have vertical component; but the condition that rectangle plane is vertical means its normal is horizontal =\u003e cross product must have zero vertical component. Let's compute condition: (u × (v+w))·k = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192177931,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192179311,"data":"       | Since k is vertical unit vector (0,0,1), dot product equals the vertical component of cross product. Equivalent to (u_h, u_z) × (v_h + w_h, p+q). The vertical component of cross product of vectors (a_h, a_z) and (b_h, b_z) is a_h × b_h (i.e., 2D cross product of horizontal components) plus something? Actually cross product in 3D: For vectors u = (u_hx, u_hy, u_z) and w = (w_hx, w_hy, w_z), the vertical component (z component) of u × w is u_hx * w_hy - u_hy * w_hx = (u_h × w_h) (2D cross product). So vertical component depends only on horizontal components, not on vertical components. So condition that rectangle plane is vertical (normal horizontal) is equivalent to (u_h × (v_h + w_h)) = 0, meaning horizontal components are parallel. That is, u_h is parallel to (v_h + w_h). Indeed the normal being horizontal means cross product vertical component zero, meaning u_h and v_h + w_h are linearly dependent (parallel) in plane.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192182225,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192183349,"data":"       | Thus we need condition: u_h is parallel to (v_h + w_h). That is additional constraint beyond orthogonality we used? Wait we earlier used that u_h, v_h, w_h are in the horizontal plane (since they are projections onto ground). But we didn't enforce that u_h is parallel to v_h + w_h. Did we incorporate that? Not yet. The determinant condition we derived (rank 2 of Gram matrix) came from the fact that vectors u_h, v_h, w_h lie in 2D, but not necessarily that u_h is parallel to v_h + w_h. However that condition may further restrict p and s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192186964,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192188478,"data":"       | Let's compute condition: u_h × (v_h + w_h) = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192190508,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192192142,"data":"       | Let’s denote vector sums: s_h = v_h + w_h.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192193447,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19219458,"data":"       | Since u_h is parallel to s_h, there exists scalar λ such that u_h = λ s_h.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192195928,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192197055,"data":"       | Thus u_h = λ (v_h + w_h).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192198293,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19219942,"data":"       | Now we can use dot product relations to determine λ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192200696,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192201864,"data":"       | Recall we have orthogonality constraints: u·v = 0 =\u003e u_h·v_h + u_z v_z = 0 =\u003e u_h·v_h = - u_z v_z = -2 p.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192203425,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192204533,"data":"       | Similarly u·w = 0 =\u003e u_h·w_h = -2 q.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192205811,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192206909,"data":"       | Also we have s_h = v_h + w_h.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192208086,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192209227,"data":"       | Now compute u_h·s_h = u_h·v_h + u_h·w_h = -2p - 2q = -2(p+q) = -2*8 = -16.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192210623,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19221228,"data":"       | But also, if u_h = λ s_h, then u_h·s_h = λ |s_h|^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192213613,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192214734,"data":"       | Thus λ |s_h|^2 = -16.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192215974,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19221714,"data":"       | Thus λ is negative (since dot product negative). So λ = -16 / |s_h|^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192218504,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192219641,"data":"       | Now also we have u_h = λ s_h, so magnitude |u_h| = |λ| |s_h|.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19222097,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192222102,"data":"       | Recall |u_h|^2 = s^2 - u_z^2 = s^2 - 4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192223305,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192224395,"data":"       | |s_h|^2 = |v_h|^2 + |w_h|^2 + 2 v_h·w_h.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192225636,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192226793,"data":"       | We have |v_h|^2 = s^2 - p^2, |w_h|^2 = s^2 - q^2, and v_h·w_h = - p q (from earlier orthogonality condition). So\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192228199,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19222955,"data":"       | |s_h|^2 = (s^2 - p^2) + (s^2 - q^2) + 2(- p q) = 2 s^2 - (p^2 + q^2) - 2 p q = 2 s^2 - (p+q)^2 = 2 s^2 - 64.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192232145,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19223328,"data":"       | Since (p+q)=8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192234456,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192235598,"data":"       | Thus |s_h|^2 = 2 s^2 - 64.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192236771,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192237914,"data":"       | Now we have λ = -16 / (2 s^2 - 64) = -16 / (2 (s^2 - 32)) = -8 / (s^2 - 32).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192239227,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192240335,"data":"       | Now |u_h|^2 = λ^2 |s_h|^2 = λ^2 * (2 s^2 - 64).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192241591,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192242734,"data":"       | Compute λ^2 * (2 s^2 - 64) = (64 / (s^2 - 32)^2) * (2 s^2 - 64) = 64 (2 s^2 - 64) / (s^2 - 32)^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192244084,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19224523,"data":"       | Set equal to s^2 - 4 (since |u_h|^2 = s^2 - 4). So:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192246444,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192247559,"data":"       | s^2 - 4 = 64 (2 s^2 - 64) / (s^2 - 32)^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192248764,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19224994,"data":"       | Now we have an equation solely for s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192251131,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192252226,"data":"       | Solve for s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192253374,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192254504,"data":"       | assistantanalysis to=python codes = sp.symbols('s', positive=True)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192255798,"data":"       | eq = sp.Eq(s**2 - 4, 64*(2*s**2 - 64)/(s**2 - 32)**2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192257045,"data":"       | sp.solve(eq, s)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192258191,"data":"       | \n"}
