,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192274506,"data":"       | Thus orientation is uniquely determined (p = q = 4). Indeed earlier we found that at s=6, discriminant zero, leading to p = q = 4. So the only solution satisfying the perpendicular condition is the case where p = q = 4 (i.e., both v_z and w_z = 4). This corresponds to symmetric case where AB has vertical component 2, other two edges have vertical component 4 each.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192277457,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19227869,"data":"       | Thus the cube is oriented such that the vertical components of the three orthogonal edges are 2,4,4. This seems plausible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192280066,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192281176,"data":"       | Thus we have side length s=6.\n"}
