,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192470151,"data":"       | We have coordinates: Let u = AB vector, length |u| = s. Let v = AD? Not defined. But we have vector AC = w = diagonal of a face, length s*sqrt(2). Similarly BD = w' = diagonal of other face, length s*sqrt(2). In rectangle ABDC, AB (u) and BD (w') are adjacent sides. So v = w' = BD vector. So we can define v = BD vector, length |v| = s*sqrt(2). And v ⟂ u (since rectangle). Also AB and CD are parallel: vector CD = u (since opposite edges parallel and equal length). Also AC is diagonal: vector AC = v (maybe? Actually AC is also a face diagonal, length s*sqrt(2). In rectangle ABDC, AC is the other side opposite to BD, also length s*sqrt(2) and parallel to BD (since opposite sides of rectangle are parallel). Indeed rectangle ABDC: sides AB and CD are parallel; sides BD and AC are parallel. So AC is parallel to BD (v). So we have vector AC = v as well (though opposite direction). So rectangle ABDC is a parallelogram with AB parallel CD and BD parallel AC, and right angle at A and B. So indeed rectangle.\n"}
