,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192490485,"data":"       | z(C) = v_z = 8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192491673,"data":"       | z(D) = (u+v)_z = u_z + v_z = 2+8 =10, matches.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192492906,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192494033,"data":"       | Thus we already have u_z = 2, v_z = 8. Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192495246,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192496373,"data":"       | Thus we can find s using lengths: s^2 = |u|^2 = u_x^2 + u_y^2 + (2)^2. Similarly, 2 s^2 = |v|^2 = v_x^2 + v_y^2 + (8)^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19249774,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192498879,"data":"       | Also orthogonality: u_x v_x + u_y v_y + (2)(8) = 0 =\u003e u_x v_x + u_y v_y = -16.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192500224,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19250137,"data":"       | We can treat unknown horizontal components (u_h = (u_x, u_y), v_h = (v_x, v_y)). Their lengths: |u_h|^2 = s^2 - 4. |v_h|^2 = 2 s^2 - 64.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192502764,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192503894,"data":"       | We also have dot product: u_h · v_h = -16.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192505164,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192506277,"data":"       | Goal: find s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192507461,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192508609,"data":"       | We have unknowns: |u_h|, |v_h|, dot product. Use relationship between magnitudes and dot product: For any vectors, (u_h·v_h)^2 ≤ |u_h|^2 |v_h|^2 (Cauchy). So we can set up equation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192510173,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192511311,"data":"       | Let a = |u_h|^2 = s^2 - 4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192512501,"data":"       | Let b = |v_h|^2 = 2 s^2 - 64.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192513689,"data":"       | Let c = u_h·v_h = -16.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192514891,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19251626,"data":"       | We have constraint: c^2 = 256 must equal a b cos^2θ? Actually c = |u_h||v_h| cosθ. So c^2 = a b cos^2θ ≤ a b. Therefore we must have a b ≥ 256.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192517847,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192518991,"data":"       | But we also need consistency with orthogonality of full vectors: u·v = 0 =\u003e u_h·v_h + u_z v_z = 0 =\u003e c + 2*8 = 0 =\u003e c = -16 (given). So that's satisfied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192520596,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192521708,"data":"       | Now we have unknown s; we need a and b defined in terms of s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19252295,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192524105,"data":"       | Thus condition that vectors u_h and v_h exist in plane (2D) with given lengths a,b and dot product c is that the Gram matrix is positive semidefinite: determinant \u003e=0: a b - c^2 \u003e= 0. Since we have equality? Actually for 2D vectors, there is always some angle satisfying dot product as long as c^2 ≤ a b. So we need inequality.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192525997,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192527114,"data":"       | Thus we need a b - c^2 ≥ 0. Compute:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19252834,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192531511,"data":"       | a b - 256 = (s^2 - 4)(2 s^2 - 64) - 256.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192532754,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192533884,"data":"       | Set \u003e=0. Let's compute expression.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192535169,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192536275,"data":"       | assistantanalysis to=python codeimport sympy as sp\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192537506,"data":"       | s = sp.symbols('s', positive=True)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192538679,"data":"       | expr = (s**2 - 4)*(2*s**2 - 64) - 256\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192539876,"data":"       | sp.expand(expr)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192541037,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192542158,"data":"     [Code]:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192543294,"data":"       | import sympy as sp\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19254446,"data":"       | s = sp.symbols('s', positive=True)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192545634,"data":"       | expr = (s**2 - 4)*(2*s**2 - 64) - 256\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192546868,"data":"       | sp.expand(expr)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192548044,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19254914,"data":"     [Output]:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192550363,"data":"       \u003e 2*s**4 - 72*s**2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192551558,"data":"       \u003e \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192552689,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192553774,"data":"     [Turn 2]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192554908,"data":"     [Reasoning] (24754 chars):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192556093,"data":"       | analysisExpression simplifies to 2 s^4 - 72 s^2 = 2 s^2 (s^2 - 36). So condition a b - 256 = 2 s^2 (s^2 - 36) \u003e= 0. Since s\u003e0, we get s^2 - 36 \u003e= 0 =\u003e s \u003e= 6. So side length s must be at least 6 meters.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192557772,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192559114,"data":"       | But we also must have a \u003e=0 and b\u003e=0 (since squares). That gives s^2 \u003e=4 (=\u003e s\u003e=2) and 2 s^2 \u003e=64 =\u003e s^2 \u003e=32 =\u003e s \u003e= sqrt(32) ≈5.657. So combined constraints: s \u003e=6 (since from determinant). So minimal s is 6. But can s be larger? Yes, any s \u003e=6 satisfies inequality, but does there exist actual vectors for any s? Let's check if we can find u_h, v_h for any s \u003e=6. The inequality is satisfied; we can choose angle to match dot product magnitude. Since we have fixed dot product -16, we need cosθ = c / (|u_h||v_h|) = -16 / sqrt(a b). Since a b \u003e=256, cosθ = -16 / sqrt(ab) will be in [-1,1]. So any s such that sqrt(ab) \u003e=16 works, which is same condition. So s can be any \u003e=6.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192562055,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192563223,"data":"       | Thus side length s is not uniquely determined by given data? But maybe there is additional condition: The rectangle ABDC plane is perpendicular to the horizontal plane, meaning its normal vector has no vertical component. However we have not used that condition yet. This will constrain orientation further and thus determine s uniquely.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192564975,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192566133,"data":"       | Let's compute normal vector of rectangle plane defined by u and v (AB and AC). Since the rectangle plane is spanned by u and v. Its normal n = u × v. Condition that n has zero vertical component (i.e., n_z = 0). Compute n_z = (u × v)_z = u_x v_y - u_y v_x = (u_h × v_h)_z (since cross product's z component depends only on x,y components). Indeed n_z = (u_h)_x (v_h)_y - (u_h)_y (v_h)_x = determinant of their horizontal components. So condition n_z = 0 means u_h and v_h are linearly dependent (collinear) in horizontal plane! Wait cross product's z component zero indicates that horizontal components are parallel (i.e., vectors u_h and v_h lie along same line in xy-plane). That would make the rectangle plane's normal have no vertical component, but does that ensure plane is vertical? Let's verify.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192570426,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192571598,"data":"       | If the plane's normal vector has zero vertical component, it lies entirely in horizontal plane. That means the plane is vertical (contains the vertical direction). Indeed, a plane is vertical if its normal is horizontal. For the plane spanned by u and v, the normal is cross product. Its vertical component is given by determinant of horizontal components. So for n_z=0, the horizontal components of u and v must be parallel (collinear). That is, u_h = λ v_h for some λ (real). So the horizontal components are linearly dependent.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192573798,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192575146,"data":"       | Thus we have condition that u_h and v_h are parallel vectors (i.e., they have the same direction in the horizontal plane). So we can write u_h = k * v_h for some scalar k (maybe negative? Could be). Then dot product u_h·v_h = k |v_h|^2 = -16. Also magnitude squared a = |u_h|^2 = k^2 |v_h|^2 = k^2 b.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192576884,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19257805,"data":"       | Thus we can derive k.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192579244,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192580382,"data":"       | Let |v_h|^2 = b, a = k^2 b.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192581581,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19258269,"data":"       | From earlier definitions: a = s^2 - 4, b = 2 s^2 - 64.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192583911,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192585021,"data":"       | Thus k^2 = a / b = (s^2 - 4)/(2 s^2 - 64).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192586213,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192587381,"data":"       | Also dot product c = u_h·v_h = k b = -16.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192588611,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192589731,"data":"       | Thus k = -16 / b.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192590876,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192591982,"data":"       | Plug into relation: k^2 = (256) / b^2 = a / b.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192593172,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192594331,"data":"       | Thus 256 / b^2 = a / b -\u003e cross-multiply: 256 = a b.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192596287,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192597449,"data":"       | But earlier we had determinant expression a b - 256 = 2 s^2 (s^2 -36). Setting a b = 256 yields 2 s^2 (s^2 - 36) = 0 =\u003e s^2 = 0 or s^2 = 36. Since s\u003e0, s^2 = 36 =\u003e s = 6.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192598988,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192600133,"data":"       | Thus the condition that plane is vertical (normal horizontal) forces equality a b = 256, giving s = 6. So side length uniquely determined as 6 meters.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192601556,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192604019,"data":"       | Great! So cube side length is 6 meters. Now we have full vector components:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192605319,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192606418,"data":"       | s=6.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192607531,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192608684,"data":"       | Compute a = s^2 - 4 = 36 - 4 = 32. So |u_h|^2 = 32 =\u003e |u_h| = sqrt(32) = 4*sqrt(2). Similarly b = 2 s^2 - 64 = 2*36 - 64 = 72 - 64 = 8. So |v_h|^2 = 8 =\u003e |v_h| = 2*sqrt(2). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192610244,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192611414,"data":"       | Now we have dot product c = -16. Indeed u_h·v_h = -16. Let's verify: Since vectors are parallel (collinear) but dot product negative: they must be opposite direction (i.e., k negative). Because magnitude product = |u_h||v_h| = sqrt(32)*sqrt(8) = sqrt(256) = 16. Since dot product is -16, they are opposite direction with angle 180 degrees: cosθ = -1. So u_h = - (|u_h|/|v_h|) v_h = - (sqrt(32)/sqrt(8)) v_h = - (sqrt(4)) v_h = -2 v_h. Indeed ratio of magnitudes: sqrt(32)/sqrt(8) = sqrt(4) = 2. So u_h = -2 v_h.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192613531,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192614649,"data":"       | Thus k = -2. Indeed we have u_h = -2 v_h. Check k = -16 / b = -16 / 8 = -2, matches.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192615943,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192617383,"data":"       | Thus we have orientation: Horizontal components of u and v are opposite directions; u horizontal component is twice magnitude but opposite direction of v horizontal component.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19261885,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19262001,"data":"       | Now we can compute actual vectors.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.1926212,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192622322,"data":"       | Let v_h be some vector in xy-plane of length sqrt(8) = 2*sqrt(2). Let v_h = (p, q) where p^2 + q^2 = 8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192623663,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192624786,"data":"       | Then u_h = -2 v_h = (-2p, -2q). Then |u_h|^2 = 4(p^2+q^2) = 4*8 = 32, matches.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192626063,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192627198,"data":"       | Now we also have vertical components: u_z = 2, v_z = 8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192628447,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192629589,"data":"       | Thus we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192630737,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192631844,"data":"       | u = (-2p, -2q, 2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19263302,"data":"       | v = (p, q, 8)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192634178,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192635387,"data":"       | Now we can compute positions of all 8 vertices of cube. The cube is spanned by three orthogonal edge vectors: u (AB), w (some other edge from A not in rectangle) maybe? Wait we have only two edges from A: AB = u, and AC = v (since C is opposite of B in rectangle). However AC is not an edge, it's a face diagonal. Indeed vector v is not an edge from A (since AC is a face diagonal, not an edge). Wait earlier we identified v = vector AC (face diagonal), but we might need to find the other edge direction(s) for cube. Actually the cube's edges from A are three mutually perpendicular vectors of length s, each pair orthogonal. One is AB = u. Another is some edge vector e (call w) that is orthogonal to u, and length s, but not lying in rectangle ABDC. The third edge is the cross product of u and w? Actually edges from A are: AB (u), something like AD? But D not adjacent to A; D is opposite vertex across rectangle. The third edge from A is maybe A to some other vertex not in rectangle, call E.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192639623,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192640789,"data":"       | Nevertheless, to compute water volume inside cube up to height 7, we might not need full orientation; we could integrate using known heights of vertices and orientation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192642239,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192643698,"data":"       | But perhaps easier: Since we know side length s=6 and we have coordinates for four vertices of rectangle. The other four vertices are obtained by adding the third edge vector w (call t) to each of these vertices, where w is orthogonal to both u and v (since cube edges are orthogonal). Indeed, in a cube, there are three orthogonal edge vectors a, b, c each of length s. The eight vertices are all combinations of adding subsets of these vectors to A. Without loss, let a = u (AB). Let b = w (some vector orthogonal to a and of length s). Let c = ??? Actually the rectangle ABDC uses a (AB) and c (??). Wait we have rectangle formed by vectors a (AB) and d (BD). But BD = a + b? Let's examine standard cube coordinates: Suppose we have standard axes: e1, e2, e3 orthogonal, length s. Then vertices are (0,0,0), e1, e2, e3, e1+e2, e1+e3, e2+e3, e1+e2+e3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192646349,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192647504,"data":"       | Now choose labeling: Let A = (0,0,0). Let B = e1 (edge). Let C = e2 (edge?). Wait but C is a face diagonal of the face containing A and B? Actually AC is a diagonal of face that includes A and B. In a standard cube, faces are spanned by pairs of edges. For example, face with vertices A (0,0,0), B (e1), some other vertex e2? Actually face with A and B also includes the vertex e1+e2 (??). The diagonal of that face from A to the opposite vertex is e1+e2. So AC being a diagonal suggests that C = e1+e2. Similarly, BD diagonal of another face: B to D maybe B + e2? Wait B to D is diagonal of face with vertices B, B+e2, etc. Let's map.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192649773,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192650895,"data":"       | If we set A = 0, let e1 = AB, e2 = AD? Not exactly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192652105,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192653253,"data":"       | But we have rectangle ABDC with vertices: A, B = A + e1 (AB), C = A + e2 (face diagonal) ??? Actually AC is a face diagonal, so e2 is not a cube edge but a diagonal of a face. In standard orientation, a face diagonal is sum of two orthogonal edge vectors. So we can define two orthogonal edge vectors: Let's call p and q orthogonal of length s. Then the face diagonal from A to opposite vertex on that face is p+q. So AC = p + q (vector). Similarly, BD = p + q? Wait BD is also a diagonal of a face, but which face? It connects B = p to D = ? If D = p + (some third edge r) + q? Let's compute.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192655446,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192656719,"data":"       | Standard cube labeling: Let edges from A: along x (vector a), along y (vector b), along z (vector c). Then vertices:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192658045,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192659166,"data":"       | A = (0,0,0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192660302,"data":"       | B = a\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192661461,"data":"       | C = a + b (face diagonal across face formed by a and b)??? Actually a+b is diagonal from A to opposite vertex of that face (plane spanned by a and b). That could be C if we want AC to be diagonal of face containing A and B. Indeed AC = a + b.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192664538,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192665749,"data":"       | Now D must be opposite edge to AB (parallel) i.e., D = b + c? Wait AB is edge a. CD is parallel edge also of length a, but not sharing vertices with AB. In standard cube, opposite edge to AB is the edge from c to b + c? Let's compute: AB is from (0,0,0) to a. The opposite edge parallel to AB is from b + c to a + b + c (i.e., shift by vector b + c). That edge's endpoints are (b + c) and (a + b + c). So D could be the vertex a + b + c (or b + c). In our rectangle ABDC, vertices are A (0), B (a), D (a + b + c?) Wait D should be opposite edge's endpoint that is not same as A or B. Let's analyze rectangle ABDC: vertices in order A-B-D-C. So after B we go to D (which is opposite edge's endpoint that is adjacent to B via diagonal BD). In standard cube, B = a. D = a + b + c? Let's test: B to D diagonal of face? Which face's diagonal? B is a; D is a + b + c. The vector BD = (a + b + c) - a = b + c. That's sum of two edges (b and c) which are orthogonal and length s each, so its magnitude sqrt(2) s, indeed a face diagonal (the face spanned by b and c). So BD is a diagonal of the face opposite to the one containing A and B. That matches property: BD is diagonal of a face, yes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192668999,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192670133,"data":"       | Now AC vector = (a + b) - 0 = a + b, also a diagonal of face spanned by a and b. So AC diagonal as given.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192671445,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192672602,"data":"       | Thus labeling matches: a = AB, b = ??? Not an edge from A? Wait b is edge from A to some vertex not in rectangle (call E). Indeed in this model, b is edge from A to a vertex we haven't named (call E). Edge c is third edge from A to another vertex (call F). In our rectangle ABDC, we used only a and (b + c) combos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192674309,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192675665,"data":"       | Thus we have vector representation:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19267684,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192677968,"data":"       | Let a = u = AB (known).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192679197,"data":"       | Let b = w (unknown edge vector from A) of length s, orthogonal to a.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19268047,"data":"       | Let c = t (third edge) orthogonal to both a and b, length s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192681702,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192682791,"data":"       | Then vertices:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19268393,"data":"       | A = 0\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192685076,"data":"       | B = a\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192686244,"data":"       | C = a + b (since AC diagonal across face spanned by a and b) ? Wait earlier we set AC = a + b, but we earlier defined C = v = (p,q,8) which is AC. So indeed AC = a + b.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192687686,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192688797,"data":"       | Thus vector v = a + b.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192689925,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192691051,"data":"       | But earlier we defined v as AC vector. So we have relation: v = a + b.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192692318,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19269344,"data":"       | Similarly D = a + b + c? Wait D should be a + b + c? Let's compute: D is opposite edge's endpoint adjacent to B via diagonal BD: B to D diagonal of face spanned by b and c: BD = b + c. So D = B + (b + c) = a + b + c. Indeed D = a + b + c.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192696366,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192697505,"data":"       | Thus our four vertices correspond to:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192698696,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192699814,"data":"       | A = 0\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192700938,"data":"       | B = a\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192702079,"data":"       | C = a + b\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19270321,"data":"       | D = a + b + c\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192704353,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192705507,"data":"       | Thus rectangle ABDC is a quadrilateral with vertices A, B, C', D? Wait we have A, B, D, C ordering but they are not in order around rectangle? Let's examine coordinates: A (0), B (a), C (a + b), D (a + b + c). Connect A to B (edge a). B to D (diagonal of face spanned by b,c) = b + c (makes rectangle). D to C (edge a, but reversed)?? D to C = (a + b + c) - (a + b) = c (edge). Wait D to C is vector c, which is an edge of cube, length s. But earlier we said CD is parallel edge AB (i.e., a). However from this labeling, CD = c, which may not be parallel to a unless c is parallel to a. This contradicts earlier property: CD is parallel to AB. So our labeling not matching property.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192707853,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192708983,"data":"       | Let's reconsider mapping.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192710159,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192711468,"data":"       | We need AB and CD to be parallel edges. In standard cube, opposite edges AB (a) and CD (??) are parallel. Which opposite edge corresponds to CD? In our rectangle ABDC, vertices are A, B, D, C. So CD is edge connecting C to D. In standard labeling, if A=0, B=a, then C should be some vertex such that CD is parallel to AB (i.e., vector a). So CD vector must be a. That suggests D = C + a. Since CD = a. Also B to D is diagonal of a face, which would be some vector sum of two edges orthogonal to a? Let's attempt to assign:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192713523,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192714658,"data":"       | Let a = AB (edge vector). Let b, c be other orthogonal edges at A.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192715922,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192717049,"data":"       | We need C such that C = b (maybe). Then D = C + a = b + a. Then B = a, A =0. Let's test properties:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192718364,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192719484,"data":"       | AB = a (edge)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192720711,"data":"       | CD = D - C = (b + a) - b = a (parallel to AB). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192721945,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192723082,"data":"       | AC = C - A = b (but AC is given as a face diagonal, not an edge). This fails because AC would be edge length s, not sqrt(2)s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192724424,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192725581,"data":"       | Thus C cannot be a single edge vector; AC must be diagonal of a face, i.e., sum of two edge vectors. So C = a + b maybe. Then AC = a + b (face diagonal). Then CD = D - C should be a (parallel). So D = C + a = a + b + a = 2a + b. But D should also satisfy that BD is a face diagonal (i.e., BD = vector from B (a) to D (2a + b) = a + b, which is same as AC, also a face diagonal. Indeed BD = a + b, which is diagonal of face spanned by a and b. However problem states BD is diagonal of a face (yes). So this works! Let's check: In this configuration, rectangle ABDC vertices: A (0), B (a), C (a + b), D (2a + b). Let's compute side lengths:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.1927295,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192730711,"data":"       | AB = a (length s)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192731897,"data":"       | BC = (a + b) - a = b (edge length s) — Wait rectangle ABDC's side BC is not part of rectangle (order A-B-D-C). Actually rectangle ABDC order: A to B (AB), B to D (BD), D to C (DC), C to A (CA). So we need B to D vector = D - B = (2a + b) - a = a + b (face diagonal) length s√2. Good. D to C = C - D = (a + b) - (2a + b) = -a (length s) parallel to AB. C to A = A - C = -(a + b) length s√2 (face diagonal). So rectangle ABDC is indeed a rectangle with sides a (AB) and a + b (BD). Works.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192734064,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192735229,"data":"       | Thus our labeling matches condition that CD is parallel to AB (since CD = -a). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19273651,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192738033,"data":"       | Now we have vector b as an edge perpendicular to a. Also there is third edge c from A, orthogonal to both a and b, but not used in rectangle ABDC (since rectangle ABDC lies in plane spanned by a and (a+b) which is same as span{a,b}. Indeed plane ABDC is spanned by a and b (since a+b is combination). So rectangle ABDC is actually a face of the cube? Wait a and b are orthogonal edges, they span a face of the cube (the \"bottom\" face). Indeed the rectangle ABDC is essentially the face formed by edges a (AB) and diagonal a+b? Actually the face spanned by a and b is a square of side s (i.e., the face with vertices 0, a, a+b, b). That's the square face of the cube. But rectangle ABDC has vertices 0 (A), a (B), 2a + b (D), a + b (C). That's a shifted version of the square, not the same as the face square. However its sides are a (AB) and a+b (BD), which are orthogonal (since a·(a+b) = a·a + a·b = s^2 + 0 = s^2, not zero! Wait a·(a+b) = a·a + a·b = s^2 + 0 = s^2, not zero, so they are not perpendicular. But rectangle requires right angle between sides AB and BD. Are they perpendicular? Let's compute dot product: a·(a+b) = s^2. That's not zero unless s=0. So they are not orthogonal. So rectangle ABDC is not right rectangle? But problem states rectangle ABDC (plane of rectangle ABDC). Actually they call \"plane of rectangle ABDC\" not \"rectangle ABDC\" maybe they treat ABDC as a rectangle (maybe not right?). Wait they said \"the plane of the rectangle ABDC is perpendicular to P\". So they assert ABDC is a rectangle. Let's verify if our labeling yields rectangle? Check shape: Points A(0), B(a), D(2a+b), C(a+b). Compute vectors:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192742512,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192743608,"data":"       | AB = a.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192744769,"data":"       | BD = a+b.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192745913,"data":"       | DC = -a.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192747095,"data":"       | CA = -(a+b).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192748216,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192749339,"data":"       | AB length s, BD length √(a·a + b·b) = √(s^2 + s^2) = s√2. DC length s, CA length s√2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192750789,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192752507,"data":"       | Angle between AB and BD: dot = a·(a+b) = s^2. Norms: |AB|=s, |BD|=s√2. Cosθ = s^2 / (s * s√2) = 1/√2 ≈ 0.7071 =\u003e θ = 45°. So rectangle ABDC has angle 45°, not 90°, thus it's not a rectangle, it's a parallelogram? Actually opposite sides are parallel and equal, but adjacent sides not perpendicular. That would be a rhombus? Not exactly: sides lengths are s and s√2, not equal. So shape is a rectangle only if sides perpendicular. Since angle 45°, not rectangle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192756361,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192757509,"data":"       | Thus our labeling fails to produce rectangle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19275876,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192759933,"data":"       | Thus we need a different mapping where AB is edge vector a, BD is diagonal of a different face, which must be perpendicular to AB (since rectangle). In a cube, the diagonal of a face that includes edge a is not perpendicular to a; it's at 45°. However there is a face that does not include a but includes another edge b, whose diagonal would be perpendicular to a? Let's examine.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192761789,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192762917,"data":"       | Take three orthogonal edge vectors a, b, c. The face spanned by b and c is perpendicular to a. Its diagonal (b + c) is also perpendicular to a? Since a·(b + c) = a·b + a·c = 0 + 0 = 0. Yes! So the diagonal of face opposite to edge a (i.e., the face formed by the other two edges) is perpendicular to a. Good! So BD being diagonal of a face that is perpendicular to AB is plausible: The face whose diagonal is BD is the face spanned by b and c (the face opposite to the edge a). Then BD = b + c, which is perpendicular to a.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192765038,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192766161,"data":"       | Thus rectangle ABDC formed by sides AB (a) and BD (b + c) are perpendicular (since a·(b + c) = 0). This matches rectangle property.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192767571,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192768693,"data":"       | Now AC is also diagonal of a face. Which face? Could be face spanned by a and b? Let's compute AC = a + b (face diagonal) which is not perpendicular to a (since dot = a·(a+b)=s^2). But rectangle requires AC parallel to BD (since opposite sides). Indeed in rectangle ABDC, opposite sides are AB || CD and BD || AC. So AC must be parallel to BD (b + c). So AC should be parallel to b + c. But AC = a + b in our labeling; not parallel to b + c unless a is linear combination.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192770729,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192772142,"data":"       | Thus we need mapping where AC is also a diagonal of a face, but the face whose diagonal is AC must be the face opposite to CD? Wait.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19277352,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192774671,"data":"       | Given rectangle ABDC, sides: AB (edge a). BD (face diagonal of some face). CD (edge parallel to AB). AC (face diagonal of another face). Moreover, AB and CD are parallel edges of the cube (i.e., they are opposite edges). So CD is edge parallel to a, maybe equal to c? Or some other edge vector parallel to a but not same location. So CD could be vector a as well, but displaced.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192776483,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192777605,"data":"       | Thus rectangle ABDC is formed by two opposite edges a (AB) and a' (CD) parallel, and two face diagonals (BD, AC) that are parallel to each other, perpendicular to a.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192779008,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192780207,"data":"       | Hence we can construct geometry: Choose orthogonal edge vectors a,b,c (length s). Let AB = a. Let CD = a (parallel, same vector). So D = C + a. Points B and C must be such that BD and AC are diagonals of faces and parallel to each other. Which faces? Since BD is diagonal of a face, the face must be spanned by two edges that are perpendicular to each other and not containing AB? For BD to be parallel to AC, both must be same direction (b + c maybe). Let's try: Let BD = b + c (face diagonal of face spanned by b and c). Then AC must also be b + c (or scalar multiple). But AC is diagonal of a face; which face includes vertices A and C? If AC = b + c, then C = A + b + c = b + c. Then D = C + a = a + b + c. Then B = A + a = a. Let's verify properties:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192784205,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192785361,"data":"       | - AB = a (edge)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192786645,"data":"       | - CD = D - C = a (parallel to AB). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192787857,"data":"       | - BD = D - B = (a + b + c) - a = b + c (face diagonal). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192789144,"data":"       | - AC = C - A = b + c (same as BD). So AC parallel to BD, also same vector.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192790449,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192791707,"data":"       | Thus rectangle ABDC has vertices A (0), B (a), D (a + b + c), C (b + c). Let's compute side lengths:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19279301,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192794106,"data":"       | AB = a (length s)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192795314,"data":"       | BD = b + c (length sqrt(2) s)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192796672,"data":"       | DC = -a (length s)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192797847,"data":"       | CA = -(b + c) (length sqrt(2) s)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192799037,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192800171,"data":"       | Angle between AB and BD: a·(b + c) = 0 (since a orthogonal to b and c). So rectangle is indeed right angle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192801633,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192802892,"data":"       | Thus rectangle ABDC is a rectangle with sides a (edge) and b + c (face diagonal). Good. This matches problem statement.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192804229,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192805423,"data":"       | Now heights: Let vertical direction be Z. Let components: a_z = 2 (since B height 2). b_z + c_z = 8 (since AC height 8). Also D height = A_z + a_z + b_z + c_z = 0+2+8 =10 matches. Great!\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192807018,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192808135,"data":"       | Thus we have decomposition: a_z = 2, (b_z + c_z) = 8. Edge vectors b and c each have length s, are orthogonal to each other and to a. Their vertical components are unknown individually, but sum to 8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192809639,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192810785,"data":"       | We also have constraints that a,b,c are orthogonal vectors of length s. So we can find s from given heights and the vertical component of a (2) and sum of vertical components of b and c (8). But we also need condition that the plane ABDC is vertical: normal vector n = a × (b + c). Since a is orthogonal to b and c, a × (b + c) = a × b + a × c. Since a is orthogonal to b and c, cross products are perpendicular to a and each other? Let's compute n_z (vertical component) of normal.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192812898,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192815446,"data":"       | Recall n = a × (b + c). Since a is orthogonal to b and c, we can compute: a × b is perpendicular to both a and b, which is parallel to c (or opposite). Similarly a × c parallel to -b. Actually in orthonormal basis: suppose a along x, b along y, c along z (but we may rotate). Then a × b = direction of c, a × c = - direction of b. So n = a × b + a × c = c - b (up to orientation). Thus n = c - b (or b - c depending on orientation). Indeed cross product yields vector in the plane spanned by b and c.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192817666,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192818851,"data":"       | Thus n is in the span of b and c (i.e., horizontal components maybe). Its vertical component (z-coordinate) is (c_z - b_z) (assuming orientation). The condition that plane ABDC is perpendicular to horizontal plane (i.e., vertical) means that its normal vector is horizontal, i.e., its vertical component is zero. So we require n_z = 0 =\u003e c_z - b_z = 0 =\u003e b_z = c_z.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192820731,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192821865,"data":"       | Thus the vertical components of b and c must be equal.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192823116,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192824449,"data":"       | Great! So we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.1928256,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192826718,"data":"       | - b_z = c_z = 4 (since sum 8). So each is 4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192827919,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192829093,"data":"       | Now we can solve for side length s using lengths: For each vector, length squared = (horizontal component)^2 + (vertical component)^2 = s^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192830469,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192831591,"data":"       | Thus for a: a_z = 2, so horizontal component magnitude squared = s^2 - 4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192832849,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192833974,"data":"       | For b: b_z = 4, so horizontal component magnitude squared = s^2 - 16.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192835252,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192836358,"data":"       | Similarly for c: same.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192837513,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192838644,"data":"       | Now also b and c are orthogonal to each other. Their dot product zero: b·c = 0. In terms of components: horizontal vectors b_h and c_h (2D vectors) plus vertical components 4 each. Since both have same vertical component, vertical dot product contributes 4*4 = 16. For total dot product zero: b_h·c_h + 16 = 0 =\u003e b_h·c_h = -16.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192840498,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192841627,"data":"       | Moreover, magnitudes: |b_h|^2 = s^2 - 16, same for c_h.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192842842,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192843966,"data":"       | Thus we have two horizontal vectors of equal magnitude L = sqrt(s^2 - 16) and dot product -16.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192845249,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192846364,"data":"       | We can compute condition: dot product = |b_h||c_h| cosθ = (s^2 - 16) cosθ = -16.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192847708,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192848846,"data":"       | Thus cosθ = -16 / (s^2 - 16). Since magnitude squared is non-negative, s^2 - 16 \u003e 0 (s \u003e 4). Also cosθ must be within [-1,1]. This imposes constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192851881,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192853082,"data":"       | Additionally, b_h and c_h must be orthogonal in 3D, but we already accounted for vertical components; we still need them to be orthogonal overall: b·c = 0. That gave dot product condition above.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192854595,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192855738,"data":"       | Now we have unknown s to satisfy that there exist b_h, c_h vectors in 2D with given magnitude and dot product -16. This is possible if absolute value of dot product ≤ product of magnitudes: |-16| ≤ (s^2 - 16). Since product of magnitudes is (s^2 - 16) (since both have same magnitude). So condition: 16 ≤ s^2 - 16 =\u003e s^2 ≥ 32 =\u003e s ≥ sqrt(32) = 4*sqrt(2) ≈ 5.657. Also need cosθ within [-1,1] which is same condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192857914,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192859242,"data":"       | Thus minimal s is sqrt(32). But we also have condition that a, b, c are orthogonal. Already satisfied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192860557,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192861749,"data":"       | Now we also need that a, b, c are orthogonal pairwise. Already we used a·b = 0, a·c = 0? Actually we haven't used a·b = 0 and a·c = 0, but we have a_z =2, b_z=4, c_z=4, and we need a·b=0, a·c=0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19286334,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192864476,"data":"       | Compute a·b = a_h·b_h + a_z * b_z = a_h·b_h + 2*4 = a_h·b_h + 8 = 0 =\u003e a_h·b_h = -8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192865907,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19286702,"data":"       | Similarly a·c: a_h·c_h + 8 = 0 =\u003e a_h·c_h = -8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192868367,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192869516,"data":"       | We also have magnitude of a_h: |a_h|^2 = s^2 - 4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192870719,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192871855,"data":"       | Thus we have unknown horizontal vectors a_h, b_h, c_h in 2D satisfying these dot product constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192873154,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192874284,"data":"       | We can attempt to solve for s using consistency of these equations.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192875576,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19287669,"data":"       | We have three vectors in 2D (since all lie in horizontal plane). They must satisfy:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192877938,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192879073,"data":"       | |a_h|^2 = s^2 - 4\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192880257,"data":"       | |b_h|^2 = |c_h|^2 = s^2 - 16\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192881449,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192882558,"data":"       | a_h·b_h = a_h·c_h = -8\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192883773,"data":"       | b_h·c_h = -16\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192884963,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192886104,"data":"       | We can treat these as Gram matrix for three vectors in 2D. Since we have three vectors in 2D, they are linearly dependent; Gram matrix must have determinant zero. That will give condition to solve for s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192887631,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192888757,"data":"       | Let’s denote vectors in R^2: a, b, c (horizontal components). The Gram matrix G is 3x3 with entries G_ij = v_i·v_j.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192891675,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192892859,"data":"       | We have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19289401,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.1928952,"data":"       | G = [[s^2 - 4, -8, -8],\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192896374,"data":"       |      [-8, s^2 - 16, -16],\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192897584,"data":"       |      [-8, -16, s^2 - 16]]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19289879,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192899913,"data":"       | We require that rank ≤2 (since vectors lie in 2D). So determinant of G must be zero.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192901224,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192902364,"data":"       | Compute determinant as function of s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192903563,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192904711,"data":"       | assistantanalysis to=python codeimport sympy as sp\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192905967,"data":"       | s = sp.symbols('s', positive=True)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192907146,"data":"       | G = sp.Matrix([[s**2 - 4, -8, -8],\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19290832,"data":"       |                [-8, s**2 - 16, -16],\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192909499,"data":"       |                [-8, -16, s**2 - 16]])\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192910668,"data":"       | detG = sp.factor(G.det())\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192911877,"data":"       | detG\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192913005,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192914102,"data":"     [Code]:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192915236,"data":"       | import sympy as sp\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192916385,"data":"       | s = sp.symbols('s', positive=True)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192917583,"data":"       | G = sp.Matrix([[s**2 - 4, -8, -8],\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192919082,"data":"       |                [-8, s**2 - 16, -16],\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192920272,"data":"       |                [-8, -16, s**2 - 16]])\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192921487,"data":"       | detG = sp.factor(G.det())\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19292265,"data":"       | detG\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192923813,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192924926,"data":"     [Output]:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192926046,"data":"       \u003e s**4*(s - 6)*(s + 6)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192927229,"data":"       \u003e \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192928356,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192929429,"data":"     [Turn 3]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192930595,"data":"     [Reasoning] (8709 chars):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192931816,"data":"       | analysisDeterminant = s^4 (s - 6)(s + 6). Since s\u003e0, determinant zero implies s = 6 (or s=0). So s=6 uniquely satisfies the condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192933211,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192934325,"data":"       | Great! So side length s = 6 meters.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192935503,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192936611,"data":"       | Now we have fully determined vectors.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19294262,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192943891,"data":"       | Now we need volume of water below height 7 within cube.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192945156,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192946314,"data":"       | Cube side s=6. Orientation: a has vertical component 2, b and c have vertical components 4 each.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192947642,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192948809,"data":"       | Thus we can define coordinate system with origin at A (0,0,0). Let a = (a_x, a_y, 2). Let b = (b_x, b_y, 4). Let c = (c_x, c_y, 4). They satisfy orthogonality and lengths as above, but we might not need explicit horizontal components to compute volume.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192950408,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192951532,"data":"       | Water height is 7. The cube's vertices heights are:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192952777,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192954706,"data":"       | A: 0\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192955851,"data":"       | B = a: height 2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192957112,"data":"       | C = b + c: height 8 (since b_z + c_z = 8)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192958307,"data":"       | D = a + b + c: height 10.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192959489,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.1929606,"data":"       | Other four vertices (not in rectangle) are:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192961922,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192963023,"data":"       | E = b (height 4)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192964162,"data":"       | F = c (height 4)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192965373,"data":"       | G = a + b (height 2+4=6)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19296672,"data":"       | H = a + c (height 2+4=6)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19296788,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192968978,"data":"       | Let's list all 8 vertices and heights:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192970162,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192971519,"data":"       | - A (0) : 0\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192972645,"data":"       | - B (a): 2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19297378,"data":"       | - E (b): 4\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192974946,"data":"       | - F (c): 4\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192976089,"data":"       | - G (a + b): 6\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192977248,"data":"       | - H (a + c): 6\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192978384,"data":"       | - C (b + c): 8\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192979551,"data":"       | - D (a + b + c): 10\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192980698,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192981836,"data":"       | Check that these heights are consistent with our orientation: yes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192983094,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192984552,"data":"       | Thus water level at 7 will fill all parts of cube with z \u003c= 7. So vertices above 7 are C (8) and D (10). Others are below 7. So the water volume corresponds to the portion of cube below a plane at height 7. Since heights are linear across the cube (the plane of constant z), the intersection shape will be a polyhedron: a truncated cube (a frustum) where top is cut by plane through edges intersecting between vertices where height crosses 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19298804,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192989264,"data":"       | Specifically, the plane z = 7 cuts the cube along a quadrilateral (or maybe a pentagon) formed by intersecting edges whose endpoints straddle height 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192990699,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192991831,"data":"       | Edges with one endpoint below 7 and other above 7 are:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192993062,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192994221,"data":"       | - Edge from C (8) to G (6): endpoints heights 8 and 6 -\u003e crosses 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192995543,"data":"       | - Edge from C (8) to H (6): also crosses 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192996794,"data":"       | - Edge from D (10) to G (6): crosses 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192998009,"data":"       | - Edge from D (10) to H (6): crosses 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.192999211,"data":"       | - Edge from D (10) to B (2): crosses 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193000422,"data":"       | - Edge from C (8) to B (2): crosses 7? Wait B height 2, C 8 -\u003e cross.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193001731,"data":"       | - Edge from D (10) to A (0): crosses.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193002925,"data":"       | - Edge from C (8) to A (0): crosses.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193004116,"data":"       | But we need to consider all 12 edges of cube.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193005319,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193006493,"data":"       | Let's list all 8 vertices with heights and edges adjacency (standard cube). The cube edges are along vectors a, b, c. So each vertex is combination of 0 or a, b, c. Edges connect vertices differing by exactly one of the vectors a, b, c.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193008065,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193009201,"data":"       | Thus we can compute which edges cross height 7: edges where one endpoint height \u003c=7 and other \u003e=7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193010577,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193011701,"data":"       | Let's enumerate using Python and compute volume of region with height ≤7 using integration perhaps by dividing into tetrahedra etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193013129,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193014257,"data":"       | We could compute volume of water as volume of region of cube below plane z=7. Since heights are linear across cube (affine), the region is a convex polyhedron (a truncated cube). Its volume can be computed using formula for volume of a convex polyhedron given coordinates of vertices. Or we can compute volume of whole cube (s^3 = 216) minus volume of top part above plane (i.e., region of cube where z \u003e= 7). Might be easier to compute volume of portion above water (the \"air\") and subtract from total.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193016428,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193017809,"data":"       | But perhaps easier: Since plane cuts the cube, the region above plane is a smaller polyhedron (a tetrahedron-like shape). Could be a smaller cube-like shape.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193019227,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193020368,"data":"       | Given that heights of vertices are linear, the plane z=7 corresponds to equation: For any point x = α a + β b + γ c where α,β,γ ∈ [0,1] (coordinates in unit cube basis), the height z = α a_z + β b_z + γ c_z = 2α + 4β + 4γ. Because vertical component of each vector is known. So height function: h(α,β,γ) = 2α + 4β + 4γ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193024114,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193025298,"data":"       | We need region where h ≤ 7 (water). The unit cube coordinates α,β,γ each between 0 and 1. The region of (α,β,γ) satisfying 2α + 4β + 4γ ≤ 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193026914,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193028062,"data":"       | Now total volume of cube = s^3 = 216. Since the mapping from unit cube coordinates to actual space is linear transformation with determinant equal to volume scaling factor = |det([a,b,c])| = s^3 (since orthogonal vectors of length s; they form an orthogonal basis scaled by s, so determinant = s^3). Indeed because a,b,c are orthogonal of length s, the transformation matrix is s * I after rotation, so determinant = s^3. So volume of region defined by α,β,γ constraints times s^3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193030139,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193031284,"data":"       | Thus volume of water = s^3 * volume in unit cube of region {0≤α,β,γ≤1, 2α + 4β + 4γ ≤ 7}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193032809,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193033947,"data":"       | Thus compute this integral: integrate over unit cube.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193035193,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193036316,"data":"       | Simplify inequality: divide by 1? Not needed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19303752,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193038673,"data":"       | Let’s compute volume region V = ∫_{α=0}^{1} ∫_{β=0}^{1} ∫_{γ=0}^{1} indicator(2α + 4β + 4γ ≤ 7) dγ dβ dα.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193040308,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193041462,"data":"       | Since the inequality involves linear combination, the region is a truncated simplex.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193042738,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193043849,"data":"       | Because maximum of 2α+4β+4γ when α=β=γ=1 is 2+4+4=10\u003e7, so cut.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193045265,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19304638,"data":"       | We can compute volume analytically.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193047584,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193048716,"data":"       | We can treat α as variable; for given α, the inequality becomes 4β + 4γ ≤ 7 - 2α =\u003e β + γ ≤ (7 - 2α)/4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193050286,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193051424,"data":"       | Define t = (7 - 2α)/4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193052622,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193053762,"data":"       | We need t between 0 and 2 (since β+γ max 2). But we also have β,γ ∈ [0,1].\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19305516,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193056412,"data":"       | So for each α, the region in β-γ plane is the set of (β,γ) in [0,1]^2 with β+γ ≤ t.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19305783,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193058951,"data":"       | If t \u003e= 2, then whole square (area 1) is included. But t max occurs at α=0: t = 7/4 = 1.75 \u003c 2. So never full square. For t \u003c=0, region empty. t becomes zero when 7 - 2α =0 =\u003e α = 3.5. But α max is 1, so t always positive. So region nonempty for all α in [0,1].\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193063376,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19306458,"data":"       | Thus for α ∈ [0,1], the area of region in β-γ plane is area of right triangle with legs of length min(1,t) but also limited by 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193066096,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193067225,"data":"       | Since t ≤ 1.75, less than 2, but may be \u003e1 for some α. When t \u003e=1, the region includes the part of the unit square where β+γ ≤ t, which is a shape: a right triangle of area 0.5*t^2 when t ≤1, but when t \u003e1, the region includes whole square except a small triangle where β+γ \u003e t. For t between 1 and 2, the area = 1 - 0.5*(2 - t)^2 (since the excluded region is a triangle with legs (2 - t)). Let's verify.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193069435,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193070553,"data":"       | General formula for area of set {(β,γ)∈[0,1]^2 | β+γ ≤ t}:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193071926,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193073048,"data":"       | If 0 ≤ t ≤ 1: area = t^2/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193074336,"data":"       | If 1 ≤ t ≤ 2: area = 1 - (2 - t)^2/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193075633,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193076744,"data":"       | Since t max is 1.75, we will have two regimes: t ≤ 1 for some α, and t \u003e1 for others.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193078156,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193079277,"data":"       | Find α where t =1 =\u003e (7 - 2α)/4 = 1 =\u003e 7 - 2α = 4 =\u003e 2α = 3 =\u003e α = 1.5. But α max is 1, so t never drops below 1? Wait compute t at α=1: t = (7 - 2)/4 = 5/4 = 1.25. So t always \u003e1 for all α in [0,1] (since at α=0 t=1.75; at α=1 t=1.25). So t is in [1.25,1.75] \u003e1. So area formula for t\u003e1 applies across entire α range.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193081502,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193082605,"data":"       | Thus area for given α is A(α) = 1 - (2 - t)^2 / 2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193083906,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19308502,"data":"       | Compute (2 - t) = 2 - (7 - 2α)/4 = (8 - (7 - 2α))/4 = (8 - 7 + 2α)/4 = (1 + 2α)/4 = (2α +1)/4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193086456,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193087566,"data":"       | Thus (2 - t)^2 = ((2α+1)/4)^2 = ( (2α+1)^2 ) / 16.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193088834,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193089991,"data":"       | Thus A(α) = 1 - 1/2 * ((2α+1)^2 / 16) = 1 - ( (2α+1)^2 ) / 32.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193091311,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193092703,"data":"       | Simplify: A(α) = 1 - ( (4α^2 + 4α +1) ) / 32 = 1 - (4α^2 + 4α +1)/32 = (32 - (4α^2 + 4α +1))/32 = (31 - 4α^2 - 4α)/32.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193094236,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193095389,"data":"       | Thus water volume fraction = ∫_{α=0}^{1} A(α) dα = ∫_0^1 (31 - 4α^2 - 4α)/32 dα.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193096915,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193098009,"data":"       | Compute integral:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193099179,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193100296,"data":"       | Integral of 31/32 over 0 to1 = 31/32.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193102984,"data":"       | Integral of (-4α^2)/32 = - (4/32) * (α^3/3) from 0 to1 = -(4/32)*(1/3) = -(4)/(96) = -1/24.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193104414,"data":"       | Integral of (-4α)/32 = - (4/32)*(α^2/2) from 0 to1 = -(4/32)*(1/2) = -(4)/(64) = -1/16.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193105782,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19310688,"data":"       | Add: volume fraction = 31/32 - 1/24 - 1/16.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193108099,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193109227,"data":"       | Compute common denominator: lcm 96? Actually 32,24,16 =\u003e lcm 96.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193110516,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19311163,"data":"       | 31/32 = (31*3)/96 = 93/96.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193112811,"data":"       | 1/24 = 4/96.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193113967,"data":"       | 1/16 = 6/96.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19311512,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193116247,"data":"       | So volume fraction = (93 - 4 - 6)/96 = 83/96.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193117465,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193118571,"data":"       | Thus water volume = s^3 * (83/96) = 216 * 83 / 96.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193119833,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193120928,"data":"       | Compute: 216/96 simplifies: divide numerator and denominator by 24: 216/24=9, 96/24=4. So 216/96 = 9/4 = 2.25.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193122259,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193123365,"data":"       | Thus volume = (9/4) * 83 = 9*83/4 = 747 /4? Let's compute: 83*9 = 747. So 747/4 = 186.75.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193124683,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193125976,"data":"       | Express as fraction m/n in lowest terms: 747/4, numerator 747, denominator 4, they are relatively prime? 747 divisible by 3? 747=3*249=3*3*83=9*83. Not divisible by 2, so gcd(747,4)=1. So m=747, n=4. m+n = 751.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193127499,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.1931286,"data":"       | Thus answer should be 751.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193129764,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193130913,"data":"       | But need to double-check calculations.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193132107,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193133207,"data":"       | Let's also compute volume directly using Monte Carlo or integration to verify.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193134489,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193135638,"data":"       | assistantanalysis to=python codeimport random, math, itertools, numpy as np, sys, time, math, itertools, collections, fractions, decimal, sys, math\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193137112,"data":"       | # Let's construct explicit vectors a,b,c satisfying constraints with s=6.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193138384,"data":"       | s = 6\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193139592,"data":"       | # Choose horizontal components conveniently.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193140796,"data":"       | # Let a_h = (sqrt(s^2-4), 0) for simplicity.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193142021,"data":"       | import sympy as sp\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193143189,"data":"       | a_h = sp.Matrix([sp.sqrt(s**2-4), 0])\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193144382,"data":"       | # Need b_h and c_h in 2D with magnitude sqrt(s^2-16) and dot product -16, and also\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19314734,"data":"       | # a_h·b_h = -8, a_h·c_h = -8, b_h·c_h = -16.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19314868,"data":"       | # Let's solve for b_h = (x,y). Then c_h must satisfy.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193149965,"data":"       | x,y = sp.symbols('x y', real=True)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193151189,"data":"       | # magnitude condition\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193152372,"data":"       | eq1 = x**2 + y**2 - (s**2 - 16)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19315358,"data":"       | # dot with a_h\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193154762,"data":"       | eq2 = a_h[0]*x + a_h[1]*y + 8  # a_h·b_h = -8 =\u003e a_h·b_h +8 =0\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193156116,"data":"       | eq2 = sp.simplify(a_h[0]*x + a_h[1]*y + 8)  # but a_h[0]=sqrt(32)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193157395,"data":"       | eq2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.193158545,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.19315966,"data":"     [Code]:\n"}
