,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.194141728,"data":"       | Let’s treat a,b unknown positive satisfying a+b=8. Then s^2 = 4 + a^2 + b^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.194143041,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.194144152,"data":"       | Thus s = sqrt(4 + a^2 + b^2). So we can compute s once a (or b) known.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.194145409,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.194146532,"data":"       | But we still have one degree of freedom: a could be any number between? However perhaps further constraints restrict a,b due to the orthogonal vectors having equal length s. Wait we already used equal length via the definition of a = s*γ2, b = s*γ3, with γ2^2+γ^2 = 1? Not exactly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.194148311,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.194149411,"data":"       | We used row orthonormal condition (sum squares of γ's = 1). That gave relation (2/s)^2 + (a/s)^2 + (b/s)^2 = 1. That already encodes equal lengths.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.194150847,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.194151959,"data":"       | Thus we have only two constraints: a+b=8, and (2/s)^2 + (a/s)^2 + (b/s)^2 =1. But s is unknown.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.194153265,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.194154411,"data":"       | This yields infinite solutions (choose a, then compute b=8-a, then s^2 = 4 + a^2 + b^2). Then check that s matches? Let's compute s from that formula; then compute (2/s)^2 + (a/s)^2 + (b/s)^2 = (4 + a^2 + b^2)/s^2 = 1 (by construction). So indeed any a satisfies both equations! Wait we derived s^2 = 4 + a^2 + b^2 from the row condition. So it's identity; thus we have one free parameter a.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.194156251,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.194157357,"data":"       | Thus side length s is not uniquely determined! So perhaps the volume of water is independent of a (i.e., independent of distribution of heights). That would be interesting.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.194158823,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.194159964,"data":"       | Thus we can compute water volume as function of a, but maybe it simplifies to a rational number independent of a.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.194161318,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.194162409,"data":"       | Let's test with symbolic integration using variable a.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.194163613,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.194164714,"data":"       | Given a + b = 8, define a as pz, b = 8 - a.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.194165944,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.194167082,"data":"       | Compute V = ∫_{α=0}^{1} area(T=7-2α) dα, where area defined piecewise.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.194168454,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.194169578,"data":"       | Let's implement symbolic piecewise integration for arbitrary a between 0 and 8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.194170842,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.194172144,"data":"       | But we need to know range of a relative to thresholds (i.e., compare with T values). For each α, T ranges from 7 to 5. So thresholds we need: compare a and b with the T interval [5,7].\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.194174933,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.194176073,"data":"       | Case categories depending on whether a \u003c=5, between 5-7, \u003e7 etc. But a + b = 8, so if a \u003c=5 then b \u003e=3. Actually b = 8-a, so if a \u003c=5 then b \u003e=3. Both a and b could be less than 5? Let's test: a=4, b=4, both 4 \u003c=5. So possible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.194177886,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4140.194179005,"data":"       | If a=1, b=7, a small.\n"}
