,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.43712874,"data":"       | analysisWe need to parse problem: Two noncongruent parallelepipeds each have all faces rhombus with same diagonal lengths sqrt(21) and sqrt(31). So each face is a rhombus with diagonals d1 = sqrt(21), d2 = sqrt(31). For a rhombus, all sides equal; side length s = (1/2) sqrt(d1^2 + d2^2) because diagonals are perpendicular? Wait, rhombus not necessarily with perpendicular diagonals unless it's a kite? Actually rhombus is a parallelogram with equal sides. Diagonals are not necessarily perpendicular; they intersect at right angles only for a rhombus that is a kite? Let's recall: In a rhombus, diagonals are perpendicular iff rhombus is a kite? Actually a rhombus is a kite with equal sides; diagonals are perpendicular if rhombus is a square or a rhombus with 90-degree angle? Wait: In a rhombus, diagonals are perpendicular if rhombus is a kite? Let's think: In a rhombus, diagonals are perpendicular if rhombus is a square? Not necessarily: a rhombus with acute angles can have diagonals that are not perpendicular. Example: rhombus with side length 1 and acute angle 60°, diagonals lengths are 1 and sqrt(3). Are they perpendicular? Let's compute: For a rhombus with side length a and interior angle θ, the diagonals lengths are d1 = a * sqrt(2+2cosθ) = 2a cos(θ/2)?? Actually formula: d1 = a * sqrt(2+2cosθ) = 2a cos(θ/2). d2 = a * sqrt(2-2cosθ) = 2a sin(θ/2). Indeed diagonals are perpendicular only when θ = 90°, i.e., square. So in general diagonals are not perpendicular.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.437159871,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.43716231,"data":"       | Thus we cannot assume perpendicular.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.437173766,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.437174969,"data":"       | Given rhombus with side length s, diagonals lengths p and q satisfy: p^2 + q^2 = 4 s^2 (since diagonals are perpendicular? Wait there is relation: In any rhombus, the sum of squares of diagonals equals sum of squares of all sides (i.e., 4s^2). Actually for any parallelogram, the sum of squares of the sides equals sum of squares of the diagonals: a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2 = p^2 + q^2 (parallelogram law). For a rhombus, all sides equal s, so 4 s^2 = p^2 + q^2. Yes that's correct. So side length s = (1/2) sqrt(p^2 + q^2). So we can compute s: p^2 = 21, q^2 = 31, sum = 52, sqrt = sqrt(52) = 2*sqrt(13). Then s = (1/2) * 2*sqrt(13) = sqrt(13). So side length of each rhombus face is sqrt(13). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.437177463,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.437178569,"data":"       | Thus each edge of the parallelepiped is a vector of length sqrt(13). However, the parallelepiped may have edges not orthogonal; it's a general parallelepiped where each face is a rhombus with side sqrt(13) and diagonals sqrt(21) and sqrt(31). But note that each face is a rhombus; the faces are defined by pairs of edge vectors. For a parallelepiped defined by three vectors a, b, c from a vertex, the faces are parallelograms spanned by (a,b), (a,c), (b,c). For each pair, the parallelogram must be a rhombus with side length sqrt(13) and diagonals sqrt(21) and sqrt(31). So each pair of vectors must have equal lengths sqrt(13) and the angle between them must be such that the resulting parallelogram has those diagonal lengths.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.437181139,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.437183074,"data":"       | Given two vectors u and v of equal length s = sqrt(13). The parallelogram spanned by u and v has sides of length s each, so it's a rhombus. Its diagonals are u+v and u−v. Their lengths squared are |u+v|^2 = |u|^2 + |v|^2 + 2 u·v = 2 s^2 + 2 u·v. Similarly |u−v|^2 = 2 s^2 − 2 u·v. So the two diagonal lengths squared are 2 s^2 ± 2 u·v. Since the diagonals lengths are sqrt(21) and sqrt(31), their squares are 21 and 31. So we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.43718525,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.437186332,"data":"       | 2 s^2 + 2 u·v = 31 (larger) and 2 s^2 − 2 u·v = 21 (smaller). Or vice versa depending which diagonal is longer. Since s^2 = 13, 2 s^2 = 26. So we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.437187926,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.43718896,"data":"       | 26 + 2 u·v = 31 =\u003e u·v = (31-26)/2 = 5/2 = 2.5\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.437190439,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.437191492,"data":"       | And 26 - 2 u·v = 21 =\u003e u·v = (26-21)/2 = 5/2 again. Good consistent. So dot product between any two edge vectors is 5/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.437192974,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.437194059,"data":"       | Thus any pair of edge vectors (a,b), (a,c), (b,c) have dot product 5/2 and each have length sqrt(13). So the three vectors a,b,c are of equal length sqrt(13) and pairwise dot product 5/2. So they form a set of three vectors with equal pairwise angles.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.437195552,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.437196615,"data":"       | Thus the shape is a rhombohedron (parallelepiped with all edges equal and all faces rhombi). Indeed a rhombohedron is defined by three equal-length vectors with equal pairwise angles. So the shape is a rhombohedron.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.437198138,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.437201573,"data":"       | Now there are two noncongruent rhombohedra with these edge length and dot product values? Wait, if we have three vectors of equal length and equal pairwise dot product, the shape is determined up to congruence (i.e., up to orthogonal transformations). But there could be two possibilities: the vectors could be arranged such that they are not all in the same plane (obviously) but there might be two possible values for the angle between them? Actually dot product is fixed: u·v = 5/2. Since |u|=|v|=sqrt(13), the cosine of angle between any pair is (u·v)/(|u||v|) = (5/2)/(13)?? Wait |u||v| = sqrt(13)*sqrt(13) = 13. So cosθ = (5/2)/13 = 5/(26) = 5/26 ≈ 0.1923. So angle θ = arccos(5/26). That's a specific angle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.437204074,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.437205634,"data":"       | Thus the shape is uniquely determined up to congruence? However, there may be two rhombohedra: one with vectors oriented such that they form a \"right-handed\" or \"left-handed\" orientation? But that would be congruent via reflection (mirror) which is considered congruent? Usually congruence includes reflections; shape can be reflected and still congruent. So not distinct.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.437207707,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.437208821,"data":"       | But the problem says there are two noncongruent parallelepipeds with those face rhombus properties. How can that be? Let's think: The condition that each face is a rhombus with given diagonal lengths does not specify which diagonal corresponds to which pair of vectors. For each pair (a,b), the two diagonals are |a+b| and |a−b|. Their lengths are sqrt(21) and sqrt(31). We assumed that the longer diagonal corresponds to a+b (i.e., 31) and the shorter to a−b (21). But perhaps for some faces the longer diagonal could be a−b and the shorter a+b? However, |a+b| \u003e= |a−b|? Not necessarily; depends on angle between a and b. Let's examine: |a+b|^2 = 2s^2 + 2 u·v = 26 + 2 u·v. |a−b|^2 = 26 - 2 u·v. Since u·v is positive (5/2), |a+b| \u003e |a−b|. So the longer diagonal is always a+b. So for any pair of vectors with dot product positive, the longer diagonal is sum. So the assignment is forced.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.437211872,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.437212917,"data":"       | Thus the dot product is determined uniquely as above.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.437214088,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.43721515,"data":"       | But there could be two possible rhombohedra because the three vectors could be arranged such that they are not all mutually at the same angle? Wait we derived that dot product between any pair must be 5/2 to satisfy the face diagonal lengths. However, we used that each face's diagonals are sqrt(21) and sqrt(31). But we didn't consider that maybe the rhombus faces could have diagonals swapped across faces: For each face, the two diagonals are sqrt(21) and sqrt(31). But we didn't specify which diagonal corresponds to which pair of vectors. However, the diagonals of a rhombus are the lengths of the sum and difference of the two edge vectors spanning that face. So for each pair (a,b), we have |a+b| = sqrt(31) and |a−b| = sqrt(21). This yields dot product 5/2. Similarly for (a,c) and (b,c). So all three pairs have same dot product.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.43721767,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.437219622,"data":"       | Thus the shape is uniquely determined (up to congruence) given the edge length sqrt(13) and dot product 5/2. So why two noncongruent? Let's think: Could there be a rhombohedron where the edge vectors have length sqrt(13) but the dot product is -5/2? That would give diagonals lengths sqrt(21) and sqrt(31) but swapped? Let's compute: If dot product negative, then |a+b|^2 = 26 + 2(-5/2) = 26 -5 =21, and |a−b|^2 = 26 +5 =31. So then the longer diagonal would be a−b, not a+b. But still the set of diagonal lengths is {sqrt(21), sqrt(31)}. So the condition \"each face is a rhombus whose diagonals have lengths sqrt(21) and sqrt(31)\" does not specify which diagonal is which. So both possibilities (positive dot product or negative) satisfy the condition. However, the dot product sign determines the angle between vectors: cosθ = ±5/26? Actually if dot product = -5/2, then cosθ = -5/26 ≈ -0.1923, angle \u003e 90°, i.e., obtuse. So there are two possible rhombohedra: one where the angle between edges is acute (cos positive) and one where it's obtuse (cos negative). Are they congruent? No, because you cannot reflect to change the sign of dot product? Actually reflection across a plane can change orientation but preserve dot products? Reflection is an orthogonal transformation with determinant -1, which preserves dot products (since it's an isometry). So dot product sign is preserved under orthogonal transformations (including reflections) because dot product is invariant under orthogonal transformations. So a shape with acute angles cannot be congruent to one with obtuse angles because the angle between edges is invariant. So there are two noncongruent rhombohedra: one acute, one obtuse.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.437226622,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.437227997,"data":"       | Thus we have two possible volumes: V = |a·(b×c)|. Since vectors have equal length and equal pairwise dot products, the volume depends on the triple product, which depends on the angle between vectors. For given lengths and pairwise dot products, the volume magnitude is determined (up to sign) by the Gram determinant: V^2 = det(G) where G is Gram matrix of vectors a,b,c: G = [[a·a, a·b, a·c], [b·a, b·b, b·c], [c·a, c·b, c·c]].\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.437230002,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.437231117,"data":"       | Given a·a = b·b = c·c = 13, and a·b = a·c = b·c = ±5/2 (same sign for all). So Gram matrix is:\n"}
