,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.437654132,"data":"       | Thus each pair of vectors could have angle either α (≈ 79.6°) or π - α (≈ 180° - 79.6° = 100.4°). So there are two possible angles for each pair: acute α or obtuse π - α. Since the parallelepiped is not congruent to another with same side lengths but different angles, we need two noncongruent rhombohedra with same rhombus faces. So we need to consider combinations of angles (α or π - α) for the three pairs (a,b), (b,c), (c,a) that produce distinct shapes (noncongruent). The volume of a rhombohedron with edge length s and pairwise angles α, β, γ is V = s^3 * sqrt(1 + 2 cos α cos β cos γ - cos^2 α - cos^2 β - cos^2 γ). This formula derived from triple scalar product magnitude: V = |a·(b×c)| = s^3 sqrt(1 + 2 cos α cos β cos γ - cos^2 α - cos^2 β - cos^2 γ). Indeed for three vectors of equal length s, the Gram matrix G has diagonal entries s^2, off-diagonal s^2 cos α etc. The determinant of G is s^6 (1 + 2 cos α cos β cos γ - cos^2 α - cos^2 β - cos^2 γ). The squared volume is det(G). So V = s^3 sqrt(det). Good.\n"}
