,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.437996613,"data":"       | Now we need to ensure that both sign patterns correspond to positive definite Gram matrices (i.e., valid vectors). For PD, we need all leading principal minors positive: first order (diagonal) 13\u003e0 satisfied. Second order minors: determinant of any 2x2 submatrix: 13^2 - s_i^2 \u003e0? Let's compute: 13^2 - (5/2)^2 = 169 - 25/4 = (676 - 25)/4 = 651/4 \u003e0. So all 2x2 minors positive regardless of sign because s_i^2 same. So PD condition reduces to overall determinant \u003e0. Since we have det = (7813 + 125 ε)/4. For ε = -1, det = (7813 -125)/4 = (7688)/4 = 1922. For ε = +1, det = (7813 +125)/4 = (7938)/4 = 1984.5? Let's compute: 7813+125=7938. 7938/4 = 1984.5 = 3969/2? Let's compute exactly: 7938/4 simplifies to 3969/2 (since divide numerator and denominator by 2). 3969/2 = 1984.5. That's positive. So both determinants are positive, thus both sign patterns yield valid Gram matrices.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.437999315,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.438000542,"data":"       | Thus there are two possible volumes: sqrt(det). The larger corresponds to ε = +1 (det larger). Ratio of larger to smaller = sqrt(det_plus / det_minus) = sqrt( (7813+125)/(7813-125) ) = sqrt( (7938)/(7688) ) = sqrt( divide both by 2? 7938/2=3969, 7688/2=3844. So ratio = sqrt(3969/3844). Let's compute: 3969 = 63^2 (since 63*63=3969). 3844 = 62^2? Let's check 62^2 = 3844 indeed. So ratio = sqrt( (63^2)/(62^2) ) = 63/62. Nice! So ratio of volumes = 63/62.\n"}
