,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.438015607,"data":"       | The shape is determined by the Gram matrix up to orthogonal transformation (i.e., rotation). Permuting the basis vectors corresponds to permuting rows/columns of Gram matrix, which does not change the multiset of off-diagonal entries but may change which signs correspond to which pairs. However the multiset of signs (i.e., number of + and -) is invariant under permutation. So shapes are classified by number of positive off-diagonal entries (i.e., number of acute angles). The possible counts are 0,1,2,3 positive signs (others negative). But note product ε = (-1)^{#negatives}. So #negatives = number of - signs. So product = +1 when #negatives is even (0,2). So possibilities: #negatives = 0 (all +), #negatives = 2 (two negatives, one positive). Both have product +1, same determinant. Are those shapes congruent? They have different number of acute angles: all three acute vs one acute (two obtuse). Are they congruent? Possibly not. But the problem says there are two noncongruent parallelepipeds. It might be that only two shapes are possible: all acute (i.e., a,b,c all pairwise acute) and all obtuse (i.e., all pairwise obtuse). But we found product -1 corresponds to odd number of negative signs: #negatives = 1 or 3. That gives shapes with one obtuse and two acute, or all three obtuse. Are both possible? Let's test if Gram matrix with product -1 (det smaller) corresponds to a valid configuration (positive definite). We computed determinant positive, so yes. So there are multiple shapes with same volume. However the problem statement says \"Each face of two noncongruent parallelepipeds is a rhombus whose diagonals have lengths sqrt21 and sqrt31.\" It doesn't say there are only two; just that there exist two noncongruent parallelepipeds satisfying condition. So we need ratio of volume of larger to smaller. So we need to consider the maximum possible volume vs minimum possible volume among all such parallelepipeds. The ratio we computed corresponds to product +1 vs -1, i.e., larger volume vs smaller volume. So answer 63/62.\n"}
