ion, regardless of whether diagonals are perpendicular. Wait, but d1 and d2 are not necessarily perpendicular; they intersect at right angles only when rhombus is a kite? Actually for rhombus, diagonals are perpendicular only if rhombus is a square? Let's test: Square has θ=90°, then d1 = d2 = s√2, they intersect at 90°. For rhombus with θ=60°, d1 = 2 s cos30 = 2 s * (√3/2) = s√3, d2 = 2 s sin30 = s. Intersection angle? Not 90°. So diagonals not perpendicular generally. So formula d1^2 + d2^2 = 4 s^2 holds for any rhombus. So given d1, d2, side length s is determined uniquely as sqrt((d1^2 + d2^2)/4). Indeed we computed s = sqrt(13). So each face is a rhombus with side sqrt(13). However, the shape (i.e., angle) is determined by ratio of diagonals: d1/d2 = sqrt(21)/sqrt(31) = sqrt(21/31). That ratio determines the angle: d1 = 2 s cos(θ/2), d2 = 2 s sin(θ/2). So tan(θ/2) = d2/d1 = sqrt(31/21). So interior angle θ = 2 arctan(sqrt(31/21)). So each face rhombus has a fixed angle (θ). So each face is congruent rhombus (same shape). So each parallelepiped has all faces congruent rhombi? Actually a parallelepiped has three pairs of opposite faces, each pair may be a rhombus but not necessarily same shape? The problem says \"Each face of two noncongruent parallelepipeds is a rhombus whose diagonals have lengths sqrt(21) and sqrt(31).\" That could be interpreted that each face (all six) is a rhombus with those diagonal lengths. That implies each face is congruent rhombus (same shape). So the parallelepiped is a rhombohedron (all faces rhombi). Indeed a rhombohedron is a parallelepiped where all faces are rhombi (i.e., all edges equal). That's a special case of a parallelepiped where all edges have equal length (i.e., a parallelepiped generated by three vectors of equal length). So the shape is determined by three vectors of equal length sqrt(13) forming a parallelepiped. The condition that each face is a rhombus with given diagonals ensures that each pair of vectors (edges) have the same angle between them? Let's analyze.\n"}
