,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.438077903,"data":"       | Let vectors a, b, c be three edge vectors emanating from a vertex of the parallelepiped. All edges have length sqrt(13). For each pair of vectors, the face formed is a rhombus with side sqrt(13). The diagonal lengths of that rhombus are given by the lengths of the sum and difference of the two vectors: For a parallelogram defined by vectors u and v, the diagonals are u+v and u−v. Their lengths are |u+v| and |u−v|. In a rhombus, these diagonals correspond to the two diagonals of the rhombus. So for each pair (a,b), (b,c), (c,a), we must have that the lengths of u+v and u−v are sqrt(21) and sqrt(31) (order maybe swapped). So for each pair of vectors, the set { |u+v|, |u−v| } = { sqrt(21), sqrt(31) }.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.438080237,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.438081523,"data":"       | Thus we have three vectors of equal length sqrt(13) such that for each pair, the sum and difference have lengths sqrt(21) and sqrt(31). This defines a rhombohedron. There may be two noncongruent such rhombohedra, presumably corresponding to two possible configurations of the three vectors (maybe one with acute angles all same, the other with some obtuse). Since the condition for each pair is symmetric, there may be two possibilities: the three vectors may be arranged such that the angles between each pair are either acute (θ) or obtuse (π-θ). Because given |u+v| and |u−v|, we can solve for the angle between u and v. Indeed, for vectors of equal length s, we have |u+v|^2 = 2 s^2 + 2 s^2 cos θ = 2 s^2 (1+cosθ) = 4 s^2 cos^2(θ/2). Similarly, |u−v|^2 = 2 s^2 - 2 s^2 cos θ = 2 s^2 (1−cosθ) = 4 s^2 sin^2(θ/2). So the longer diagonal corresponds to the larger of cos^2(θ/2) and sin^2(θ/2). Since cos^2 \u003e sin^2 when θ \u003c π/2 (acute), and opposite for obtuse. So the two diagonal lengths correspond to 2s cos(θ/2) and 2s sin(θ/2). So given d1 = sqrt(21), d2 = sqrt(31). Which is larger? sqrt(31) \u003e sqrt(21). So the larger diagonal is sqrt(31). So we have 2 s cos(θ/2) = sqrt(31) and 2 s sin(θ/2) = sqrt(21) if θ/2 is acute (i.e., θ \u003c π/2). Or the opposite if θ \u003e π/2 (i.e., obtuse), then cos(θ/2) \u003c sin(θ/2) because θ/2 \u003e π/4, so the larger diagonal corresponds to sin term. So we have two possibilities:\n"}
