,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.438167137,"data":"       | analysisYes, θ2 = π - θ1. So the two possibilities for the angle between each pair of vectors are θ (acute) or π - θ (obtuse). So the three vectors can be arranged such that each pair has angle either θ or π - θ. However, we must have consistent configuration: For three vectors, there are three pairwise angles: angle between a and b, between b and c, between c and a. Each must be either θ or π - θ, and they must satisfy the condition that the three vectors are linearly independent (nonzero volume). The shape of the rhombohedron (parallelepiped) will depend on these three angles.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.438171132,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.43817237,"data":"       | We are told there are two noncongruent parallelepipeds meeting the condition. Likely the two correspond to the two possible sets of angles: either all three pairwise angles are acute (θ) or all three are obtuse (π - θ). But would those be congruent? If we invert one vector direction maybe? Let's think.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.438174048,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.438175255,"data":"       | If we take vectors a, b, c all of length s, with pairwise angle θ (all acute). That defines a rhombohedron with all edges equal and all face angles acute. The volume V = |a·(b×c)| = s^3 * sqrt(1 + 2 cosθ^3 - 3 cos^2θ?) Actually volume of a parallelepiped formed by three equal-length vectors with mutual angles α, β, γ (angles between a and b, b and c, c and a) is V = s^3 sqrt(1 + 2 cosα cosβ cosγ - cos^2α - cos^2β - cos^2γ). That's formula for triple product magnitude of three vectors of equal length s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.4381774,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.438178844,"data":"       | If all three pairwise angles are equal (θ), then V = s^3 sqrt(1 + 2 cos^3θ - 3 cos^2θ). That's s^3 sqrt(1 - 3 cos^2θ + 2 cos^3θ). Factor maybe (1 - cosθ)^2 (1 + 2 cosθ)? Let's test: (1 - cosθ)^2 (1 + 2 cosθ) = (1 - 2cosθ + cos^2θ)(1 + 2cosθ) = (1 + 2cosθ - 2cosθ -4cos^2θ + cos^2θ + 2cos^3θ) = 1 - 3cos^2θ + 2cos^3θ. Yes! So V = s^3 sqrt((1 - cosθ)^2 (1 + 2 cosθ)) = s^3 (1 - cosθ) sqrt(1 + 2 cosθ). Since 1 - cosθ \u003e 0 for acute θ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.438181063,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.438182132,"data":"       | If all three angles are obtuse (π - θ), then cos(π - θ) = -cosθ. So we substitute cosα = cosβ = cosγ = -cosθ. Then V = s^3 sqrt(1 + 2 (-cosθ)^3 - 3 (-cosθ)^2) = s^3 sqrt(1 - 2 cos^3θ - 3 cos^2θ). Wait compute: (-cosθ)^3 = -cos^3θ, times 2 gives -2 cos^3θ. (-cosθ)^2 = cos^2θ, times -3 gives -3 cos^2θ. So expression: 1 - 2 cos^3θ - 3 cos^2θ. Let's factor maybe? Let's compute (1 + cosθ)^2 (1 - 2 cosθ)? Actually try (1 + cosθ)^2 (1 - 2 cosθ) = (1 + 2cosθ + cos^2θ)(1 - 2cosθ) = 1 - 2cosθ + 2cosθ -4cos^2θ + cos^2θ -2cos^3θ = 1 -3cos^2θ -2cos^3θ. That's close but sign of cos^3 term is negative, matches -2 cos^3θ, yes. So expression = (1 + cosθ)^2 (1 - 2 cosθ). So V = s^3 sqrt((1 + cosθ)^2 (1 - 2 cosθ)) = s^3 (1 + cosθ) sqrt(1 - 2 cosθ). Since for obtuse angle (θ\u003eπ/2), cosθ negative, 1 - 2 cosθ \u003e 1 \u003e0, okay.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.438185299,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.438186708,"data":"       | Thus there are two possible volumes: V_acute = s^3 (1 - cosθ) sqrt(1 + 2 cosθ). V_obtuse = s^3 (1 + cosθ) sqrt(1 - 2 cosθ). Are these the only possibilities? Could we have a mixture of acute and obtuse angles among the three pairs? Possibly there could be configurations where two angles are acute and one obtuse, etc. But the condition that each face's diagonals are sqrt(21) and sqrt(31) does not distinguish which diagonal is longer for each face. For each pair of vectors, the longer diagonal could correspond to either sum or difference depending on whether angle is acute or obtuse. Since the condition only says each face is a rhombus whose diagonals have lengths sqrt(21) and sqrt(31), not specifying which is which, we could assign either orientation. So each pair of vectors could be either acute (so sum longer) or obtuse (difference longer). So there may be multiple configurations: each pair's angle can be either θ or π - θ, independently. However, the three vectors must be realizable in Euclidean space. The volume formula depends on the cos values of each pair. So we need to find all possible sets of cos values (c1, c2, c3) each equal to either cosθ or -cosθ (since cos(π-θ) = -cosθ). The volume magnitude squared is s^6 * (1 + 2 c1 c2 c3 - c1^2 - c2^2 - c3^2). Since c_i are either c = cosθ or -c. So we can treat each c_i = ε_i c, where ε_i = ±1. Then expression becomes:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.438192435,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.43819355,"data":"       | Δ = 1 + 2 (ε1 ε2 ε3) c^3 - (c^2 + c^2 + c^2) = 1 + 2 ε c^3 - 3 c^2, where ε = ε1 ε2 ε3 (product of signs). Because each c_i^2 = c^2 independent of sign. So the volume depends only on the product of the signs, not the individual signs. So there are two possible volumes: ε = +1 (even number of obtuse angles, product positive) vs ε = -1 (odd number of obtuse angles). Indeed, if we have 0 or 2 obtuse angles (product +1) we get same volume as all acute? Wait, all acute corresponds to all ε_i = +1, product ε = +1. Two obtuse (two signs -1) gives product (+1) as well, same volume. So there are only two distinct volumes: one for ε = +1 (i.e., product of signs positive) and one for ε = -1 (product negative). The problem says there are two noncongruent parallelepipeds meeting condition. That matches: there are two possible volumes: one larger, one smaller. Indeed, the ratio of larger to smaller is asked.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.438196365,"data":"       | \n"}
