,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439042484,"data":"       | Thus the parallelepiped is a rhombohedron: all edges equal length L but not necessarily orthogonal. All faces are rhombi.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439043749,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439044784,"data":"       | Now for each pair (a,b), we also need diagonal lengths: |a+b| and |a−b| are sqrt(21) and sqrt(31) (order unknown). Similarly for (a,c) and (b,c). Since each pair must have same diagonal lengths (since each face same rhombus shape). So the magnitude of sum vector and difference vector for any pair is same set {sqrt(21), sqrt(31)}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439046459,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439047519,"data":"       | Hence we need three vectors a,b,c of equal length L such that for any distinct pair, the squared lengths of sum and difference are 21 and 31 (in some order).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439048814,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439049896,"data":"       | Let’s denote for a,b:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439051002,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439052045,"data":"       | |a+b|^2 = s1, |a−b|^2 = s2, with {s1,s2} = {21,31}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439053223,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439054255,"data":"       | Similarly for a,c: |a+c|^2 = s1', |a−c|^2 = s2' with same set {21,31}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439055433,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439056442,"data":"       | Similarly for b,c.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.43905748,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439059993,"data":"       | We need to find all possible configurations of three unit vectors (scaled by L) meeting these constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439061255,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439062289,"data":"       | Let’s use dot product formulas.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439063394,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439064455,"data":"       | Let a·b = A. Since |a|=|b|=L, we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439065572,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439066635,"data":"       | |a+b|^2 = |a|^2 + |b|^2 + 2a·b = 2L^2 + 2A.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.43906776,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439068806,"data":"       | |a−b|^2 = 2L^2 - 2A.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439069893,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439070973,"data":"       | Thus the two numbers are 2L^2 ± 2A (where + corresponds to sum, - to difference). So set {2L^2 + 2A, 2L^2 - 2A} = {21,31}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439072244,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439073287,"data":"       | Hence we can solve for L^2 and A.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439074372,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439075411,"data":"       | Let’s denote larger value = 31 (since 31\u003e21). So we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.43907659,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.43907765,"data":"       | Either 2L^2 + 2A = 31 and 2L^2 - 2A = 21, leading to solving:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439078872,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439080129,"data":"       | Add: 4L^2 = 52 =\u003e L^2 = 13. Then 2A = 31 - 2L^2 = 31 - 26 = 5 =\u003e A = 2.5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439081382,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439082471,"data":"       | Alternatively, the other assignment: 2L^2 + 2A = 21, 2L^2 - 2A = 31 =\u003e impossible because sum would be less than difference; leads to negative A? Let's compute: Add: 4L^2 = 52 again L^2=13. Then 2A = 21 - 2L^2 = 21 - 26 = -5 =\u003e A = -2.5. So we have two possibilities: A = ±2.5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439084095,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.43908515,"data":"       | Thus for any pair of vectors, the dot product is either +2.5 or -2.5 (with magnitude L^2=13). But note: the sign of A determines which diagonal is longer (sum vs difference). The pair (a,b) may have dot product +2.5 (if sum longer) or -2.5 (if sum shorter). Since we have three pairs, we need to assign dot products among them (each pair can be either +2.5 or -2.5). However all three vectors have equal length and the dot products must be consistent (i.e., they must exist simultaneously). So we need to find triples of vectors of length sqrt(13) with pairwise dot products each being either 2.5 or -2.5, but not all same sign? Let's explore.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439087207,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439088242,"data":"       | Let’s denote the Gram matrix G = [[13, A12, A13], [A12, 13, A23], [A13, A23, 13]]. The off-diagonal entries are each either +2.5 or -2.5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439089511,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439090657,"data":"       | The vectors exist in Euclidean 3D (since we need a 3D parallelepiped). The Gram matrix must be positive semidefinite with rank ≤3; also the volume squared is determinant of Gram matrix (since volume = sqrt(det(G))). Actually volume of parallelepiped formed by vectors a,b,c is sqrt(det(G)). Because determinant of matrix of dot products is squared volume.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439094016,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439095097,"data":"       | Thus we need to compute determinant for each possible pattern of signs (i.e., each off-diagonal ±2.5). Since there are 2^3 = 8 possible sign assignments. However some may be congruent under permutation or overall sign changes? We'll compute volumes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439096613,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.43909769,"data":"       | But also need to ensure that the three vectors are not coplanar (determinant \u003e 0) else volume zero not a 3D shape. So we need patterns where determinant \u003e0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439099089,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439100467,"data":"       | Now, the two noncongruent parallelepipeds correspond to two distinct Gram matrices up to orthogonal transformations, leading to two possible volumes. Likely there are exactly two possible determinants \u003e0 (others produce same determinant or negative). Let's compute.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439101985,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439103001,"data":"       | We'll treat A = ±5/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.43910411,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439105153,"data":"       | Let’s compute determinant for generic signs s12, s13, s23 each = ±1 times 5/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439106395,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.43910746,"data":"       | G = 13*I + (5/2) * S where S has 0 diagonal, off-diagonal entries s_ij.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439108629,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439109674,"data":"       | Specifically, G = [[13, (5/2)s12, (5/2)s13], [(5/2)s12, 13, (5/2)s23], [(5/2)s13, (5/2)s23, 13]].\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439110887,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439111922,"data":"       | Compute determinant as function of signs.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439113015,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439114078,"data":"       | Let's use python to enumerate.assistantanalysis to=python codeimport itertools, sympy as sp\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439115265,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439116259,"data":"       | L2 = 13\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439117326,"data":"       | A = sp.Rational(5,2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.43911842,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439119456,"data":"       | def determinant(signs):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439120559,"data":"       |     s12,s13,s23 = signs\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439121622,"data":"       |     G = sp.Matrix([[L2, A*s12, A*s13],\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439122739,"data":"       |                    [A*s12, L2, A*s23],\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439123842,"data":"       |                    [A*s13, A*s23, L2]])\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.43912493,"data":"       |     return sp.simplify(G.det())\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4194.439126017,"data":"       | \n"}
