,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.936637094,"data":"       | Let’s denote x = cos A. Then tan A = sin A / cos A = sqrt(1 - x^2) / x (since sin positive). Equation: sqrt(1 - x^2) / x = 2 x =\u003e sqrt(1 - x^2) = 2 x^2 =\u003e square both sides: 1 - x^2 = 4 x^4 =\u003e rearr: 4 x^4 + x^2 - 1 = 0. Let y = x^2. Then 4 y^2 + y - 1 = 0. Solve: y = [-1 ± sqrt(1 + 16)]/(8) = [-1 ± sqrt(17)]/8. Since y = x^2 positive less than 1 (cos^2). sqrt(17) ≈ 4.123. So y = (-1 + sqrt(17))/8 ≈ (3.123)/8 ≈ 0.3904. The other root (-1 - sqrt(17))/8 negative, discard. So cos^2 A = y = (sqrt(17)-1)/8. Then cos A = sqrt(y) = sqrt((sqrt(17)-1)/8). Since acute, cos positive.\n"}
