,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.936792181,"data":"       | But we can consider that s and t are roots of x^2 - (s + t) x + 2 = 0. So they satisfy recurrence: For any n ≥ 2, s^n = (s + t) s^{n-1} - 2 s^{n-2}. Similarly for t^n. Adding: s^n + t^n = (s + t)(s^{n-1} + t^{n-1}) - 2 (s^{n-2} + t^{n-2}). So sequence a_n = s^n + t^n satisfies linear recurrence: a_n = (s + t) a_{n-1} - 2 a_{n-2}. However coefficient (s + t) is not rational; it's sqrt(4 + √17). So a_n may not be integer.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.936794118,"data":"       | \n"}
