,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.937336171,"data":"       | Given acute angle A such that tan A = 2 cos A. Find number of positive integers n ≤ 1000 such that sec^n A + tan^n A is a positive integer whose units digit is 9.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.937338299,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.937341682,"data":"       | We need to compute sec A and tan A values based on equation tan A = 2 cos A. Solve for A (acute). Then compute sec A = 1/cos A, tan A = sin A / cos A.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.937343769,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.937345412,"data":"       | Equation: tan A = 2 cos A =\u003e sin A / cos A = 2 cos A =\u003e sin A = 2 cos^2 A.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.93736293,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.937364992,"data":"       | Since sin^2 + cos^2 = 1, we can solve.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.937367,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.937368681,"data":"       | Let c = cos A. Then sin A = 2 c^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.937370408,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.937372045,"data":"       | But sin^2 + cos^2 = 1 =\u003e (2c^2)^2 + c^2 = 1 =\u003e 4c^4 + c^2 - 1 = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.93737403,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.937375625,"data":"       | Let x = c^2. Then 4x^2 + x - 1 = 0 =\u003e quadratic: 4x^2 + x - 1 = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.937377591,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.937379198,"data":"       | Solve: x = [-1 ± sqrt(1 + 16)]/(8) = [-1 ± sqrt(17)]/8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.937381162,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.93738289,"data":"       | Since x = c^2 positive, and A acute =\u003e c \u003e 0 =\u003e c = sqrt(x). Choose positive root.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.937385035,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.937386714,"data":"       | Compute: sqrt(17) ≈ 4.1231. So [-1 + sqrt(17)]/8 = ( -1 + 4.1231)/8 = 3.1231/8 = 0.3904 approx. The other root [-1 - sqrt(17)]/8 negative, discard.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.937388959,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.937390547,"data":"       | Thus c^2 = ( -1 + sqrt(17))/8. So cos A = sqrt( ( -1 + sqrt(17))/8 ). Positive.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.937392442,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.93739419,"data":"       | Now compute tan A = 2 cos A = 2c. Wait from equation tan A = 2 cos A, so tan A = 2c.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.93739607,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.937397835,"data":"       | Thus tan A = 2c. So we have c known; tan A = 2c.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.93739958,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.937402033,"data":"       | Now sec A = 1/c.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.937403701,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.937405443,"data":"       | Thus sec A = 1/c, tan A = 2c.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.937407164,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.937408843,"data":"       | Goal: For integer n≥1, compute S_n = sec^n A + tan^n A = (1/c)^n + (2c)^n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.937410685,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.937412462,"data":"       | We need S_n to be a positive integer with units digit 9. Since c is irrational (involving sqrt(17)), we need expression to be integer for some n due to algebraic conjugates perhaps.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.937414661,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.937416448,"data":"       | Observation: c = sqrt( ( -1 + sqrt(17))/8 ). Let's denote s = sqrt(17). Then c^2 = ( -1 + s)/8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.937418338,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.937421583,"data":"       | Thus c = sqrt( ( -1 + s)/8 ). This is algebraic of degree 4? Actually s = sqrt(17) is degree 2, then c involves sqrt of expression containing s, maybe degree 4. However we might find that (1/c) and (2c) are algebraic conjugates under some field automorphism, leading to integer sums for certain n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.937423221,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.937424253,"data":"       | Let’s compute (1/c) and (2c). Let’s denote a = 1/c, b = 2c. Then a*b = (1/c)*(2c) = 2.\n"}
