,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.938417386,"data":"       | analysisWe need to parse problem: Let A acute angle such that tan A = 2 cos A. Find number of positive integers n ≤ 1000 such that sec^n A + tan^n A is a positive integer whose units digit is 9.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.93841881,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.938419865,"data":"       | We need to find A satisfying tan A = 2 cos A. Solve for A. Then compute expression S_n = sec^n A + tan^n A. Determine for which n (positive integer ≤1000) S_n is integer with unit digit 9.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.938421266,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.938422689,"data":"       | First, find A. Use trig identity: tan A = sin A / cos A. So sin A / cos A = 2 cos A =\u003e sin A = 2 cos^2 A = 2 (1 - sin^2 A)?? Wait cos^2 = 1 - sin^2. So sin A = 2 cos^2 A = 2 (1 - sin^2 A). Rearr: sin A = 2 - 2 sin^2 A =\u003e bring all: 2 sin^2 A + sin A - 2 = 0. Let x = sin A. Then 2x^2 + x - 2 = 0. Solve: discriminant D = 1 + 16 = 17. So x = (-1 ± sqrt(17))/4. Since A acute, sin positive, between 0 and 1. sqrt(17) ≈ 4.123. So (-1 + sqrt17)/4 ≈ (3.123)/4 = 0.7808. That's within (0,1). The other root (-1 - sqrt17)/4 negative. So sin A = (sqrt(17)-1)/4. Cos A = sqrt(1 - sin^2). Could also compute using tan = 2 cos A. Let's compute cos A directly from equation tan = 2 cos. tan = sin/cos = 2 cos =\u003e sin = 2 cos^2. So cos^2 = (sin)/2. But sin = 2 cos^2 =\u003e cos^2 = sin/2 =\u003e sin = 2 cos^2 =\u003e consistent. Use sin = 2 cos^2 =\u003e cos^2 = sin/2. So cos^2 = sin/2. Using sin value we can compute cos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.93842769,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.938429565,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can find cos directly from sin expression: sin = (sqrt17-1)/4. Then cos^2 = sin/2 = (sqrt17-1)/8. So cos = sqrt((sqrt17-1)/8). Since A acute, cos positive.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.938431851,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.938433551,"data":"       | Now compute sec = 1/cos = sqrt(8/(sqrt17-1)). Simplify maybe rationalizing.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.93843544,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.938437128,"data":"       | Let’s compute sec and tan in algebraic forms.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.938439113,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.938440853,"data":"       | We have sin = s = (√17 - 1)/4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.93844273,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.938444518,"data":"       | cos^2 = s/2 = (√17 - 1)/8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.938446353,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.938448041,"data":"       | Thus cos = sqrt((√17 - 1)/8). Let's rationalize: cos = sqrt(√17 - 1)/√8 = sqrt(√17 - 1)/(2√2). Because √8 = 2√2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.938450154,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.938451893,"data":"       | Thus sec = 1/cos = (2√2)/sqrt(√17 - 1). Multiply numerator and denominator rationalizing: sec = (2√2) * sqrt(√17 + 1) / sqrt((√17 - 1)(√17 + 1)) = (2√2) * sqrt(√17 + 1) / sqrt(17 - 1) = (2√2) * sqrt(√17 + 1) / sqrt(16) = (2√2) * sqrt(√17 + 1) / 4 = (√2/2) * sqrt(√17 + 1). Wait compute: (2√2)/4 = √2/2. So sec = (√2/2) * sqrt(√17 + 1) = sqrt( (√2)^2 * (√17+1) )? Not needed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.938454928,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.938456545,"data":"       | Simplify: sec = (√2/2) * sqrt(√17 + 1) = sqrt( ( (√2)^2 (√17+1) ) )/2? Actually (√2/2) * sqrt(√17+1) = sqrt(2) * sqrt(√17+1) /2 = sqrt(2(√17+1))/2 = sqrt(2√17 + 2)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.938458859,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.938460485,"data":"       | Thus sec = sqrt(2√17 + 2)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.938462205,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.938463834,"data":"       | Similarly, tan = 2 cos = 2 * cos = 2 * sqrt((√17 - 1)/8) = sqrt((√17 - 1)/2). Since 2 * sqrt((√17 - 1)/8) = sqrt(4 * (√17 - 1)/8) = sqrt((√17 - 1)/2). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.93846602,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.938467658,"data":"       | Thus tan = sqrt( (√17 - 1)/2 ).\n"}
