,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.939988784,"data":"       | Thus s satisfies quartic: s^4 - s^2 - 4 = 0. Similarly t also satisfies same quartic? Let's test: t^2 = (-1+√17)/2. Then 2 t^2 + 1 = √17. Square: (2 t^2 + 1)^2 = 17 =\u003e 4 t^4 + 4 t^2 + 1 - 17 = 0 =\u003e 4 t^4 + 4 t^2 - 16 = 0 =\u003e divide 4: t^4 + t^2 - 4 = 0. Wait sign differs. Let's compute correctly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.939990465,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.939991759,"data":"       | Take t^2 = (-1+√17)/2. Then 2 t^2 + 1 = √17. Square: (2 t^2 + 1)^2 = 17 =\u003e 4 t^4 + 4 t^2 + 1 - 17 = 0 =\u003e 4 t^4 + 4 t^2 - 16 = 0 =\u003e divide 4: t^4 + t^2 - 4 = 0. So t satisfies t^4 + t^2 - 4 = 0 (note sign of t^2 term is +). Wait s satisfies s^4 - s^2 - 4 = 0 (negative). So s and t are not both roots of same quartic; they satisfy different quartics due to sign.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.939993489,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.939994521,"data":"       | But maybe we can find recurrence for sequence a_n = s^n + t^n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.939995645,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.939996702,"data":"       | Because s and t satisfy s t = 2, we can use recurrence:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.939998558,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940000312,"data":"       | Let a_n = s^n + t^n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940002098,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940003825,"data":"       | We have relation: a_{n+2} = (s^{n+2} + t^{n+2}) = (s^{n} s^2 + t^{n} t^2) = s^2 s^{n} + t^2 t^{n}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940005718,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940007404,"data":"       | But we can express s^2 and t^2 in terms of s + t and product? Not straightforward.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940009435,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940011254,"data":"       | Alternatively, using that s and t are roots of quadratic equation x^2 - (s + t) x + 2 = 0. So each root satisfies x^2 = (s + t) x - 2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940013301,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940014872,"data":"       | Thus for any n ≥ 2, we have s^{n} = (s + t) s^{n-1} - 2 s^{n-2}, similarly for t.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940016985,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940020575,"data":"       | Thus a_n = s^{n} + t^{n} = (s + t)(s^{n-1} + t^{n-1}) - 2 (s^{n-2} + t^{n-2}) = (s + t) a_{n-1} - 2 a_{n-2}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940022548,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940024105,"data":"       | Thus recurrence with coefficient (s + t) which is irrational. However a_n may still be integer because irrational coefficient may cancel due to structure.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940026171,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940027822,"data":"       | But we can also consider that a_n satisfies linear recurrence with integer coefficients of order 4 maybe.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940029745,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940031415,"data":"       | Because s satisfies quartic with integer coefficients: s^4 - s^2 - 4 = 0. Similarly t satisfies t^4 + t^2 - 4 = 0. Not same.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940033401,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940035065,"data":"       | But we can derive recurrence for a_n using characteristic polynomial of s and t as roots of x^2 - (s + t) x + 2 = 0, but coefficient not integer.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940037089,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940038695,"data":"       | Better approach: Since t = 2 / s, we can write a_n = s^n + 2^n s^{-n}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940040525,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940042145,"data":"       | Let’s define u = s^2. Then s = sqrt(u). Then a_n = u^{n/2} + 2^n u^{-n/2}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940044051,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940045692,"data":"       | If n is even, say n = 2k, then a_{2k} = u^{k} + 2^{2k} u^{-k} = u^{k} + 4^{k} u^{-k}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940047581,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940049218,"data":"       | Since u = s^2 = (1+√17)/2, which is of the form α + β √17 with rational α, β.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940051248,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.94005305,"data":"       | Specifically, u = (1+√17)/2 = (1/2) + (√17)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940054895,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940056537,"data":"       | Thus u is an algebraic integer in Q(√17). Its conjugate (replace √17 with -√17) is (1-√17)/2 = - (√17 -1)/2, which is negative.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940058632,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940060261,"data":"       | Now expression u^{k} + 4^{k} u^{-k} may be rational/integer.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940062004,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940063658,"data":"       | Because u * (its conjugate) = ( (1+√17)/2 ) * ( (1-√17)/2 ) = (1 - 17)/4 = -16/4 = -4. Indeed product is -4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.94006543,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.94006709,"data":"       | Thus u^{-1} = -1/4 * conjugate(u). Indeed u^{-1} = - (1/4) * (1-√17)/2? Wait compute: u^{-1} = 1/u = 2/(1+√17). Multiply numerator and denominator by (1-√17): 2(1-√17)/(1-17) = 2(1-√17)/(-16) = -(1-√17)/8 = (√17 -1)/8. Indeed that's positive (since √17 \u003e1). That's consistent with earlier c^2 = x = (√17 -1)/8. Indeed u^{-1} = x = c^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940069847,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940070985,"data":"       | Thus u^{-k} = (u^{-1})^{k} = ( (√17 -1)/8 )^{k}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940072152,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940075618,"data":"       | Now a_{2k} = u^{k} + 4^{k} u^{-k}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940076778,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940077852,"data":"       | But note that 4^{k} u^{-k} = (4 u^{-1})^{k} = (4 * (√17 -1)/8)^{k} = ( (√17 -1)/2 )^{k}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940079112,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940080598,"data":"       | Thus a_{2k} = u^{k} + ( (√17 -1)/2 )^{k}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940082395,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940084244,"data":"       | Now note that u = (1+√17)/2, and the other term is its conjugate? Let's compute conjugate of u: u' = (1-√17)/2 = -(√17 -1)/2. That's negative of (√17 -1)/2. So not same.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940086451,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940087995,"data":"       | But ( (√17 -1)/2 ) is positive, not the conjugate.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940089862,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940091637,"data":"       | Thus a_{2k} = ((1+√17)/2)^k + ((√17 -1)/2)^k.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940093505,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940095235,"data":"       | Now both terms are of the form (a + b √17)^k where a,b rational.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940097207,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940098864,"data":"       | Now maybe sum of these two is integer for all k? Let's test for small k.\n"}
