,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940317592,"data":"       | We have acute angle A (0 \u003c A \u003c π/2) satisfying tan A = 2 cos A. Need to find number of positive integers n ≤ 1000 such that sec^n A + tan^n A is a positive integer whose units digit (i.e., last decimal digit) is 9.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940319928,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940321682,"data":"       | Goal: compute value of sec A and tan A based on equation, then evaluate expression for n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940322949,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.94032396,"data":"       | Let’s solve equation: tan A = 2 cos A.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940326558,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940327625,"data":"       | Recall tan A = sin A / cos A. So sin A / cos A = 2 cos A =\u003e sin A = 2 cos^2 A.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940328862,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940329931,"data":"       | But sin^2 + cos^2 = 1. So (2 cos^2 A)^2 + cos^2 A = 1? Wait sin A = 2 cos^2 A, not squared.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940331485,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940333365,"data":"       | We can use sin A = 2 cos^2 A. Then sin^2 A = 4 cos^4 A.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.94033504,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940336618,"data":"       | But sin^2 A + cos^2 A = 1 =\u003e 4 cos^4 A + cos^2 A = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940339465,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940341074,"data":"       | Let x = cos^2 A. Since A acute, cos A \u003e 0, so cos A = sqrt(x) with x in (0,1). Equation: 4 x^2 + x - 1 = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940343125,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.94034495,"data":"       | Solve quadratic: 4x^2 + x - 1 = 0 =\u003e x = [-1 ± sqrt(1 + 16)]/(8) = [-1 ± sqrt(17)]/8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940353849,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940356282,"data":"       | Since x positive, we take (-1 + sqrt(17))/8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940358171,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940359895,"data":"       | Thus cos^2 A = ( -1 + sqrt(17) )/8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940361645,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940363271,"data":"       | Thus cos A = sqrt( ( -1 + sqrt(17) )/8 ). Positive.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940365005,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940366689,"data":"       | Now tan A = 2 cos A =\u003e tan A = 2 cos A. So tan A = 2 cos A = 2 sqrt( ( -1 + sqrt(17) )/8 ) = sqrt( ( -1 + sqrt(17) )/2 ). Wait compute: 2 * sqrt( ( -1 + sqrt(17) )/8 ) = sqrt(4 * ( -1 + sqrt(17) )/8 ) = sqrt( ( -1 + sqrt(17) )/2 ). Yes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940369062,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940370692,"data":"       | Thus tan A = sqrt( ( -1 + sqrt(17) )/2 ). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940372431,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940374102,"data":"       | Now sec A = 1 / cos A = 1 / sqrt( ( -1 + sqrt(17) )/8 ) = sqrt( 8 / ( -1 + sqrt(17) ) ).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940375935,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940377521,"data":"       | Simplify maybe rationalize.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940379201,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940380884,"data":"       | But we need sec^n A + tan^n A to be integer with units digit 9. Since sec and tan are algebraic numbers of degree 2 (in sqrt(17)), maybe they are conjugates? Let's examine.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940383045,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940384951,"data":"       | Let’s compute the two numbers:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940386738,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940388368,"data":"       | Let c = cos A = sqrt( ( -1 + sqrt(17) )/8 ).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940390108,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940391737,"data":"       | Let t = tan A = 2c = sqrt( ( -1 + sqrt(17) )/2 ).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940393482,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940396998,"data":"       | Let s = sec A = 1/c = sqrt( 8 / ( -1 + sqrt(17) ) ).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940398825,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.94040047,"data":"       | Now note that t = 2c, s = 1/c.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940402159,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.94040379,"data":"       | Thus s = 1/c, t = 2c.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940405455,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940407232,"data":"       | Thus s * t = (1/c) * (2c) = 2. So product s * t = 2. That's nice.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940408892,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.94041044,"data":"       | Also s + t? Not needed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940412017,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940413639,"data":"       | Now we need s^n + t^n integer. Since s and t are algebraic conjugates? Let's check.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940414871,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940416259,"data":"       | Consider the quadratic equation satisfied by s and t.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940417792,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940419421,"data":"       | Let’s find minimal polynomial of s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940421272,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940422879,"data":"       | Since s = 1/c, and c^2 = ( -1 + sqrt(17) )/8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940424602,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940426305,"data":"       | Thus c^2 = (sqrt(17)-1)/8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940428044,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940429785,"data":"       | Thus 1/c^2 = 8/(sqrt(17)-1). Multiply numerator denominator rationalization: Multiply numerator and denominator by (sqrt(17)+1): 8 (sqrt(17)+1) / ( (sqrt(17)-1)(sqrt(17)+1) ) = 8 (sqrt(17)+1) / (17-1) = 8 (sqrt(17)+1)/16 = (sqrt(17)+1)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940432162,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940433722,"data":"       | Thus s^2 = (sqrt(17)+1)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940435579,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940437299,"data":"       | Thus s = sqrt( (sqrt(17)+1)/2 ). Since s positive.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940439051,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940440685,"data":"       | Similarly t^2 = (sqrt(17)-1)/2 (from earlier). Indeed t^2 = (sqrt(17)-1)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940442601,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940444221,"data":"       | Thus s^2 = (sqrt(17)+1)/2, t^2 = (sqrt(17)-1)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940445982,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940447665,"data":"       | Thus s^2 + t^2 = sqrt(17). And s^2 * t^2 = ((sqrt(17)+1)/2)*((sqrt(17)-1)/2) = ( ( (sqrt(17))^2 - 1^2) )/4 = (17 - 1)/4 = 16/4 = 4. So (s t)^2 = 4 =\u003e s t = 2, matches earlier.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940449871,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940451587,"data":"       | Thus s^2 and t^2 are conjugate numbers: they are of the form (sqrt(17) ± 1)/2. So s and t themselves are square roots of those numbers. But s and t are not conjugate directly because they involve sqrt of sqrt(17). However s and t are algebraic numbers of degree 4? Let's see.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940454005,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940455648,"data":"       | Let’s define u = sqrt(17). Then s^2 = (u+1)/2, t^2 = (u-1)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940459345,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940461134,"data":"       | Thus s = sqrt((u+1)/2), t = sqrt((u-1)/2). So each is sqrt of a linear expression in u.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940463031,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940464879,"data":"       | Thus the field Q(u, sqrt((u+1)/2)). Since u = sqrt(17). The extension degree might be 4 (since sqrt((u+1)/2) not in Q(u) generally). But maybe s and t are related: s = sqrt((u+1)/2), t = sqrt((u-1)/2). Indeed s^2 - t^2 = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940467155,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940468841,"data":"       | Now s t = sqrt( ((u+1)/2)*((u-1)/2) ) = sqrt( (u^2 -1)/4 ) = sqrt( (17 -1)/4 ) = sqrt(16/4) = sqrt(4) = 2. Since both positive, s t = 2. Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940470905,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940472575,"data":"       | Thus s and t are positive algebraic numbers satisfying s t = 2 and s^2 - t^2 = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940474412,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.94047612,"data":"       | Now we need s^n + t^n integer. This resembles sequences from recurrence relations for numbers satisfying certain quadratic equations. For instance, if we have two numbers a and b with a + b integer and a b integer, then a^n + b^n integer (by linear recurrence). Here we have product 2 and difference of squares 1, not directly sum integer. Let's compute s + t maybe.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940478788,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940480411,"data":"       | Compute s + t numerically to see if maybe integer? Let's approximate.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940482225,"data":"       | \n"}
