,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940512244,"data":"       | analysiss + t = sqrt(4 + sqrt(17)). Not integer.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940514148,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940515905,"data":"       | But s^n + t^n may be integer due to recurrence with integer coefficients because s and t are roots of quadratic equation? Let's see.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940518154,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940519908,"data":"       | Consider x = s, y = t. They satisfy x y = 2 and x^2 - y^2 = 1. Could we find a quadratic polynomial with integer coefficients that has x and y as roots? Let's attempt.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940522072,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940523794,"data":"       | Let’s compute sum: x + y = sqrt(4 + sqrt(17)). Not rational.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940525638,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940527258,"data":"       | Product: xy = 2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940528912,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940530575,"data":"       | Thus x and y are roots of equation z^2 - (x + y) z + 2 = 0. Coefficients not rational because sum not rational. So not a quadratic with rational coefficients.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940532652,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940534277,"data":"       | But maybe we can consider the numbers s and t as conjugates under automorphism that sends sqrt(17) to -sqrt(17). Let's see.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940536302,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940537935,"data":"       | If we replace sqrt(17) by -sqrt(17), then s^2 = ( -sqrt(17) + 1)/2 which is t^2? Wait t^2 = (sqrt(17)-1)/2. Replace sqrt(17) by -sqrt(17): ( -sqrt(17) + 1)/2 = (1 - sqrt(17))/2 = -(sqrt(17)-1)/2 = -t^2. Not same.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940540201,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940541847,"data":"       | But maybe s and t are related by mapping sqrt(17) to -sqrt(17) and also taking square root of negative? Not straightforward.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940543879,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940545529,"data":"       | Nevertheless we can compute s^n + t^n directly using recurrence derived from s and t's algebraic relations.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940547474,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940549092,"data":"       | We have s t = 2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940550824,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940552459,"data":"       | Also s^2 - t^2 = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940554089,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940555762,"data":"       | We can attempt to derive recurrence for sequence a_n = s^n + t^n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940557564,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940559219,"data":"       | Since s and t are roots of equation z^2 - (s + t)z + 2 = 0, but coefficient not rational. However we can find recurrence with integer coefficients using the fact that s and t satisfy a quadratic with integer coefficients after squaring maybe.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940561495,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940563139,"data":"       | Let’s consider u_n = s^{2n} + t^{2n} maybe integer? Because s^2 and t^2 are conjugates (they are (sqrt(17) ± 1)/2). Those are algebraic numbers of degree 2 (quadratic) with integer coefficients. Indeed let α = s^2 = (sqrt(17)+1)/2, β = t^2 = (sqrt(17)-1)/2. Then α + β = sqrt(17), α β = 4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940569668,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940571372,"data":"       | Thus α and β are roots of X^2 - (α+β) X + αβ = 0 =\u003e X^2 - sqrt(17) X + 4 = 0. Not rational coefficients.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940573165,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940574438,"data":"       | But α and β are conjugates under sqrt(17) -\u003e -sqrt(17) (since α = (u+1)/2, β = (u-1)/2). Indeed swapping sign changes α to ( -u + 1)/2 which is not β, but maybe β = (u-1)/2. Under u -\u003e -u, α becomes ( -u + 1)/2 = (1 - u)/2 = - (u-1)/2 = -β. So they are not conjugates but up to sign.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940576207,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940577397,"data":"       | But note that α and -β are conjugates. Hmm.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940578539,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940579572,"data":"       | But perhaps s^n + t^n integer for all n? Let's test numerically for n up to some values.\n"}
