,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940759664,"data":"       | We need to find recurrence for a_k to compute modulo 10 maybe.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940760885,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940761912,"data":"       | Observation: Since s t = 2, we can define b_n = s^{n} + t^{n}. Then we have recurrence: b_{n} = (s + t) b_{n-1} - (s t) b_{n-2} = (s + t) b_{n-1} - 2 b_{n-2}. But (s + t) is irrational, not integer. However for n multiple of 2 maybe we can get integer recurrence with integer coefficients.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940763451,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940764478,"data":"       | Alternatively, consider c_n = s^{2n} + t^{2n} = (s^2)^n + (t^2)^n. Since s^2 and t^2 are α = (sqrt(17)+1)/2, β = (sqrt(17)-1)/2. Then c_n = α^n + β^n. α + β = sqrt(17), α β = 4. So c_n satisfies recurrence: c_{n} = (α + β) c_{n-1} - (α β) c_{n-2} = sqrt(17) c_{n-1} - 4 c_{n-2}. Not integer coefficients.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940766255,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940767285,"data":"       | But we can consider d_n = c_{2n} maybe integer? Wait c_n for n even gave integer? Let's test.\n"}
