,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940776434,"data":"       | Since c_n = α^n + β^n with αβ = 4, α + β = sqrt(17). Define e_n = α^{2n} + β^{2n}. Since (α^2) and (β^2) maybe have integer coefficients? Let's compute α^2 and β^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940777981,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940790887,"data":"       | α = (sqrt(17)+1)/2, β = (sqrt(17)-1)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940792025,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940793042,"data":"       | Compute α^2 = ( (sqrt(17)+1)^2 )/4 = (17 + 2 sqrt(17) + 1)/4 = (18 + 2 sqrt(17))/4 = (9 + sqrt(17))/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940794308,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940795452,"data":"       | Similarly β^2 = ( (sqrt(17)-1)^2 )/4 = (17 - 2 sqrt(17) + 1)/4 = (18 - 2 sqrt(17))/4 = (9 - sqrt(17))/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940798145,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940799209,"data":"       | Thus α^2 + β^2 = (9 + sqrt(17))/2 + (9 - sqrt(17))/2 = 9.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940800397,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940801425,"data":"       | And α^2 * β^2 = ((9)^2 - (sqrt(17))^2)/4? Actually compute directly: α^2 * β^2 = (α β)^2 = 4^2 = 16. Wait α β = 4, so square =\u003e 16. Indeed α^2 β^2 = 16.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940802907,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940803921,"data":"       | Thus α^2 and β^2 are roots of quadratic X^2 - (α^2 + β^2) X + α^2 β^2 = X^2 - 9 X + 16 = 0. That's integer coefficients! Great.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940805312,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.940806329,"data":"       | Thus we have that for m ≥ 2, the sequence a_m = α^{2m} + β^{2m} = (α^2)^m + (β^2)^m satisfies recurrence:\n"}
