,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.941228453,"data":"       | But s and s' are conjugates under sqrt(17) -\u003e -sqrt(17)? Let's compute s' in terms of sqrt(17): s' = (sqrt(17)-1)/2. If we replace sqrt(17) by -sqrt(17), we get (-sqrt(17)-1)/2 = -(sqrt(17)+1)/2 = -s. That's not s'. So not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.941230026,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4266.941231136,"data":"       | Nevertheless s and s' are both algebraic numbers with quadratic field Q(sqrt(17)). Their sum s + s' = sqrt(17). Wait compute: s + s' = (sqrt(17)+1)/2 + (sqrt(17)-1)/2 = (2 sqrt(17))/2 = sqrt(17). So s + s' = sqrt(17). Their product s * s' = ((sqrt(17)+1)(sqrt(17)-1))/4 = (17 -1)/4 = 16/4 = 4. Indeed s*s' =4.\n"}
