,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.28923161,"data":"     [Reasoning] (9912 chars):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289233071,"data":"       | analysisWe need to solve geometry problem: equilateral triangle side 55. Points D on BC with BD=7 (so from B towards C). So BC length 55, BD=7, so CD = 55-7 = 48. Similarly, E on CA with CE=30 (from C towards A). So CA length 55, CE=30, so AE = 55-30 = 25. F on AB with AF=40 (from A towards B). So AB length 55, AF=40, so FB = 55-40 = 15.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289236011,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289237217,"data":"       | We have point P inside triangle such that angle AEP = angle BFP = angle CDP. Denote common angle θ = ∠AEP = ∠BFP = ∠CDP. Need tan^2 θ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289239295,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289240463,"data":"       | We have geometry with points on sides, and lines from vertices? Actually angles defined at points E, F, D with lines to A, B, C and P. For example ∠AEP is angle between EA and EP at point E. Similarly ∠BFP is angle between FB and FP at point F. ∠CDP is angle between DC and DP at point D.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289242205,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289245233,"data":"       | We need to find point P inside such that those three angles equal. This reminiscent of isogonal conjugate or something: maybe P is the Brocard point? Indeed, in any triangle, there exists a point (Brocard point) such that ∠PAB = ∠PBC = ∠PCA = ω (Brocard angle). But here the angles are at points on sides, not at vertices. However there is also concept of \"Brocard points\" with property that lines from vertices to points on opposite sides produce equal angles? Wait typical Brocard point: For triangle ABC, there is point Ω inside such that ∠ΩAB = ∠ΩBC = ∠ΩCA = ω (Brocard angle). Equivalent to ∠BAΩ = ∠CBΩ = ∠ACΩ = ω (maybe). But our condition is different: angle at point on side between side segment and line to P equals same for each side.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289249024,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289250205,"data":"       | Consider point D on BC, angle CDP is angle between CD (segment from C to D along side BC) and DP. Similarly at E, angle AEP between EA (segment from A to E along side CA) and EP. At F, angle BFP between FB (segment from B to F along side AB) and FP.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289254585,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289255757,"data":"       | Thus each angle is measured between the segment from the vertex opposite the side to the point on that side (i.e., from vertex to point on side) and the line from that point to P.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289257211,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289258303,"data":"       | We have side lengths and distances along sides. This may be solved using trigonometric Ceva or law of sines in triangles.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289259623,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289260751,"data":"       | Let’s define coordinates: Place equilateral triangle ABC with convenient coordinates: Let A = (0,0), B = (55,0), C = (55/2, 55*sqrt(3)/2). Actually equilateral side length s = 55. Coordinates: A (0,0), B (s,0), C (s/2, s*sqrt(3)/2). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289262345,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289263496,"data":"       | Then compute points:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289264729,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289265887,"data":"       | - F on AB with AF = 40 from A towards B, so F = A + (AF/AB)*(B-A) = (40,0) (since AB along x-axis). So F = (40,0). Then FB = 15.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289267313,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289268559,"data":"       | - D on BC with BD = 7 from B towards C. Parameter t = BD/BC = 7/55. So D = B + t*(C-B). B = (55,0). C-B = (-55/2, s*sqrt(3)/2). Actually C = (27.5, 55*sqrt(3)/2). So C-B = (-27.5, 55*sqrt(3)/2). Multiply by 7/55 = 7/55. So D = (55 - 27.5*7/55, 0 + (55*sqrt(3)/2)*(7/55)). Compute: 27.5*7/55 = (27.5*7)/55 = (27.5/55)*7 = 0.5*7 = 3.5. So x-coordinate: 55 - 3.5 = 51.5. y-coordinate: (55*sqrt(3)/2)*(7/55) = (sqrt(3)/2)*7 = 7*sqrt(3)/2 ≈ 6.062... So D = (51.5, 7*sqrt(3)/2).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289270925,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289272533,"data":"       | - E on CA with CE = 30 from C towards A. Parameter from C to A: length CA = 55. So moving from C towards A by distance 30. So t = CE/CA = 30/55 = 6/11 ≈ 0.54545. Vector from C to A = A - C = (-27.5, -55*sqrt(3)/2). Multiply by t: (-27.5*t, -55*sqrt(3)/2 * t). Then E = C + t*(A-C) = C + t*(A-C) = (27.5 - 27.5*t, 55*sqrt(3)/2 - (55*sqrt(3)/2)*t) = (27.5*(1 - t), (55*sqrt(3)/2)*(1 - t)). Since t = 30/55 = 6/11, 1 - t = 5/11. So E = (27.5*5/11, (55*sqrt(3)/2)*5/11). Compute: 27.5*5/11 = (27.5/11)*5 = 2.5*5 = 12.5. Indeed 27.5/11 = 2.5. So x = 12.5. y = (55*sqrt(3)/2)*(5/11) = (55/11)*(5/2)*sqrt(3) = 5*5/2*sqrt(3) = 25/2*sqrt(3) = 12.5*sqrt(3). Wait compute: 55/11 = 5. So (5)*(5/2)*sqrt(3) = 25/2*sqrt(3) = 12.5*sqrt(3). So E = (12.5, 12.5*sqrt(3)). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289275033,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289276309,"data":"       | Thus coordinates: A (0,0), B (55,0), C (27.5, 27.5*sqrt(3)). Actually C y-coordinate is s*sqrt(3)/2 = 55*sqrt(3)/2 = 27.5*sqrt(3). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289277907,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289279225,"data":"       | Now D (51.5, 7*sqrt(3)/2). Compute 7*sqrt(3)/2 = 3.5*sqrt(3). So D = (51.5, 3.5*sqrt(3)). F = (40,0). E = (12.5, 12.5*sqrt(3)). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289280562,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289281768,"data":"       | Now we need point P = (x,y) inside triangle such that angle between vector EA and EP equals angle between FB and FP equals angle between DC and DP.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289285125,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289286362,"data":"       | Define vectors:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.28928757,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289289067,"data":"       | - At E: vector EA = A - E = (0-12.5, 0-12.5*sqrt(3)) = (-12.5, -12.5*sqrt(3)). Vector EP = P - E = (x-12.5, y-12.5*sqrt(3)). Angle between EA and EP: compute using dot product: cos θ = (EA·EP) / (|EA||EP|). Similarly at F: vector FB = B - F = (55-40, 0-0) = (15,0). Vector FP = P - F = (x-40, y-0) = (x-40, y). Angle between FB and FP: cos θ = (FB·FP) / (|FB||FP|). At D: vector DC = C - D = (27.5-51.5, 27.5*sqrt(3) - 3.5*sqrt(3)) = (-24, (27.5-3.5)*sqrt(3) = 24*sqrt(3)). Wait compute: 27.5 - 3.5 = 24. So DC = (-24, 24*sqrt(3)). Vector DP = P - D = (x-51.5, y-3.5*sqrt(3)). Angle between DC and DP: cos θ = (DC·DP) / (|DC||DP|).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289291458,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289292977,"data":"       | Thus we have three equations: cosθ equal for each pair. Actually we need equality of angles, not necessarily cos values sign? Since angles are acute? P inside triangle, likely each angle is acute. So we can equate cos values.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289294567,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289295801,"data":"       | Thus we have two independent equations: angle at E equals angle at F, and angle at F equals angle at D. Solve for (x,y). Then compute tan^2 θ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289297189,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289298373,"data":"       | But perhaps there is a known formula: For any triangle with points on sides at distances a,b,c from vertices, there is a unique point P such that these equal angles; maybe P is the isogonal conjugate of the symmedian point? Not sure.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289299918,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289301158,"data":"       | Let's attempt to solve using algebraic equations.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289302366,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289303548,"data":"       | Let’s denote:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289304713,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289305929,"data":"       | EA = vector vE = (-12.5, -12.5*sqrt(3)). FB = vF = (15,0). DC = vD = (-24, 24*sqrt(3)).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289307257,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289308374,"data":"       | Let’s compute lengths:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289309648,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.28931081,"data":"       | |vE| = sqrt(12.5^2 + (12.5*sqrt(3))^2) = 12.5*sqrt(1 + 3) = 12.5*2 = 25. Indeed because triangle side length 55, AE = 25, and EA is along side CA direction; length AE = 25, consistent.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289312245,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289313333,"data":"       | |vF| = sqrt(15^2 + 0) = 15 = FB length.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289314523,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289315605,"data":"       | |vD| = sqrt((-24)^2 + (24*sqrt(3))^2) = 24*sqrt(1 + 3) = 24*2 = 48 = CD length (makes sense). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289316929,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289318012,"data":"       | Thus each vector magnitude equals the side segment length from vertex to point.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289319255,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289320391,"data":"       | Now we need angle between each vector and line to P.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289323636,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289324787,"data":"       | Let’s define unknown P = (x,y). Then we can compute cosθ for each:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289326117,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289327189,"data":"       | cosθ_E = (vE·(P-E)) / (|vE| * |P-E|)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289328457,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289329559,"data":"       | cosθ_F = (vF·(P-F)) / (|vF| * |P-F|)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289330692,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289331777,"data":"       | cosθ_D = (vD·(P-D)) / (|vD| * |P-D|)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289332929,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289334033,"data":"       | Set cosθ_E = cosθ_F = cosθ_D.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289335266,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289336334,"data":"       | We can set two equations: cosθ_E = cosθ_F and cosθ_F = cosθ_D.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.28933764,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289338715,"data":"       | But these are rational equations with square roots due to lengths.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289339937,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289341392,"data":"       | Alternatively we could use tangent of angle: tanθ = |v × (P - point)| / (v·(P - point)). Because for two vectors a,b, angle between them: tanθ = |a×b| / (a·b). In 2D, cross product magnitude = |a_x b_y - a_y b_x|. Since we are dealing with interior point, orientation may be consistent.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289343221,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289344313,"data":"       | Thus equality of angles implies ratio of cross to dot is same for each pair. So we can set:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289345607,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289346675,"data":"       | |vE × (P-E)| / (vE·(P-E)) = |vF × (P-F)| / (vF·(P-F)) = |vD × (P-D)| / (vD·(P-D))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289347986,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289364487,"data":"       | Since all angles are acute, dot product positive? Actually angle between side segment (from vertex to point) and line to P inside triangle likely acute (\u003c 90). So dot product positive. Cross product magnitude positive (since orientation maybe same). So we can drop absolute values.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289366317,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289367564,"data":"       | Thus we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289368759,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289369877,"data":"       | (vE × (P-E)) / (vE·(P-E)) = (vF × (P-F)) / (vF·(P-F)) = (vD × (P-D)) / (vD·(P-D))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289371221,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289372299,"data":"       | Let’s denote k = tanθ (positive). Then we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289373691,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289374766,"data":"       | vE × (P-E) = k * (vE·(P-E))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.28937601,"data":"       | vF × (P-F) = k * (vF·(P-F))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289377143,"data":"       | vD × (P-D) = k * (vD·(P-D))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289378339,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289379447,"data":"       | Thus we have three equations linear in x,y and k? Actually cross product and dot product are linear in x,y (since vE is constant). Let's compute.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289382711,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289383834,"data":"       | In 2D, for vectors a = (a1,a2), b = (b1,b2), cross product magnitude (signed) = a1*b2 - a2*b1. Dot = a1*b1 + a2*b2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289385243,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289386367,"data":"       | Thus for each point we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289387526,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289388669,"data":"       | Let’s compute for E:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289389877,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289390947,"data":"       | vE = (-12.5, -12.5*sqrt(3)). Let’s denote s = sqrt(3). So vE = (-12.5, -12.5 s). P-E = (x-12.5, y-12.5 s).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289392357,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289393437,"data":"       | Compute dot_E = vE·(P-E) = (-12.5)*(x-12.5) + (-12.5 s)*(y-12.5 s) = -12.5(x-12.5) -12.5 s (y-12.5 s).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289394782,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289395861,"data":"       | Simplify: -12.5(x-12.5) -12.5 s y +12.5 s *12.5 s = -12.5(x-12.5) -12.5 s y +12.5*12.5 * s^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289397177,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289398313,"data":"       | Since s^2 = 3. So term = 12.5*12.5*3 = (12.5^2)*3 = (156.25)*3 = 468.75.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289399551,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289400629,"data":"       | Thus dot_E = -12.5(x-12.5) -12.5 s y + 468.75.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289401855,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289402958,"data":"       | But we can compute more elegantly using python.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289404125,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289405258,"data":"       | Cross_E = vE_x*(P-E)_y - vE_y*(P-E)_x = (-12.5)*(y-12.5 s) - (-12.5 s)*(x-12.5) = -12.5(y-12.5 s) + 12.5 s (x-12.5) = 12.5 s (x-12.5) -12.5(y-12.5 s).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289406675,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289408073,"data":"       | Simplify: 12.5 s (x-12.5) -12.5 y +12.5*12.5 s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289409283,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289410347,"data":"       | = 12.5 s (x-12.5) -12.5 y + 156.25 s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289411513,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289412613,"data":"       | Now equation: Cross_E = k * dot_E.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289413798,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289415009,"data":"       | Similarly for F:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289416123,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289417317,"data":"       | vF = (15,0). P-F = (x-40, y).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289418464,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289419702,"data":"       | dot_F = 15*(x-40) + 0*y = 15(x-40).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289420874,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289422105,"data":"       | cross_F = vF_x*(P-F)_y - vF_y*(P-F)_x = 15*y - 0*(x-40) = 15 y.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289423393,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.28942455,"data":"       | Thus equation: 15 y = k * 15 (x-40) =\u003e y = k (x-40). So we have linear relation between x,y,k.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289427786,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289429024,"data":"       | Similarly for D:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289430193,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289431396,"data":"       | vD = (-24, 24 s). P-D = (x-51.5, y-3.5 s).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289432591,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.28943382,"data":"       | dot_D = (-24)*(x-51.5) + (24 s)*(y-3.5 s) = -24(x-51.5) + 24 s y - 24 s * 3.5 s = -24(x-51.5) + 24 s y - 24*3.5 * s^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289435143,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289436351,"data":"       | Since s^2=3, term = -24*3.5*3 = -24*10.5 = -252. So dot_D = -24(x-51.5) + 24 s y - 252.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289437651,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289438911,"data":"       | Cross_D = vD_x*(P-D)_y - vD_y*(P-D)_x = (-24)*(y-3.5 s) - (24 s)*(x-51.5) = -24 y + 24*3.5 s - 24 s x + 24 s *51.5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289440227,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.28944134,"data":"       | Simplify: -24 y + 84 s - 24 s x + 24*51.5 s = -24 y - 24 s x + (84 + 24*51.5) s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289442596,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289443656,"data":"       | Compute 24*51.5 = 1236? Let's compute: 51.5*24 = (50*24)+(1.5*24)=1200+36=1236. So (84+1236) s = 1320 s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289445001,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289446067,"data":"       | Thus cross_D = -24 y - 24 s x + 1320 s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289447256,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289448405,"data":"       | Equation: cross_D = k * dot_D.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289449567,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289450717,"data":"       | Thus we have three equations:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289451844,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289452921,"data":"       | (1) cross_E = k * dot_E.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289454035,"data":"       | (2) y = k (x-40).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289455173,"data":"       | (3) cross_D = k * dot_D.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289456297,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289457369,"data":"       | We have unknowns x,y,k. Solve.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289458543,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289461545,"data":"       | Let's compute using sympy.assistantanalysis to=python codeimport sympy as sp\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289463018,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289464108,"data":"       | # define symbols\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289465343,"data":"       | x, y, k = sp.symbols('x y k', real=True)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289466504,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289467711,"data":"       | s = sp.sqrt(3)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289468882,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289470112,"data":"       | # define equations\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289471263,"data":"       | # Equation from F: y = k*(x-40)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289472559,"data":"       | eqF = sp.Eq(y, k*(x-40))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289473659,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289476542,"data":"       | # For E:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.28947768,"data":"       | dotE = -12.5*(x-12.5) - 12.5*s*y + 468.75\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.28947904,"data":"       | crossE = 12.5*s*(x-12.5) - 12.5*y + 156.25*s\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289480245,"data":"       | eqE = sp.Eq(crossE, k*dotE)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.2894815,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289482561,"data":"       | # For D:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289483971,"data":"       | dotD = -24*(x-51.5) + 24*s*y - 252\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289485265,"data":"       | crossD = -24*y - 24*s*x + 1320*s\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289486404,"data":"       | eqD = sp.Eq(crossD, k*dotD)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289487569,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289488653,"data":"       | eqF, eqE, eqD\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289489861,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289491849,"data":"     [Code]:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289493045,"data":"       | import sympy as sp\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.28949418,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289495364,"data":"       | # define symbols\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289496577,"data":"       | x, y, k = sp.symbols('x y k', real=True)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289497891,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289499109,"data":"       | s = sp.sqrt(3)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289500354,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289501518,"data":"       | # define equations\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289502617,"data":"       | # Equation from F: y = k*(x-40)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289503947,"data":"       | eqF = sp.Eq(y, k*(x-40))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.28950506,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289506215,"data":"       | # For E:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289507303,"data":"       | dotE = -12.5*(x-12.5) - 12.5*s*y + 468.75\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.28950844,"data":"       | crossE = 12.5*s*(x-12.5) - 12.5*y + 156.25*s\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289509601,"data":"       | eqE = sp.Eq(crossE, k*dotE)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289510741,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289511811,"data":"       | # For D:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289512886,"data":"       | dotD = -24*(x-51.5) + 24*s*y - 252\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289514045,"data":"       | crossD = -24*y - 24*s*x + 1320*s\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289515169,"data":"       | eqD = sp.Eq(crossD, k*dotD)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.28951628,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289517342,"data":"       | eqF, eqE, eqD\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289518457,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289519505,"data":"     [Output]:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289520668,"data":"       \u003e Eq(y, k*(x - 40)) Eq(-12.5*y + 12.5*sqrt(3)*(x - 12.5) + 156.25*sqrt(3), k*(-12.5*x - 12.5*sqrt(3)*y + 625.0)) Eq(-24*sqrt(3)*x - 24*y + 1320*sqrt(3), k*(-24*x + 24*sqrt(3)*y + 984.0))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289522149,"data":"       \u003e \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289523279,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289524343,"data":"     [Turn 2]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289527708,"data":"     [Reasoning] (1790 chars):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.28952897,"data":"       | analysisNote: dotE expression simplified: -12.5*x -12.5*sqrt(3)*y + 625? Let's verify: earlier we had dot_E = -12.5(x-12.5) -12.5 s y + 468.75. Expand: -12.5 x + 12.5*12.5 -12.5 s y + 468.75 = -12.5 x + 156.25 -12.5 s y + 468.75 = -12.5 x -12.5 s y + 625. Indeed 156.25+468.75=625. Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289530704,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289531951,"data":"       | Cross_E expression: 12.5 s (x-12.5) -12.5 y + 156.25 s = 12.5 s x - 12.5 s*12.5 -12.5 y + 156.25 s = 12.5 s x - 156.25 s -12.5 y + 156.25 s = 12.5 s x -12.5 y. Wait the -156.25 s + 156.25 s cancel! So cross_E simplifies to 12.5 s x -12.5 y. Indeed cross_E = 12.5 (s x - y). Let's verify: cross_E = 12.5*sqrt(3)*x - 12.5*y. Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289533641,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289534757,"data":"       | Thus eqE simplifies to: 12.5 (s x - y) = k*(-12.5 x -12.5 s y + 625). Divide both sides by 12.5: (s x - y) = k*(-x - s y + 50). Since 625/12.5 = 50.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289536185,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289537289,"data":"       | Thus eqE: s*x - y = k*(-x - s*y + 50). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289538479,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289539893,"data":"       | Similarly for D: cross_D = -24*y -24 s x + 1320 s = -24 (y + s x) + 1320 s. dot_D = -24*(x-51.5) + 24 s y - 252 = -24 x + 24*51.5 + 24 s y - 252 = -24 x + 1236 + 24 s y - 252 = -24 x + 24 s y + 984. Indeed 1236-252=984. So dot_D = -24 x + 24 s y + 984.\n"}
