,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289775165,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289776504,"data":"       | Now we have points A (0,0), B (55,0), C (27.5, 55*sqrt(3)/2) = (27.5, (55*sqrt(3))/2) = (27.5, 27.5 sqrt(3)). Indeed because 55/2 =27.5. So C = (27.5, 27.5 sqrt(3)).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289778049,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289779144,"data":"       | Now we need point P interior such that angle AEP = angle BFP = angle CDP = θ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289784249,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289785503,"data":"       | Interpretation: angle AEP is angle between EA and EP? Actually angle AEP is angle with vertex at E, formed by points A-E-P. So angle between EA and EP. Similarly BFP: vertex at F, between FB and FP. CDP: vertex at D, between DC and DP.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289787013,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289788261,"data":"       | Thus we need P such that lines EP, FP, DP make equal angles with the sides EA, FB, DC respectively (the sides of triangle adjacent to those points). This suggests that P is the isogonal conjugate of something? Might be intersection of three lines each making same angle with side lines.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289789831,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289791241,"data":"       | Specifically, at each point on side, we have a ray from point to P that makes angle θ with the side segment connecting that point to the opposite vertex? Wait: For E on CA, side EA is along CA from E to A (since E lies on CA). So EA is a segment along side CA, direction from E to A (or A to E). So the angle between EA (i.e., along side CA) and EP is θ. Similarly, at F on AB, side FB is along side AB from F to B (since F on AB). So angle between FB (i.e., along side AB) and FP is θ. At D on BC, side DC is along side BC from D to C (since D on BC). So angle between DC (along side BC) and DP is θ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289793454,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289794845,"data":"       | Thus we have three lines from points D,E,F to P each making same angle θ with the respective side direction towards the far vertex (C for D, A for E, B for F). This is reminiscent of constructing a point P such that the three lines are symmetric with respect to the sides? Could be that P is the Brocard point of triangle ABC. Indeed the Brocard point Ω of a triangle has property that each of the angles ΩAB = ΩBC = ΩCA = ω (Brocard angle). But here the condition is about angles at points on sides, not at vertices. However there is also \"Brocard point\" property: In any triangle, there exists a point Ω such that lines from Ω to vertices make equal angles with the sides: ∠ΩAB = ∠ΩBC = ∠ΩCA = ω. That's at vertices, not at points on sides.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289797635,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289798775,"data":"       | Our condition is different: At points on sides, the line to P makes equal angle with the side segment towards the opposite vertex (i.e., the segment from the point to the far vertex). This might correspond to the \"Brocard point of the medial triangle\"? Not sure.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.28980045,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289801581,"data":"       | But maybe we can treat this as intersection of three lines each defined by a given angle condition. Since we have coordinates, we can set up unknown P = (x,y). Then we can write condition that angle between vectors EA and EP equals angle between vectors FB and FP equals angle between vectors DC and DP. This yields equations.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289803219,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289804309,"data":"       | Let’s define vectors:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289805498,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289806581,"data":"       | EA = A - E (since direction from E to A). EP = P - E.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289807845,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.28980906,"data":"       | Angle between EA and EP: cos θ = (EA·EP) / (|EA||EP|). Similarly, cos θ = (FB·FP) / (|FB||FP|) = (DC·DP) / (|DC||DP|).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289812436,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.28981358,"data":"       | Thus we can set equality of cos values: (EA·EP)/(|EA||EP|) = (FB·FP)/(|FB||FP|) = (DC·DP)/(|DC||DP|).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289814919,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289816024,"data":"       | Thus we have two equations: equality of first two, and equality of first and third. Unknowns x,y, and maybe θ (but we can eliminate). So we can solve for P.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289818123,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289819315,"data":"       | We can solve using sympy.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289820551,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289821756,"data":"       | We also need tan^2 θ. Once we have P, compute cosθ from dot product ratio, then tan^2 = (1 - cos^2)/cos^2 = sec^2 -1 = (1/cos^2) -1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289823287,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289824483,"data":"       | Alternatively compute sinθ via cross product magnitude: sinθ = |EA × EP|/(|EA||EP|) (in 2D cross product magnitude). Then tanθ = sinθ/cosθ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289826071,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289827557,"data":"       | We can compute tan^2 directly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289828819,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.289830075,"data":"       | Let's do with sympy rational numbers and sqrt(3). Use exact rational sqrt(3) symbolic.\n"}
