,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290428666,"data":"       | Observation: This is reminiscent of isogonal conjugate or something: The condition that the angles between lines from P to vertices and the sides are equal maybe indicates that P is the Brocard point of triangle? Indeed, in any triangle there is a Brocard point Ω such that ∠ΩAB = ∠ΩBC = ∠ΩCA = ω (Brocard angle). But here the points are not vertices but points on sides. Wait typical Brocard point condition: For triangle ABC, there exists point Ω such that ∠ΩAB = ∠ΩBC = ∠ΩCA = ω. That's angles at Ω formed with each side and adjacent vertex. Equivalent to ∠ΩAB = ∠ΩBC = ∠ΩCA = ω. Another property: The lines ΩA, ΩB, ΩC make equal angles with sides.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290431443,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290432563,"data":"       | Our condition: ∠AEP = ∠BFP = ∠CDP. Points D,E,F are on sides BC, CA, AB respectively but not at vertices. So maybe P is the Brocard point of triangle DEF? Let's examine: Triangle DEF is formed by points on sides of ABC. The condition uses vertices A,E,P; B,F,P; C,D,P. That's not symmetrical: each angle uses a vertex of original triangle and a point on opposite side.\n"}
