,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290435511,"data":"       | Consider triangle formed by lines AE, BF, CD? Actually A-E is a segment from vertex A to point E on CA (i.e., along side CA). Similarly B-F is along side AB, C-D along side BC. So each angle is between a side of triangle ABC (or a segment from vertex to point on side) and line from that point to P.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290438805,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290440127,"data":"       | Maybe there is a known concept: The point P such that the angles between lines from P to the vertices and the opposite sides are equal is the Brocard point of triangle ABC itself? Let's test: For triangle ABC, the Brocard point Ω satisfies ∠ΩAB = ∠ΩBC = ∠ΩCA = ω. That's angle between line ΩA and side AB? Wait ∠ΩAB is angle with vertex A? Actually notation ∠ΩAB is angle formed by points Ω, A, B, i.e., angle at A between lines AΩ and AB. That's angle between line from A to Ω and side AB. Similarly ∠ΩBC is angle at B between BΩ and BC. ∠ΩCA is angle at C between CΩ and CA. So each uses vertex and adjacent side.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290442645,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290443738,"data":"       | Our condition uses angle at point on side, not at vertex. So it's different.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290444991,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290446108,"data":"       | But maybe we can transform: For point D on BC, angle CDP is at D between DC (segment along side BC) and DP. Since D is on BC, DC is a subsegment of BC (pointing from D to C). So angle CDP is angle between side BC (direction from D to C) and line DP. Similarly, angle AEP is between EA (which is along side CA but reversed direction) and EP. Since E is on CA, EA is along side CA from E to A (i.e., opposite direction of EC). So angle AEP is angle between side CA (direction from E to A) and EP. And angle BFP is between FB (direction from F to B) and FP, which is along side AB direction from F to B.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.29044824,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290449316,"data":"       | Thus each angle is between a side of triangle ABC (or its extension) and line from point on that side to P, measured at the point on side.\n"}
