,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290493576,"data":"       | But perhaps we can use the fact that the three angles at P (∠AP E, ∠BP F, ∠CP D) sum to 360 degrees? Actually P is inside triangle ABC, but triangles AEP, BFP, CDP overlap? Points A, B, C are vertices, and P inside. The three triangles share P but not necessarily cover the whole triangle. However, the angles at P formed by lines PA, PB, PC are the angles of triangle ABC as seen from P; they sum to 360°? Wait lines from P to A, B, C produce three angles around P that sum to 360° (full circle). Indeed ∠APB + ∠BPC + ∠CPA = 360°.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290495776,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290496908,"data":"       | But the angles at P in each small triangle are not these same angles: In triangle AEP, the angle at P is ∠AP E, which is angle between PA and PE. Similarly, in triangle BFP, angle at P is ∠BP F (between PB and PF). In triangle CDP, angle at P is ∠CP D (between PC and PD). The sum of these three angles is not necessarily 360°, because there are also angles between PE and PF, PF and PD, PD and PE. However, note that points E, F, D lie on sides of triangle, so lines PE, PF, PD may intersect each other.\n"}
