,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290518645,"data":"       | But there is known concept: For a given triangle, there is a unique point inside such that the lines from that point to the sides make equal angles with the sides. This point is the Brocard point of the triangle, but measured at the point on the side? Actually the Brocard point is defined by equal angles between lines from the point to the vertices and the sides, not between the lines to the sides themselves.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290520475,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290521549,"data":"       | Specifically, for Brocard point Ω, we have ∠ΩAB = ∠ΩBC = ∠ΩCA = ω. That's angle between line ΩA and side AB (at vertex A). Equivalent to angle between line ΩA and side AC? Wait not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290523101,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290524175,"data":"       | But we can reinterpret: Consider line through Ω intersecting side AB at some point? Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290525472,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290526539,"data":"       | Maybe we should compute P directly using coordinates and solve for equality of angles.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290527806,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290530623,"data":"       | We have coordinates for A, B, C, D, E, F. Unknown P = (x,y). Condition: angle between vector EA (from E to A) and vector EP (from E to P) equals angle between FB and FP, equals angle between DC and DP.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290532138,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290533244,"data":"       | Define angle between vectors u and v as arccos( (u·v) / (|u||v|) ), but we need equality of angles.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290534585,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290535664,"data":"       | We can set equality of cosines or sines.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290536853,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290537939,"data":"       | Let’s define:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290539065,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290540143,"data":"       | cosθ_E = (EA·EP) / (|EA||EP|). Similarly cosθ_F = (FB·FP) / (|FB||FP|). cosθ_D = (DC·DP) / (|DC||DP|).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290541533,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290542623,"data":"       | Set cosθ_E = cosθ_F = cosθ_D. Since θ in (0,π) maybe acute? P inside triangle, side directions outward? The angle between side direction (EA pointing from E to A) and EP should be interior, likely acute. So we can equate cos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290544191,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290545266,"data":"       | Thus we need to find P such that the three dot product ratios are equal.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290546509,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290547585,"data":"       | Let's compute vectors:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290548715,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290549778,"data":"       | - EA = A - E.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290550897,"data":"       | - FB = B - F.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290551992,"data":"       | - DC = C - D.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.29055311,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290554219,"data":"       | We already have coordinates.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290555602,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290556708,"data":"       | Compute them using python and solve for (x,y). Use sympy solving.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290557909,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290558995,"data":"       | We also need to ensure P inside triangle (barycentric coordinates positive). Then compute tan^2 θ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290560325,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290561407,"data":"       | Recall tan^2 θ = (1 - cos^2 θ)/cos^2 θ = (1/cos^2 θ) - 1 = sec^2 θ - 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290562728,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.29056381,"data":"       | So once we have cosθ, compute tan^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290565015,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290566108,"data":"       | Let's proceed with symbolic solving.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290567287,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290568353,"data":"       | We have unknowns x,y. We'll set equality of cosines: (EA·EP)/(|EA||EP|) = (FB·FP)/(|FB||FP|) and = (DC·DP)/(|DC||DP|). That's two equations: cosE = cosF and cosE = cosD.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290569793,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290572517,"data":"       | We can solve for x,y.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290573725,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290574811,"data":"       | But these equations involve square roots due to magnitudes; we can square both sides to avoid radicals. However, careful with sign; but cos positive? The angles likely acute, cos positive, so we can square.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.29057631,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290577568,"data":"       | Set (EA·EP)^2 * (|FB|^2 * |FP|^2) = (FB·FP)^2 * (|EA|^2 * |EP|^2). Similarly for D.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290578862,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290579924,"data":"       | But that's messy.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290581049,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290582367,"data":"       | Alternatively, we could treat using tangent of angle: tanθ = |EA × EP| / (EA·EP) where cross product magnitude (2D) is area of parallelogram = |EA_x EP_y - EA_y EP_x|. In 2D, cross product magnitude equals |EA||EP| sinθ. So tanθ = sinθ / cosθ = (|EA×EP|)/ (EA·EP). So we could set equality of tan across sides: (|EA×EP|)/(EA·EP) = (|FB×FP|)/(FB·FP) = (|DC×DP|)/(DC·DP). Since tan positive (angles acute). That may be easier as we can avoid square roots.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290584588,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290585679,"data":"       | But cross product magnitude includes absolute value; sign may be positive for interior orientation. Since P inside triangle, orientation of vectors maybe consistent (counterclockwise). We'll assume positive.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290587139,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290588209,"data":"       | Thus we can set equality of ratios of cross product magnitude to dot product.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290589447,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290590534,"data":"       | Let’s compute using sympy.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290591739,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290592825,"data":"       | Potentially there could be two solutions (mirror). We'll find one inside triangle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290594078,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290595155,"data":"       | Let's attempt with python.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290596322,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290597587,"data":"       | assistantanalysis to=python codeimport sympy as sp\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290598779,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290599874,"data":"       | # define symbols\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290601032,"data":"       | x, y = sp.symbols('x y', real=True)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290602178,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290603281,"data":"       | # constants\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290604379,"data":"       | s = 55\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290605529,"data":"       | sqrt3 = sp.sqrt(3)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290606624,"data":"       | # coordinates\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290607763,"data":"       | A = sp.Point(s/2, s*sqrt3/2)  # (27.5, 27.5*sqrt3)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290608938,"data":"       | B = sp.Point(0, 0)\n"}
