,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290885189,"data":"       | analysisWe need to solve geometry problem: equilateral triangle side length 55. Points D on BC with BD=7 (so from B towards C). E on CA with CE=30 (so from C towards A). F on AB with AF=40 (from A towards B). So we have points dividing sides.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290888957,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290890136,"data":"       | Let’s set coordinates: equilateral triangle ABC with A at (0,0), B at (55,0), C at (55/2, 55*sqrt(3)/2). Actually typical orientation: A at (0,0), B at (55,0), C at (55/2, 55*sqrt(3)/2). Then side length 55.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290891687,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290892806,"data":"       | Now D on BC: B (55,0) to C (55/2, 55*sqrt(3)/2). BD=7 along side length 55. So D is at distance 7 from B towards C. Parameter t = BD / BC = 7/55. So D = B + t*(C - B). Similarly E on CA: C to A, with CE=30 from C towards A. So along CA length 55, parameter s = CE/CA = 30/55 = 6/11. So E = C + s*(A - C). F on AB: A to B, with AF=40 from A towards B. Parameter r = AF/AB = 40/55 = 8/11. So F = A + r*(B - A) = (40,0). Indeed A (0,0) to B (55,0) so F at (40,0). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290894773,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290895869,"data":"       | Now we need point P inside triangle such that angle AEP = angle BFP = angle CDP. Let's denote these equal angle as θ. So we need to find P satisfying three equal angle conditions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290897307,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290898407,"data":"       | We need tan^2(θ). So we need compute angle between lines AE and EP? Wait angle AEP: vertex at E, lines EA and EP. So angle between EA and EP equals θ. Similarly angle BFP: vertex at F, lines FB and FP equals θ. Similarly angle CDP: vertex at D, lines DC and DP equals θ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290900049,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290901137,"data":"       | Thus we need to find point P such that the angle between EA and EP equals angle between FB and FP equals angle between DC and DP.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.29090246,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290903676,"data":"       | This looks like some concurrency property of Apollonius circles? Could be that P is the isogonal conjugate of a point? Actually condition that angles at three points on sides equal suggests that lines EP, FP, DP are isogonal to the sides? Let's think.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290905225,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290906307,"data":"       | In triangle ABC, points E on CA, F on AB, D on BC. Condition that ∠AEP = ∠BFP = ∠CDP suggests that P is the isogonal conjugate of the point where lines through vertices intersect? Wait.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290907801,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290908894,"data":"       | Consider triangle AEF? Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290910055,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290911177,"data":"       | Alternatively maybe we can use trilinear coordinates: For point P inside triangle, the angles between lines from P to vertices and to points on sides? Not typical.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290912569,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290913675,"data":"       | Better to set up coordinates and solve for P (x,y) such that these three angle equalities hold. Use equations for tan of angle between two vectors. For each vertex (E, F, D) we have two vectors: from E to A and from E to P; angle between them is θ. Similarly from F to B and F to P; and from D to C and D to P.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290915351,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290916439,"data":"       | Thus we need to find P such that the three angles are equal. So we can set equality between first two angles: angle(AE, PE) = angle(BF, PF). And equality between first and third: angle(AE, PE) = angle(CD, PD). So two equations for unknown (x,y) and unknown θ maybe. But we can eliminate θ by equating tangents of angles.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290919859,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290920959,"data":"       | Angle between vectors u and v: cos = (u·v)/(|u||v|). Or we could use tan of angle: tanθ = |u×v|/(u·v). In 2D, cross product magnitude is |u_x v_y - u_y v_x|. Since we only need equality of angles, we could set the ratio of cross to dot equal across pairs. However sign may be positive as angles are interior (0 to π). Since P inside triangle, the angles likely acute? Not sure but we can consider absolute values.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290922881,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290924642,"data":"       | Thus we need:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290925935,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290927025,"data":"       | | (AE) × (PE) | / (AE·PE) = | (BF) × (PF) | / (BF·PF) = | (CD) × (PD) | / (CD·PD)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290928396,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290929881,"data":"       | But we also need P inside triangle, giving positive dot maybe? Actually dot could be positive if angle \u003c 90°, else negative. But we can treat tan^2, which uses squared ratio, eliminating sign.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290931393,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290932569,"data":"       | Thus we can set equality of squares: ((AE)×(PE))^2 / (AE·PE)^2 = ((BF)×(PF))^2 / (BF·PF)^2 = ((CD)×(PD))^2 / (CD·PD)^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290933923,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290935014,"data":"       | Thus we can equate the first two: ((AE×PE)^2)*(BF·PF)^2 = ((BF×PF)^2)*(AE·PE)^2. Similarly with CD.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290936397,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.290937575,"data":"       | We can solve for (x,y). Might be messy but doable with sympy.\n"}
