,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291599504,"data":"       | analysisWe need to solve geometry problem: equilateral triangle side 55. Points D on BC with BD = 7 (so from B towards C). E on CA with CE=30 (so from C towards A). F on AB with AF=40 (from A towards B). P interior such that angle AEP = angle BFP = angle CDP. Need tan^2 of that common angle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291601069,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.29160211,"data":"       | Let’s denote triangle ABC coordinates. Let’s set A at (0,0), B at (55,0), C at (55/2, 55*sqrt(3)/2). That's standard orientation: equilateral with side s =55.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291603474,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291604514,"data":"       | Compute coordinates of points D, E, F:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291605632,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291606837,"data":"       | - D lies on BC: B (55,0) to C (55/2, h) where h = 55*sqrt(3)/2. Parameter t from B: BD =7 along BC length 55. Since BC length is 55, the ratio BD/BC = 7/55. So D = B + (7/55)*(C - B). Vector C-B = (-55/2, h). So D coordinates: (55 + (7/55)*(-55/2), 0 + (7/55)*h) = (55 - (7/2), (7/55)*h) = (55 - 3.5 = 51.5, (7/55)*h). Compute h = 55*sqrt(3)/2 =\u003e h = (55*sqrt(3))/2. So (7/55)*h = (7/55)*(55*sqrt(3)/2) = 7*sqrt(3)/2 ≈ 6.062... So D = (51.5, 7*sqrt(3)/2).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291608719,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291609792,"data":"       | - E on CA: C to A. CE = 30 measured from C towards A along CA length 55. So ratio CE/CA = 30/55 = 6/11? Actually 30/55 simplifies to 6/11. So E = C + (30/55)*(A - C) = C - (30/55)*(C - A). Vector C-A = (55/2, h). So E = (55/2, h) - (30/55)*(55/2, h) = (55/2)*(1 - 30/55) , h*(1 - 30/55). 30/55 = 6/11 ≈0.54545. So 1 - 30/55 = 25/55 =5/11. So coordinates: x_E = (55/2)*(5/11) = (55*5)/(22) = (275)/22 = 12.5? Wait compute: 55/2 =27.5; times 5/11 =27.5*0.4545=12.5 indeed. So x_E =12.5. y_E = h*(5/11) = (55*sqrt(3)/2)*(5/11) = (55*5*sqrt(3))/(22) = (275*sqrt(3))/22 = (125*sqrt(3))/10? Let's compute: 275/22 = 12.5. So y_E =12.5*sqrt(3). Indeed because h=27.5*sqrt(3). Multiply by 5/11 gives 27.5*sqrt(3)*(5/11)= (27.5*5/11)*sqrt(3)= (137.5/11)*sqrt(3)=12.5*sqrt(3). Good. So E = (12.5, 12.5*sqrt(3)).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291612167,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291613187,"data":"       | - F on AB: AF =40 from A towards B along AB length 55. So ratio AF/AB = 40/55 = 8/11 ≈0.72727. So F = A + (40/55)*(B - A) = (40/55)*(55,0) = (40,0). Indeed because AB along x-axis. So F = (40,0).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291614602,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.29161562,"data":"       | Now we have coordinates for D, E, F.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.29161669,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291617724,"data":"       | We need a point P inside triangle such that angle AEP = angle BFP = angle CDP. These are angles at points E, F, D respectively, with rays from each point to the two vertices (E to A and P; F to B and P; D to C and P). So we need angle between EA and EP equal to angle between FB and FP equal to angle between CD and DP.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291619297,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291622589,"data":"       | Let denote common angle = θ. Want tan^2 θ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291623772,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.29162492,"data":"       | Thus we need to find P satisfying these angle equalities. This is reminiscent of \"Brocard point\" of a triangle: For any triangle, there exists a point Ω such that ∠ΩAB = ∠ΩBC = ∠ΩCA = ω (Brocard angle). However here we have a point with equal angles at three vertices but not the same vertices: AEP uses vertex at E (point on side CA), BFP uses vertex at F (point on side AB), CDP uses vertex at D (point on side BC). Not typical.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291626822,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291627842,"data":"       | But perhaps there is property: For any triangle ABC and points D,E,F on sides with given distances from vertices, there exists point P such that ∠AEP = ∠BFP = ∠CDP = ω. Might be solved using Ceva-like condition with oriented angles. Possibly P is the isogonal conjugate of something.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291629433,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291630464,"data":"       | Let's attempt analytic solution: Unknown coordinates (x,y) for P. We need to enforce equality of two angles. Could use vector dot product to express angle between vectors.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291631791,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291632787,"data":"       | Define vectors:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.29163387,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291634909,"data":"       | - At E: vectors EA = A - E, EP = P - E. Angle between them: θ = angle(EA, EP). So cos θ = (EA·EP)/(|EA||EP|). Similarly at F: angle between FB = B - F and FP = P - F. At D: angle between DC = C - D and DP = P - D.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291636381,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291637414,"data":"       | Equality of angles means cos values equal? However angle equality can be expressed via tan maybe easier. But we can set up equations using dot products: The angle equality implies that the ratio of sine to cosine same across; but we could set equality of tangents: tan θ = |EA × EP| / (EA·EP) etc. In 2D cross product magnitude equals determinant.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.29163928,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291640287,"data":"       | Thus we can write:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291641351,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291642364,"data":"       | tan θ = (|det(EA, EP)|) / (EA·EP) = (|det(FB, FP)|) / (FB·FP) = (|det(DC, DP)|) / (DC·DP).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291643727,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291644754,"data":"       | Since P interior, orientation maybe same sign for determinants (positive). So we can drop absolute.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291645947,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291646987,"data":"       | Thus we have two equations:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291648047,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291649062,"data":"       | det(EA, EP) / (EA·EP) = det(FB, FP) / (FB·FP)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291650217,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291651227,"data":"       | det(EA, EP) / (EA·EP) = det(DC, DP) / (DC·DP)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291652396,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.291653427,"data":"       | We can solve for (x,y). Then compute tan θ = that ratio.\n"}
