,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.29210194,"data":"       | analysisWe need tan^2 of angle AEP where P interior such that angles AEP = BFP = CDP. Given triangle equilateral side 55. Points D on BC with BD=7, so from B to C length 55, BD=7, so DC = 55-7=48. E on CA with CE=30, note CA side 55, so from C to A length 55, CE=30 measured from C along CA towards A, so point E is such that segment from C to E length 30, leaving EA = 55-30=25. F on AB with AF=40, measured from A towards B? Usually AF = 40 along AB from A, so FB = 55-40 = 15.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292103771,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292104817,"data":"       | We need point P inside satisfying equal angles at three different points: angle AEP (at E) formed by lines EA and EP? Wait angle AEP means angle with vertex E, arms EA and EP. Similarly BFP with vertex F, arms FB and FP. CDP with vertex D, arms DC and DP. So we need point P such that these three angles are equal. Let common angle = θ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292108384,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292109441,"data":"       | Goal: tan^2 θ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292110579,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292111603,"data":"       | This is reminiscent of problem with isogonal conjugates? Perhaps P is the isogonal conjugate of the Fermat point? Not exactly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292112863,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292113883,"data":"       | Given equilateral triangle, there is symmetry. Points D,E,F are on sides, not symmetric.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292115062,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292116085,"data":"       | We can set coordinates: Let equilateral triangle ABC with A at (0,0), B at (55,0), C at (55/2, (55*sqrt(3))/2). Standard orientation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292117331,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292118376,"data":"       | Compute coordinates:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292119771,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.29212077,"data":"       | A = (0,0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292121801,"data":"       | B = (55,0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292122837,"data":"       | C = (55/2, 55*sqrt(3)/2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292123886,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292124922,"data":"       | Now D on BC with BD=7. Parameter along BC from B to C distance 7. So D = B + (7/55)*(C-B). Since BC length 55. So D coordinates = B + (7/55)*(C-B). Similarly, E on CA with CE=30, measured from C towards A, length 30 along CA (which length 55). So E = C + (30/55)*(A-C) = C - (30/55)*(C-A). Similarly, F on AB with AF=40 from A towards B: F = A + (40/55)*(B-A) = (40/55)*B.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292126541,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292127562,"data":"       | Now we need point P interior such that angle between EA and EP equals angle between FB and FP equals angle between DC and DP.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292128835,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292129872,"data":"       | Let vector EA = A - E. Vector EP = P - E. Angle between them: angle between EA and EP is θ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292131107,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292132154,"data":"       | Similarly, angle between FB and FP: vector FB = B - F, FP = P - F, angle = θ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.29213337,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292134387,"data":"       | Angle between DC and DP: vector DC = C - D, DP = P - D, angle = θ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292135573,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292136617,"data":"       | So we need to find P such that these three angles equal. Equivalent to requiring that the oriented lines EP and EA form same angle as FP and FB, etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292137935,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292138997,"data":"       | Potentially we can use law of sines in triangles? Another approach: For each vertex (E,F,D) we have condition that P lies on a circular arc (or a ray) that makes angle θ with a given segment. For given point X and segment XY, the set of points P such that angle YXP = θ is a circular arc (a circular sector) of a circle passing through X and Y with some angle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.29214305,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292144084,"data":"       | But we need intersection of three such loci.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292145227,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292146265,"data":"       | Perhaps there is a unique solution for P determined by the three lengths? Maybe we can solve using coordinates and unknown P = (x,y), and unknown θ, requiring equality of cosines of angles via dot product.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292147693,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292148713,"data":"       | Specifically, cosθ = (EA·EP) / (|EA||EP|) = (FB·FP)/(|FB||FP|) = (DC·DP)/(|DC||DP|)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292149947,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292151105,"data":"       | Thus we have two equations: (EA·EP)/(|EA||EP|) = (FB·FP)/(|FB||FP|) and equal to (DC·DP)/(|DC||DP|). This gives two equations in x,y (and maybe θ). Actually we can write equality of cosines; we don't need θ separately.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292152605,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292153619,"data":"       | Set cosθ1 = cosθ2, cosθ1 = cosθ3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292154793,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.2921558,"data":"       | So we have two equations rational in x,y.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.2921569,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292157906,"data":"       | We can solve for P coordinates using sympy.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.29215907,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292160381,"data":"       | Then compute tan^2 θ = (1 - cos^2θ)/cos^2θ = (1/cos^2θ) - 1 = sec^2θ - 1 = tan^2θ. So we can compute cosθ from any of the dot product expressions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292161787,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292162796,"data":"       | Thus plan: compute coordinates, unknowns x,y. Write equations:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292163939,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.29216515,"data":"       | (EA·(P-E))/ (|EA||P-E|) - (FB·(P-F))/ (|FB||P-F|) = 0\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292166372,"data":"       | (EA·(P-E))/ (|EA||P-E|) - (DC·(P-D))/ (|DC||P-D|) = 0\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292167536,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.29216861,"data":"       | Solve for x,y.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292169647,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.29217071,"data":"       | Potentially there could be two solutions (maybe symmetric across some line). But we need interior point; choose appropriate.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292171957,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292172973,"data":"       | Then compute cosθ = (EA·(P-E))/(|EA||P-E|). Then tan^2 = (1 - cosθ**2)/cosθ**2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292174216,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292175214,"data":"       | We can compute exact rational plus sqrt expressions.\n"}
