,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292390855,"data":"     [Reasoning] (4608 chars):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292391919,"data":"       | analysisWe have fractions; we can multiply denominators to get polynomial equations after clearing radicals? But we have square roots of quadratic forms (distances). This leads to system of equations with radicals. Could solve numerically first then maybe rationalize.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292393397,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292394817,"data":"       | Better approach: Since we only need tan^2 θ, which is (1 - cos^2)/cos^2, maybe we can use law of sines: For triangle with known sides and angle at vertex? Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292396187,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292397243,"data":"       | Alternative: Use complex numbers? Might lead to Ceva-like condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292398386,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292399392,"data":"       | Observation: Condition that ∠AEP = ∠BFP = ∠CDP suggests that lines EP, FP, DP are isogonal lines with respect to triangle formed by points A, B, C? Not exactly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292400774,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292401825,"data":"       | Consider triangle with vertices A, B, C and points E,F,D on sides opposite them. The condition that angle between EA and EP equals angle between FB and FP etc. That suggests that P is the isogonal conjugate of some point relative to triangle formed by lines through D,E,F? Wait.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292404929,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292405985,"data":"       | If we think of each side: At point E on CA, line EA is along side CA (since E lies on CA). So the angle between CA (direction from E to A) and EP is θ. Similarly at F, the angle between FB (direction from F to B) and FP is θ. At D, angle between DC (direction from D to C) and DP is θ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292407543,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292408657,"data":"       | Thus each condition says that EP makes a fixed angle θ with side CA, measured interior of triangle. Similarly, FP makes angle θ with side AB (since FB is along side AB but from F to B). And DP makes angle θ with side BC (since DC is along side BC from D to C). So P is a point such that lines from each point on side to P make equal angles with the side (but possibly on same side of side). This is reminiscent of a point that is the \"center of an inscribed spiral similarity\" that maps side CA to something? Wait.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292410692,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292411979,"data":"       | If we consider the rays from each point (E,F,D) to P that make angle θ with the side, then we can rotate the side segment by θ to get a line through the point that passes through P. So each condition defines a line: rotate CA about E by +θ (or -θ) to get line EP. Similarly rotate AB about F by +θ to get line FP, rotate BC about D by +θ to get line DP. The intersection of these three lines is P. So P is the common intersection of three rotated lines. This suggests that there is a unique θ such that the three rotated lines concur.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.29241401,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292415031,"data":"       | Thus we can treat θ as unknown; we can compute the rotated lines using rotation matrix.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292416217,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292417251,"data":"       | Let side vectors: For CA, vector from E to A: EA = A - E. Rotating this vector by angle θ (counterclockwise) yields direction of EP. Similarly, for FB: vector from F to B: FB = B - F. Rotating FB by angle θ (counterclockwise) yields direction of FP. For DC: vector from D to C: DC = C - D. Rotating DC by angle θ (counterclockwise) yields direction of DP.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292418937,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.29241996,"data":"       | Thus we can write parametric lines:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292421041,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292422055,"data":"       | EP: point E + t * Rθ(EA)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292423166,"data":"       | FP: point F + u * Rθ(FB)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292424275,"data":"       | DP: point D + v * Rθ(DC)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292425336,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292426357,"data":"       | Where Rθ is rotation matrix [[cosθ, -sinθ], [sinθ, cosθ]].\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292427562,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292428619,"data":"       | These three lines must intersect at a common point P. So there exists t,u,v such that these are equal.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292429805,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292430822,"data":"       | Thus we can set up equations: E + t Rθ(EA) = F + u Rθ(FB) = D + v Rθ(DC). We can solve for t,u,v and θ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292433963,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292435015,"data":"       | This is a system with unknowns t,u,v,θ (or cosθ, sinθ). We can treat cosθ = c, sinθ = s, with c^2 + s^2 = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292436323,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292437382,"data":"       | Then we have vector equations: E + t*R(EA) = F + u*R(FB) and also equal to D + v*R(DC). So we have two vector equations (each 2 components) for unknowns t,u,c,s (since R depends linearly on c,s). That's 4 equations. Additional unknowns: v also appears but we could just equate first two lines; then verify third line intersection maybe yields extra condition which will determine c,s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292439024,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292440111,"data":"       | But we have many unknowns.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292441191,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292442217,"data":"       | Better: We can set up intersection of first two rotated lines to get P in terms of c,s, then impose that P also lies on third rotated line, giving condition on c,s.\n"}
