,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292781417,"data":"       | analysisWe need to solve geometry problem: equilateral triangle side 55. Points D on BC with BD=7, thus DC = 55-7=48. E on CA with CE=30, meaning from C towards A along CA: CA side length 55, CE=30 measured from C to E, so EA = 55-30=25. F on AB with AF=40, so FB = 55-40 =15.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292782987,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292784049,"data":"       | We have interior point P such that angle AEP = angle BFP = angle CDP. So P is such that these three angles equal some common value θ. Need tan^2(θ).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292785406,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292786513,"data":"       | This is reminiscent of equal angles to three sides from points on sides, maybe P is Brocard point? In a triangle, there is the Brocard point Ω such that ∠ΩAB = ∠ΩBC = ∠ΩCA = ω (Brocard angle). But here the points are on sides, not vertices. However there is concept of \"Brocard points relative to points on sides\"? Wait we have angles with vertex at points on sides: AEP with vertex at E (on CA) formed by AE and EP; BFP at vertex F formed by BF and FP; CDP at vertex D formed by CD and DP. So lines AE, BF, CD each from vertices to points on opposite sides? Actually AE is along side CA, from A to E (part of side). So angle AEP is angle between AE (which is a segment of side CA) and EP (line from E to interior point P). Similarly BFP angle between BF (part of side AB) and FP. CDP angle between CD (part of side BC) and DP.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.29278912,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292790261,"data":"       | Thus we have a point P inside such that the angles between each side segment (from vertex to a point on adjacent side) and line to P are equal. This resembles \"equal angles from points dividing sides\" maybe leads to concurrency of certain Cevians.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292791794,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.29279288,"data":"       | We can consider coordinates: Place triangle ABC equilateral with side length 55. Choose coordinates: Let A = (0,0), B = (55,0), C = (55/2, (55*sqrt(3))/2). That's standard orientation: base AB horizontal.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292794254,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292795297,"data":"       | Compute coordinates of D, E, F along sides:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292796407,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292797443,"data":"       | - D on BC: B (55,0) to C (27.5, 55*sqrt(3)/2). Parameter t = BD/BC = 7/55. Starting from B towards C. So D = B + t*(C-B). So D coordinates.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292798723,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292799732,"data":"       | - E on CA: C to A? CA from C to A direction. CE = 30 measured from C towards A along CA, length CA =55, so t = CE/CA = 30/55 = 6/11. Starting from C towards A. So E = C + ( - (C-A) )? Actually vector from C to A = A - C. So E = C + (CE/CA)*(A - C). So compute.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292801211,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292802286,"data":"       | - F on AB: from A to B along AB; AF =40 measured from A towards B, so t = AF/AB = 40/55 = 8/11. So F = A + t*(B-A) = (40,0) (since AB horizontal). So F = (40,0).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292804956,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292806013,"data":"       | Now we need point P (x,y) such that the angles at E, F, D between side segment and line to P are equal: angle between vector AE (pointing from E to A? Wait angle AEP: vertex at E, sides EA and EP. So vectors EA = A - E, EP = P - E. Similarly angle BFP: vectors FB = B - F, FP = P - F. Angle CDP: vectors DC = C - D? Actually angle CDP: vertex D, sides DC and DP, i.e., vectors DC = C - D, DP = P - D.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292807861,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292808873,"data":"       | We need these three angles equal: denote θ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292810047,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292811074,"data":"       | Thus we need to enforce that the cosine of each angle equal cosθ. Cosine of angle between vectors u and v is (u·v)/(|u||v|). So we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292812384,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292813477,"data":"       | cosθ = (EA·EP) / (|EA||EP|) = (FB·FP) / (|FB||FP|) = (DC·DP) / (|DC||DP|).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292814702,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292815829,"data":"       | But we don't know θ; we need tan^2 θ. Equivalent: we can set equality of ratios of dot products, maybe solve for P coordinates.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292817126,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292818156,"data":"       | Let us denote unknown P (x,y). Compute vectors:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292819261,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.29282025,"data":"       | EA = A - E.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.29282128,"data":"       | EP = P - E.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.292822362,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.29282339,"data":"       | FB = B - F.\n"}
