,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293237866,"data":"       | Alternative: Use ratio of sines from law of sines in each small triangle. For triangle AEP, we have side AE known (distance along side from A to E), which is length AE = 25 (since CA side length 55, CE=30). Indeed AE = 25. Similarly, FB = 15, CD = 48.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293239331,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293240353,"data":"       | But note that AE is along side CA, not the segment length from A to E. The triangle AEP includes side AE known, and we have angle at E (θ). Similarly for BFP includes side FB known, angle at F is θ. Also for CDP includes side CD known, angle at D is θ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293241876,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.2932429,"data":"       | Perhaps we can use law of sines in each triangle to relate distances from P to vertices.\n"}
