,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293247404,"data":"       | - In triangle AEP: side AE = 25 opposite angle ∠AP E? Actually need labeling: vertices are A, E, P. Known side AE opposite angle at P (∠AP E). Unknown sides: AP and EP. Angles: at A is ∠EAP (some value), at E is θ, at P is other.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293248941,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293249973,"data":"       | But we also have triangle BFP with side FB=15 opposite angle at P? Wait side FB opposite angle at P? In triangle BFP, side BF is between B and F, opposite angle at P (∠B P F). So again side length opposite angle at P.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293251444,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293252461,"data":"       | Similarly CD is opposite angle at P in triangle CDP.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293253601,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.29325612,"data":"       | Thus all three triangles share P as a vertex, and have known side opposite angle at P.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293257324,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293258364,"data":"       | If we denote α = ∠AP E, β = ∠B P F, γ = ∠C P D. Then by law of sines:\n"}
