,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293262914,"data":"       | Better: In triangle AEP:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293263991,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293265017,"data":"       | AE / sin(∠AP E) = AP / sin(θ) = EP / sin(∠E A P). Because angle at E is θ. So AE opposite angle at P.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293266303,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293267334,"data":"       | Thus we have AE / sin(∠AP E) = AP / sin(θ). Similarly, for BFP: BF / sin(∠B P F) = BP / sin(θ). For CDP: CD / sin(∠C P D) = CP / sin(θ).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293268726,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293269754,"data":"       | Thus AP = AE * sin(θ) / sin(∠AP E). Similarly, BP = BF * sin(θ) / sin(∠BPF). CP = CD * sin(θ) / sin(∠CPD).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293271113,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293272143,"data":"       | Now note that angles at P in each triangle sum? Not directly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293273293,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293274301,"data":"       | But maybe we can apply law of sines in triangle ABC with point P internal and Cevians connecting P to vertices? Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293275547,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293276563,"data":"       | Alternative: Use trilinear coordinates: For a point P inside triangle, the angles between the sides and lines to P are given by its trilinear coordinates. Indeed, for any point P with trilinear coordinates (α:β:γ), the angle between side BC and line AP is something like? Wait.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293278138,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293279144,"data":"       | In trilinear coordinates, a point P is represented by distances to sides (or ratios). The angles at points on sides may correspond.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293280413,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.29328145,"data":"       | But coordinate approach is straightforward.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293282561,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293283683,"data":"       | We can produce final solution: Choose coordinate system with A(0,0), B(55,0), C(55/2, 55√3/2). Determine D, E, F coordinates as above. Let P = (x,y). Write equality of the angles using dot product condition: cos(∠AEP) = cos(∠BFP) = cos(∠CDP). Compute vectors, set equality leads to two equations. Solve for x,y. Get tan^2 = 75.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293285386,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293286411,"data":"       | But we should find a more elegant algebraic elimination to directly compute tan^2 without heavy solving? However we can present the solution using coordinates, solving the system leads to a rational expression for tan^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293287826,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293288836,"data":"       | Let's attempt to compute tan^2 directly using symbolic elimination rather than solving for x,y to produce neat result.\n"}
