,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293428378,"data":"       | analysisWe need to find tan^2 of angle AEP where point P inside equilateral triangle ABC (side 55) with points D on BC, E on CA, F on AB such that BD = 7, CE =30, AF=40. So we know positions: triangle ABC equilateral side 55.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293429763,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293430787,"data":"       | Let’s coordinate. Place A at (0,0), B at (55,0), C at (55/2, 55*sqrt(3)/2). That's usual orientation base AB horizontal.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293432097,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293433121,"data":"       | But note: side length 55, equilateral.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293434201,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293435183,"data":"       | Coordinates:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293436208,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293437227,"data":"       | A = (0,0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293438263,"data":"       | B = (55,0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293439297,"data":"       | C = (27.5, 55*sqrt(3)/2) = (27.5, (55*sqrt(3))/2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293440393,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293441397,"data":"       | Let’s compute numeric values later.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293442487,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293443487,"data":"       | Points:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293444544,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293445614,"data":"       | F lies on AB, AF = 40. Since AB from A(0,0) to B(55,0). So F = (AF,0) = (40,0). Indeed distance AF = 40.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293446813,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293447834,"data":"       | E lies on CA, CE =30. Edge CA from C to A. So we need point E on CA such that distance from C to E equals 30. Let's param.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293449091,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293450101,"data":"       | Coordinates along CA: vector from C to A = A - C = (-27.5, - (55*sqrt(3))/2). Its length = side 55.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293451281,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293453731,"data":"       | Parameter t from C to A: E = C + (30/55)*(A - C) = C + (30/55)*(vector). Equivalent to moving fraction 30/55 of the way from C toward A.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293455017,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293456203,"data":"       | But note: CE =30, yes. So E = C + (30/55)*(A - C) = (1 - 30/55)C + (30/55)A? Wait compute: Starting at C then move towards A. So E = C + (30/55)*(A - C) = (1 - 30/55)*C + (30/55)*A = (25/55)C + (30/55)A.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293457593,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293458667,"data":"       | Similarly D lies on BC, BD =7. B to C vector = (27.5-55, 55*sqrt(3)/2 - 0) = (-27.5, 55*sqrt(3)/2). Length 55. Starting at B then moving towards C distance 7: D = B + (7/55)*(C - B) = B + (7/55)*(vector). Equivalent to (1 - 7/55)B + (7/55)C = (48/55)B + (7/55)C.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293460131,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293461136,"data":"       | Thus coordinates known.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293462156,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293463177,"data":"       | Now we have P inside triangle such that angles AEP, BFP, CDP are equal.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293464318,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293465347,"data":"       | Let angle = θ = ∠AEP = ∠BFP = ∠CDP.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293466516,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293467514,"data":"       | Goal find tan^2 θ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.2934686,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293469643,"data":"       | We need to find P satisfying equal oriented angles at points E, F, D with lines to A, B, C and P.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293470838,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.29347187,"data":"       | Specifically, at E, angle between AE and EP? Wait ∠AEP: vertex at E, sides EA and EP. So angle between EA and EP.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293473094,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293474146,"data":"       | Similarly BFP: vertex at F, between FB and FP.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293475211,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293476244,"data":"       | CDP: vertex at D, between DC and DP.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293477316,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293478303,"data":"       | All these equal.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293479331,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293480347,"data":"       | Thus P is interior point such that lines EP, FP, DP make equal angles with the respective sides of triangle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293481564,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293482643,"data":"       | Seems like maybe P is isogonal conjugate of something? Perhaps intersection of certain circles? Could be related to Brocard point: For any triangle, there exists a point (Brocard point) Ω such that ∠ΩAB = ∠ΩBC = ∠ΩCA = ω (Brocard angle). That's at vertex angles? Actually property: For triangle ABC, there exists a point Ω such that lines ΩA, ΩB, ΩC make equal angles ω with sides BC, CA, AB respectively: ∠ΩAB = ∠ΩBC = ∠ΩCA = ω. That's Brocard point, first Brocard point.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293484667,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293485857,"data":"       | But here we have point P inside equilateral triangle and points D, E, F on sides, not necessarily vertices. However the condition resembles: ∠AEP (at point E) equal to something; note AE is along side CA (since E lies on CA). Wait AE is a segment from A to point E on CA, which is along CA (since both A and C are on same line). Indeed AE is a subsegment of CA. So line AE is same as line CA (the side). Similarly FB is subsegment of AB? Wait B is vertex B, F on AB, so line BF is along AB. Similarly D C? D on BC, C vertex, line DC along BC.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293489473,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293490583,"data":"       | Thus ∠AEP is angle between CA (or EA direction) and EP; ∠BFP is angle between AB (or BF direction) and FP; ∠CDP is angle between BC (or DC) and DP.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293491921,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293492963,"data":"       | Thus we have point P such that at each side point (on side) the line to P makes equal angle with the side. That is reminiscent of the Brocard point but with the side points not the vertices: It is like we have a point P such that the lines from P to each side are equally inclined to the sides, at points D,E,F selected such that distances from vertices are given. Wait but the side points D,E,F are predetermined (by BD, CE, AF). So we have P such that at those points the angle between the side and the line to P is same for all three sides.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293494881,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293495927,"data":"       | Thus we need to find point P satisfying three equations.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.29349705,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293498078,"data":"       | Given coordinates, we can set up unknown (x,y) for P and require angle between vectors:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293499237,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293500328,"data":"       | Let vector along side CA direction: from E to A is A - E. That direction is same as along CA. Similarly vector from F to B is B - F (direction AB). And vector from D to C is C - D (direction BC).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293501674,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293502707,"data":"       | Define angle between vector v (side direction) and vector w (from side point to P) should be equal θ for all three.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293503951,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293504988,"data":"       | Thus we need:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293506023,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293507044,"data":"       | cos θ = ( (A - E)·(P - E) ) / (|A - E| * |P - E| ) = same for the other two.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293508277,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293509277,"data":"       | Or we could set ratios of sines? Or we can use tangent formula maybe.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.2935104,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293511397,"data":"       | Goal compute tan^2 θ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293512488,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293513521,"data":"       | Thus we can solve for P coordinates using the equalities, then compute tan of angle between vectors.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293514691,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293515684,"data":"       | But there might be two solutions? P interior.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293516793,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293517796,"data":"       | Let's attempt via solving equations using sympy.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293518881,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293521407,"data":"       | We'll have variables x,y for P. Use equations: angle equality between first and second, and first and third.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293522644,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293523698,"data":"       | We can set equality of cosθ: (dot1)/(|A-E|*|P-E|) = (dot2)/(|B-F|*|P-F|) etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293524914,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293525927,"data":"       | But unknown denominator includes |P-E| etc, which also unknown. Could solve rationally.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293527094,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293528124,"data":"       | Alternative: Use tangent formula: Since we only need tan^2 θ, maybe we can avoid solving for P.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293529373,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293530373,"data":"       | But we may just compute numerically and then find tan^2 rational maybe.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293531503,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293532581,"data":"       | However problem likely expects rational result.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293534293,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293535354,"data":"       | Given triangle equilateral with side 55, points distances given: BD=7, CE=30, AF=40. Since side 55, these distances are along each side starting from certain vertices:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293536677,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293537697,"data":"       | - On BC, from B to D distance 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.29353878,"data":"       | - On CA, from C to E distance 30.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293539861,"data":"       | - On AB, from A to F distance 40.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.29354093,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293541953,"data":"       | Thus the points D, E, F are not symmetric.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293543057,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293544074,"data":"       | Nevertheless, there is likely a unique interior point P satisfying equal angles.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293545243,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293546254,"data":"       | We can solve numerically first to guess angle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.29354735,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293548397,"data":"       | Let's use python to compute coordinates and solve.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293549503,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293550584,"data":"       | We'll set unknown x,y.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293551671,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293552674,"data":"       | Define vectors:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293553754,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293554801,"data":"       | v1 = A - E (CA direction). Since side is oriented from C to A, but direction doesn't matter for angle magnitude (absolute). Use absolute angle between v1 and EP.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293556178,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293557191,"data":"       | Thus cos1 = dot(v1, P - E) / (|v1| * |P - E|)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293558277,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293559318,"data":"       | Similarly cos2 = dot(v2, P - F)/(|v2| * |P - F|) where v2 = B - F (AB direction). v3 = C - D.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293561964,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293563018,"data":"       | Set cos1 = cos2 and cos1 = cos3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293564088,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.2935651,"data":"       | Solve for (x,y). That's two equations.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293566191,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293567193,"data":"       | Also P must be inside triangle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293568251,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4322.293569294,"data":"       | Let's compute numerically using sympy nsolve.\n"}
