,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461480888,"data":"       | analysisWe need to solve geometry problem. Triangle sides 13,14,15. M midpoint of BC. P is point on circumcircle such that M lies on line AP (i.e., AP passes through M). There is unique point Q on segment AM such that angle PBQ = angle PCQ. Find AQ length expressed as m/√n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461484974,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.46148741,"data":"       | Goal: compute AQ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461489793,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461492143,"data":"       | We need to find geometry relationships.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461494547,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461496938,"data":"       | Let's set up coordinates. Use triangle coordinates: place B at (0,0), C at (14,0) (since BC=14). Then we need coordinates of A such that AB=13, AC=15. So A is somewhere above x-axis.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.46149979,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461502143,"data":"       | Let A = (x_A, y_A). Then distances:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461513007,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461514844,"data":"       | AB^2 = x_A^2 + y_A^2 = 13^2 = 169.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461516727,"data":"       | AC^2 = (x_A-14)^2 + y_A^2 = 15^2 = 225.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461518747,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.46152047,"data":"       | Subtract: (x_A-14)^2 - x_A^2 = 225-169 = 56.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461522325,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461524057,"data":"       | Compute: (x_A^2 -28 x_A +196) - x_A^2 = -28 x_A +196 = 56 =\u003e -28 x_A = -140 =\u003e x_A = 5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461526198,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461527914,"data":"       | Then y_A^2 = 169 - x_A^2 = 169 - 25 = 144 =\u003e y_A = 12 (positive). So A = (5,12). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.46152997,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.46153175,"data":"       | Now M midpoint of BC: B (0,0), C (14,0) =\u003e M = (7,0).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461533774,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461537388,"data":"       | Now circumcircle of triangle ABC: find its center O (circumcenter). Since triangle not right? Actually sides 13-14-15, it's scalene but close to right? Let's compute: 13^2 +14^2 = 169+196=365, 15^2=225, not right. So compute circumcenter using perpendicular bisectors.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461540027,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461541814,"data":"       | But we need point P on circumcircle such that line AP passes through M (i.e., M lies on line AP). So P is intersection of circumcircle with line through A and M (i.e., line AM) other than A (since A also on circumcircle). So P is the second intersection of line AM with circumcircle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461544377,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461546072,"data":"       | Thus we can compute coordinates of P by intersecting line AM with circumcircle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461548098,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461549823,"data":"       | Then we need point Q on segment AM such that angle PBQ = angle PCQ. Q is on AM interior.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461551807,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.4615538,"data":"       | We need to find Q satisfying equality of angles at B and C with lines BQ, CQ to P.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461555888,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461557612,"data":"       | Interpretation: For point Q on line AM, we consider angles ∠PBQ (angle between PB and QB) and ∠PCQ (angle between PC and QC). We need them equal.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461559961,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.46156165,"data":"       | Goal: find AQ length (distance from A to Q). Since Q lies on AM, which is a line segment from A (5,12) to M (7,0). So we can param.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461563915,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461565618,"data":"       | Let’s compute using analytic geometry.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461567507,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.46156924,"data":"       | Let’s compute coordinates using Python sympy to solve for Q.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461571232,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461572905,"data":"       | Approach:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461577287,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461579173,"data":"       | - Compute circumcircle equation: find center O and radius R.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461581091,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461582821,"data":"       | - Compute line AM equation param: A + t*(M-A), where t from 0 to 1 for points between A and M. At t=0 =\u003e A; t=1 =\u003e M.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.46158504,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461586791,"data":"       | - Find P: intersection of line AM with circumcircle other than A. Solve for t such that point lies on circle. There will be two solutions: t=0 (A) and t=t_P (for P). Compute t_P.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461589088,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461590797,"data":"       | - Then we have coordinates of P.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.46159265,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461594358,"data":"       | - Next, we need Q on AM: param with variable s (0 \u003c= s \u003c= 1). Coordinates: Q = A + s*(M-A). Need to satisfy angle equality.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461596707,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461598444,"data":"       | Angle equality condition: angle between vectors PB and QB equals angle between PC and QC.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461600467,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461602567,"data":"       | We can use dot product formula: For vectors u,v, angle between them satisfies cosθ = (u·v)/(|u||v|). So equality of angles means cosθ1 = cosθ2, but also angles are between 0 and π. Since both angles are acute? Not sure but we can equate cos values.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461605292,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461607023,"data":"       | Thus condition: (PB·QB)/(|PB||QB|) = (PC·QC)/(|PC||QC|).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461609014,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461610707,"data":"       | Simplify: cross-multiply: (PB·QB) * |PC||QC| = (PC·QC) * |PB||QB|.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461612701,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461614378,"data":"       | But we can square both sides to avoid square roots: ((PB·QB)^2) * (|PC|^2 * |QC|^2) = ((PC·QC)^2) * (|PB|^2 * |QB|^2). However that's messy.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461616643,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.461618324,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can use oriented angle equality: maybe there is a property: Q is the internal center of Apollonius? Not.\n"}
