,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.462508247,"data":"       | analysisWe need to solve geometry problem.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.462510057,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.462511788,"data":"       | We have triangle ABC with sides 13,14,15. So it's scalene. Compute coordinates, circumcircle, etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.462513805,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.462515518,"data":"       | Let’s set up coordinate system: place B at (0,0), C at (14,0) (since BC=14). Then we need point A such that AB=13, AC=15.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.462521525,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.462523251,"data":"       | Let A = (x_A, y_A). Then distance to B: sqrt(x_A^2 + y_A^2) = 13 =\u003e x_A^2 + y_A^2 = 169.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.462525307,"data":"       | Distance to C: sqrt((x_A-14)^2 + y_A^2) = 15 =\u003e (x_A-14)^2 + y_A^2 = 225.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.462527282,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.462529346,"data":"       | Subtract first equation from second: (x_A-14)^2 - x_A^2 = 225-169 = 56.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.462531282,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.462532931,"data":"       | Compute: (x_A^2 -28 x_A +196) - x_A^2 = -28 x_A +196 = 56 =\u003e -28 x_A = -140 =\u003e x_A = 5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.462535102,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.462536849,"data":"       | Then plug into x_A^2 + y_A^2 = 169 =\u003e 25 + y_A^2 = 169 =\u003e y_A^2 = 144 =\u003e y_A = 12 or -12. Choose positive for orientation: y_A = 12.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.462539072,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.46254079,"data":"       | Thus coordinates: A(5,12), B(0,0), C(14,0). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.462542626,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.462544352,"data":"       | Now M is midpoint of BC: M = (7,0).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.462546144,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.462547911,"data":"       | We need point P on circumcircle of triangle ABC such that M lies on line AP (i.e., A, M, P are collinear). Since M is on BC line (x-axis), line AM is from A(5,12) to M(7,0). So line passes through these points; we need to extend beyond M to intersect circumcircle again at P (distinct from A). So P is second intersection of line AM with circumcircle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.462550614,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.462552322,"data":"       | Compute circumcircle of triangle ABC: find its center O and radius R.\n"}
