,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.463671219,"data":"       | Now we have line AM param equation: we can find Q on segment AM such that angle PBQ = angle PCQ. Q lies on AM between A and M.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.463675196,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.463676985,"data":"       | Define Q = A + t*(M-A) where t in [0,1] (t=0 at A, t=1 at M). So coordinates: Q = (5 + t*(7-5), 12 + t*(0-12)) = (5+2t, 12-12t) = (5+2t, 12(1 - t)). So Q = (5+2t, 12-12t).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.463679179,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.463680921,"data":"       | We need condition: angle PBQ = angle PCQ. That is, the measure of angle between lines BP and BQ equals angle between CP and CQ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.463683008,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.463684745,"data":"       | Angles measured at B and C respectively? Wait: angle PBQ is angle with vertex B? Actually notation ∠PBQ means angle with vertex B, formed by points P-B-Q. So lines BP and BQ. Similarly ∠PCQ is angle with vertex C, lines CP and CQ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.463687276,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.463689037,"data":"       | Thus condition: ∠(BP, BQ) = ∠(CP, CQ). Equivalent to equality of oriented angles? Usually magnitude.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.463691077,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.463692789,"data":"       | Thus we need to find t such that the angle between vectors (P - B) and (Q - B) equals angle between vectors (P - C) and (Q - C). Use dot product to equate cos of angles.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.463695012,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.463696732,"data":"       | Let vectors: u = P - B, v = Q - B. Cosθ1 = (u·v)/(|u||v|). Similarly for C: u' = P - C, v' = Q - C. Condition: cosθ1 = cosθ2 (since angle between 0 and π). Should hold.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.46369909,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.463700844,"data":"       | Alternatively we could use equality of oriented angle using cross product magnitude ratio? But cos equality may give two possibilities: θ and π-θ produce same cos. However angles likely acute? Let's check.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.463703293,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.463705016,"data":"       | We can solve equation (u·v)/(|u||v|) = (u'·v')/(|u'||v'|). Since lengths positive.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.4637071,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.463709124,"data":"       | Simplify: (u·v) * |u'||v'| = (u'·v') * |u||v|.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.463711062,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.463712772,"data":"       | But we can also square both sides to avoid radicals.\n"}
