,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.464360183,"data":"     [Reasoning] (2486 chars):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.464361964,"data":"       | analysisWe need to solve geometry problem.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.46436384,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.464366006,"data":"       | We have triangle ABC with side lengths AB=13, BC=14, CA=15. So it's scalene. M is midpoint of BC. P is point on circumcircle such that M lies on line AP (i.e., line through A and M extended meets circumcircle again at P). So P is the second intersection of line AM with circumcircle (other than A). Then there exists a unique point Q on segment AM such that angle PBQ = angle PCQ. So Q is interior point on AM where BQ and CQ make equal angles with lines PB and PC respectively? Wait: angle PBQ is angle between PB and BQ; angle PCQ is angle between PC and CQ. So we need Q on AM such that those two angles are equal. Equivalent to Q being point on AM where the lines BQ and CQ are symmetric with respect to some line? Actually condition: ∠PBQ = ∠PCQ. This is angle at B and C? Wait: ∠PBQ is angle with vertex B? Actually notation: ∠PBQ means angle formed by points P-B-Q, vertex at B. So angle between BP and BQ. Similarly ∠PCQ is angle between CP and CQ, vertex at C. So condition: angle between BP and BQ equals angle between CP and CQ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.464370782,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.464372631,"data":"       | We need to find AQ length.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.464374421,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.464376151,"data":"       | Goal: compute AQ expressed as m/√n, with m,n relatively prime positive integers. Find m+n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.464378182,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.464379882,"data":"       | We need to compute geometry.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.464383797,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.464385564,"data":"       | Let's set coordinates: Use standard coordinate geometry.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.464387479,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.46438929,"data":"       | Given side lengths, we can place triangle conveniently. Let's put B at (0,0), C at (14,0) (since BC=14). Then we need point A such that AB=13, AC=15. So A coordinates (x_A, y_A) satisfying distance to B: sqrt(x^2 + y^2) = 13; distance to C: sqrt((x-14)^2 + y^2) = 15. Solve.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.464391816,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.464393532,"data":"       | Let x^2 + y^2 = 13^2 = 169. (1)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.464395442,"data":"       | (x-14)^2 + y^2 = 15^2 = 225. (2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.464397174,"data":"       | Subtract (2)-(1): (x-14)^2 - x^2 = 225-169=56.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.464399014,"data":"       | Expand: (x^2 -28x +196) - x^2 = -28x +196 =56 =\u003e -28x = -140 =\u003e x = 5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.464401121,"data":"       | Plug into (1): 5^2 + y^2 = 169 =\u003e 25 + y^2 = 169 =\u003e y^2 = 144 =\u003e y = 12 (positive orientation). So A = (5,12). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.464403458,"data":"       | \n"}
