,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.465582217,"data":"       | analysisSo P = (567/74, -147/37). Let's simplify: -147/37 = -147/37 ≈ -3.97297. 567/74 ≈ 7.66216.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.465586406,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.465588172,"data":"       | Check that M is between A and P? Let's compute vector: A (5,12) to M (7,0) to P (approx 7.662,-3.973). Indeed M lies between A and P along line.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.465590292,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.46559255,"data":"       | Now we have points B(0,0), C(14,0). Need point Q on segment AM such that angle PBQ = angle PCQ. That is, from B and C to Q, the lines BQ and CQ make equal angles with BP and CP respectively? Wait angle PBQ is angle formed by PB and BQ at B? Actually angle PBQ: vertex at B, sides BP and BQ. Similarly angle PCQ: vertex at C, sides PC and CQ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.465595223,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.46559691,"data":"       | So we need Q on AM such that angle between PB and BQ equals angle between PC and CQ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.465598836,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.465600546,"data":"       | This is reminiscent of Apollonius circle or some equal angle condition leads to Q being intersection of some circle with AM.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.465602576,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.465604285,"data":"       | Goal: find Q on AM such that ∠PBQ = ∠PCQ. Equivalent to BQ and CQ being isogonal lines with respect to angle BPC? Not exactly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.465606418,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.465608212,"data":"       | But we can treat as unknown point Q = A + t*(M-A) for t in [0,1]. Find t such that angle equality holds.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.465610189,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.465611872,"data":"       | Let Q = (1-s)*A + s*M (or using parameter λ). Let's param.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.465613799,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.465615573,"data":"       | Let Q = A + u*(M - A) where u in [0,1]. So coordinates: Q = (5+2u, 12 -12u) because M-A = (2, -12). Indeed A (5,12) to M (7,0) vector (2,-12). So Q = (5+2u, 12-12u). When u=0 =\u003e A, u=1 =\u003e M.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.465617876,"data":"       | \n"}
