,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.467224632,"data":"       | Given points B, C, P, the set of points Q such that angle PBQ = angle PCQ is the perpendicular bisector of arc BC of circumcircle of triangle BPC? Let's analyze.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.467226866,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.467228749,"data":"       | Consider quadrilateral B P C Q. Condition angle PBQ (at B) equals angle PCQ (at C). This suggests that arcs subtended by same chord? For a given circle passing through B, C, P, and Q, the inscribed angles that subtend the same chord are equal. For example, if we consider chord PQ, then angle PBQ is subtended by chord PQ at point B, and angle PCQ is subtended by chord PQ at point C. If points B and C lie on the same circle as P and Q, then indeed angle PBQ = angle PCQ because they subtend chord PQ. However does condition guarantee that Q lies on circumcircle of triangle BPC? Not necessarily; but if Q lies on circle through B, C, P, then both angles subtend the same chord PQ, giving equality. Conversely, if angle PBQ = angle PCQ, then B, C, P, Q are concyclic? Let's think: For quadrilateral BPCQ, equality of angles subtended by same chord PQ would require B and C to be on the same side of line PQ. Indeed, if B, C, P, Q are concyclic, then inscribed angles subtending same chord PQ are equal: angle PBQ equals angle PCQ. So condition is equivalent to B, C, P, Q concyclic (provided they are not degenerate). However there is also possibility where B, P, Q are collinear? No.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.467235951,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.467238224,"data":"       | Thus condition \"angle PBQ = angle PCQ\" is equivalent to points B, C, P, Q being concyclic. Because for quadrilateral BPCQ, equality of angles at B and C subtended by same chord PQ indicates cyclic.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.467240695,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.467242443,"data":"       | Therefore Q is intersection of line AM with the circumcircle of triangle BPC (the circle passing through B, C, P). Since Q is on segment AM, there may be two intersections (line AM may intersect the circle at two points; one may be between A and M). The problem says there exists a unique point Q on segment AM satisfying condition, so we need the intersection of line AM with circumcircle of BPC that lies on segment AM.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.467245405,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.467247238,"data":"       | Thus we need to compute intersection of line AM and circumcircle of BPC, then find distance from A to that intersection point Q (AQ). That's our target.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.467249436,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.467251169,"data":"       | Given triangle ABC sides 13,14,15. We can coordinate geometry.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.467253121,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.467254841,"data":"       | Set coordinates: Let's place triangle conveniently.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.467256742,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.467258449,"data":"       | Let B = (0,0). Let C = (14,0) because BC = 14 along x-axis.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.467260405,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.467262136,"data":"       | Then we need coordinates of A such that AB=13, AC=15.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.467264046,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.467265862,"data":"       | Let A = (x_A, y_A) with y_A\u003e0 (choose orientation). Then AB^2 = x_A^2 + y_A^2 = 13^2=169.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.467267933,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.467269692,"data":"       | AC^2 = (x_A-14)^2 + y_A^2 = 15^2=225.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.467271582,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.467273283,"data":"       | Subtract: (x_A-14)^2 - x_A^2 = 225-169=56.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.467275169,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.467276952,"data":"       | Expand: (x_A^2 -28x_A +196) - x_A^2 = -28x_A +196 =56 -\u003e -28x_A = -140 -\u003e x_A = 5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.467280158,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.467282029,"data":"       | Plug into AB: 5^2 + y_A^2 = 169 =\u003e 25 + y_A^2 = 169 =\u003e y_A^2 = 144 -\u003e y_A=12 (positive). So A = (5,12). Nice! Indeed triangle sides 13-14-15 is right? Wait 13-14-15 not right triangle, but coordinates produce right? Actually AB^2+BC^2 = 13^2+14^2=169+196=365 not 15^2=225. So not right. But coordinates show A at (5,12), B(0,0), C(14,0). Indeed AB length sqrt(5^2+12^2)=13; AC length sqrt( (5-14)^2+12^2 )= sqrt((-9)^2+12^2)= sqrt(81+144)= sqrt(225)=15. Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.467285311,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4372.467287072,"data":"       | Thus coordinates done.\n"}
