,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.315739704,"data":"       | analysisWe need number of subsets of {1,...,10} that contain exactly one pair of consecutive integers. That is, among all pairs (i,i+1) with i=1..9, exactly one of those pairs is present as both elements in the subset, and no other consecutive pair appears. Also note that a subset could contain more than two elements, but only one adjacent pair. Also note that the pair could be part of larger runs? If we have three consecutive numbers like {3,4,5}, that contains two pairs (3,4) and (4,5). That would be two pairs, not allowed. So any subset cannot contain three consecutive numbers because that would create at least two pairs. Also cannot contain two disjoint pairs like {1,2,5,6}. So exactly one adjacent pair, and all other elements must be isolated (no adjacency with any other element). Also the pair itself must be exactly two consecutive numbers; we cannot have larger block.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.315742188,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.315743253,"data":"       | Thus we need to count subsets of {1..10} such that the set of indices includes exactly one adjacency.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.31574445,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.315745459,"data":"       | We can approach combinatorially.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.315746539,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.315748488,"data":"       | Let’s think of representing the subset as a binary string of length 10 (positions 1..10) where 1 indicates element present. Condition: exactly one occurrence of \"11\" as a substring, and no overlapping occurrences; also cannot have \"111\" because that would contain two overlapping \"11\". So we need binary strings of length 10 with exactly one occurrence of \"11\", and no other occurrence of \"11\". Also we cannot have \"1011\"? Wait \"1011\" includes \"11\" at positions 3-4 only, but also there is a \"1\" at position 1 separated by 0, fine. So condition: exactly one pair of consecutive 1's, and no other consecutive 1's anywhere else. So the string must have exactly one block of two consecutive 1's, and all other 1's must be isolated (i.e., separated by at least one 0 from any other 1). Also we cannot have block longer than 2 because that would contain more than one pair. So the string consists of isolated 1's and exactly one block of length 2, with zeros elsewhere.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.315754521,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.315755644,"data":"       | Thus we need to count binary strings length 10 with exactly one block of length 2 and any number (including zero) of isolated 1's elsewhere, such that isolated 1's are not adjacent to each other or to the block (i.e., there must be zeros separating). However, the block of length 2 can be adjacent to isolated 1's? If we have block at positions i,i+1, and an isolated 1 at position i-1, then we would have consecutive 1's at i-1,i (since i-1 is 1, i is 1) which would create another pair. So not allowed. Similarly position i+2 cannot be 1. So the block must be surrounded by zeros (or edges). So the block must be isolated by zeros on both sides unless at boundaries.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.315757786,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.315758916,"data":"       | Thus we have to place a block of length 2 somewhere, and then place isolated 1's in the remaining positions such that no two are adjacent and also not adjacent to the block.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.315760273,"data":"       | \n"}
