,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.315982182,"data":"       | Let’s suppose we have k isolated singletons (k≥0). Then total occupied positions = 2 (double block) + k*1 = k+2. The number of blocks total = k + 1 (the double block plus k singles). We need to place these blocks in order along the line, with gaps of zeros between them: there are (k+1) blocks, so there are k internal gaps between consecutive blocks that must be at least 1 zero each. Additionally there are two external gaps (leftmost and rightmost) which can be zero or more zeros.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.315984545,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.315986344,"data":"       | Let the lengths of gaps be: g0 (before first block), g1 (between block1 and block2), ..., g_{k} (between block_{k} and block_{k+1}), g_{k+1} (after last block). There are (k+2) gaps total (including ends). Internal gaps g1,...,g_k must be at least 1. g0,g_{k+1} ≥ 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.315991623,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.315993151,"data":"       | Total zeros = total length 10 minus occupied positions (k+2). So sum_{i=0}^{k+1} g_i = 10 - (k+2) = 8 - k.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.315995056,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.315996907,"data":"       | We need to count the number of nonnegative integer solutions to these gaps with constraints: g0,g_{k+1} ≥ 0; g1,...,g_k ≥ 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.315999201,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316000637,"data":"       | Let’s define h_i = g_i for i=0,k+1; and for internal gaps define h_i = g_i - 1 (so h_i ≥ 0). Then sum_{i=0}^{k+1} g_i = g0 + g_{k+1} + Σ_{i=1}^{k} (h_i+1) = g0 + g_{k+1} + Σ_{i=1}^{k} h_i + k = 8 - k.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316002905,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316004509,"data":"       | Thus g0 + g_{k+1} + Σ_{i=1}^{k} h_i = 8 - 2k.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316006279,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316007896,"data":"       | All variables nonnegative integers. Number of solutions = C((8 - 2k) + (k+2) -1, (k+2)-1) = C(8 - 2k + k+1, k+1) = C(9 - k, k+1). Wait compute: number of variables = (k+2) (g0,g_{k+1},h_1,...,h_k) = k+2 variables. Sum = 8 - 2k.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316010295,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316011922,"data":"       | Number of nonnegative integer solutions to sum of m variables = N is C(N+m-1, m-1) provided N ≥ 0. Here m = k+2, N = 8 - 2k. So count = C((8 - 2k)+(k+2)-1, (k+2)-1) = C(8 - 2k + k + 1, k+1) = C(9 - k, k+1). Indeed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316014247,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316016144,"data":"       | But we also need to consider ordering of blocks: the double block can be placed among the singles in any position (i.e., the sequence of blocks). Since we treat blocks as distinct types: one double block (special) and k identical singletons (identical). The arrangement of blocks matters because the gaps are defined in order. However our counting using gaps implicitly considered a specific order: we have a sequence of (k+1) blocks, but we didn't specify which block is double. Actually we need to consider that the double block could be any of the (k+1) positions among the blocks. The gaps variables g0,...,g_{k+1} are defined relative to the order of blocks as they appear left to right. If we fix which block is double and which are singles, the gaps count is as above. Since the singles are indistinguishable, the number of distinct block orderings is (k+1) possible positions for the double block among the k+1 blocks (since the rest are all singles). So we need to multiply by (k+1) for each arrangement.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316020043,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316021071,"data":"       | Thus for each k (number of isolated singles), the number of subsets is (k+1) * C(9 - k, k+1), provided 8 - 2k \u003e= 0 (i.e., k ≤ 4). Also k must be integer \u003e=0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.3160225,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316023498,"data":"       | So k can be 0,1,2,3,4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316024699,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316025695,"data":"       | Let's compute each term.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316026988,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316029642,"data":"       | k=0: No isolated singles, only the double block. Then we have (k+1)=1 * C(9 - 0, 1) = C(9,1)=9. This counts subsets with exactly the pair and no other elements. Indeed there are 9 possible pairs (1-2,...,9-10). So 9 subsets.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316031099,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316032119,"data":"       | k=1: One isolated singleton besides the pair. Count = (k+1)=2 * C(9 - 1, 2) = 2 * C(8,2) = 2 * 28 = 56.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316033318,"data":"       | \n"}
