,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316184632,"data":"       | Thus the problem reduces to: For each i from 1 to 9, count subsets S of {1..10} that contain i,i+1, exclude i-1 and i+2 (if they exist), and among remaining numbers (excluding i,i+1,i-1,i+2) we may select any subset with no consecutive numbers (within themselves). Also we must ensure no other consecutive pairs across the gap? Since we excluded i-1 and i+2, there is no adjacency with the pair. Among the rest, we must avoid any consecutive numbers (like 4 and 5) that are both selected. So it's a standard \"no two consecutive numbers\" subset counting problem on a set of size N' (the remaining numbers after removing the pair and its immediate neighbors). However, note that removal of i-1 and i+2 may split the remaining numbers into up to three blocks: left side before i-1 (i.e., numbers 1..i-2), middle block maybe empty (since i-1 excluded), right side after i+2 (i.e., numbers i+3..10). Actually after excluding i-1 and i+2, the remaining numbers are: 1..i-2 (if i≥3) and i+3..10 (if i≤8). So there are at most two blocks: left block of length L = i-2 (since numbers up to i-2 inclusive), and right block of length R = 10-(i+2) = 8 - i (since i+3..10 inclusive). For i=1, left block length L = -1? Actually i-2 = -1, meaning no left block. For i=1, we exclude i-1=0 (nonexistent) and i+2=3. So remaining numbers are {4,5,6,7,8,9,10}. That's block length 7 (since 4..10). So left block length 0, right block length 10-(i+2)=10-3=7. For i=2, left block numbers up to i-2 =0 (none), right block numbers i+3..10 =5..10 length 6. For i=9, left block numbers 1..7? Wait i=9: i-2=7 left block length 7, right block numbers i+3..10 =12..10 none. So length 0.\n"}
