,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316393489,"data":"       | analysisWe need to find number of subsets of {1,...,10} that contain exactly one pair of consecutive integers. Means subset can have any size, but among its elements, there is exactly one pair (i,i+1) both present, and no other consecutive pair present. Also note that a subset could contain three consecutive numbers? That would contain two pairs (i,i+1) and (i+1,i+2) which would be more than one pair, so not allowed. So subset must avoid any other consecutive pairs besides the unique pair. So we must count subsets where there exists exactly one adjacency.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316395435,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316396493,"data":"       | Interpretation: a subset S ⊆ {1,...,10} such that the set of i with i,i+1 ∈ S has size exactly 1. So there is exactly one i where both i and i+1 are in S. No other i.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.3163979,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316398931,"data":"       | Goal: count number of subsets satisfying that.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316400051,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316401059,"data":"       | We can approach combinatorially.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316402128,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316403153,"data":"       | Let the unique consecutive pair be (k, k+1) for some k ∈ {1,...,9}. Then we need to choose other elements of S, but must avoid any other adjacency, i.e., we cannot include any element adjacent to any other chosen element besides the pair.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.31640461,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316405689,"data":"       | Specifically, we cannot include (k-1) because then (k-1,k) would be another consecutive pair. Similarly cannot include (k+2) because (k+1,k+2) would be another pair. Also we cannot include any other pair of consecutive numbers elsewhere (i,i+1) for i ≠ k. So the rest of the selected elements must be from the set {1,...,10} \\ {k-1,k,k+1,k+2} (with adjustments at ends). And they must be chosen such that no two are consecutive.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.31640751,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.31640855,"data":"       | Thus we reduce to counting subsets of a reduced set (with some forbidden elements) with no consecutive numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316409778,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316412589,"data":"       | But we also need to consider that the pair (k,k+1) themselves are included. So each subset is formed by taking the pair (k,k+1) and adding a subset T of the remaining numbers (excluding k-1,k,k+1,k+2) such that T contains no consecutive numbers (within the original ordering) and also none of its elements is adjacent to each other (obviously) and also not adjacent to the pair (but we already removed k-1 and k+2). However, there might be adjacency across the gap due to removal? For example, if we remove k-1 and k+2, the remaining numbers may have a gap at those positions. But we also need to ensure that T does not contain any consecutive numbers among themselves. Since we removed k-1 and k+2, the remaining numbers are a collection of intervals separated by the removed positions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316414967,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316416023,"data":"       | Thus the counting problem reduces to: For each k, consider the set of numbers from 1..10 excluding the four numbers {k-1,k,k+1,k+2} (with the understanding that if k=1, then k-1=0 not in set, similarly if k=9, k+2=11 not in set). Then count the number of subsets of that reduced set with no two consecutive numbers. That's the number of ways to choose additional elements besides the mandatory pair.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316417719,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316418739,"data":"       | We then sum over k.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316419816,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316420851,"data":"       | But we must be careful: there may be overlapping counts? No, each subset has exactly one pair; that pair is uniquely determined (the unique consecutive pair). So each subset counted exactly once.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316422221,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316423243,"data":"       | Thus answer = Σ_{k=1}^{9} f(k) where f(k) = number of subsets of {1,...,10}\\{k-1,k,k+1,k+2} (excluding out-of-range) with no consecutive numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316424615,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316425643,"data":"       | Now compute f(k). Let's define intervals after removal.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316426751,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316427791,"data":"       | General case: For interior k (2 ≤ k ≤ 8). Then we remove four numbers: k-1, k, k+1, k+2. So the remaining numbers are 1..k-2 and k+3..10. These are two intervals: left interval length L = k-2 (since numbers 1 to k-2 inclusive). Right interval length R = 10 - (k+2) = 8 - k (since from k+3 to 10 inclusive). Actually 10 - (k+2) = 8 - k? Let's compute: 10 - (k+2) = 8 - k. Yes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316429643,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316430924,"data":"       | So we have two disjoint intervals of lengths L and R, each independent for selecting subsets with no consecutive numbers (within each interval). The total number of subsets with no consecutive numbers from each interval is product of counts for each interval (since no adjacency across intervals due to gap). So f(k) = a(L) * a(R), where a(n) = number of subsets of {1,...,n} with no consecutive numbers (including empty set). That's classic: a(n) = F_{n+2}, where F_1=1, F_2=1, etc. Because number of binary strings length n with no consecutive 1's is F_{n+2}. So a(n) = F_{n+2}.\n"}
