,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316660285,"data":"       | analysisWe need number of subsets of {1,...,10} that contain exactly one pair of consecutive integers. That means within the subset, there is exactly one occurrence of two elements that are consecutive; all other pairs of elements are non-consecutive. Also note that a subset could contain more than two elements, but only one adjacent pair. Also, the pair could be part of larger block? For example subset {1,2,3}? That contains consecutive pairs (1,2) and (2,3) which are two pairs, so not allowed. Also {1,2,4,5}? Contains pairs (1,2) and (4,5): two pairs, not allowed. Also {1,2,3,5}? Contains pairs (1,2) and (2,3) (two pairs) not allowed. So exactly one adjacent pair.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316662302,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316663517,"data":"       | Interpretation: subset may contain the two consecutive numbers (i,i+1) as a pair, and no other consecutive numbers appear. That means we cannot have any other j such that both j and j+1 are in subset. Also we cannot have any other occurrence of consecutive numbers overlapping with the pair? Overlap would be like {i-1,i,i+1}? That would have pairs (i-1,i) and (i,i+1) two pairs, not allowed. So can't have any additional element adjacent to either i or i+1 (except the pair themselves). So the subset must contain exactly one adjacent pair and all other elements are isolated, i.e., not adjacent to any other element in the subset.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316665471,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.31666645,"data":"       | Thus we need to count subsets of {1,...,10} where we have exactly one block of size 2 consisting of consecutive numbers, and all other elements are separated by at least 2 from each other and also from the block.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316667751,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316668718,"data":"       | Essentially we can think of arranging \"selected\" positions with constraint that there is exactly one adjacency of length 2, and no other adjacencies.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316670036,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316671002,"data":"       | We can approach combinatorially. Let's denote the pair as (i,i+1). For each possible i from 1 to 9 (since pair must be within 1..10). Choose i for the adjacent pair. Then we need to select any subset of the remaining numbers (excluding i,i+1) such that none of the selected numbers are consecutive with each other, nor consecutive with i or i+1. That is, from the set {1,...,10} \\ {i,i+1}, we must select a subset with no two consecutive numbers, and also cannot select numbers i-1 or i+2 (if they exist) because they would be consecutive with the pair.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316672884,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316674424,"data":"       | Thus after fixing i, we forbid i-1 and i+2 as well (if within range). So the available numbers to possibly select are the remaining numbers after removing i,i+1,i-1,i+2 (where applicable). Among these, we must select a subset with no two consecutive numbers (i.e., an independent set in a path graph). That's classic combinatorial count: number of subsets of a set of size n with no consecutive elements is F_{n+2} (Fibonacci). But we also need to consider that the set is not contiguous; after removing certain elements, the remaining numbers may split into segments. The condition of no consecutive numbers across the removed gaps is automatically satisfied because the removed numbers create breaks. So we can treat each contiguous block of available numbers independently, count subsets with no adjacencies within each block, and multiply.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316678439,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316679466,"data":"       | Thus we need to sum over i from 1 to 9 the number of subsets of the remaining numbers (excluding i,i+1,i-1,i+2) with no adjacencies.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316680669,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.31668163,"data":"       | Let's compute manually using combinatorics or via program to verify.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.31668274,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316683726,"data":"       | But also note that we could also have subsets where the pair is at the ends (i=1 or i=9). For i=1, the pair is {1,2}. Then i-1=0 not in set, i+2=3, which we must exclude (cannot select 3). So we forbid 3 as well. So available numbers are {4,5,6,7,8,9,10} (since 1,2,3 removed). That's a contiguous block of size 7. Need subsets with no consecutive numbers from {4..10}. That's same as subsets of size 7 line with no adjacencies: count = F_{7+2}=F_9. Fibonacci numbers: F1=1, F2=1, F3=2, F4=3, F5=5, F6=8, F7=13, F8=21, F9=34. So 34 subsets.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316685536,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316686514,"data":"       | But also note we could have the empty subset (i.e., no extra elements beyond the pair). That's allowed because we need exactly one pair. So the subset consisting only of the pair {1,2} is counted. Indeed our counting includes empty selection from remaining numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316687943,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316688901,"data":"       | Similarly for i=9 pair {9,10}, exclude i-1=8 and i+2=11(not exist). So remove 8,9,10. Available numbers are {1,2,3,4,5,6,7}. That's block size 7, also 34 subsets.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.31669014,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316691095,"data":"       | For i=2 pair {2,3}. Then we must exclude i-1=1 and i+2=4. So removed numbers: 1,2,3,4. Remaining numbers: {5,6,7,8,9,10}. That's block size 6. Count subsets with no adjacencies in size 6: F_{8}=21.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316692433,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.316693635,"data":"       | Similarly i=3 pair {3,4}. Exclude 2 and 5. Removed: 2,3,4,5. Remaining numbers: {1,6,7,8,9,10}. That's two blocks: block1 size 1 (just {1}) and block2 size 5 ({6..10}). Need subsets with no adjacencies within each block. For block of size 1, subsets with no adjacencies: either empty or {1} =\u003e 2 possibilities (F_{1+2}=F_3=2). For block of size 5, subsets count = F_{7}=13. Multiply: 2*13=26.\n"}
