,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.317019447,"data":"       | analysisWe need to find the number of subsets of {1,...,10} that contain exactly one pair of consecutive integers. That means among all elements in the subset, there is exactly one pair (i,i+1) that both appear in the subset. No other consecutive pairs allowed. So the subset can contain any number of elements, but the only adjacency is exactly one pair.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.317020977,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.317022191,"data":"       | Interpretation: \"Exactly one pair of consecutive integers\" means there exists exactly one unordered pair {i,i+1} such that both are present, and for all other i, not both i,i+1 present. The pair could be part of a larger block? Wait: Could we have three consecutive numbers like {3,4,5}? That would contain two pairs of consecutive integers: (3,4) and (4,5). That would be two pairs, not allowed. So any subset that contains a block of length \u003e2 would have multiple consecutive pairs, violating condition. So the subset may contain at most one block of length 2, and otherwise no two elements are consecutive. So the subset structure: Choose one adjacent pair (i,i+1) as the unique consecutive pair. Then choose any subset of the remaining numbers such that none of those numbers are adjacent to each other, nor adjacent to the selected pair (i,i+1) (except the pair itself). Also we must ensure we don't create another consecutive pair with the chosen pair's members. That is, we cannot select i-1 or i+2 because those would create a new consecutive pair with i or i+1 respectively.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.317024901,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.317025901,"data":"       | Thus the problem reduces to: Choose an index i (1 \u003c= i \u003c= 9) for the unique adjacent pair (i,i+1). Then from the remaining numbers (i.e., the set {1,...,10} minus {i,i+1}), we need to select a subset S such that (a) no two elements of S are consecutive, (b) S does not contain i-1 or i+2 (if they exist). Also S may be empty. Also ensure that i-1 and i+2 are not selected; they are removed from available numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.317027625,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.317028609,"data":"       | So effectively we have a set of numbers where we have removed i-1, i, i+1, i+2 (if they exist) leaving some \"gaps\" and we want to select a subset with no consecutive numbers. However note that numbers far from the pair could still be adjacent among themselves, but we must avoid that. So we need to count the number of subsets of a certain \"remaining\" set with no consecutive numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.317030395,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.317031356,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can think of constructing subsets with exactly one adjacency, using combinatorial enumeration via stars and bars or recurrence.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.317032775,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4400.317033734,"data":"       | But we can also brute-force compute using Python: iterate over all subsets of {1,...,10} (2^10=1024) and test condition, count. That is easy. But the problem likely expects a combinatorial solution. But we can compute answer quickly and then attempt to produce reasoning.\n"}
