,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66828118,"data":"       | Thus cos A cos B = sin A sin B - 1/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66828224,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668283623,"data":"       | But sin A sin B - cos A cos B = -cos(A+B)? Wait identity: cos(A+B) = cos A cos B - sin A sin B. So sin A sin B - cos A cos B = -cos(A+B). So RHS = -cos(A+B) - 1/2? Actually sin A sin B - 1/2 = cos A cos B? Wait we have equation cos A cos B = sin A sin B - 1/2 =\u003e bring terms: sin A sin B - cos A cos B = 1/2 =\u003e -(cos(A+B)) = 1/2 =\u003e cos(A+B) = -1/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668285554,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668286603,"data":"       | Thus A + B = 2π/3? Actually cos θ = -1/2 =\u003e θ = 2π/3 or 4π/3 etc. Since A,B are acute (0 to π/2), sum likely between 0 and π, so A+B = 2π/3 = 120°. Indeed A=45°, B=75°, sum=120°. Works.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668288273,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668289268,"data":"       | Similarly equation (3): sqrt{(1 - r^2)(1 - p^2)} = r p + 1/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668290386,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668291367,"data":"       | Left side = cos C cos A. RHS = sin C sin A + 1/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668292481,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668293478,"data":"       | Thus cos C cos A = sin C sin A + 1/2 =\u003e sin C sin A - cos C cos A = -1/2 =\u003e -cos(A+C) = -1/2 =\u003e cos(A+C) = 1/2 =\u003e A + C = π/3 = 60°. Indeed 45° + 15° = 60°.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668294951,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668295954,"data":"       | Thus we have system of angles A,B,C satisfying:\n"}
