,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66854208,"data":"       | analysisSeems only one positive solution.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668543194,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66854421,"data":"       | But we need to be rigorous. Let's attempt to solve analytically.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668545353,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66854658,"data":"       | Observation: The equations reminiscent of cos and sin? Because sqrt(2x - xy) = sqrt(x(2 - y)). Might relate to law of cos? Not sure.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668547854,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668548887,"data":"       | Let’s set a = sqrt(x), b = sqrt(y), c = sqrt(z). Then x = a^2, y = b^2, z = c^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66855005,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668551064,"data":"       | Compute sqrt(2x - xy) = sqrt(2a^2 - a^2 b^2) = a sqrt(2 - b^2). Similarly sqrt(2y - xy) = b sqrt(2 - a^2). So equation (1) becomes:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668552334,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668553339,"data":"       | a sqrt(2 - b^2) + b sqrt(2 - a^2) = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668554444,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668555454,"data":"       | Similarly:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668556501,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668557504,"data":"       | b sqrt(2 - c^2) + c sqrt(2 - b^2) = sqrt(2).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668558591,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668559616,"data":"       | c sqrt(2 - a^2) + a sqrt(2 - c^2) = sqrt(3).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668560693,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668561736,"data":"       | Now note that a,b,c are positive reals less than sqrt(2) maybe because sqrt(2 - a^2) must be real =\u003e a^2 \u003c= 2 =\u003e a \u003c= sqrt(2). Similarly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668563157,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668564177,"data":"       | Maybe we can set a = sqrt(2) sin α? Let's test: Let a = sqrt(2) sin A, then sqrt(2 - a^2) = sqrt(2 - 2 sin^2 A) = sqrt(2 cos^2 A) = sqrt(2) cos A. Then a sqrt(2 - b^2) becomes sqrt(2) sin A * sqrt(2) cos B = 2 sin A cos B.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66856564,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66856663,"data":"       | Similarly b sqrt(2 - a^2) = sqrt(2) sin B * sqrt(2) cos A = 2 sin B cos A.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668567793,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668568787,"data":"       | Thus equation (1) becomes:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668569844,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66857085,"data":"       | 2 sin A cos B + 2 sin B cos A = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66857192,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668572953,"data":"       | But 2 sin A cos B + 2 sin B cos A = 2 (sin A cos B + sin B cos A) = 2 sin(A + B). Because sin(A+B)= sin A cos B + cos A sin B.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66857589,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66857691,"data":"       | Thus equation (1) becomes:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668577991,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66857905,"data":"       | 2 sin(A + B) = 1 =\u003e sin(A + B) = 1/2 =\u003e A + B = π/6 or 5π/6 etc. Since A,B positive and less than π/2 (since sin positive and a,b positive). Also a,b \u003c= sqrt(2) =\u003e sin A \u003c=1 =\u003e A in [0,π/2]. So A+B in [0,π] . sin = 1/2 occurs at π/6 (≈30°) and 5π/6 (≈150°). But A+B cannot exceed π because each ≤ π/2, sum ≤ π. Both possible. But we need to consider positivity.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668581443,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668582427,"data":"       | Similarly define B = arcsin(b/√2), C = arcsin(c/√2). Then second equation becomes:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66858367,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668584706,"data":"       | 2 sin B cos C + 2 sin C cos B = sqrt(2) =\u003e 2 sin(B + C) = sqrt(2) =\u003e sin(B + C) = sqrt(2)/2 = 1/√2 =\u003e B + C = π/4 or 3π/4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668586193,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668587513,"data":"       | Third equation: 2 sin C cos A + 2 sin A cos C = sqrt(3) =\u003e 2 sin(C + A) = sqrt(3) =\u003e sin(C + A) = sqrt(3)/2 =\u003e C + A = π/3 or 2π/3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66858951,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668590531,"data":"       | Thus we have three equations:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668591714,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668592896,"data":"       | A + B = α where α ∈ {π/6, 5π/6}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668594359,"data":"       | B + C = β where β ∈ {π/4, 3π/4}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668595677,"data":"       | C + A = γ where γ ∈ {π/3, 2π/3}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66859695,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66859795,"data":"       | We need A,B,C ∈ (0,π/2). Also sin values positive.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66859926,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668600347,"data":"       | Now we need to find A,B,C satisfying these possibilities.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668601554,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668602676,"data":"       | Let’s denote unknowns A,B,C.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668603773,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668604769,"data":"       | We can solve linear system: adding first two: (A+B)+(B+C)=α+β =\u003e A+2B+C = α+β.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668606214,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668607216,"data":"       | But also C+A = γ =\u003e A+C = γ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668608496,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668609486,"data":"       | Subtract: (A+2B+C) - (A+C) = 2B = α+β - γ =\u003e B = (α+β-γ)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668610771,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668611751,"data":"       | Similarly, we can get A = α - B, C = β - B.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668612899,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66861392,"data":"       | Now we need to check which combination of α,β,γ yields positive angles less than π/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668615187,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668616209,"data":"       | Let's enumerate possibilities.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668619099,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668620134,"data":"       | assistantanalysis to=python codeimport sympy as sp\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668621267,"data":"       | import itertools, math\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668622306,"data":"       | poss_alpha=[sp.pi/6, 5*sp.pi/6]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668623376,"data":"       | poss_beta=[sp.pi/4, 3*sp.pi/4]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668624447,"data":"       | poss_gamma=[sp.pi/3, 2*sp.pi/3]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66862554,"data":"       | solutions=[]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66862658,"data":"       | for alpha in poss_alpha:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66862763,"data":"       |     for beta in poss_beta:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668628693,"data":"       |         for gamma in poss_gamma:\n"}
