,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668986967,"data":"       | analysisThat's 0.03125 = 1/32. So ((1-x)(1-y)(1-z))^2 = 1/32. Then m=1, n=32, m+n=33.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668988134,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668989147,"data":"       | But we must verify rational exactly. Let's attempt to find exact solution using algebraic manipulations.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668990354,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668991441,"data":"       | We suspect that (1-x)(1-y)(1-z) = sqrt(1/32) = 1/(4*sqrt{2})? Actually sqrt(1/32) = 1/(4*sqrt{2}) = sqrt{2}/8? Wait compute: sqrt(1/32) = 1/ sqrt{32} = 1/(4*sqrt{2}) = sqrt{2}/8. But squared is 1/32.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668992794,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668993813,"data":"       | Thus answer m+n = 33.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668994856,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66899589,"data":"       | But we need to be sure that there is unique solution positive reals. Could there be other solutions leading to same value? Let's attempt to find all solutions. Use solving with sympy rationalizing maybe.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668997234,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668998271,"data":"       | We can square equations to eliminate radicals. But careful about extraneous solutions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.668999443,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.6690006,"data":"       | Let's attempt to solve analytically.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669001684,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669003026,"data":"       | Let’s define:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669005824,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669006854,"data":"       | Equation 1: sqrt{x(2-y)} + sqrt{y(2-x)} = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669007974,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669008976,"data":"       | Let’s denote A = sqrt{x(2-y)}, B = sqrt{y(2-x)}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669010111,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669011134,"data":"       | Then A + B = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66901219,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669013194,"data":"       | Square: A^2 + B^2 + 2AB = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669014273,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669015317,"data":"       | But A^2 = x(2-y), B^2 = y(2-x). AB = sqrt{x(2-y) y(2-x)} = sqrt{xy (2-y)(2-x)}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669016474,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669017477,"data":"       | Thus we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669018514,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669019533,"data":"       | x(2-y) + y(2-x) + 2 sqrt{xy (2-y)(2-x)} = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669020616,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669021633,"data":"       | Simplify x(2-y) + y(2-x) = 2x - xy + 2y - xy = 2x + 2y - 2xy = 2(x + y - xy).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669022771,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669023763,"data":"       | Thus equation becomes:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66902482,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669025807,"data":"       | 2(x + y - xy) + 2 sqrt{xy (2-y)(2-x)} = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66902688,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669027883,"data":"       | Divide by 2: (x + y - xy) + sqrt{xy (2-y)(2-x)} = 1/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669028987,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66902999,"data":"       | Let’s set S = sqrt{xy (2-y)(2-x)}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669031073,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669032064,"data":"       | Thus we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669033154,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669034167,"data":"       | S = 1/2 - (x + y - xy). (Equation A)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66903527,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669036346,"data":"       | Similarly from second equation:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66903741,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669038406,"data":"       | sqrt{y(2-z)} + sqrt{z(2-y)} = sqrt{2}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669039523,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669040533,"data":"       | Let’s denote C = sqrt{y(2-z)}, D = sqrt{z(2-y)}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669041679,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669042673,"data":"       | C + D = sqrt{2}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669043717,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669044717,"data":"       | Squaring: y(2-z) + z(2-y) + 2 sqrt{yz (2-z)(2-y)} = 2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669045826,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669046857,"data":"       | Compute y(2-z) + z(2-y) = 2y - yz + 2z - yz = 2(y+z) - 2yz = 2(y+z - yz).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669048004,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66905068,"data":"       | Thus we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669051753,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66905284,"data":"       | 2(y+z - yz) + 2 sqrt{yz (2-z)(2-y)} = 2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669053931,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669054944,"data":"       | Divide by 2: (y+z - yz) + sqrt{yz (2-z)(2-y)} = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66905606,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669057073,"data":"       | Let T = sqrt{yz (2-z)(2-y)}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66905813,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669059124,"data":"       | Thus T = 1 - (y+z - yz). (Equation B)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669060223,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66906121,"data":"       | Third equation:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669062243,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66906324,"data":"       | sqrt{z(2-x)} + sqrt{x(2-z)} = sqrt{3}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669064314,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669065324,"data":"       | Let U = sqrt{z(2-x)}, V = sqrt{x(2-z)}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669066409,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669067393,"data":"       | U + V = sqrt{3}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66906844,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669069423,"data":"       | Square: z(2-x) + x(2-z) + 2 sqrt{xz (2-x)(2-z)} = 3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669070527,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669071564,"data":"       | Compute z(2-x) + x(2-z) = 2z - xz + 2x - xz = 2(x+z) - 2xz = 2(x+z - xz).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669073277,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66907426,"data":"       | Thus 2(x+z - xz) + 2 sqrt{xz (2-x)(2-z)} = 3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669075487,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66907674,"data":"       | Divide by 2: (x+z - xz) + sqrt{xz (2-x)(2-z)} = 3/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669077847,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669078994,"data":"       | Let W = sqrt{xz (2-x)(2-z)}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669080203,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66908137,"data":"       | Thus W = 3/2 - (x+z - xz). (Equation C)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669082566,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669083626,"data":"       | Now we have three equations for S, T, W expressed in terms of x,y,z.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669084873,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66908592,"data":"       | But also S^2 = xy (2-y)(2-x), etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669087127,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669088111,"data":"       | Potentially we can solve system.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669089283,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669090413,"data":"       | Alternatively maybe there is substitution using trigonometric forms.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669091544,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66909257,"data":"       | Observation: The expressions sqrt{x(2-y)} reminiscent of law of cosines? For triangle with sides? Not sure.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669093771,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669096407,"data":"       | Maybe we can set x = 1 - a, y = 1 - b, z = 1 - c? Because we need (1-x)(1-y)(1-z). Let's try.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669097599,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66909866,"data":"       | Let u = 1 - x, v = 1 - y, w = 1 - z. Then x = 1 - u, etc. Since x,y,z positive, u \u003c 1? Actually x positive =\u003e u \u003c 1, but could be \u003e0? Not necessarily; x could be \u003e1? But from numeric solution, x≈0.2929 \u003c1, y≈0.034 \u003c1, z≈0.741 \u003c1. So u,v,w positive.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669100449,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669101467,"data":"       | Now compute sqrt{x(2-y)} = sqrt{(1-u)(2 - (1 - v))} = sqrt{(1-u)(1+v)}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669102614,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669103607,"data":"       | Similarly sqrt{y(2-x)} = sqrt{(1-v)(1+u)}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669104723,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669105733,"data":"       | Thus first equation becomes sqrt{(1-u)(1+v)} + sqrt{(1-v)(1+u)} = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66910687,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669107863,"data":"       | Second: sqrt{(1-v)(1+w)} + sqrt{(1-w)(1+v)} = sqrt{2}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66910908,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669110147,"data":"       | Third: sqrt{(1-w)(1+u)} + sqrt{(1-u)(1+w)} = sqrt{3}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66911125,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669112254,"data":"       | Now this looks like maybe we can set u = sin^2 A? Not sure.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669113371,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669114397,"data":"       | Let’s denote p = sqrt{1-u}, q = sqrt{1+u}. Similarly for v,w.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66911557,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669116544,"data":"       | But note that (1-u) and (1+v) etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669117647,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669118663,"data":"       | Define a = sqrt{1-u}, a' = sqrt{1+u}. Then sqrt{(1-u)(1+v)} = a * sqrt{1+v}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669119826,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669120877,"data":"       | Similarly sqrt{(1-v)(1+u)} = sqrt{1-v} * a'.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669121954,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669122977,"data":"       | Thus first equation: a * sqrt{1+v} + sqrt{1-v} * a' = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66912411,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669125103,"data":"       | Similarly second: sqrt{1-v} * sqrt{1+w} + sqrt{1-w} * sqrt{1+v} = sqrt{2}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66912624,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669127237,"data":"       | Third: sqrt{1-w} * a' + a * sqrt{1+w} = sqrt{3}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669128334,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669129373,"data":"       | Now maybe we can set angles such that sqrt{1±u} = something like cosθ ± sinθ? Since cos^2θ = (1+cos2θ)/2 etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66913072,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66913192,"data":"       | Alternatively, maybe we can set u = cos 2α? Because 1±cos 2α = 2 cos^2 α or 2 sin^2 α.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669133194,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.6691342,"data":"       | Indeed, using double-angle: cos 2α = 1 - 2 sin^2 α = 2 cos^2 α - 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669137,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66913806,"data":"       | Thus 1 + cos 2α = 2 cos^2 α, and 1 - cos 2α = 2 sin^2 α.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669139244,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669140243,"data":"       | Thus sqrt{1 + cos 2α} = sqrt{2} |cos α|, sqrt{1 - cos 2α} = sqrt{2} |sin α|.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669141494,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669142533,"data":"       | If we let u = cos 2A, then sqrt{1+u} = sqrt{2} cos A, sqrt{1-u} = sqrt{2} sin A (assuming A between 0 and π/2 for positivity). Similarly for v = cos 2B, w = cos 2C.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669143867,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669144861,"data":"       | Thus define angles A,B,C ∈ (0,π/2) such that u = cos 2A, v = cos 2B, w = cos 2C.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669146084,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669147144,"data":"       | Then:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669148177,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66914919,"data":"       | sqrt{1-u} = sqrt{2} sin A.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669150264,"data":"       | sqrt{1+u} = sqrt{2} cos A.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669151334,"data":"       | Similarly for v: sqrt{1-v} = sqrt{2} sin B, sqrt{1+v} = sqrt{2} cos B.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669152487,"data":"       | For w: sqrt{1-w} = sqrt{2} sin C, sqrt{1+w} = sqrt{2} cos C.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66915361,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669154603,"data":"       | Now plug into equations.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669155671,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669156663,"data":"       | First equation: sqrt{(1-u)(1+v)} + sqrt{(1-v)(1+u)} = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669157783,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669158773,"data":"       | Compute sqrt{(1-u)(1+v)} = sqrt{2} sin A * sqrt{2} cos B = 2 sin A cos B.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669159931,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669160923,"data":"       | Similarly sqrt{(1-v)(1+u)} = 2 sin B cos A.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669162,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669162984,"data":"       | Thus equation becomes: 2 sin A cos B + 2 sin B cos A = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66916416,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669165213,"data":"       | Factor 2: 2 (sin A cos B + sin B cos A) = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669166453,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669167436,"data":"       | But sin A cos B + sin B cos A = sin(A + B). Indeed sin(A+B) = sin A cos B + cos A sin B.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669168584,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.6691696,"data":"       | Thus we have: 2 sin(A + B) = 1 → sin(A + B) = 1/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66917074,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669171794,"data":"       | Thus A + B = π/6 or 5π/6 etc, but A,B in (0,π/2) so A+B ∈ (0,π). Since sin positive 1/2 occurs at π/6 and 5π/6. Both possible but need A+B ≤ π (since each \u003cπ/2, sum \u003cπ). Both are possible: π/6 ≈ 30°, 5π/6 ≈ 150°. Since each \u003c 90°, sum could be up to \u003c180°, so both possible. We'll consider later.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669175434,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66917647,"data":"       | Second equation: sqrt{(1-v)(1+w)} + sqrt{(1-w)(1+v)} = sqrt{2}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669177619,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66917864,"data":"       | Compute sqrt{(1-v)(1+w)} = sqrt{2} sin B * sqrt{2} cos C = 2 sin B cos C.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66917978,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669180773,"data":"       | sqrt{(1-w)(1+v)} = 2 sin C cos B.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669181859,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669183116,"data":"       | Thus equation: 2 sin B cos C + 2 sin C cos B = sqrt{2}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669184437,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66918543,"data":"       | Thus 2 sin(B + C) = sqrt{2} → sin(B + C) = sqrt{2}/2 = 1/√2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669186654,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669187686,"data":"       | Thus B + C = π/4 or 3π/4 (since sin=√2/2). Both possible within (0,π). Since B,C \u003c π/2, sum \u003c π, both possible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66918913,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669193749,"data":"       | Third equation: sqrt{(1-w)(1+u)} + sqrt{(1-u)(1+w)} = sqrt{3}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66919492,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66919592,"data":"       | Compute sqrt{(1-w)(1+u)} = 2 sin C cos A.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669197034,"data":"       | sqrt{(1-u)(1+w)} = 2 sin A cos C.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669198121,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669199137,"data":"       | Thus equation: 2 sin C cos A + 2 sin A cos C = sqrt{3}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669200243,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669201246,"data":"       | Thus 2 sin(A + C) = sqrt{3} → sin(A + C) = sqrt{3}/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669202397,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669203397,"data":"       | Thus A + C = π/3 or 2π/3 (since sin 60° = √3/2). Both possible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669204604,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669205631,"data":"       | Now we have three unknown angles A, B, C in (0,π/2) satisfying:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669206794,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669207837,"data":"       | (1) sin(A + B) = 1/2 → A + B = π/6 or 5π/6 (but must be less than π). Since each \u003c π/2, sum can be up to \u003cπ. So both possible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66920928,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669210276,"data":"       | (2) sin(B + C) = √2/2 → B + C = π/4 or 3π/4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669211489,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669212483,"data":"       | (3) sin(A + C) = √3/2 → A + C = π/3 or 2π/3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669213657,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669214717,"data":"       | We must pick consistent combination such that A,B,C each in (0,π/2). Let's examine possibilities.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669215984,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669217023,"data":"       | Let’s denote the possible values:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66921812,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669219164,"data":"       | Option for each sum: choose either the acute angle (π/6, π/4, π/3) or the obtuse (5π/6, 3π/4, 2π/3). Since each variable is less than π/2, sum of two variables could be \u003e π/2 but less than π. So both acute and obtuse possibilities may be feasible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66922266,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66922369,"data":"       | We need to find A,B,C such that all three equations hold simultaneously.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66922485,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669225884,"data":"       | Let's solve for A,B,C using linear equations if we choose specific branch values.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669227053,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669228054,"data":"       | Let’s denote:\n"}
