,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66940776,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66940877,"data":"       | Observation: expressions inside sqrt: 2x - xy = x(2 - y). Similarly 2y - xy = y(2 - x). So sqrt(x(2 - y)) + sqrt(y(2 - x)) = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.6694101,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669411093,"data":"       | Similarly sqrt(y(2 - z)) + sqrt(z(2 - y)) = sqrt2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669412226,"data":"       | And sqrt(z(2 - x)) + sqrt(x(2 - z)) = sqrt3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669413307,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669414313,"data":"       | Let’s denote a = sqrt{x(2 - y)}; b = sqrt{y(2 - x)}. Then Eq1: a + b = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66941548,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66941648,"data":"       | Similarly define c = sqrt{y(2 - z)}; d = sqrt{z(2 - y)}; Eq2: c + d = sqrt2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669417633,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669418664,"data":"       | Define e = sqrt{z(2 - x)}; f = sqrt{x(2 - z)}; Eq3: e + f = sqrt3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669419804,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669421136,"data":"       | We have relationships: a^2 = x(2 - y); b^2 = y(2 - x); c^2 = y(2 - z); d^2 = z(2 - y); e^2 = z(2 - x); f^2 = x(2 - z).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669422354,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66942338,"data":"       | We might attempt to solve for x,y,z via substitution.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669424497,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669427033,"data":"       | Note that a^2 + b^2 = x(2 - y) + y(2 - x) = 2x - xy + 2y - xy = 2(x + y) - 2xy = 2[(x + y) - xy].\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669429037,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669430053,"data":"       | But not directly helpful.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669431116,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669432134,"data":"       | Alternatively, maybe we can set variables like u = sqrt{x}, v = sqrt{y}, w = sqrt{z}. Then x = u^2, y = v^2, z = w^2. Then expressions become sqrt{2u^2 - u^2 v^2} = u sqrt{2 - v^2}. Similarly sqrt{2v^2 - u^2 v^2} = v sqrt{2 - u^2}. So Eq1: u sqrt{2 - v^2} + v sqrt{2 - u^2} = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669433659,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669434663,"data":"       | Similarly Eq2: v sqrt{2 - w^2} + w sqrt{2 - v^2} = sqrt2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669435804,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669436864,"data":"       | Eq3: w sqrt{2 - u^2} + u sqrt{2 - w^2} = sqrt3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669437971,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669439006,"data":"       | Now this resembles a system reminiscent of law of cos maybe? Could be related to dot products: If we think of vectors with lengths u and v and angle? Not exactly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669440291,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669441287,"data":"       | Let’s define angles: Let’s set u = sqrt{2} sin A? Wait.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669442457,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669443503,"data":"       | Consider expression u sqrt{2 - v^2}. If we let u = sqrt{2} sin α, and v = sqrt{2} sin β, then sqrt{2 - v^2} = sqrt{2 - 2 sin^2 β} = sqrt{2 cos^2 β} = sqrt{2} |cos β| = sqrt{2} cos β (since β in [0,π/2] maybe). Then u sqrt{2 - v^2} = (sqrt{2} sin α)(sqrt{2} cos β) = 2 sin α cos β.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.6694452,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669446204,"data":"       | Similarly v sqrt{2 - u^2} = 2 sin β cos α.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669447326,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669448367,"data":"       | Thus Eq1 becomes 2 (sin α cos β + sin β cos α) = 2 sin(α + β) = 1 =\u003e sin(α + β) = 1/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669449759,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669450767,"data":"       | Similarly Eq2: v sqrt{2 - w^2} + w sqrt{2 - v^2} = 2 sin β cos γ + 2 sin γ cos β = 2 sin(β + γ) = sqrt2 =\u003e sin(β + γ) = sqrt2/2 = 1/√2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669452216,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669453214,"data":"       | Eq3: w sqrt{2 - u^2} + u sqrt{2 - w^2} = 2 sin γ cos α + 2 sin α cos γ = 2 sin(γ + α) = sqrt3 =\u003e sin(γ + α) = sqrt3/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.6694546,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669455604,"data":"       | Great! This transformation seems promising.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.6694567,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669457726,"data":"       | Thus we can set:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66945878,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669460016,"data":"       | Let u = sqrt{2} sin α, v = sqrt{2} sin β, w = sqrt{2} sin γ, where α,β,γ ∈ (0, π/2) because u,v,w positive and sin positive; also need u^2 = x ≤? Actually x = u^2 = 2 sin^2 α, which is ≤2, but we have positivity.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66946162,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66946476,"data":"       | Now we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669465917,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66946693,"data":"       | sin(α + β) = 1/2,\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669468021,"data":"       | sin(β + γ) = 1/√2,\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669469153,"data":"       | sin(γ + α) = √3/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669470241,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66947128,"data":"       | Thus α+β = arcsin(1/2) = π/6 or 5π/6, but sum of two angles each \u003c π/2, so α+β \u003c π, could be π/6 or 5π/6. Since α,β positive, and each less than π/2, sum could be up to π. But sin=1/2 corresponds to angles π/6 (30°) or 5π/6 (150°). Which is plausible? Let's examine constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669473127,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669474131,"data":"       | Similarly β+γ: sin = 1/√2 =\u003e angle = π/4 (45°) or 3π/4 (135°). γ+α: sin = √3/2 =\u003e angle = π/3 (60°) or 2π/3 (120°).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66947566,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669476683,"data":"       | Since α,β,γ each between 0 and π/2, the sums α+β, β+γ, γ+α each between 0 and π. Could be the larger possibilities as well (e.g., 5π/6 \u003e π/2). Let's test possibilities.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669478209,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669479253,"data":"       | We need to find α,β,γ satisfying these three equations (mod π). Let's denote:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669480497,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669481494,"data":"       | α + β = A,\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669482596,"data":"       | β + γ = B,\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669483684,"data":"       | γ + α = C,\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669484774,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669485917,"data":"       | where sin A = 1/2, sin B = 1/√2, sin C = √3/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669487087,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66948817,"data":"       | We also have that α,β,γ positive and less than π/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66948936,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669490433,"data":"       | We can solve for α,β,γ by adding equations: (α+β)+(β+γ)+(γ+α) = 2(α+β+γ) = A + B + C =\u003e α+β+γ = (A + B + C)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669491904,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66949293,"data":"       | Then we can find each variable: α = (A + C - B)/2, β = (A + B - C)/2, γ = (B + C - A)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669494163,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669495186,"data":"       | Thus we need to choose appropriate branch for each angle (i.e., which of the possible arcsin values). Since sin values correspond to two possibilities each: A ∈ {π/6, 5π/6}; B ∈ {π/4, 3π/4}; C ∈ {π/3, 2π/3}. Choose combination such that resulting α,β,γ are positive and less than π/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669496966,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669497977,"data":"       | Let's compute possibilities.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669499037,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669500046,"data":"       | We'll use Python to test combos.assistantanalysis to=python codeimport sympy as sp\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669501216,"data":"       | import itertools, math\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669504029,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669505061,"data":"       | possible_A = [sp.pi/6, 5*sp.pi/6]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669506171,"data":"       | possible_B = [sp.pi/4, 3*sp.pi/4]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66950756,"data":"       | possible_C = [sp.pi/3, 2*sp.pi/3]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669508697,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669509703,"data":"       | solutions = []\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669510753,"data":"       | for A in possible_A:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669511824,"data":"       |     for B in possible_B:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66951292,"data":"       |         for C in possible_C:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66951402,"data":"       |             alpha = (A + C - B)/2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66951508,"data":"       |             beta = (A + B - C)/2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669516156,"data":"       |             gamma = (B + C - A)/2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669517247,"data":"       |             # simplify\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669518293,"data":"       |             alpha_s = sp.nsimplify(alpha)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669519386,"data":"       |             beta_s = sp.nsimplify(beta)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669520461,"data":"       |             gamma_s = sp.nsimplify(gamma)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.669521544,"data":"       |             # check positivity and less than pi/2\n"}
