,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.66962635,"data":"       | But we must verify that the solution indeed satisfies original equations, not just derived from transformation (which is reversible). The transformation used: u = sqrt{x} = sqrt{2} sin α, etc. Since we defined u = sqrt{x}, v = sqrt{y}, w = sqrt{z} positive. Then we set u = sqrt{2} sin α, which is valid because u \u003c= sqrt{2} (since x \u003c=2). Indeed sin α = u/√2 ∈ (0,1). So α ∈ (0,π/2). So transformation is bijective between (0, sqrt{2}) and (0,π/2). So no extraneous solutions.\n"}
