,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67045485,"data":"       | Thus answer m+n = 33.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670455898,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67045696,"data":"       | But we need to be careful: The problem says x,y,z positive real numbers satisfying the system. It doesn't specify uniqueness, but we can compute the expression; both solutions yield same value. So answer 33.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670458358,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670459365,"data":"       | Now we need to produce final answer in required format.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67046048,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670461534,"data":"       | But we need to show reasoning steps elegantly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670465535,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670466567,"data":"       | Let's craft solution:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670467623,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670468647,"data":"       | - Transform equations using substitution a = sqrt{x}, etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67046981,"data":"       | - Recognize that a^2 + A^2 = 2 etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.6704709,"data":"       | - Use trigonometric substitution: a = √2 sin α, A = √2 cos α, etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67047219,"data":"       | - Convert equations to sin sums: sin(α+β)=1/2, sin(β+γ)=√2/2, sin(α+γ)=√3/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670473724,"data":"       | - Determine possible sums; solving linear system yields two possible sets of angles: (α,β,γ) = (π/8, π/24, 5π/24) or (3π/8, 11π/24, 7π/24).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670475175,"data":"       | - Compute x = 2 sin^2 α = 1 - cos 2α, etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670476357,"data":"       | - Then (1-x)(1-y)(1-z) = cos 2α cos 2β cos 2γ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670477783,"data":"       | - Using the found angles, compute product; both give cos 2α cos 2β cos 2γ = 1/√32? Wait product squared is 1/32, product itself is ±1/√32; but positive because cos values positive? Let's compute cos 2α for each set: For α = π/8, cos(π/4)=√2/2 \u003e0; β = π/24 =\u003e cos(π/12)=cos 15° = √6+√2 /4 ≈ 0.9659 \u003e0; γ =5π/24 =\u003e cos(5π/12)=cos 75° = √6 - √2 /4 ≈0.2588 \u003e0. Their product positive. So product positive; square is 1/32.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670480205,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670481274,"data":"       | Thus expression squared = 1/32 = m/n =\u003e m=1, n=32 =\u003e m+n=33.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670482508,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670483524,"data":"       | Now produce final answer.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670484607,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670485605,"data":"       | Check rational simplification: 1/32 is already reduced.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670486724,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670487745,"data":"       | Thus final answer: 33.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670488788,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670489803,"data":"       | Now produce final message.assistantfinal\\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670490941,"data":"       | \\begin{cases}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670492008,"data":"       | \\sqrt{2x-xy}+\\sqrt{2y-xy}=1\\\\[2mm]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670493157,"data":"       | \\sqrt{2y-yz}+\\sqrt{2z-yz}= \\sqrt2\\\\[2mm]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670494333,"data":"       | \\sqrt{2z-zx}+\\sqrt{2x-zx}= \\sqrt3\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670495457,"data":"       | \\end{cases}\\qquad (x,y,z\u003e0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67049663,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
