,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670636247,"data":"     [Reasoning] (5512 chars):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670637465,"data":"       | analysisWe need to solve system of equations:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670638563,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670639675,"data":"       | Given x,y,z \u003e0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67064079,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670643717,"data":"       | Equations:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670644774,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670645795,"data":"       | (1) sqrt(2x - x y) + sqrt(2y - x y) = 1\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670646867,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670647877,"data":"       | (2) sqrt(2y - y z) + sqrt(2z - y z) = sqrt(2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670648983,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67064998,"data":"       | (3) sqrt(2z - z x) + sqrt(2x - z x) = sqrt(3)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670651064,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670652084,"data":"       | Goal: compute ((1 - x)(1 - y)(1 - z))^2 = ((1-x)(1-y)(1-z))^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670653244,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670654277,"data":"       | Find rational number m/n (in lowest terms). Compute m+n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670655567,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670656614,"data":"       | We need to solve for x,y,z. Could be rational numbers maybe.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67065783,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670658877,"data":"       | Observations: The terms under radicals: 2x - xy = x(2 - y). Similarly 2y - xy = y(2 - x). So sqrt(x(2 - y)) + sqrt(y(2 - x)) = 1. Similarly other equations.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670660144,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670661171,"data":"       | Let’s define a = sqrt{x}, b = sqrt{y}, c = sqrt{z}. Might simplify? Because we have sqrt{x(2-y)} = sqrt{x} * sqrt{2-y}. Not quite.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670662475,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670663465,"data":"       | Let’s define u = sqrt{x(2 - y)}, v = sqrt{y(2 - x)}. Then u+v=1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670664628,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670665637,"data":"       | Similarly sqrt{y(2 - z)} + sqrt{z(2 - y)} = sqrt{2}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670666774,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67066779,"data":"       | And sqrt{z(2 - x)} + sqrt{x(2 - z)} = sqrt{3}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670668877,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670669877,"data":"       | We can perhaps square each equation to get relationships.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670671,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670672014,"data":"       | Let’s denote:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670673104,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67067412,"data":"       | A = sqrt{x(2 - y)} = sqrt{x} sqrt{2 - y}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670675244,"data":"       | B = sqrt{y(2 - x)} = sqrt{y} sqrt{2 - x}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670676344,"data":"       | C = sqrt{y(2 - z)} = sqrt{y} sqrt{2 - z}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670677424,"data":"       | D = sqrt{z(2 - y)} = sqrt{z} sqrt{2 - y}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67067852,"data":"       | E = sqrt{z(2 - x)} = sqrt{z} sqrt{2 - x}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670679617,"data":"       | F = sqrt{x(2 - z)} = sqrt{x} sqrt{2 - z}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670680697,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670681903,"data":"       | Then equations:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670682943,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670685574,"data":"       | A + B = 1 (Eq1)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670686654,"data":"       | C + D = sqrt{2} (Eq2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670687694,"data":"       | E + F = sqrt{3} (Eq3)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670688724,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67068977,"data":"       | Also note symmetry: A uses x and y; B also uses x and y but swapped. So A^2 = x(2 - y). B^2 = y(2 - x).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67069101,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670692017,"data":"       | Similarly C^2 = y(2 - z), D^2 = z(2 - y). etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670693214,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670694285,"data":"       | Maybe we can solve using substitution: Let’s define variables p = sqrt{x}, q = sqrt{y}, r = sqrt{z}. Then x = p^2, y = q^2, z = r^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67069559,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67069663,"data":"       | Then A = sqrt{p^2 (2 - q^2)} = p sqrt{2 - q^2}. B = q sqrt{2 - p^2}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670697775,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670698801,"data":"       | Similarly C = q sqrt{2 - r^2}. D = r sqrt{2 - q^2}. E = r sqrt{2 - p^2}. F = p sqrt{2 - r^2}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67070001,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670701014,"data":"       | Thus we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670702055,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670703063,"data":"       | p sqrt{2 - q^2} + q sqrt{2 - p^2} = 1 (1')\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670704147,"data":"       | q sqrt{2 - r^2} + r sqrt{2 - q^2} = sqrt{2} (2')\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67070527,"data":"       | r sqrt{2 - p^2} + p sqrt{2 - r^2} = sqrt{3} (3')\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670706367,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.6707074,"data":"       | Now this looks like we may suspect that p,q,r are cosines or sines? Since sqrt{2 - something} reminiscent of sqrt{2} * something?\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67070869,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670709697,"data":"       | Let's set p = sqrt{2} sin(alpha) maybe? But constraints: p^2 = x, must be positive but less than maybe 2? Because 2 - q^2 must be non-negative for sqrt. So q^2 \u003c=2, similarly each variable \u003c= sqrt{2} maybe. So p,q,r \u003c= sqrt{2}. So they are at most about 1.414.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67071129,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670712345,"data":"       | The expressions reminiscent of law of cosines? Actually sqrt{2 - q^2} = sqrt{(sqrt{2})^2 - q^2}. So we could set q = sqrt{2} sin(theta) perhaps, then sqrt{2 - q^2} = sqrt{2} cos(theta). Then p sqrt{2 - q^2} = p * sqrt{2} cos(theta). If also p = sqrt{2} sin(phi), then p sqrt{2 - q^2} = 2 sin(phi) cos(theta). Similarly q sqrt{2 - p^2} = 2 sin(theta) cos(phi). Then sum = 2 (sin(phi) cos(theta) + sin(theta) cos(phi)) = 2 sin(phi + theta). So Eq1 becomes 2 sin(phi+theta) = 1 =\u003e sin(phi+theta) = 1/2 =\u003e phi+theta = pi/6 or 5pi/6 etc but within range.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670714345,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670715371,"data":"       | Similarly Eq2: q sqrt{2 - r^2} + r sqrt{2 - q^2} = 2 sin(theta+psi) = sqrt{2}. So sin(theta+psi) = sqrt{2}/2 = 1/√2 =\u003e theta+psi = π/4 or 3π/4 etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670716763,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670718068,"data":"       | Eq3: r sqrt{2 - p^2} + p sqrt{2 - r^2} = 2 sin(psi+phi) = sqrt{3}. So sin(phi+psi) = sqrt{3}/2 =\u003e phi+psi = π/3 or 2π/3 etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670721145,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67072221,"data":"       | Thus the substitution seems promising.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67072331,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670724374,"data":"       | Let's define p = sqrt{2} sin α, q = sqrt{2} sin β, r = sqrt{2} sin γ, where α,β,γ in [0, π/2] maybe because p,q,r positive and sin positive. Also need sin α \u003c= 1 =\u003e p \u003c= sqrt{2} which matches.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670725957,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67072698,"data":"       | Then sqrt{2 - p^2} = sqrt{2 - 2 sin^2 α} = sqrt{2 (1 - sin^2 α)} = sqrt{2} cos α.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670728205,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670729235,"data":"       | Thus expression: p sqrt{2 - q^2} = (sqrt{2} sin α) * (sqrt{2} cos β) = 2 sin α cos β.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67073048,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67073151,"data":"       | Similarly q sqrt{2 - p^2} = 2 sin β cos α.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670732663,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670733764,"data":"       | Thus Eq1: 2 sin α cos β + 2 sin β cos α = 1 =\u003e 2 (sin α cos β + sin β cos α) = 1 =\u003e 2 sin(α+β) = 1 =\u003e sin(α+β) = 1/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670735273,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670736294,"data":"       | Eq2: 2 sin β cos γ + 2 sin γ cos β = sqrt{2} =\u003e 2 sin(β+γ) = sqrt{2} =\u003e sin(β+γ) = sqrt{2}/2 = 1/√2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670737735,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670738747,"data":"       | Eq3: 2 sin γ cos α + 2 sin α cos γ = sqrt{3} =\u003e 2 sin(γ+α) = sqrt{3} =\u003e sin(γ+α) = sqrt{3}/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670740065,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670741094,"data":"       | Great! So we have system:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670742185,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670743241,"data":"       | sin(α+β) = 1/2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670744323,"data":"       | sin(β+γ) = 1/√2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670745457,"data":"       | sin(γ+α) = √3/2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67074656,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67074758,"data":"       | All α,β,γ in (0,π/2) maybe but α+β etc may be between 0 and π maybe.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67074889,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67074991,"data":"       | Now we can solve for α+β, β+γ, γ+α.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670751123,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670752181,"data":"       | Let’s denote:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670753257,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670754284,"data":"       | S1 = α+β\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67075539,"data":"       | S2 = β+γ\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670756487,"data":"       | S3 = γ+α\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67075753,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670758545,"data":"       | Then we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670759618,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67076069,"data":"       | sin S1 = 1/2 → S1 could be π/6 or 5π/6 (or other multiples). Since α,β positive less than π/2, S1 is between 0 and π. So both π/6 (≈0.523) and 5π/6 (≈2.618) are possible. But α+β \u003c= π (since each ≤π/2). So both possible. But we need consistency with other sums.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670764944,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670766038,"data":"       | Similarly sin S2 = 1/√2 → S2 could be π/4 (≈0.785) or 3π/4 (≈2.356). Both within (0,π).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670767403,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67076845,"data":"       | Sin S3 = √3/2 → S3 could be π/3 (≈1.047) or 2π/3 (≈2.094). Both within (0,π).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670769771,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670771,"data":"       | Now we also have relationship between α,β,γ. Since S1+S2+S3 = 2(α+β+γ). Because each pair sum appears twice: (α+β)+(β+γ)+(γ+α) = 2(α+β+γ). So α+β+γ = (S1+S2+S3)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670772634,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670773707,"data":"       | But also each α,β,γ individually must be positive and less than π/2 (since sin positive). And sum of any two is S1 etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670775,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670776063,"data":"       | We can attempt to find consistent combination of S1,S2,S3 values (choose each from two possibilities) such that there exist α,β,γ satisfying pairwise sums.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670777555,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670778544,"data":"       | Given S1 = α+β, S2 = β+γ, S3 = γ+α. Solve for α,β,γ:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67077996,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670780967,"data":"       | Add all: S1+S2+S3 = 2(α+β+γ) =\u003e α+β+γ = (S1+S2+S3)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670782314,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67078337,"data":"       | Then α = (S1+S3 - S2)/2 (since α = (α+β)+(α+γ) - (β+γ) all over 2). Indeed α = (S1 + S3 - S2)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670784825,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670785831,"data":"       | Similarly β = (S1 + S2 - S3)/2, γ = (S2 + S3 - S1)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67078712,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.670788134,"data":"       | Thus α,β,γ must be positive (and \u003c= π/2). So we need to choose S1,S2,S3 values among the possible arcs that satisfy positivity.\n"}
