,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671075607,"data":"     [Turn 1]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671076663,"data":"     [Reasoning] (6592 chars):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671077704,"data":"       | analysisWe need to solve for positive reals x,y,z satisfying:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671078863,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67108005,"data":"       | (1) sqrt(2x - xy) + sqrt(2y - xy) = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671081134,"data":"       | (2) sqrt(2y - yz) + sqrt(2z - yz) = sqrt(2).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671082403,"data":"       | (3) sqrt(2z - zx) + sqrt(2x - zx) = sqrt(3).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671083504,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671084625,"data":"       | Goal: compute ((1-x)(1-y)(1-z))^2 = ((1-x)*(1-y)*(1-z))^2 = (product (1 - variable))^2. Then express as reduced fraction m/n, find m+n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671085897,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671087018,"data":"       | We need to solve system. It might have a unique solution.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671088138,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67108933,"data":"       | We have square roots of expressions: 2x - xy = x(2 - y). Similarly, 2y - xy = y(2 - x). So first equation: sqrt( x (2 - y) ) + sqrt( y (2 - x) ) = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67109234,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671093513,"data":"       | Second: sqrt( y (2 - z) ) + sqrt( z (2 - y) ) = sqrt(2).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671094624,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67109575,"data":"       | Third: sqrt( z (2 - x) ) + sqrt( x (2 - z) ) = sqrt(3).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671096874,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671098007,"data":"       | Thus we have symmetry among variables but with different RHS constants.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671099174,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671100163,"data":"       | Let’s denote A = sqrt(x (2 - y)), B = sqrt(y (2 - x)). Then A + B = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671101318,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671102298,"data":"       | Similarly C = sqrt(y (2 - z)), D = sqrt(z (2 - y)), C + D = sqrt2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671103433,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671104417,"data":"       | And E = sqrt(z (2 - x)), F = sqrt(x (2 - z)), E + F = sqrt3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671105557,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67110659,"data":"       | We also have relationships: A^2 = x (2 - y). B^2 = y (2 - x). etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671107727,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671108743,"data":"       | We might try to solve analytically.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671109807,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671110843,"data":"       | Observation: Since x, y, z positive, and for sqrt arguments to be nonnegative, we need x(2 - y) \u003e=0, y(2 - x) \u003e=0, etc. Since x\u003e0, y\u003e0, this implies 2 - y \u003e=0 =\u003e y \u003c=2; similarly 2 - x \u003e=0 =\u003e x \u003c=2; similarly for other pairs: y \u003c=2, z \u003c=2, etc. So each variable \u003c=2. Also arguments must be \u003e=0, but also not negative.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67111292,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67111394,"data":"       | Also 2 - y may be positive; could be zero but then sqrt zero.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671115134,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671116137,"data":"       | Goal: compute product (1 - x)(1 - y)(1 - z). Since x,y,z \u003c=2, 1 - variable could be negative if variable\u003e1, but product squared ensures non-negative.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67111749,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671118485,"data":"       | Potential approach: square each equation to derive relationships.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671119603,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671120604,"data":"       | Let’s denote:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671121681,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67112267,"data":"       | Equation (1): sqrt{x (2 - y)} + sqrt{y (2 - x)} = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671123785,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671124775,"data":"       | Square: x(2 - y) + y(2 - x) + 2 sqrt{ x (2 - y) * y (2 - x) } = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671125905,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671127154,"data":"       | Compute: x(2 - y) = 2x - xy. y(2 - x) = 2y - xy. Sum = 2x + 2y - 2xy = 2(x + y - xy). So:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671129754,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67113082,"data":"       | 2(x + y - xy) + 2 sqrt{ xy (2 - x)(2 - y) } = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671131928,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671132964,"data":"       | Divide 2: (x + y - xy) + sqrt{ xy (2 - x)(2 - y) } = 1/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67113411,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.6711351,"data":"       | Thus sqrt{ xy (2 - x)(2 - y) } = 1/2 - (x + y - xy) = 1/2 - x - y + xy.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671136247,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671137234,"data":"       | We must ensure RHS nonnegative.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671138311,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671139321,"data":"       | Now square again: xy (2 - x)(2 - y) = (1/2 - x - y + xy)^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67114044,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671141433,"data":"       | Similarly for others.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67114249,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671143476,"data":"       | But perhaps there is a more clever substitution.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671144584,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67114568,"data":"       | Let’s set a = sqrt{x}, b = sqrt{y}, c = sqrt{z}. Then x = a^2, y = b^2, z = c^2. Then sqrt{x (2 - y)} = sqrt{ a^2 (2 - b^2) } = a sqrt{2 - b^2}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671146974,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671147976,"data":"       | Similarly sqrt{ y (2 - x) } = b sqrt{2 - a^2}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67114905,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671150054,"data":"       | Equation (1): a sqrt{2 - b^2} + b sqrt{2 - a^2} = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671151206,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671152186,"data":"       | Equation (2): b sqrt{2 - c^2} + c sqrt{2 - b^2} = sqrt2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671153323,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671154308,"data":"       | Equation (3): c sqrt{2 - a^2} + a sqrt{2 - c^2} = sqrt3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671155413,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671156417,"data":"       | Now note that 0 \u003c a,b,c \u003c= sqrt{2}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67115758,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671158584,"data":"       | Let’s define u = sqrt{2 - a^2}, v = sqrt{2 - b^2}, w = sqrt{2 - c^2}. Then a = sqrt{2 - u^2}, etc. But maybe not needed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67115987,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671160884,"data":"       | Alternatively, define angles: Let’s set a = sqrt{2} sin A, b = sqrt{2} sin B, c = sqrt{2} sin C? Because then sqrt{2 - a^2} = sqrt{2 - 2 sin^2 A} = sqrt{2 (1 - sin^2 A)} = sqrt{2 cos^2 A} = sqrt{2} |cos A|. Since A in [0,π/2] (positive), we can take cos positive. So we can set:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671162466,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671163453,"data":"       | Let a = sqrt{2} sin α, b = sqrt{2} sin β, c = sqrt{2} sin γ, where α,β,γ ∈ [0, π/2] (since a,b,c ≤ sqrt{2}). Then:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671164813,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67116583,"data":"       | sqrt{2 - a^2} = sqrt{2 - 2 sin^2 α} = sqrt{2 cos^2 α} = sqrt{2} cos α.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671167064,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671169546,"data":"       | Similarly sqrt{2 - b^2} = sqrt{2} cos β, sqrt{2 - c^2} = sqrt{2} cos γ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671170808,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67117204,"data":"       | Now compute equation (1): a sqrt{2 - b^2} + b sqrt{2 - a^2} = (sqrt{2} sin α)*(sqrt{2} cos β) + (sqrt{2} sin β)*(sqrt{2} cos α) = 2 ( sin α cos β + sin β cos α ) = 2 sin(α + β). Indeed sin α cos β + cos α sin β = sin(α+β). So equation (1) becomes:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671173783,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67117483,"data":"       | 2 sin(α + β) = 1 ⇒ sin(α + β) = 1/2 ⇒ α + β = π/6 or 5π/6 (but α,β ∈ [0,π/2]; sum in [0,π]; sin=1/2 at π/6 and 5π/6). Since each ≤ π/2, sum ≤ π. Both possible: α+β could be π/6 (≈30°) or 5π/6 (≈150°). But α+β cannot exceed π (since each ≤ π/2, max sum = π). So both possibilities are allowed. However we must ensure other equations.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671177054,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671178121,"data":"       | Equation (2): b sqrt{2 - c^2} + c sqrt{2 - b^2} = (sqrt{2} sin β)*(sqrt{2} cos γ) + (sqrt{2} sin γ)*(sqrt{2} cos β) = 2 ( sin β cos γ + sin γ cos β) = 2 sin(β + γ) = sqrt{2}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671179621,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67118064,"data":"       | Thus sin(β + γ) = sqrt{2} / 2 = 1/√2 ≈ 0.7071. So β + γ = π/4 (45°) or 3π/4 (135°). Since each ≤ π/2, sum up to π, both possible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671182324,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671183344,"data":"       | Equation (3): c sqrt{2 - a^2} + a sqrt{2 - c^2} = 2 sin(γ + α) = sqrt{3}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.6711846,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67118561,"data":"       | Thus sin(γ + α) = sqrt{3} / 2 = ~0.866025. So γ + α = π/3 (60°) or 2π/3 (120°). Both possible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671186927,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671187948,"data":"       | Thus we have three unknown angles α,β,γ in [0,π/2] satisfying:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671189157,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671192834,"data":"       | α + β = either π/6 or 5π/6.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67119406,"data":"       | β + γ = either π/4 or 3π/4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671195187,"data":"       | γ + α = either π/3 or 2π/3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671196273,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671197291,"data":"       | Now we need to find which combination yields angles within [0,π/2] (0 to 90°). Also each angle nonnegative.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671198568,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671199594,"data":"       | Let's denote possibilities: For each equation, we have two options. So 2^3 = 8 possible sets. But also we need consistency: adding all three equations:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671200897,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671201907,"data":"       | (α + β) + (β + γ) + (γ + α) = 2(α + β + γ) = sum of chosen RHS values.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671203201,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671204203,"data":"       | Thus α + β + γ = (sum of three chosen RHS)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671205334,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671206343,"data":"       | Compute each RHS: For each equation we pick either the smaller angle (π/6, π/4, π/3) or larger (5π/6, 3π/4, 2π/3). Let's compute possibilities.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671209483,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67121081,"data":"       | But note that each angle ≤ π/2 ≈ 1.5708 rad. So each sum α+β etc must be ≤ π (since each ≤ π/2). The larger options 5π/6 ≈ 150°, 3π/4 = 135°, 2π/3 = 120°, all ≤ π (180°). So still possible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67121256,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671213576,"data":"       | But also we need each individual angle ≤ π/2. Let's test.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671214757,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671215794,"data":"       | Let’s solve linear equations: α+β = S1, β+γ = S2, γ+α = S3. Solve for α,β,γ: Adding first two: α+2β+γ = S1+S2. But γ+α = S3, subtract: (α+2β+γ) - (γ+α) = 2β = S1+S2 - S3. Thus β = (S1+S2 - S3)/2. Similarly α = (S1+S3 - S2)/2, γ = (S2+S3 - S1)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671217638,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671218646,"data":"       | Thus for each choice of S1,S2,S3 (each either small or large), compute α,β,γ and check if they are within [0,π/2] (nonnegative and ≤ π/2). Also we need sin values positive (angles within range). Additionally we need a,b,c positive (they will be sqrt2 sin α etc). Since sin α positive for α\u003e0, but α could be 0; that's allowed? x\u003e0 positive real numbers, so a\u003e0, so sin α \u003e0 =\u003e α\u003e0. Similarly for β,γ \u003e0. So angles must be \u003e0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671220866,"data":"       | \n"}
