,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.6713101,"data":"       | Now we must decide which one corresponds to original variables x,y,z positive real numbers maybe not all same but produce the given sums. Both sets produce same RHS values because they satisfy the equations (since we derived them using trigonometric substitution). So both sets correspond to solutions for (α,β,γ). But we must check whether the substitution a = sqrt{2} sin α yields x = a^2 = 2 sin^2 α etc. So we can compute x,y,z for each solution and verify the original equations (including the sign of sqrt?). The equations involve square roots of positive numbers; the expression sqrt{2x-xy} = a sqrt{2 - b^2} = sqrt{2} sin α * sqrt{2} cos β = 2 sin α cos β = sin(α+β)*2? Wait we earlier derived that equals 2 sin(α+β) = RHS (1 for first). That holds for both small and large solutions because sin(α+β) equals 1/2 for both α+β = π/6 (sin=1/2) and 5π/6 (sin=1/2). Indeed sin(5π/6)=1/2 as well. So both satisfy equation 1 (since sin(α+β) = 1/2). Similarly for others.\n"}
