,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671508777,"data":"       | sqrt(2y - yz) + sqrt(2z - yz) = sqrt(2) ...(2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.6715099,"data":"       | sqrt(2z - zx) + sqrt(2x - zx) = sqrt(3) ...(3)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671510998,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671511987,"data":"       | We need ( (1 - x)(1 - y)(1 - z) )^2 = (product (1 - x) )^2. So essentially we need product (1-x) etc squared.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671513197,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67151419,"data":"       | Goal: find rational number m/n, reduce, then output m+n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67151531,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671516318,"data":"       | Approach: Solve for x, y, z. This looks like maybe variables have specific values such that each equation reminiscent of cos angles? Let's try to interpret.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67151761,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671518647,"data":"       | Let’s denote A = sqrt(2x - xy) = sqrt(x(2 - y)). Similarly, sqrt(2y - xy) = sqrt(y(2 - x)). So equation (1):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671519884,"data":"       | sqrt{x(2 - y)} + sqrt{y(2 - x)} = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67152099,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671522003,"data":"       | Similarly, (2): sqrt{y(2 - z)} + sqrt{z(2 - y)} = sqrt{2}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671523137,"data":"       | (3): sqrt{z(2 - x)} + sqrt{x(2 - z)} = sqrt{3}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67152422,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671525237,"data":"       | These look like maybe there is substitution using sin, cos? Because expression sqrt{x(2 - y)} reminiscent of something like sqrt{ (1 - a)(1 - b) }? Not exactly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671526537,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671527574,"data":"       | Let’s attempt to solve algebraically using sympy. Use substitution of squares maybe.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67152878,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671529784,"data":"       | Let’s denote a = sqrt{x(2 - y)}; b = sqrt{y(2 - x)}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671530934,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671531958,"data":"       | Equation (1): a + b = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671533013,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671535504,"data":"       | Similarly define c = sqrt{y(2 - z)}; d = sqrt{z(2 - y)}: c + d = sqrt{2}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671536731,"data":"       | And e = sqrt{z(2 - x)}; f = sqrt{x(2 - z)}: e + f = sqrt{3}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671537875,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67153888,"data":"       | We also have relationships: a^2 = x(2 - y); b^2 = y(2 - x). etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671540018,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671541034,"data":"       | Goal: compute (1 - x)(1 - y)(1 - z). Perhaps there is nice pattern: maybe x=cos^2(alpha), etc? Let's explore.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671542265,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671543257,"data":"       | Suppose we set x = 2 sin^2(A)? Not sure.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67154433,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671545374,"data":"       | Alternatively, maybe we can square each equation and use relations to get linear equations.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671546587,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671547805,"data":"       | Equation (1): (a+b)^2 = a^2 + b^2 + 2ab = 1 =\u003e (x(2 - y) + y(2 - x) + 2 sqrt{x(2 - y) y(2 - x)}) = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671549051,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671550767,"data":"       | Simplify: x(2 - y) + y(2 - x) = 2x - xy + 2y - xy = 2(x + y) - 2xy = 2(x + y - xy).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671551937,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671552947,"data":"       | Thus we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671553974,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671554995,"data":"       | 2(x + y - xy) + 2 sqrt{x(2 - y) y(2 - x)} = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671556107,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671557104,"data":"       | Divide by 2:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67155815,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671559147,"data":"       | (x + y - xy) + sqrt{x(2 - y) y(2 - x)} = 1/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671560235,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671561251,"data":"       | Let’s denote S1 = sqrt{x(2 - y) y(2 - x)}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671562387,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671563377,"data":"       | Then we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671564424,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67156543,"data":"       | x + y - xy + S1 = 1/2. (Eq A)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671566494,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671567568,"data":"       | Similarly from (2): (c+d)^2 = 2 =\u003e c^2 + d^2 + 2cd = 2. c^2 = y(2 - z); d^2 = z(2 - y); cd = sqrt{ y(2 - z) z(2 - y) }.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67156893,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67156994,"data":"       | Compute y(2 - z) + z(2 - y) = 2(y+z) - 2yz = 2(y+z - yz). So\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671571045,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671572054,"data":"       | 2(y+z - yz) + 2 sqrt{ y(2 - z) z(2 - y)} = 2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67157324,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67157427,"data":"       | Divide by 2: (y+z - yz) + sqrt{ y(2 - z) z(2 - y)} = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671575387,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671576385,"data":"       | Thus\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671578898,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671579917,"data":"       | y + z - yz + S2 = 1, where S2 = sqrt{ y(2 - z) z(2 - y)}. (Eq B)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671581067,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671582107,"data":"       | Similarly from (3): (e+f)^2 = 3 =\u003e e^2+f^2+2ef = 3. e^2 = z(2 - x); f^2 = x(2 - z). Sum = 2(z + x) - 2zx = 2(z + x - zx). So\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671583383,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67158446,"data":"       | 2(z + x - zx) + 2 sqrt{ z(2 - x) x(2 - z)} = 3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671585553,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671586574,"data":"       | Divide by 2: (z + x - zx) + sqrt{ z(2 - x) x(2 - z)} = 3/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671587714,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671588721,"data":"       | Thus\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671589727,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671590725,"data":"       | z + x - zx + S3 = 3/2, where S3 = sqrt{ z(2 - x) x(2 - z)}. (Eq C)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671591901,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671592941,"data":"       | Now we have three equations with variables x,y,z and the square root cross terms. Maybe we can find x,y,z that satisfy.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67159417,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671595207,"data":"       | Observe that S1 = sqrt{x y (2 - y)(2 - x)} = sqrt{ x y (2 - x)(2 - y)}. Similarly S2 = sqrt{ y z (2 - y)(2 - z)}. S3 = sqrt{ z x (2 - z)(2 - x)}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671596483,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671597507,"data":"       | Thus each S term is sqrt of product of two variables times product of (2 - each). So reminiscent of expression sqrt{ (x)(2 - x) (y)(2 - y) }.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671598794,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671599823,"data":"       | Let’s denote p = sqrt{x(2 - x)}; q = sqrt{y(2 - y)}; r = sqrt{z(2 - z)}. Then we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67160104,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671602035,"data":"       | S1 = sqrt{ xy (2 - x)(2 - y)} = sqrt{ [x(2 - x)] [y(2 - y)] } = p q.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671603173,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671604433,"data":"       | Indeed because x(2 - x) = p^2, y(2 - y) = q^2. So S1 = p q.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671605577,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671606577,"data":"       | Similarly S2 = q r; S3 = r p.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67160764,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671608628,"data":"       | Nice!\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671609654,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671610714,"data":"       | Now rewrite equations A,B,C in terms of x,y,z and p,q,r.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671611851,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.6716129,"data":"       | Recall p = sqrt{x(2 - x)}; note x is positive real and presumably less than 2? Because (2-x) must be nonnegative to have real p; but the sqrt in original equations required expressions inside sqrt nonnegative: x(2 - y) \u003e=0 etc. Since x\u003e0 and y\u003e0, we need 2 - y \u003e=0 =\u003e y \u003c=2; similarly 2 - x \u003e=0 =\u003e x \u003c=2 etc. So x,y,z in (0,2] maybe.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671614705,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671617288,"data":"       | Now we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671618337,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67161934,"data":"       | Equation A: x + y - xy + p q = 1/2. (1)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.6716205,"data":"       | Equation B: y + z - yz + q r = 1. (2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671621685,"data":"       | Equation C: z + x - zx + r p = 3/2. (3)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671622794,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671623855,"data":"       | Now we have unknowns x,y,z, and p,q,r with relation p^2 = x(2 - x). Similarly q^2 = y(2 - y), r^2 = z(2 - z). So we have 6 unknowns, 6 equations (three eq above and three p^2 definitions). Solve.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671625194,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671626207,"data":"       | Goal: compute (1 - x)(1 - y)(1 - z). Let's denote a = 1 - x, etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67162735,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671628345,"data":"       | We might try to solve directly using sympy.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671629441,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671630434,"data":"       | Let's do that.assistantanalysis to=python codeimport sympy as sp\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67163157,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671632591,"data":"       | # define symbols\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67163365,"data":"       | x,y,z,p,q,r = sp.symbols('x y z p q r', positive=True, real=True)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671634783,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671635793,"data":"       | eq1 = sp.Eq(x + y - x*y + p*q, sp.Rational(1,2))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671636887,"data":"       | eq2 = sp.Eq(y + z - y*z + q*r, 1)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671637974,"data":"       | eq3 = sp.Eq(z + x - z*x + r*p, sp.Rational(3,2))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67163909,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671640091,"data":"       | eq4 = sp.Eq(p**2, x*(2 - x))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671641174,"data":"       | eq5 = sp.Eq(q**2, y*(2 - y))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671642261,"data":"       | eq6 = sp.Eq(r**2, z*(2 - z))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67164331,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671644314,"data":"       | solutions = sp.solve([eq1,eq2,eq3,eq4,eq5,eq6], (x,y,z,p,q,r), domain=sp.S.Complexes, dict=True)\n"}
