,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671769074,"data":"       | analysisBoth give same value 1/32. Good. So answer is 1/32 = m/n with m=1, n=32, relatively prime. m+n = 33.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671770327,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671771362,"data":"       | But we should confirm that (1-x) etc may be negative for some solutions causing squared product still same? Indeed for solution 1: x≈1.707\u003e1, y≈1.966\u003e1, z≈1.259\u003e1 =\u003e (1-x) negative for x\u003e1, same for y maybe, but product's square unaffected.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671773074,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671774111,"data":"       | For solution 2: x≈0.293\u003c1, y≈0.034\u003c1, z≈0.741\u003c1 =\u003e (1-x) positive etc. Both produce same squared product.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671777347,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671778382,"data":"       | Thus answer: 33.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671779427,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671780482,"data":"       | We must produce reasoning. Provide analytic solution.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671781652,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67178266,"data":"       | Let's attempt to produce derivation more elegantly: using substitution p = sqrt{x(2 - x)} etc gave nice linear equations after introducing pq terms.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671783932,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671784944,"data":"       | But we can also solve directly: we have three equations:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671786085,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671787137,"data":"       | x + y - xy + sqrt{x(2 - x) y(2 - y)} = 1/2,\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671788331,"data":"       | y + z - yz + sqrt{y(2 - y) z(2 - z)} = 1,\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67178943,"data":"       | z + x - zx + sqrt{z(2 - z) x(2 - x)} = 3/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671790524,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671791552,"data":"       | Set a = sqrt{x(2 - x)}, b = sqrt{y(2 - y)}, c = sqrt{z(2 - z)}. Then equations become:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67179275,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671793765,"data":"       | x + y - xy + a b = 1/2 ...(A)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671794841,"data":"       | y + z - yz + b c = 1 ...(B)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671795995,"data":"       | z + x - zx + c a = 3/2 ...(C)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67179707,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671798215,"data":"       | We also have x = 1 - sqrt{1 - a^2/...? Wait not direct. But we can consider substitution: from definition a^2 = x(2 - x) = 2x - x^2. Solve for x: quadratic: x^2 - 2x + a^2 = 0 =\u003e x = 1 ± sqrt{1 - a^2}. Since x positive and ≤2, we take x = 1 ± sqrt{1 - a^2}. Similarly y = 1 ± sqrt{1 - b^2}, z = 1 ± sqrt{1 - c^2}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67179997,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671800981,"data":"       | Let’s define u = sqrt{1 - a^2}, etc. Then x = 1 ± u. Because a^2 = 1 - u^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671802204,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671803224,"data":"       | But perhaps easier: define A = 1 - x, B = 1 - y, C = 1 - z. Then we want (ABC)^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671804421,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671805458,"data":"       | Maybe we can express in terms of a,b,c and simplify.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671806577,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.6718076,"data":"       | Let’s compute with symbolic manipulations to deduce rational.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67180879,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671809798,"data":"       | But for explanation, we can show solving leads to unique value for product.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671810961,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671811965,"data":"       | Potential more clever approach: maybe we can consider rewriting each original equation as\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671813175,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671815617,"data":"       | sqrt{(1 - x)(1 - y)} + sqrt{(1 - x) (1 - y)}? Not exactly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671816782,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67181779,"data":"       | Let's compute:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671818832,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671819835,"data":"       | x(2 - y) = 2x - xy = (x + x - xy). Not helpful.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67182097,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671821997,"data":"       | But there may be transformation using substitution x = 1 - p, etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671823144,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671824204,"data":"       | Let’s try to see:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67182532,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671826325,"data":"       | Let’s define u = 1 - x, v = 1 - y, w = 1 - z.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67182745,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67182845,"data":"       | Then x = 1 - u, y = 1 - v, z = 1 - w.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671829538,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671830575,"data":"       | Now compute expression inside sqrt: 2x - xy = 2(1 - u) - (1 - u)(1 - v) = 2 - 2u - (1 - u - v + uv) = 2 - 2u - 1 + u + v - uv = 1 - u + v - uv = (1 - u)(1 - v) + v? Let's compute: (1 - u)(1 - v) = 1 - u - v + uv. Not same. Let's compute actual expression again:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671832074,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671833145,"data":"       | 2x - xy = 2(1-u) - (1-u)(1-v) = 2 - 2u - [1 - u - v + uv] = 2 - 2u - 1 + u + v - uv = 1 - u + v - uv = (1 - u)(1 - v) + v? Because (1 - u)(1 - v) = 1 - u - v + uv. Adding v yields 1 - u + uv? Not right.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671834514,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671835612,"data":"       | Wait compute (1-u)(1-v) = 1 - u - v + uv.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.6718367,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671837704,"data":"       | But we have 1 - u + v - uv = (1 - u) + (v - uv) = (1 - u) + v(1 - u) = (1 - u)(1 + v). That's not correct.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671838932,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.6718401,"data":"       | Let's factor: 1 - u + v - uv = (1 - u)(1 - v?) Actually (1 - u)(1 - v) = 1 - u - v + uv, which is different sign for v and uv.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671841368,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671842454,"data":"       | Maybe rewrite as (1 - u) + v(1 - u)?? Let's factor (1 - u) common: (1 - u)(1 + v?). Because (1 - u)(1 + v) = (1 - u) + (1 - u)v = 1 - u + v - uv. Yes! That's exactly our expression! Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671843818,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671844834,"data":"       | Thus:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671846625,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67184764,"data":"       | 2x - xy = (1 - u)(1 + v) where u = 1 - x, v = 1 - y.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671848737,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67184974,"data":"       | Similarly 2y - xy = (1 - v)(1 + u). Let's verify:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671850848,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.6718519,"data":"       | 2y - xy = 2(1 - v) - (1-u)(1-v) = 2 - 2v - [1 - u - v + uv] = 2 - 2v -1 + u + v - uv = 1 + u - v - uv = (1 - v)(1 + u). Indeed factor (1 - v)(1 + u).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67185472,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671855737,"data":"       | Thus original first equation becomes:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671856817,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671857815,"data":"       | sqrt{ (1 - u)(1 + v) } + sqrt{ (1 - v)(1 + u) } = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671858914,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671859954,"data":"       | Similarly for second equation: let v = 1 - y, w = 1 - z.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671861074,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671862095,"data":"       | 2y - yz = (1 - v)(1 + w)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671863165,"data":"       | 2z - yz = (1 - w)(1 + v)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671864248,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671865272,"data":"       | Equation 2: sqrt{ (1 - v)(1 + w) } + sqrt{ (1 - w)(1 + v) } = sqrt{2}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671866417,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671867407,"data":"       | Third: define w = 1 - z, u = 1 - x again.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671868494,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671869507,"data":"       | 2z - zx = (1 - w)(1 + u)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671870575,"data":"       | 2x - zx = (1 - u)(1 + w)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671871632,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671872661,"data":"       | Equation 3: sqrt{ (1 - w)(1 + u) } + sqrt{ (1 - u)(1 + w) } = sqrt{3}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671873795,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671874814,"data":"       | Now we have symmetric system in u,v,w (which are 1 - x etc). Interesting! This is perhaps the intended path.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671876017,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671877011,"data":"       | Let’s denote:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671878047,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671879064,"data":"       | A = sqrt{ (1 - u)(1 + v) } = sqrt{ (1 - u)(1 + v) }.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671880205,"data":"       | B = sqrt{ (1 - v)(1 + u) }.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671881251,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671882247,"data":"       | Equation (1): A + B = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.6718833,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671884324,"data":"       | Similarly define C = sqrt{ (1 - v)(1 + w) }, D = sqrt{ (1 - w)(1 + v) }, equation (2): C + D = sqrt2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671885532,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671886528,"data":"       | Equation (3): E = sqrt{ (1 - w)(1 + u) }, F = sqrt{ (1 - u)(1 + w) }, equation (3): E + F = sqrt3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671887735,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67188879,"data":"       | But note A = sqrt{ (1 - u)(1 + v)}; B = sqrt{ (1 - v)(1 + u)}. So AB = sqrt{ (1 - u)(1 - v)(1 + u)(1 + v)} = sqrt{ (1 - u^2)(1 - v^2) }. Similarly, C*D = sqrt{ (1 - v^2)(1 - w^2) }, E*F = sqrt{ (1 - w^2)(1 - u^2)}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671890155,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671891324,"data":"       | Now note that the values of u,v,w are perhaps cos angles? Because (1 - u^2) reminiscent of sin^2? If we set u = cos 2α? Not sure.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671892632,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671895084,"data":"       | But we can attempt to solve for u,v,w.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67189622,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671897234,"data":"       | Let's try to compute squares of the equations:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671898367,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671899371,"data":"       | (A + B)^2 = A^2 + B^2 + 2AB = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671900444,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671901475,"data":"       | But A^2 = (1 - u)(1 + v) = 1 + v - u - uv.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67190258,"data":"       | B^2 = (1 - v)(1 + u) = 1 + u - v - uv.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671903662,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671904672,"data":"       | Thus A^2 + B^2 = 2 + u + v - (u + v) - 2uv? Wait compute:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671905778,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67190682,"data":"       | A^2 + B^2 = (1 + v - u - uv) + (1 + u - v - uv) = 2 + (v - u) + (u - v) - 2uv = 2 - 2uv.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671908001,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671908995,"data":"       | So A^2 + B^2 = 2 - 2uv.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67191006,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671911061,"data":"       | Thus 2AB = 1 - (A^2 + B^2) = 1 - (2 - 2uv) = -1 + 2uv.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671912164,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671913178,"data":"       | So AB = (-1 + 2uv)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67191422,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67191527,"data":"       | But AB also equals sqrt{ (1 - u^2)(1 - v^2) }. So we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671916385,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671917411,"data":"       | sqrt{ (1 - u^2)(1 - v^2) } = (2uv - 1)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671918484,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671919535,"data":"       | Since sqrt is nonnegative, right-hand side must be nonnegative. So 2uv - 1 \u003e= 0 =\u003e uv \u003e= 1/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671920874,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671921884,"data":"       | Similarly for other equations: (C + D)^2 = 2 =\u003e C^2 + D^2 + 2CD = 2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671923074,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671924074,"data":"       | Compute C^2 = (1 - v)(1 + w) = 1 + w - v - vw.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671925164,"data":"       | D^2 = (1 - w)(1 + v) = 1 + v - w - vw.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671926244,"data":"       | Sum = 2 + (w - v) + (v - w) - 2vw = 2 - 2vw.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67192733,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67192833,"data":"       | Thus 2CD = 2 - (C^2 + D^2) = 2 - (2 - 2vw) = 2vw.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671929465,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671930455,"data":"       | Hence CD = vw.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671931481,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671932485,"data":"       | But also CD = sqrt{ (1 - v^2)(1 - w^2) }.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671933557,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671934565,"data":"       | Thus sqrt{ (1 - v^2)(1 - w^2) } = vw.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671935638,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.6719382,"data":"       | Since square root nonnegative, vw \u003e= 0 (true). Square both sides: (1 - v^2)(1 - w^2) = v^2 w^2 =\u003e Expand: 1 - v^2 - w^2 + v^2 w^2 = v^2 w^2 =\u003e cancel v^2 w^2 both sides -\u003e 1 - v^2 - w^2 = 0 =\u003e v^2 + w^2 = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671939732,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67194074,"data":"       | Interesting! So we get a relation: v^2 + w^2 = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671941854,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671942917,"data":"       | Similarly for third equation: (E + F)^2 = 3 =\u003e E^2 + F^2 + 2EF = 3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.67194411,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671945105,"data":"       | E^2 = (1 - w)(1 + u) = 1 + u - w - uw.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4452.671946182,"data":"       | F^2 = (1 - u)(1 + w) = 1 + w - u - uw.\n"}
