,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.930307714,"data":"       | analysisWe need to parse problem: \"The nth term of a certain geometric series is given by a·r^{n-1}, where a and r are positive integers and r \u003e 1. Bill picks out k different numbers in this sequence, all of which have the same number of digits. What is the largest possible value of k?\"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.930309624,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.93031079,"data":"       | Interpretation: We have a geometric progression (GP) with integer first term a and integer ratio r \u003e 1. Terms: a, a r, a r^2, a r^3, ... (positive integers). Bill selects k distinct terms from this sequence, all having the same number of digits (i.e., same decimal length). We need to find the maximum possible k (over all choices of a, r, and selection of terms) such that there exists a set of k distinct terms in the GP that all have the same number of digits. So essentially we need to find the maximum size of a subset of terms of a GP (with integer a, r\u003e1) that lie within a given range of numbers with a fixed number of digits (i.e., between 10^{d-1} and 10^{d}-1 inclusive). So we need to maximize the number of terms of the GP that can fall into a single decade interval (range of numbers with same digit length). Since a and r are positive integers, we can adjust them to maximize density of terms within a given range.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.93031574,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.930316912,"data":"       | Goal: Find largest possible number of terms of a geometric progression with integer ratio \u003e1 that can have the same number of digits. Equivalent to: For some integer ratio r\u003e1 and initial term a (positive integer), there exists an interval [10^{d-1}, 10^{d} - 1] that contains as many terms of the GP as possible. Determine the maximum possible count.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.93031872,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.930319838,"data":"       | Observation: Since r\u003e1 integer, the GP grows exponentially. The number of terms within a given interval of size about factor 10 (since 10^{d} / 10^{d-1} = 10). So we ask: How many consecutive powers r^n (scaled by a) can stay within a factor of 10? That is, we need to find largest m such that there exist integers a, r\u003e1, and indices i\u003cj\u003c...\u003ci+m-1 such that a r^{i-1} ... a r^{i+m-2} all lie between 10^{d-1} and 10^{d} - 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.930321892,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.9303232,"data":"       | Since a r^{i-1} \u003e= 10^{d-1}, and a r^{i+m-2} \u003c= 10^{d} - 1 \u003c 10^{d}. So we have:\n"}
