,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.930331702,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.930332824,"data":"       | Thus we need r^{m-1} \u003c 10. Since r is integer \u003e1, the maximum possible m is limited by r. For a given r, the max number of consecutive terms that can fit within a factor of 10 is floor(log_{r} 10) + 1? Let's compute: we need r^{m-1} \u003c 10, i.e., m-1 \u003c log_{r} 10, so m \u003c= floor(log_{r} 10) + 1. Since m must be integer.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.930334676,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.930335966,"data":"       | But we can also potentially skip terms (non-consecutive indices) because Bill picks any k different numbers, not necessarily consecutive. However, if we skip, the ratio between smallest and largest may be larger than r^{m-1} if we skip some exponents. But we can always choose a block of consecutive terms to maximize count because skipping would increase ratio, making it harder to stay within factor 10. So the maximum possible k is the maximum over r of the largest number of terms of the GP that can be within a factor of 10, i.e., the maximum possible length of a subsequence (not necessarily consecutive) such that the ratio between max and min is \u003c 10. Since ratio between any two terms is r^{difference in indices}. So the largest set is essentially the largest size of a set of exponents where the difference between max and min is at most floor(log_{r} 9?). Wait: we need the largest term \u003c 10 times smallest term (strictly less than 10). So if we have exponents e1 \u003c e2 \u003c ... \u003c ek, then r^{e_k - e_1} \u003c 10. So the maximum possible k for given r is the maximum number of integers we can choose within an interval of length less than log_r 10. Since we can choose any integers in that interval, the maximum number of distinct integers we can fit is floor(log_r 10) + 1 (since interval length L = log_r 10, we can have at most floor(L) + 1 integers). Because we can choose e1, e1+1, ..., e1+floor(L). That's exactly same as consecutive case. So maximum k for given r is floor(log_r 10) + 1.\n"}
