,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.931129209,"data":"       | analysisWe need to understand the problem: We have a geometric sequence with terms a, ar, ar^2, ... where a and r are positive integers, r\u003e1. Bill picks out k different numbers in this sequence, all of which have the same number of digits. We need the largest possible value of k. So we need to consider any possible choice of a, r (positive integers \u003e0) and pick as many distinct terms of the geometric progression as possible that all have the same number of digits (i.e., same decimal length). The question: \"What is the largest possible value of k?\" Means over all possible geometric sequences (with integer a, r\u003e1) and over all possible subsets of terms having equal digit length, what's the maximum size of such a subset? Equivalent to: For given a, r, consider the set of n such that the term a r^{n-1} has, say, d digits (i.e., 10^{d-1} ≤ a r^{n-1} \u003c 10^{d}). The number of such n is at most something. Want to maximize over a,r,d.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.931132172,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.931133284,"data":"       | Thus we need to find maximum length of consecutive n intervals such that a r^{n-1} stays within the same power-of-ten interval. Since geometric growth is exponential, as n increases the term multiplies by r each step, eventually jumps to next digit count. So the number of n for which the term stays within same digit count is limited by the factor r. Specifically, we need:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.931135054,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.93113614,"data":"       | Let T_n = a r^{n-1}. Condition: 10^{d-1} ≤ T_n \u003c 10^{d}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.931138324,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.931139442,"data":"       | Take logs: d-1 ≤ log10 a + (n-1) log10 r \u003c d.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.931140686,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.931141758,"data":"       | Thus n must satisfy:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.931142856,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.931143948,"data":"       | (d-1 - log10 a)/log10 r + 1 ≤ n \u003c (d - log10 a)/log10 r + 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.931145236,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.93114631,"data":"       | Let L = (d-1 - log10 a)/log10 r + 1, U = (d - log10 a)/log10 r + 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.931147524,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.931148818,"data":"       | Number of integer n in [L, U) is floor(U-1) - ceil(L) + 1? Actually we need integer n such that L ≤ n ≤ U - epsilon (since n \u003c U). So max integer n count = floor(U - 1e-... ) - ceil(L) + 1 = floor(U - 1e-... ) - ceil(L) + 1. Since U not inclusive, we can count as floor(U-1e-...) = ceil(U)-1. So count = (ceil(U)-1) - ceil(L) + 1 = ceil(U) - ceil(L). Equivalent to floor(U-1) - floor(L-1)? Let's compute more directly: integer n satisfy n ≥ ceil(L) and n ≤ floor(U-1e-...). Since U not inclusive, the largest integer n is floor(U-ε) = floor(U) if U not integer; if U integer, then n ≤ U-1. So count = floor(U - 1e-...) - ceil(L) + 1 = ceil(U)-1 - ceil(L) +1 = ceil(U)-ceil(L). So k = ceil(U) - ceil(L).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.931151396,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.931152498,"data":"       | But easier: the inequality: 10^{d-1} ≤ a r^{n-1} \u003c 10^{d}. Divide lower bound: a r^{n-1} ≥ 10^{d-1}. Upper bound: a r^{n-1} ≤ 10^{d} - 1. However, integer range: We need count of n satisfying both.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.93115403,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.931155128,"data":"       | Define ratio between successive terms: each step multiplies by r. So the digit count stays same as long as term stays below 10^{d}. Starting from smallest n for which term is at least 10^{d-1}, up to the largest n before crossing 10^{d}. Since multiplication by r each step, the number of terms in same digit length is limited by how many times you can multiply by r before reaching or exceeding 10^{d}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.931156941,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.931158038,"data":"       | Given start term X = a r^{n0-1} where X ∈ [10^{d-1}, 10^{d} -1]. Next term X r. Condition for still same digit length: X r \u003c 10^{d}. So need X \u003c 10^{d} / r. So the initial term must be less than 10^{d} / r. Similarly earlier terms need to be ≥10^{d-1}. So we can think of the set of n such that 10^{d-1} ≤ a r^{n-1} \u003c 10^{d}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.931159894,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.93116098,"data":"       | Let n_min = smallest n such that a r^{n-1} ≥ 10^{d-1}, n_max = largest n such that a r^{n-1} \u003c 10^{d}. Then k = n_max - n_min + 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.931162419,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.931163473,"data":"       | Now the ratio between term at n_max and term at n_min is r^{k-1}. Since a r^{n_min-1} ≥ 10^{d-1} and a r^{n_max-1} \u003c 10^{d}, we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4482.931164891,"data":"       | \n"}
