,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417018798,"data":"       | Let’s denote for each pair, there are 4 possible colorings: RR, BB, RB, BR. That's 4 possibilities per pair, total 4^6 = 4096 total colorings (which matches 2^12 = 4096). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.41702029,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417021376,"data":"       | Now we need to count assignments where #RR ≤1 and #BB ≤1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417022629,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417023742,"data":"       | Let’s compute total number of assignments with #RR ≥2 or #BB ≥2 (or both) and subtract from total.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.41702511,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.41702623,"data":"       | But easier: Count assignments with #RR =0 or 1 and #BB =0 or 1 simultaneously.\n"}
