,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417065823,"data":"       | But we must verify that condition \"no four vertices colored the same color are the four vertices of a rectangle\" includes also rectangles that are not aligned with antipodal pairs? Let's double-check geometry: In a regular 12-gon, there are many rectangles formed by vertices not necessarily opposite pairs? Let's examine: For a rectangle inscribed in a circle, opposite vertices must be antipodal (diameter). In a regular 12-gon, there are 6 diameters (pairs of opposite vertices). Any rectangle must consist of two diameters. However, there are also rectangles that are not aligned with the polygon's symmetry? But any rectangle's vertices must be four points on the circle; they must be endpoints of two diameters. In a regular 12-gon, the vertices are equally spaced. So any rectangle's vertices correspond to selecting two diameters (i.e., two opposite pairs). However, there is a subtlety: The order of vertices around circle matters: The rectangle's vertices are in alternating order: A, C, B, D where A opposite B, C opposite D. So any two diameters produce a rectangle (maybe a degenerate line if diameters coincide). So our condition is correct.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417073624,"data":"       | \n"}
