,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417085625,"data":"     [Reasoning] (8195 chars):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417087176,"data":"       | analysisWe need to count colorings of vertices of a regular dodecagon (12 vertices) with red/blue such that no rectangle formed by four vertices all same color. The rectangle's vertices must be among the 12 vertices. Since the vertices lie on a circle, rectangles correspond to sets of four vertices that are opposite pairs: i.e., quadrilaterals inscribed in a circle that are rectangles iff they are cyclic quadrilaterals with opposite vertices antipodal (i.e., arcs of 180 degrees). For a regular 12-gon, vertices are equally spaced. A rectangle inscribed in a circle with vertices among the 12 must have opposite vertices separated by 6 steps (half the circle). So rectangle vertices are two opposite pairs: choose a vertex i, then opposite vertex i+6 (mod 12). For rectangle we need the other two vertices also opposite each other, and they must be such that the quadrilateral is a rectangle, i.e., edges are chords of equal length? Actually any quadrilateral inscribed in a circle with opposite vertices antipodal is a rectangle (since central angles subtended by opposite sides are equal). Let's verify: In a circle, a quadrilateral is a rectangle iff it is cyclic and its diagonals are diameters (i.e., opposite vertices are endpoints of diameters). So condition: choose two diameters (pairs of opposite vertices) that are not the same, and they form a rectangle (the vertices are the four endpoints). However need to ensure that the quadrilateral is not self-intersecting (i.e., not a bowtie). With two diameters, there are two ways to connect them: the rectangle formed by connecting each vertex to the adjacent vertices along the circle (i.e., order i, j, i+6, j+6). This yields a rectangle if the two diameters are not the same and not coincident. Indeed any two distinct diameters of a circle intersect at the center, and the quadrilateral formed by their endpoints is a rectangle (actually a parallelogram with right angles). So each unordered pair of opposite vertex pairs yields a rectangle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417093652,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417094788,"data":"       | Thus we need to avoid having any rectangle where all four vertices are same color. That is, we must avoid having both opposite pairs of a rectangle being monochromatic of same color. Equivalent: For any two distinct opposite pairs (diameters), we cannot have both pairs colored all red or all blue.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417096464,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417097593,"data":"       | Let’s label vertices 0..11. Opposite pairs are (0,6), (1,7), (2,8), (3,9), (4,10), (5,11). There are 6 diameters.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.4170989,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417100012,"data":"       | A rectangle corresponds to choosing two distinct diameters: say (i,i+6) and (j,j+6) with i≠j (mod 12). The four vertices are i, i+6, j, j+6. So condition: For any unordered pair of diameters, not all four vertices same color.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417101594,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417102713,"data":"       | Thus we need colorings of 12 vertices with red/blue such that for any two diameters, the colors of the four vertices are not all equal. This is a constraint.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417104136,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417105282,"data":"       | Let's think about the structure: The 12 vertices partition into 6 opposite pairs. Let's define each pair's color pattern: could be (R,R), (B,B), (R,B), (B,R). But order within pair doesn't matter for rectangle condition? Since rectangle uses both vertices of each pair, the colors of both vertices in a pair matter: For a rectangle formed by diameters A and B, we need both vertices of each pair to be same color (if we want rectangle monochromatic). Actually rectangle monochromatic of same color requires all four vertices same color. That implies each of the two diameters must have both vertices same color, and both diameters must have same color. So condition: No two distinct diameters are both monochromatic of the same color.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417107695,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417108925,"data":"       | Thus we can treat each opposite pair as either \"red pair\" (both red), \"blue pair\" (both blue), or \"mixed pair\" (one red, one blue). The condition forbids having at least two red pairs (both all-red) simultaneously, and also forbids having at least two blue pairs simultaneously. Mixed pairs are okay.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417110879,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417111997,"data":"       | Thus we need colorings where at most one pair is all-red, and at most one pair is all-blue. Mixed pairs can be any number.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417113328,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.41711445,"data":"       | But also note that a rectangle could be formed using a mixed pair and a monochromatic pair? Would that produce all four vertices same color? No, because mixed pair includes both colors, so rectangle can't be monochromatic. So only rectangles with all four same color are those where both diameters are monochromatic of same color. So condition reduces to: Not have two red pairs, not have two blue pairs.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417116266,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417117382,"data":"       | Thus the problem reduces to counting colorings of 12 vertices such that among the six opposite pairs, at most one pair is both red, and at most one pair is both blue. Mixed pairs can be any.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417120971,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417123323,"data":"       | Now we need to count total colorings of 12 vertices (2^12) satisfying this.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417124716,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.41712591,"data":"       | Let's compute combinatorially.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417127069,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417128252,"data":"       | Let’s denote each pair can be in three states: RR, BB, RB (mixed). For mixed, there are 2 ways (RB or BR). For RR, 1 way (both red). For BB, 1 way.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417129934,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417131206,"data":"       | Thus total colorings = sum over assignments of states to 6 pairs, multiplied by internal arrangements.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417132522,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417133587,"data":"       | Let’s define numbers: Let r = number of RR pairs, b = number of BB pairs, m = number of mixed pairs. Then r+b+m = 6. Condition: r ≤ 1, b ≤ 1. So possible (r,b) are (0,0), (1,0), (0,1), (1,1). For each case, m = 6 - r - b.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417135172,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.41713634,"data":"       | Now count colorings for each case.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417137664,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417138843,"data":"       | For each pair:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417140072,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417141145,"data":"       | - RR: 1 coloring (both red)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417142452,"data":"       | - BB: 1 coloring (both blue)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417143664,"data":"       | - Mixed: 2 colorings (RB or BR)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417144944,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417146263,"data":"       | Thus number of colorings for given (r,b) = (choose which pairs are RR) * (choose which pairs are BB) * (2^{m}) where m = 6 - r - b.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.41714772,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417149029,"data":"       | But note that RR and BB pairs are distinct categories; we need to choose r pairs to be RR, b pairs to be BB, from 6 pairs, with order not overlapping. So number of ways = C(6, r) * C(6 - r, b) * 2^{6 - r - b}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417150627,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417151711,"data":"       | Now compute for each case.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417152862,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417153936,"data":"       | Case (0,0): r=0,b=0,m=6. Number = C(6,0)*C(6,0)*2^6 = 1*1*64 = 64.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417155817,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417156889,"data":"       | Case (1,0): r=1,b=0,m=5. Number = C(6,1)*C(5,0)*2^5 = 6*1*32 = 192.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.41715828,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417159445,"data":"       | Case (0,1): r=0,b=1,m=5. Number = C(6,0)*C(6,1)*2^5 = 1*6*32 = 192.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417160796,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417161988,"data":"       | Case (1,1): r=1,b=1,m=4. Number = C(6,1)*C(5,1)*2^4 = 6*5*16 = 480.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417165136,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417166253,"data":"       | Sum = 64 + 192 + 192 + 480 = 928.\n"}
