,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417171275,"data":"       | But need to verify that we haven't missed any rectangle condition beyond opposite pairs. Are there rectangles whose vertices are not opposite pairs? In a regular 12-gon, any rectangle inscribed must have opposite vertices opposite each other (diameter). Let's confirm: For a rectangle inscribed in a circle, its diagonals are diameters (since rectangle is cyclic and opposite angles are 90°, central angle subtended by each side is 180°? Actually property: In a cyclic quadrilateral, if it's a rectangle, then its diagonals are diameters of the circumcircle. Because angle subtended by a diameter is a right angle (Thales theorem). Conversely, if a quadrilateral has both diagonals as diameters, then each angle is right. So rectangle vertices must be endpoints of two diameters. So yes, rectangle must consist of two opposite pairs.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417174436,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417176498,"data":"       | But there may be degenerate rectangles? For a set of four vertices that form a rectangle but not aligned with diameters? In a regular 12-gon, the vertices are on a circle; any rectangle inscribed must have its diagonals as diameters, as argued. So our reduction is correct.\n"}
