,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417320781,"data":"       | analysisWe need number of colorings of vertices of regular dodecagon (12 vertices) with red/blue such that there is no rectangle formed by four vertices all same color. In a regular dodecagon, vertices are equally spaced on a circle. A rectangle inscribed in a circle must have opposite vertices antipodal (diameter). For a rectangle inscribed in a circle, it's a cyclic quadrilateral with right angles; property: its vertices are two pairs of opposite points (i.e., a rectangle inscribed in a circle must be a rectangle whose center coincides with circle center, so vertices are at positions i, i+a, i+6, i+6+a (mod 12) where a not equal to 0,6. So rectangle vertices are two opposite vertices (diameter) and the other two are also opposite (diameter) and the sides are chords of equal length. For a regular 12-gon, any rectangle must have vertices at positions (i, i+k, i+6, i+6+k) where k is not 0,6. Since 12 even, there are 6 diameters (pairs of opposite vertices). Choose two diameters that are not the same; they form a rectangle if they are not collinear (i.e., not same line). Actually any two distinct diameters intersect at center; the four endpoints form a rectangle (maybe a square if diameters are perpendicular). So any unordered pair of distinct diameters yields a rectangle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417324093,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417325237,"data":"       | Thus the condition: there is no rectangle all of whose four vertices are same color. So for each unordered pair of distinct diameters (i.e., 6 choose 2 = 15 rectangles), we cannot have all four vertices same color. Equivalent: For each pair of opposite vertices (diameter), we cannot have both of them red (or both blue) simultaneously with another pair of opposite vertices also both red (or both blue). In other words, we cannot have two diameters where both endpoints are same color (i.e., both red or both blue). So the forbidden configuration: there exist two diameters each monochromatic with same color.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417327394,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417328671,"data":"       | Thus we need colorings of 12 vertices such that at most one diameter is monochromatic red, and at most one diameter is monochromatic blue. But also we could have a diameter with mixed colors (one red one blue) - that's fine. Also we could have a monochromatic diameter red and another monochromatic diameter blue - that's okay because rectangle would have two red vertices and two blue vertices, not all same color. The condition forbids rectangle with all four same color, i.e., both diameters monochromatic with same color.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.41733264,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.41733383,"data":"       | Thus we need to count colorings where there is no color (red or blue) that appears on both vertices of two different diameters. Equivalent: For each color, the set of diameters that are monochromatic in that color has size at most 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417335359,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417336455,"data":"       | Let’s label vertices 0..11 around circle. Diameters are pairs (i, i+6) for i=0..5 (since (i+6,i) same). So there are 6 diameters D_i = {i, i+6} for i=0..5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417337878,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417338973,"data":"       | Define for each i, the colors of vertices i and i+6: could be (R,R), (B,B), (R,B), (B,R). The latter two are mixed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417340268,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.41734138,"data":"       | We need to avoid having two i's where both are (R,R) (i.e., two red monochromatic diameters) and similarly avoid two (B,B). So at most one i with (R,R), at most one i with (B,B). Mixed diameters any number allowed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417342864,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.41734402,"data":"       | Thus counting colorings of 12 vertices with this restriction.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417345288,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417346413,"data":"       | We can treat each diameter independently, but there is dependency because vertices are shared? No, each vertex belongs to exactly one diameter (since opposite pairs partition vertices). Indeed vertices 0..5 each paired with 6..11. So each vertex appears in exactly one diameter. So the 12 vertices are partitioned into 6 pairs (diameters). So colorings correspond to choices for each pair independently: each pair can be assigned one of 4 possibilities: RR, BB, RB, BR. Since vertices are ordered (positions distinct), RB and BR are distinct assignments (since vertex i vs i+6). So total 4^6 = 4096 colorings, which matches 2^12 = 4096. Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417367737,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417369599,"data":"       | Now the restriction: among the 6 pairs, at most one pair may be RR, and at most one pair may be BB. No restriction on RB/BR.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417371619,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417373379,"data":"       | Thus count = number of sequences of length 6 over alphabet {RR, BB, RB, BR} with at most one RR and at most one BB.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417375447,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417377244,"data":"       | We can count using inclusion-exclusion or direct enumeration.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417379168,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417381078,"data":"       | Let’s compute: total sequences = 4^6.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417383126,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.41738428,"data":"       | Let A = set of sequences with at least two RR pairs. B = set with at least two BB pairs. We need sequences not in A ∪ B.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417387446,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417388605,"data":"       | Count = total - |A| - |B| + |A∩B|.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417389815,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417390934,"data":"       | Because symmetry, |A| = |B|.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417392106,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.4173932,"data":"       | Compute |A|: number of sequences where at least two positions are RR. Count using complement: total sequences where number of RR \u003e= 2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417394654,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417395776,"data":"       | We can compute using combinatorial sum: choose k \u003e=2 positions to be RR, remaining positions can be any of the other 3 possibilities (BB, RB, BR). So |A| = Σ_{k=2}^{6} C(6,k) * 3^{6-k}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417397357,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417398443,"data":"       | Similarly |B| same.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417399594,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417400743,"data":"       | Now |A∩B|: sequences with at least two RR and at least two BB. So need at least 2 RR positions and at least 2 BB positions; remaining positions can be RB/BR (2 possibilities). Also could have more than 2 RR or BB.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417402249,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417403344,"data":"       | Thus |A∩B| = Σ_{i=2}^{6} Σ_{j=2}^{6-i} C(6, i) * C(6-i, j) * 2^{6-i-j}. Where i = number of RR, j = number of BB, remaining positions (6-i-j) are RB/BR.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417404879,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417405937,"data":"       | Simplify.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417407048,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417408142,"data":"       | We can compute using Python to ensure correct count.assistantanalysis to=python codeimport math, itertools, functools, collections, sys, math, itertools, sympy as sp\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.41740954,"data":"       | def count():\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417410689,"data":"       |     total = 4**6\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417411803,"data":"       |     # compute A\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417412944,"data":"       |     A = sum(math.comb(6,k)*(3**(6-k)) for k in range(2,7))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417414158,"data":"       |     B = A\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417415272,"data":"       |     # compute A intersect B\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417416412,"data":"       |     intersect = 0\n"}
