,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417743007,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can think of each diameter (pair of opposite vertices) as a \"pair\". There are 6 diameters (since 12 vertices, opposite pairs). Let's label them D0,...,D5. Each Dk consists of vertices k and k+6 (mod 12). For each diameter we have a color pattern: could be (R,R), (B,B), (R,B) or (B,R). Since order doesn't matter for the pair, we can treat each pair as either both same color (monochromatic) or mixed. The condition prohibits having two diameters both monochromatic of same color. But having one monochromatic red and another monochromatic blue is allowed? Let's check: rectangle formed by those two diameters would have vertices all same color? No, because they would be red red and blue blue; not all same. So rectangle would have two reds and two blues, not violating condition. So only prohibited: two diameters both monochromatic red, or two diameters both monochromatic blue.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417745806,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417746908,"data":"       | Thus we need to count colorings of 6 pairs (diameters) where each pair can be one of four types: RR, BB, RB (mixed). But RB has two possibilities (R on first vertex, B on opposite) but order within pair doesn't matter for rectangle condition (since rectangle uses both vertices). However, the actual colorings of vertices differentiate the order; but for counting colorings, each pair has 2 mixed possibilities (RB or BR). So each pair has 4 possibilities: RR, BB, RB, BR. So total naive colorings: 4^6 = 4096 = 2^12 (makes sense). Condition: no two pairs both RR (i.e., at most one RR pair) and no two pairs both BB (at most one BB pair). Mixed pairs unrestricted.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417749175,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417750272,"data":"       | Thus we need to count assignments of 6 pairs where number of RR ≤ 1 and number of BB ≤ 1. Mixed pairs can be any count. Also note that RR and BB can coexist (i.e., one RR pair and one BB pair) because rectangle formed by those two diameters would have vertices of two colors, not all same. That's allowed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417752006,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417753054,"data":"       | Thus we need to count colorings where at most one RR and at most one BB.\n"}
