,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417780747,"data":"     [Reasoning] (7432 chars):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417781872,"data":"       | analysisWe need number of colorings of vertices of regular dodecagon (12 vertices) with colors red/blue such that there is no rectangle formed by four vertices of same color.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417783294,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417784943,"data":"       | We consider the vertices equally spaced on circle. A rectangle inscribed in a circle must have opposite vertices diametrically opposite (i.e., endpoints of a diameter). In a regular 12-gon, diametrically opposite vertices are pairs separated by 6 steps (since 12/2 = 6). A rectangle inscribed in circle with vertices among the 12 vertices must have its vertices be two opposite pairs: choose two diameters (i.e., two pairs of opposite vertices) such that the four vertices are distinct and form a rectangle (they will be vertices of a cyclic quadrilateral with opposite sides parallel? Actually any quadrilateral formed by two diameters is a rectangle: if we pick two diameters (i.e., two opposite vertex pairs) that are not the same line (i.e., not same pair), then the four vertices are the endpoints of two diameters intersecting at the center; they form a rectangle (actually a parallelogram with equal diagonals crossing at center; for a cyclic quadrilateral with equal diagonals intersecting at center, it's a rectangle). Indeed any quadrilateral formed by two diameters of a circle is a rectangle (maybe a square if diameters are perpendicular). In a regular 12-gon, there are 6 diameters (pairs of opposite vertices). Choose any two distinct diameters; they intersect at center and give 4 vertices that form a rectangle. However, are there any other rectangles formed by vertices not using diameters? For a rectangle inscribed in a circle, the center is the intersection of its diagonals, which must be the center of the circle, thus each diagonal is a diameter. So yes, a rectangle inscribed in a circle must have its vertices opposite pairs across the center. So the condition \"no four vertices colored the same color are the four vertices of a rectangle\" means that we cannot have a monochromatic set of vertices containing both endpoints of two distinct diameters.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417789315,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417790435,"data":"       | Thus we need colorings such that for any pair of distinct diameters (i.e., any pair of opposite vertex pairs), at least one of the four vertices is of opposite color (i.e., not all four same). Equivalent: there is no color (red or blue) that contains both endpoints of two different diameters.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417792034,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417793206,"data":"       | Let's label vertices 0..11 around circle. Opposite pairs: (0,6), (1,7), (2,8), (3,9), (4,10), (5,11). Let's call these 6 pairs. Condition: For each color (red/blue), the set of vertices of that color cannot contain two full opposite pairs. That is, within each color, at most one opposite pair can be completely monochromatic (i.e., both vertices of that pair same color). Because if there were two opposite pairs both monochromatic with same color, they'd give a rectangle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417796837,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.41779816,"data":"       | Thus we can think of each pair (i,i+6) as a \"diameter pair\". For each pair, there are three possible color patterns: both red (RR), both blue (BB), or mixed (RB or BR). Mixed counts as not a full pair for either color. So we need to count colorings of 6 pairs where each pair can be in one of 4 possibilities: RR, BB, RB, BR. However, we have a restriction: across the 6 pairs, the number of RR pairs is at most 1 (since having two would give rectangle of red). Similarly the number of BB pairs is at most 1 (since having two would give rectangle of blue). So at most one pair fully red, at most one pair fully blue. Mixed pairs unlimited.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417800488,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417801663,"data":"       | Thus we need to count number of assignments of each of the 6 pairs to patterns with constraints: #RR ≤ 1, #BB ≤ 1. Additionally, within each mixed pair, there are 2 possibilities (RB or BR). So we can compute total number.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417803218,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417804278,"data":"       | Let’s denote:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417805425,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417806507,"data":"       | - Choose which pair (if any) is RR: either none or choose 1 out of 6. Similarly for BB.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417807754,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.41780885,"data":"       | But we need to ensure we don't double count cases where both RR and BB chosen possibly same pair? Can't be same pair because a pair cannot be both RR and BB simultaneously. So the RR pair and BB pair must be distinct if both exist.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417810362,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417811461,"data":"       | Thus we can count cases based on numbers:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417812607,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417813697,"data":"       | Case A: No RR pair, no BB pair. All 6 pairs are mixed (RB/BR). For each pair, 2 possibilities. So total = 2^6 = 64.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417814979,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417816105,"data":"       | Case B: Exactly one RR pair, no BB pair. Choose which pair is RR: 6 choices. Remaining 5 pairs must be mixed (RB/BR) (cannot be BB because that would be a BB pair). Actually they could be BB? Wait condition is at most one BB pair, but we have zero BB pairs. So remaining pairs could be mixed or could be BB? If we allow a BB pair, that would give a BB pair, violating zero BB condition. But we are counting case exactly one RR pair and zero BB pairs, so remaining pairs cannot be BB; they can be mixed (RB/BR) or possibly RR? But we already restrict at most one RR pair, so cannot have another RR pair. So remaining pairs are mixed. So each of the 5 remaining pairs has 2 possibilities. Thus total = 6 * 2^5 = 6 * 32 = 192.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417818474,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417819709,"data":"       | Case C: Exactly one BB pair, no RR pair. Symmetric same count: 192.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.41782091,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417823811,"data":"       | Case D: Exactly one RR pair and exactly one BB pair (distinct pairs). Choose which pair is RR (6 ways), which other pair is BB (5 ways after choosing RR). The remaining 4 pairs must be mixed (cannot be RR or BB). So each has 2 possibilities. Total = 6*5 * 2^4 = 30 * 16 = 480.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417825422,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417826522,"data":"       | Thus total colorings = 64 + 192 + 192 + 480 = 928.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417827714,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417828834,"data":"       | But need to verify: Are there any colorings that satisfy condition but have both RR and BB pairs with same pair? Not possible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417830196,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417831306,"data":"       | Now we must ensure that condition \"no four vertices colored the same color are the four vertices of a rectangle\" is equivalent to \"no color contains two diameters\". Let's double-check: If a color has two diameters (i.e., two opposite pairs) then the four vertices are rectangle (true). Conversely, if there is a rectangle formed by four vertices of same color, then those vertices must be endpoints of two diameters. So that color must contain both endpoints of two diameters. So condition is exactly as we used. So counting should be correct.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417833507,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417834651,"data":"       | But we must be careful: Could there be a rectangle formed by vertices that are not opposite pairs? As argued, any rectangle inscribed in a circle must have its diagonals as diameters. For a rectangle inscribed in a circle, the center is intersection of diagonals, which is the circumcenter. Since the circle is the circumcircle, the center is the same as the circle's center. Therefore each diagonal passes through center, thus each diagonal is a diameter. So vertices are opposite pairs. So our equivalence stands.\n"}
