,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417955096,"data":"       | ### 1.  Geometry of a rectangle on a circle  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417956346,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417957438,"data":"       | A rectangle inscribed in a circle has its diagonals as diameters; hence its\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417958689,"data":"       | four vertices are exactly the endpoints of two distinct diameters.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417959887,"data":"       | Consequently a monochromatic rectangle occurs **iff** one colour contains the\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417961132,"data":"       | two opposite vertices of **two different** diameters.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417962364,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417963459,"data":"       | Thus the condition “no four vertices of the same colour form a rectangle’’ is\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417964858,"data":"       | equivalent to  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417965994,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417967074,"data":"       | *For each colour, at most one opposite pair may be monochromatic.*\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417968324,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417969406,"data":"       | --------------------------------------------------------------------\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417970788,"data":"       | ### 2.  Colour patterns for a diameter pair  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417971961,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417973018,"data":"       | For a pair \\((i,i+6)\\) there are four possible colourings:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417974333,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417975438,"data":"       | * both red  \\(RR\\)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417976688,"data":"       | * both blue \\(BB\\)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.41797789,"data":"       | * red‑blue \\(RB\\)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417979122,"data":"       | * blue‑red \\(BR\\)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417980322,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417981418,"data":"       | The mixed patterns \\(RB,BR\\) are harmless for either colour.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417983542,"data":"       | Let  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417984653,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417985752,"data":"       | * \\(r\\) = number of pairs coloured \\(RR\\),\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417987031,"data":"       | * \\(b\\) = number of pairs coloured \\(BB\\).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417988264,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417989383,"data":"       | The required condition forces  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417990534,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417991617,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417992717,"data":"       | r\\le 1,\\qquad b\\le 1 .\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417993962,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.41799679,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417997884,"data":"       | --------------------------------------------------------------------\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.417999186,"data":"       | ### 3.  Counting the admissible colourings  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.418000346,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.418001414,"data":"       | We count assignments of the six opposite pairs under the above restriction.\n"}
