,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.41832455,"data":"       | analysisThe number of unordered sets of four vertices that form a rectangle (i.e., two antipodal pairs) is 15. Indeed choose 2 antipodal pairs out of 6 possible antipodal pairs (since 12 vertices, there are 6 opposite pairs). Number of ways: C(6,2) = 15. So there are 15 distinct rectangles (each rectangle corresponds to two opposite pairs). However, each rectangle may be counted multiple times depending on order? But for condition \"no four vertices colored the same color are the four vertices of a rectangle.\" Means: we cannot have a monochromatic rectangle: four vertices of same color that constitute a rectangle. So any set of four vertices forming a rectangle cannot all be same color.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.418329078,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.418330236,"data":"       | Thus we need colorings of 12 vertices with 2 colors such that for each of 15 rectangles (i.e., each unordered set of 4 vertices defined by two opposite pairs), the colors are not all red nor all blue. Equivalent to forbidding monochromatic rectangles.\n"}
