,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.418332904,"data":"       | We need count of colorings of 12 vertices with no monochromatic rectangle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.418334138,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.418335339,"data":"       | Problem reminiscent of combinatorial designs, 2-colorings avoiding monochromatic 4-cycles in bipartite graph? Let's model: vertices are arranged in 6 antipodal pairs. Let’s label the pairs as P0, P1, ..., P5. Within each pair, there are two opposite vertices. A rectangle corresponds to picking two distinct pairs Pi and Pj; then the four vertices are the two vertices from each pair (both vertices of each pair). So rectangle's vertices are exactly the union of two antipodal pairs. So condition: for any two distinct pairs Pi, Pj, the four vertices (two from each) cannot all be same color. In other words, for any two pairs, it cannot be that all four vertices are red or all four are blue.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.418338748,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.418340034,"data":"       | Thus we can reframe problem as: we have 6 pairs, each pair contains 2 vertices opposite. Color each vertex red/blue. For each pair Pi, we may have colors (R,R), (R,B), (B,R), (B,B). But order within pair may not matter? Actually the two opposite vertices are distinct positions, but rectangle condition only cares about colors of both vertices of each pair together. For rectangle formed by Pi and Pj, we need that the colors of the four vertices are not all same. This means: it cannot happen that Pi's both vertices are red and Pj's both vertices are red (i.e., both pairs are monochromatic red). Similarly cannot have both monochromatic blue. But if a pair is mixed (one red, one blue), then rectangle containing that pair and any other pair cannot be monochromatic because the mixed pair already contributes both colors. So condition reduces to: No two distinct pairs are both monochromatic with same color.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.418342836,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.41834399,"data":"       | Thus we can treat each pair as having three types: monochromatic red (RR), monochromatic blue (BB), or mixed (RB/BR). Mixed pairs are safe; they can coexist with any other pair. The restriction: among the 6 pairs, you cannot have two or more pairs of type RR simultaneously, nor two or more pairs of type BB simultaneously. Actually you cannot have two distinct pairs both RR because then rectangle formed by them would have all red. Similarly cannot have two distinct BB pairs. However you can have at most one RR pair and at most one BB pair, but you could have both one RR and one BB pair (the rectangle formed by these two pairs would have 2 red (RR) and 2 blue (BB), not monochromatic). So the condition is: number of RR pairs ≤ 1, number of BB pairs ≤ 1. Mixed pairs unrestricted.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.418355264,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.418357383,"data":"       | Thus the problem reduces to counting colorings of 12 vertices (6 pairs) such that at most one pair is all red, at most one pair is all blue.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.418358929,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.418360063,"data":"       | But careful: Mixed pair can be either RB or BR (i.e., two orientations). There are exactly 2 colorings for a mixed pair (since two vertices opposite; they can be (R,B) or (B,R)). For monochromatic pair, there is exactly 1 coloring for RR, 1 for BB.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.418361633,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.418362746,"data":"       | Thus we need to count assignments to 6 pairs, each with 4 possible colorings: RR, BB, RB, BR. Constraint: among 6 pairs, at most one RR, at most one BB.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.418364204,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.418365621,"data":"       | We can count using inclusion-exclusion: total assignments without restrictions: 4^6 = 4096.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.41836691,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4511.418367982,"data":"       | We must subtract those where there are at least two RR pairs or at least two BB pairs (or both). Let's count.\n"}
