,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657399051,"data":"       | analysisWe need to solve for real numbers x\u003e1, y\u003e1 such that:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657400325,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657401387,"data":"       | log_x(y^x) = 10\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657402496,"data":"       | log_y(x^{4y}) = 10\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657403601,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657404681,"data":"       | We need xy.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657405759,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657406882,"data":"       | Let’s write equations.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657408023,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657409138,"data":"       | First: log base x of y^x = 10. Means (y^x) = x^{10}. Because log base a of b = c =\u003e b = a^c.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657410443,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657411489,"data":"       | Thus y^x = x^{10}. (1)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657412617,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657413669,"data":"       | Second: log base y of x^{4y} = 10 =\u003e x^{4y} = y^{10}. (2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657414924,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657416027,"data":"       | We have two equations.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657417152,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657418209,"data":"       | We need x\u003e1, y\u003e1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657419385,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657420435,"data":"       | Let’s solve.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657422768,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657423915,"data":"       | From (1): y^x = x^{10}. Take logs maybe.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657425116,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657426217,"data":"       | Take natural logs: x ln y = 10 ln x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657427359,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657428453,"data":"       | From (2): 4y ln x = 10 ln y.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657429816,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657430889,"data":"       | Thus we have system:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657431999,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657433057,"data":"       | x ln y = 10 ln x  (A)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657434176,"data":"       | 4y ln x = 10 ln y  (B)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657435441,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657436541,"data":"       | We can treat unknowns positive.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657437677,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657438772,"data":"       | Let’s solve for ln y in terms of ln x etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657440039,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657441329,"data":"       | From (A): ln y = (10 ln x)/x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657442462,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657443541,"data":"       | Plug into (B): 4y ln x = 10 * ((10 ln x)/x) =\u003e 4y ln x = (100 ln x)/x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657444827,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657445913,"data":"       | Assuming ln x ≠ 0 (since x\u003e1, ln x\u003e0), we can divide both sides by ln x:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.6574473,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657448397,"data":"       | 4y = 100 / x =\u003e y = 25 / x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657449575,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.65745065,"data":"       | Thus we have relation y = 25/x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657451784,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657452891,"data":"       | Now plug back into (A) or (B) to find x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657454068,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.65745513,"data":"       | From (A): x ln y = 10 ln x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657456256,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657457331,"data":"       | But y = 25/x. So ln y = ln 25 - ln x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657458503,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657459567,"data":"       | Thus left side: x (ln 25 - ln x) = 10 ln x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.65746071,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657461769,"data":"       | =\u003e x ln 25 - x ln x = 10 ln x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657462986,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657464054,"data":"       | Bring terms: x ln 25 = x ln x + 10 ln x = (x+10) ln x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657465287,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.65746634,"data":"       | Thus:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657467448,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657468523,"data":"       | ln 25 = ((x+10)/x) ln x = (1 + 10/x) ln x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657469663,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657471971,"data":"       | So we have equation:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657473125,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.65747419,"data":"       | ln 25 = ln x + (10/x) ln x = ln x (1 + 10/x).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657475357,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657476441,"data":"       | Let’s denote t = ln x \u003e0. Then x = e^t.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657477686,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657478744,"data":"       | Equation becomes:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657479863,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657480955,"data":"       | ln 25 = t (1 + 10/e^t).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657482109,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657483223,"data":"       | We need to solve for t.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657484328,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657485409,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can solve directly for x using algebraic manip.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657486608,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657487669,"data":"       | Let’s rewrite equation:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657488807,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657489863,"data":"       | x ln 25 = (x+10) ln x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657490989,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657492059,"data":"       | We can exponentiate maybe.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657493192,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657494245,"data":"       | Let’s treat as:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657495354,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657496438,"data":"       | ln 25^{x} = ln x^{x+10}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657497575,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657498641,"data":"       | Thus 25^{x} = x^{x+10}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657499769,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657500829,"data":"       | But we also have y = 25/x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657501946,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657503027,"data":"       | Now we need xy = x * (25/x) = 25.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657504161,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657505367,"data":"       | Thus product xy = 25 regardless of x! Let's verify.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657506531,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657507639,"data":"       | Because we derived y = 25/x from equations, product xy = 25.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657508963,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657510873,"data":"       | But need to ensure that x\u003e1, y\u003e1 and both satisfy original equations.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657512301,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657513367,"data":"       | We need to find any solution such that x\u003e1, y\u003e1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657514779,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.65751599,"data":"       | Given y = 25/x, we need both \u003e1 =\u003e x\u003e1 and 25/x \u003e1 =\u003e x \u003c25.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657517515,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657518567,"data":"       | Thus 1 \u003c x \u003c 25.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657521002,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657522239,"data":"       | Now we need to satisfy the remaining equation: x ln 25 = (x+10) ln x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657523507,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657524754,"data":"       | We need to ensure there exists solution in that interval.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657525986,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657527051,"data":"       | Let's solve for x numerically.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.657528206,"data":"       | \n"}
