,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659020441,"data":"     [Reasoning] (2251 chars):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659021685,"data":"       | analysisWe need to solve for real numbers x\u003e1, y\u003e1 satisfying:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659023089,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659024209,"data":"       | log_x ( y^x ) = 10,\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659025337,"data":"       | log_y ( x^{4y} ) = 10.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659026469,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659027595,"data":"       | Interpret logs: log base a of b.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659028739,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659029872,"data":"       | So first equation: log_x( y^x ) = 10 =\u003e y^x = x^{10}. Because log base x of something = exponent needed to raise x to get that something. Wait: Actually log_x( y^x ) = 10 =\u003e x^{10} = y^x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659031394,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659035453,"data":"       | Similarly second: log_y( x^{4y}) = 10 =\u003e y^{10} = x^{4y}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659036721,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659037805,"data":"       | Thus we have system:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659038949,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659040025,"data":"       | (1) y^x = x^{10}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659041155,"data":"       | (2) x^{4y} = y^{10}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659042265,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659043361,"data":"       | We need xy.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659044497,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659045601,"data":"       | Let’s take logs. Use natural logs.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659046769,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659047849,"data":"       | From (1): x ln y = 10 ln x =\u003e (ln y)/(ln x) = 10 / x. (Equation A)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659049117,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659050188,"data":"       | From (2): 4y ln x = 10 ln y =\u003e (ln x)/(ln y) = 10 / (4y) = 5/(2y). (Equation B)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659051441,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659052536,"data":"       | Multiply A and B: (ln y)/(ln x) * (ln x)/(ln y) = 1 = (10 / x) * (5/(2y)) = (50)/(2xy) = 25/(xy).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659053835,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659054929,"data":"       | Thus 1 = 25/(xy) =\u003e xy = 25.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659056134,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659057267,"data":"       | That's promising! However need to confirm that there indeed exist x\u003e1, y\u003e1 satisfying both equations with xy=25. The derived condition from multiplying may be enough, but we also need consistency.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659058789,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659059909,"data":"       | From A: (ln y)/(ln x) = 10/x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659061047,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659062149,"data":"       | Let’s denote t = ln y / ln x. Then t = 10/x. Also from B: (ln x)/(ln y) = 5/(2y). Inverse: 1/t = 5/(2y) =\u003e t = 2y/5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659063517,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659064613,"data":"       | Thus we have t = 10/x = 2y/5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659065759,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659066841,"data":"       | Thus 10/x = 2y/5 =\u003e cross-multiply: 50 = 2xy =\u003e xy = 25. Consistent with earlier.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659068142,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659069225,"data":"       | Now we can solve for x and y individually.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659070419,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659071499,"data":"       | From t = 10/x, also t = ln y / ln x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659072659,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659073707,"data":"       | We also have xy = 25 =\u003e y = 25/x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659074901,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659075994,"data":"       | Plug into t = 10/x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659077118,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659078193,"data":"       | Now compute ln y / ln x = 10/x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659080569,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659081654,"data":"       | Let y = 25/x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659082812,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659083877,"data":"       | Thus equation: ln(25/x) / ln x = 10/x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659085061,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659086125,"data":"       | Let’s solve for x\u003e1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659087303,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659088519,"data":"       | We can solve numerically, but perhaps we can find a simple solution.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659089748,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659090883,"data":"       | Let’s set x = 5? Then y = 5 (since product 25). Check equation: ln(5)/ln(5) = 1. 10/x = 10/5=2. Not equal. So not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659092447,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659093527,"data":"       | x= 25? y=1 (but need \u003e1). Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659094739,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659095847,"data":"       | x maybe 10? y=2.5. compute ln(2.5)/ln(10). Approx? ln(2.5)=0.9163, ln(10)=2.3026 ratio=0.398, 10/x=1. So not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659097167,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4611.659098263,"data":"       | Need ratio = 10/x, which decreases as x increases (since 10/x). So we need ratio that matches.\n"}
