,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861301717,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861302792,"data":"       | We have two equations linear in f(x) and f(1 - x). Solve for f(x) unless determinant zero.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86130406,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861305156,"data":"       | Let’s write system:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861306335,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86130743,"data":"       | Equation A: x^2 f(x) + f(1 - x) = -x^4 + 2x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861311102,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86131221,"data":"       | Equation B: f(x) + (1 - x)^2 f(1 - x) = -(1 - x)^4 + 2(1 - x).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861313429,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861314543,"data":"       | We can treat unknowns u = f(x), v = f(1 - x). Write matrix:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861315779,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861316875,"data":"       | [ x^2 , 1 ] [u] = RHS1\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861318067,"data":"       | [ 1 , (1 - x)^2 ] [v] = RHS2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861320416,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861321555,"data":"       | Thus determinant D = x^2 * (1 - x)^2 - 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861322735,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861323852,"data":"       | If D ≠ 0, we can solve uniquely for u and v. If D = 0, system may be dependent or inconsistent leading to non-uniqueness for f(x) (and f(1 - x)). So α and β are the x values where D=0? Let's examine.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861325469,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861326785,"data":"       | D = x^2 (1 - x)^2 - 1 = (x(1 - x))^2 - 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861328007,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861329102,"data":"       | Set D = 0 =\u003e (x(1 - x))^2 = 1 =\u003e |x(1 - x)| = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86133041,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861331523,"data":"       | Thus x(1 - x) = 1 or x(1 - x) = -1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861332713,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861333819,"data":"       | Solve each quadratic: x - x^2 = 1 =\u003e -x^2 + x - 1 = 0 =\u003e x^2 - x + 1 = 0. Discriminant Δ = (-1)^2 - 4 = -3 \u003c 0 =\u003e no real solutions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861335485,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861336672,"data":"       | Second: x - x^2 = -1 =\u003e -x^2 + x + 1 = 0 =\u003e x^2 - x - 1 = 0 =\u003e solutions: x = (1 ± sqrt(1 + 4))/2 = (1 ± sqrt5)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861338175,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861339262,"data":"       | Thus there are two real solutions: α = (1 + sqrt5)/2, β = (1 - sqrt5)/2 (or vice versa). Are they distinct? Yes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861340682,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86134185,"data":"       | But we must verify that at these x, the system may be dependent leading to infinite possibilities for f(x) and f(1 - x). However the problem says \"f(x) can be uniquely determined for all values of x, except f(α) and f(β) for some real numbers α and β.\" So indeed α and β are those points where uniqueness fails.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861343802,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861344872,"data":"       | Thus compute α^2 + β^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861346055,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861359135,"data":"       | Let’s compute.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861361227,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86136247,"data":"       | α = (1 + √5)/2, β = (1 - √5)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861363746,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861364856,"data":"       | Compute squares: α^2 = ((1 + √5)^2)/4 = (1 + 2√5 + 5)/4 = (6 + 2√5)/4 = (3 + √5)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861368052,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861369196,"data":"       | Similarly β^2 = ((1 - √5)^2)/4 = (1 - 2√5 + 5)/4 = (6 - 2√5)/4 = (3 - √5)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861370546,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861371737,"data":"       | Sum: α^2 + β^2 = ((3 + √5)/2) + ((3 - √5)/2) = (3+3)/2 = 6/2 = 3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861373102,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861374212,"data":"       | Thus answer should be 3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861375406,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861376845,"data":"       | But we must double-check that indeed D=0 leads to non-uniqueness and not inconsistency. Let's verify that at those x, the two equations are consistent (i.e., RHS satisfy same linear relation). If they are inconsistent, there would be no function satisfying the equation at those x, but problem says there is a function defined for all real numbers (maybe not uniquely). So there must be at least one solution for f at those points, but not unique.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86137935,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861380489,"data":"       | Let's test using symbolic solving.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86138164,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861382756,"data":"       | We can compute RHS1 and RHS2 for x = α.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861384029,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861385095,"data":"       | RHS1 = -x^4 + 2x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86138625,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861387332,"data":"       | RHS2 = -(1 - x)^4 + 2(1 - x).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861388505,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861389595,"data":"       | If D=0, we have linear dependence: the two equations are multiples of each other? Let's check.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861390923,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861391995,"data":"       | When D=0, we have x^2 (1 - x)^2 = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861393172,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861394279,"data":"       | Thus (1 - x)^2 = 1 / x^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861395427,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861396522,"data":"       | Thus the matrix becomes:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861397646,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861398715,"data":"       | [ x^2 , 1 ]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861399852,"data":"       | [ 1 , 1/x^2 ]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861401023,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861402179,"data":"       | Thus second row is (1/x^2) times first row? Let's see: Multiply first row by (1/x^2): we get [1, 1/x^2]. That's not equal to second row [1, 1/x^2]? Wait second row is [1, (1 - x)^2] = [1, 1/x^2] indeed. So second row equals (1/x^2) times first row. So the equations are linearly dependent: second equation is (1/x^2) times first equation if RHS also satisfy same scaling: RHS2 must equal (1/x^2) * RHS1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861404042,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861405146,"data":"       | Let's verify that for x satisfying x(1 - x) = -1 (since that's the real case). Let's compute RHS2 - (1/x^2)*RHS1 to see if zero.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861406522,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861407635,"data":"       | We'll compute symbolic.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861410556,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861411707,"data":"       | assistantanalysis to=python codeimport sympy as sp\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861412989,"data":"       | x = sp.symbols('x')\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861414145,"data":"       | RHS1 = -x**4 + 2*x\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861415312,"data":"       | RHS2 = -(1 - x)**4 + 2*(1 - x)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861416625,"data":"       | expr = sp.simplify(RHS2 - (1/x**2)*RHS1)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861417802,"data":"       | sp.factor(expr)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861418916,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86141999,"data":"     [Code]:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861421239,"data":"       | import sympy as sp\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861422472,"data":"       | x = sp.symbols('x')\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861423747,"data":"       | RHS1 = -x**4 + 2*x\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861424865,"data":"       | RHS2 = -(1 - x)**4 + 2*(1 - x)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861426216,"data":"       | expr = sp.simplify(RHS2 - (1/x**2)*RHS1)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861427367,"data":"       | sp.factor(expr)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86142849,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86142956,"data":"     [Output]:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861430865,"data":"       \u003e -(x - 2)*(x**2 - x - 1)*(x**2 - x + 1)/x\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86143222,"data":"       \u003e \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861433345,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861434399,"data":"     [Turn 2]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86143564,"data":"     [Reasoning] (2608 chars):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861436849,"data":"       | analysisSo RHS2 - (1/x^2)*RHS1 = -(x - 2)*(x**2 - x - 1)*(x**2 - x + 1)/x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861438119,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86143951,"data":"       | Now for x satisfying x^2 - x - 1 = 0 (i.e., α, β), the expression becomes zero (since factor). Also note x=0 is problematic (division by zero). But x=0 is not a solution to D=0 because D = x^2 (1 - x)^2 - 1 = -1 at x=0, not zero. So fine.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861441453,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861442735,"data":"       | Thus at α, β, RHS2 = (1/x^2) * RHS1, making equations dependent, giving infinite solutions for f(x) and f(1 - x). So uniqueness fails exactly at those points.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861444185,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861445265,"data":"       | Thus α^2 + β^2 = 3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861446477,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861447593,"data":"       | But we should double-check that there aren't any other points where D=0 but not real? Already we solved for real solutions: only α, β. So answer 3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861449002,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861450086,"data":"       | Let's produce final answer.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861451247,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861452455,"data":"       | But we must ensure that the problem statement: \"f(x) can be uniquely determined for all values of x, except f(α) and f(β) for some real numbers α and β.\" Means there are exactly two points where f is not uniquely determined. Indeed we found them.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861456339,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861457429,"data":"       | Thus answer 3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861458585,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861459665,"data":"       | Now produce final answer.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861460819,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861461892,"data":"       | assistantfinalThe functional equation  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861463066,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86146422,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861465442,"data":"       | x^{2}f(x)+f(1-x)=-x^{4}+2x\\qquad (\\forall x\\in\\mathbb R)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861466877,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861468022,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861469132,"data":"       | gives a linear system for the two unknowns \\(f(x)\\) and \\(f(1-x)\\).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861470555,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861471625,"data":"       | ---\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861472755,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861473865,"data":"       | ### 1. Form the system\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861475023,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861476119,"data":"       | Replace \\(x\\) by \\(1-x\\):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861477432,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861478516,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861479655,"data":"       | \\begin{cases}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861480833,"data":"       | x^{2}f(x)+f(1-x)= -x^{4}+2x \\quad \u0026(1)\\\\[2mm]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861482306,"data":"       | f(x)+(1-x)^{2}f(1-x)=-(1-x)^{4}+2(1-x) \\quad \u0026(2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861483672,"data":"       | \\end{cases}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861484832,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861485923,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861487005,"data":"       | Write it as  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861488182,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861489252,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861490379,"data":"       | \\begin{pmatrix}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861491562,"data":"       | x^{2} \u0026 1\\\\[1mm]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861492865,"data":"       | 1 \u0026 (1-x)^{2}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861494059,"data":"       | \\end{pmatrix}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861495176,"data":"       | \\begin{pmatrix}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861496347,"data":"       | f(x)\\\\[1mm] f(1-x)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861497566,"data":"       | \\end{pmatrix}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861498706,"data":"       | =\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861499799,"data":"       | \\begin{pmatrix}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861500929,"data":"       | -x^{4}+2x\\\\[1mm]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861502285,"data":"       | -(1-x)^{4}+2(1-x)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861503426,"data":"       | \\end{pmatrix}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861504562,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861505666,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86151026,"data":"       | ---\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86151141,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861512499,"data":"       | ### 2. When is the system singular?\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861513695,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861514816,"data":"       | The determinant of the coefficient matrix is  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861516017,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861517085,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861518685,"data":"       | \\Delta = x^{2}(1-x)^{2}-1 = \\bigl[x(1-x)\\bigr]^{2}-1 .\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861520032,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861521135,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861522252,"data":"       | If \\(\\Delta\\neq0\\) the system has a unique solution, so \\(f(x)\\) is uniquely determined.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861523769,"data":"       | Non‑uniqueness occurs exactly when \\(\\Delta=0\\):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861525313,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861526367,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861527502,"data":"       | \\bigl[x(1-x)\\bigr]^{2}=1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x(1-x)=\\pm1 .\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861528935,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861530015,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861531142,"data":"       | * \\(x(1-x)=1\\) gives \\(x^{2}-x+1=0\\) – no real roots.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861532509,"data":"       | * \\(x(1-x)=-1\\) gives \\(x^{2}-x-1=0\\).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861533802,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86153491,"data":"       | Hence the real solutions are  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861536099,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861537167,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861538279,"data":"       | \\alpha=\\frac{1+\\sqrt5}{2},\\qquad \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861539565,"data":"       | \\beta =\\frac{1-\\sqrt5}{2}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861540822,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861541935,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861543055,"data":"       | At these two points the two equations are linearly dependent, so \\(f(\\alpha)\\) and \\(f(\\beta)\\) are not uniquely determined.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861544662,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861545727,"data":"       | ---\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861546829,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861547942,"data":"       | ### 3. Compute \\(\\alpha^{2}+\\beta^{2}\\)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861549243,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861550332,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861551422,"data":"       | \\begin{aligned}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86155259,"data":"       | \\alpha^{2}\u0026=\\left(\\frac{1+\\sqrt5}{2}\\right)^{2}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861554033,"data":"       |           =\\frac{6+2\\sqrt5}{4}= \\frac{3+\\sqrt5}{2},\\\\[2mm]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861555429,"data":"       | \\beta^{2}\u0026=\\left(\\frac{1-\\sqrt5}{2}\\right)^{2}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861556795,"data":"       |           =\\frac{6-2\\sqrt5}{4}= \\frac{3-\\sqrt5}{2}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861560012,"data":"       | \\end{aligned}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86156119,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861562286,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86156335,"data":"       | Adding,\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861564512,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861565615,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861566772,"data":"       | \\alpha^{2}+\\beta^{2}= \\frac{3+\\sqrt5}{2}+\\frac{3-\\sqrt5}{2}= \\frac{6}{2}=3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861568185,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861569285,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861570345,"data":"       | ---\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861571437,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861572509,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861573596,"data":"       | \\boxed{3}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861574765,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861575855,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861576969,"data":"     - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - \n"}
