,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861586943,"data":"       | analysisWe have functional equation: for all real x,\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861588213,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861589452,"data":"       | x^2 f(x) + f(1 - x) = -x^4 + 2x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861590625,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861591796,"data":"       | We need to determine f(x) uniquely for all x except at two points α, β where it's not determined (maybe due to singularities). Compute α^2 + β^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861593399,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861594846,"data":"       | We need to solve functional equation for f.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861596119,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861597307,"data":"       | Let’s treat as linear equation in unknown function values at x and 1 - x. For each x, we have equation linking f(x) and f(1 - x). Similarly, plugging x' = 1 - x gives another equation linking f(1 - x) and f(x). Solve system for f(x) and f(1 - x) unless coefficient matrix singular.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861598992,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861600236,"data":"       | Let’s define equation (1): x^2 f(x) + f(1 - x) = -x^4 + 2x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861601505,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861602583,"data":"       | Now replace x with 1 - x: (1 - x)^2 f(1 - x) + f(x) = -(1 - x)^4 + 2(1 - x).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86160385,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861605055,"data":"       | So we have linear system:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861608109,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86160921,"data":"       | (1) x^2 f(x) + 1 * f(1 - x) = A(x) where A(x) = -x^4 + 2x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861610462,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861611659,"data":"       | (2) 1 * f(x) + (1 - x)^2 f(1 - x) = B(x) where B(x) = -(1 - x)^4 + 2(1 - x).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861612899,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86161399,"data":"       | We can solve for f(x) and f(1 - x) as long as determinant not zero.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861615225,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861616305,"data":"       | Matrix:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861617455,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861618532,"data":"       | [ x^2 , 1 ]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861619683,"data":"       | [ 1 , (1 - x)^2 ]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861620812,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86162191,"data":"       | Determinant D = x^2 * (1 - x)^2 - 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861623115,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861624209,"data":"       | If D ≠ 0, we can solve uniquely.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861625415,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861626553,"data":"       | If D = 0, then the system may be dependent or inconsistent, leading to non-uniqueness for those x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86162784,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861628915,"data":"       | Thus α and β are values of x where D = 0. Solve x^2 (1 - x)^2 = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861630182,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861631272,"data":"       | Take square root? Actually D = x^2 (1 - x)^2 - 1 = 0 =\u003e x^2 (1 - x)^2 = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861632579,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861633676,"data":"       | Take square root both sides: |x (1 - x)| = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861634849,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861635975,"data":"       | Thus x(1 - x) = 1 or x(1 - x) = -1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861637119,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861638196,"data":"       | Solve each quadratic.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861639339,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861640452,"data":"       | Case 1: x(1 - x) = 1 =\u003e x - x^2 = 1 =\u003e -x^2 + x - 1 = 0 =\u003e x^2 - x + 1 = 0. Discriminant Δ = (-1)^2 - 4*1*1 = 1 - 4 = -3 \u003c 0. No real solutions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861641999,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86164309,"data":"       | Case 2: x(1 - x) = -1 =\u003e x - x^2 = -1 =\u003e -x^2 + x + 1 = 0 =\u003e x^2 - x - 1 = 0 =\u003e solutions: x = [1 ± sqrt(1 + 4)]/2 = [1 ± sqrt5]/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861644633,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861645753,"data":"       | Thus α and β are the two real solutions: (1 + sqrt5)/2 and (1 - sqrt5)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861647045,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861648122,"data":"       | But note that (1 - sqrt5)/2 is negative approx -0.618. Both real.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86164935,"data":"       | \n"}
