,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.861752372,"data":"       | Thus f(x) = 1 - x^2 works for all x, including α and β. So why is f not uniquely determined at those points? Actually, we solved linear system assuming determinant non-zero, but we found solution that works for all x, even where determinant zero. However, at those points, the system does not uniquely determine f(x) because there may be multiple functions that satisfy the equation at those points while still satisfying elsewhere? Let's examine.\n"}
