,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862070523,"data":"       | Thus equation (2): (1 - x)^2 f(1 - x) + f(x) = -(1 - x)^4 + 2(1 - x).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862071742,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862072852,"data":"       | Now we have two linear equations in unknowns f(x) and f(1 - x):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862074045,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862075138,"data":"       | (1) x^2 f(x) + f(1 - x) = -x^4 + 2x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862076313,"data":"       | (2) f(x) + (1 - x)^2 f(1 - x) = -(1 - x)^4 + 2(1 - x).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862077562,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86207877,"data":"       | Write as matrix:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862079895,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862081015,"data":"       | [ x^2 , 1 ] [f(x)] = RHS1\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86208215,"data":"       | [ 1 , (1 - x)^2 ] [f(1 - x)] = RHS2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862083302,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862084412,"data":"       | Solve for f(x) and f(1 - x) provided determinant D = x^2 * (1 - x)^2 - 1 ≠ 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862085722,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862086835,"data":"       | Thus D = x^2 (1 - x)^2 - 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86208796,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862089066,"data":"       | If D ≠ 0, we can solve uniquely. If D = 0, the system may be dependent or inconsistent, leading to non-uniqueness for those x values.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862090463,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86209156,"data":"       | Thus α and β are the real solutions of D = 0, i.e., x^2 (1 - x)^2 = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862092838,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862093926,"data":"       | Solve equation: (x (1 - x))^2 = 1 =\u003e x (1 - x) = ±1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862095205,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862096315,"data":"       | Thus we need to solve x(1 - x) = 1 and x(1 - x) = -1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862097516,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862098602,"data":"       | Let’s solve.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862099756,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862100813,"data":"       | Let’s compute: x (1 - x) = x - x^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862101986,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862103083,"data":"       | Equation1: x - x^2 = 1 =\u003e -x^2 + x - 1 = 0 =\u003e x^2 - x + 1 = 0 (multiply by -1). Discriminant Δ = (-1)^2 - 4*1*1 = 1 - 4 = -3 \u003c 0. No real solutions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862104595,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862105688,"data":"       | Equation2: x - x^2 = -1 =\u003e -x^2 + x + 1 = 0 =\u003e x^2 - x - 1 = 0. Solutions: x = [1 ± sqrt(1 + 4)]/2 = [1 ± sqrt5]/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862107103,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862109875,"data":"       | Thus two real solutions: α = (1 + sqrt5)/2, β = (1 - sqrt5)/2. Or order doesn't matter.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862111179,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862112265,"data":"       | Thus α^2 + β^2 = ?\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862113416,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862114549,"data":"       | Compute using formula: For roots r1, r2 of quadratic x^2 - x - 1 = 0, we have sum S = r1 + r2 = 1 (coefficient -b/a). Product P = r1 * r2 = -1 (c/a). Then we need r1^2 + r2^2 = (r1 + r2)^2 - 2 r1 r2 = S^2 - 2P = 1^2 - 2*(-1) = 1 + 2 = 3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862116076,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862117198,"data":"       | Thus answer 3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862118299,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862119609,"data":"       | But we must verify that indeed at those x values the system cannot uniquely determine f(x). Let's verify: D=0 leads to infinite possibilities? Need to ensure that RHS also consistent (i.e., system may have either no solution or infinite solutions). But problem says f(x) can be uniquely determined for all values except f(α) and f(β) for some real numbers α and β. So there are exactly two points where not uniquely determined; that matches our solutions.\n"}
