,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862455132,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862456215,"data":"       | Now compute α^2 + β^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862457408,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862458482,"data":"       | Let α = (1 + sqrt(5))/2, β = (1 - sqrt(5))/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862459709,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862460772,"data":"       | Compute squares sum.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862461888,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862463135,"data":"       | We can use algebra: α^2 + β^2 = ((1 + √5)/2)^2 + ((1 - √5)/2)^2 = ( (1 + 2√5 + 5)/4 ) + ( (1 - 2√5 + 5)/4 ) = ( (6 + 2√5)/4 ) + ( (6 - 2√5)/4 ) = ( (6+2√5 + 6-2√5) )/4 = (12)/4 = 3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862464799,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862465895,"data":"       | Thus α^2 + β^2 = 3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.8624671,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862468196,"data":"       | Check: Indeed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862469298,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862470355,"data":"       | Thus answer: 3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862471456,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862472589,"data":"       | But we need to ensure there are not any other points where function not uniquely determined. Could there be points where denominator zero leads to infinite possibilities? The determinant zero is the only case where linear system singular. Since we have two equations for each x (original and transformed), we can solve for f(x) uniquely except when determinant zero. So there are exactly two real solutions as found.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862474405,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862475472,"data":"       | Thus answer is 3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862476572,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862477725,"data":"       | But we should also double-check that the functional equation uniquely determines f(x) for all other x, i.e., the system yields unique solution for f(x). Since the coefficient matrix invertible for all other x, we can solve uniquely for f(x). So yes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862479275,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862480345,"data":"       | Thus answer: 3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862481453,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862482549,"data":"       | Let's produce final answer.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86248367,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862484749,"data":"       | assistantfinalThe functional equation  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862485918,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862486976,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86248812,"data":"       | x^{2}f(x)+f(1-x)=-x^{4}+2x\\qquad(\\forall x\\in\\mathbb R)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862489532,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862492138,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862493319,"data":"       | can be paired with the same equation after replacing \\(x\\) by \\(1-x\\):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862494762,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862495833,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862496945,"data":"       | \\begin{cases}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862498129,"data":"       | x^{2}f(x)+f(1-x)= -x^{4}+2x \\\\\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862499372,"data":"       | f(x)+(1-x)^{2}f(1-x)= -(1-x)^{4}+2(1-x)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862500566,"data":"       | \\end{cases}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862501692,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862502826,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862503946,"data":"       | These are two linear equations for the two unknowns \\(f(x)\\) and \\(f(1-x)\\).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86250544,"data":"       | The coefficient matrix is  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862506569,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862507643,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862508756,"data":"       | A=\\begin{pmatrix}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862509936,"data":"       | x^{2} \u0026 1\\\\[2pt]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862511176,"data":"       | 1 \u0026 (1-x)^{2}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862512485,"data":"       | \\end{pmatrix},\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862513652,"data":"       | \\qquad \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862514785,"data":"       | \\det A = x^{2}(1-x)^{2}-1 .\n"}
