,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862566918,"data":"       | If D ≠ 0, we can uniquely solve for f(x) and f(1 - x). If D = 0, the system is singular, and may have either no solutions or infinite solutions (depending on consistency). Since we are told f can be uniquely determined for all values except at two points α and β, likely D = 0 for two x values, leading to non-uniqueness.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862568676,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862569889,"data":"       | Thus we need to find x such that D = 0: (x(1 - x))^2 = 1 =\u003e (x - x^2)^2 = 1. So x - x^2 = ±1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862571368,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862573375,"data":"       | Solve x - x^2 = 1 =\u003e -x^2 + x - 1 = 0 =\u003e x^2 - x + 1 = 0 =\u003e discriminant Δ = (-1)^2 - 4*1*1 = 1 - 4 = -3 \u003c 0, no real solutions.\n"}
