,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862605839,"data":"       | When D = 0, the system may have either no solutions or infinite solutions depending on whether RHS vector is in column space. The original equation alone gives one equation; but we also have the second equation derived by plugging x→1-x; but the original functional equation only gives the first equation; the second equation is also derived from the same functional equation but for argument 1 - x. So the functional equation must hold for all x, i.e., both equations hold for each x. So the system is consistent (since it's derived from same function). However, at x values where D=0, the two equations may be linearly dependent; i.e., the second equation may be a multiple of the first, leading to insufficient constraints to determine f(x) uniquely (there will be infinite solutions). But we must verify that at those x, the two equations are indeed linearly dependent, not contradictory (which would make no solution). Since the function exists (the problem statement says function satisfies equation for all real numbers), there must be at least one solution; thus the equations are consistent.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862610625,"data":"       | \n"}
