,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862756623,"data":"       | If D = 0, then system may have either no solution or infinite solutions; but given that there is a function satisfying for all x, the equation must be consistent even when determinant zero. The problem says f(x) can be uniquely determined for all x except at two points α and β. Likely those correspond to x values where determinant zero, causing underdetermined; but there might be only two such x values (i.e., solutions to D = 0). So α and β are the x values where x^2 (1 - x)^2 = 1, i.e., (x(1 - x))^2 = 1. So x(1 - x) = ±1.\n"}
