,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862793005,"data":"       | Thus we have two equations: For x = α:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862794229,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862795312,"data":"       | α^2 f(α) + f(β) = -α^4 + 2α.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862796519,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862797592,"data":"       | For x = β:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862798783,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862799862,"data":"       | β^2 f(β) + f(α) = -β^4 + 2β.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862801056,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86280217,"data":"       | That's a system of two linear equations for unknowns f(α) and f(β). But note that the determinant of the coefficient matrix for this system is: α^2*β^2 - 1? Wait the coefficient matrix is:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86280372,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862804799,"data":"       | [α^2, 1] * [f(α), f(β)]^T = RHS1\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862806009,"data":"       | [1, β^2] * [f(α), f(β)]^T = RHS2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862807235,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862808339,"data":"       | Determinant = α^2 * β^2 - 1. But α β =? Since α and β are roots of x^2 - x - 1 = 0, product αβ = -1 (by constant term). So α^2 β^2 = (αβ)^2 = (-1)^2 = 1. So determinant = 1 - 1 = 0. So system is singular, consistent, giving infinite solutions. So indeed f(α) and f(β) cannot be uniquely determined.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862810345,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862811555,"data":"       | Thus α,β as above.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862812739,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862813829,"data":"       | Now compute α^2 + β^2 = (α + β)^2 - 2αβ = 1^2 - 2*(-1) = 1 + 2 = 3. Indeed also matches earlier.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862815206,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862817927,"data":"       | Thus answer is 3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862819065,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862820632,"data":"       | But we must ensure there aren't any other points where function not uniquely determined due to other issues, like dividing by zero when solving for f(x) maybe using expression (something)/D. But D zero only at those points.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862822142,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86282328,"data":"       | Thus answer: 3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862824382,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862825495,"data":"       | But let's also double-check using symbolic solving for general x, compute expression for f(x) for x not equal to α,β.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862826852,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.862827935,"data":"       | We'll compute using sympy to confirm.\n"}
