,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863082859,"data":"       | analysisThus answer 3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86308398,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863085096,"data":"       | But we must verify that for those x, the determinant zero leads to infinite solutions not just no solution. Check compatibility condition: The RHS of the two equations should be consistent (i.e., the equations be multiples). For D=0, we have x^2 (1 - x)^2 = 1. That implies (1 - x)^2 = 1/x^2. Let's see if the RHS satisfy same proportional relation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863086864,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863087987,"data":"       | Original equations: Eq1: x^2 f(x) + f(1 - x) = R1 = -x^4 + 2x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863089254,"data":"       | Eq2: f(x) + (1 - x)^2 f(1 - x) = R2 = -(1 - x)^4 + 2(1 - x).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863090474,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86309159,"data":"       | If coefficient matrix has determinant zero, meaning rows are linearly dependent. Let's check if RHS also satisfy same linear dependence, else no solution. Since we have found a solution f(x)=1-x^2 that works for all x, it must be that RHS is consistent, i.e., there is at least one solution at those points (the given f works). So infinite solutions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863093349,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863094426,"data":"       | Thus answer 3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863095523,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863096594,"data":"       | Now produce final answer.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863097734,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863098809,"data":"       | assistantfinalThe functional equation  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863099986,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863101042,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863102194,"data":"       | x^{2}f(x)+f(1-x)=-x^{4}+2x\\qquad (\\forall x\\in\\mathbb R)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863103546,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863104673,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86310574,"data":"       | relates the values \\(f(x)\\) and \\(f(1-x)\\).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863107056,"data":"       | Replace \\(x\\) by \\(1-x\\):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863108223,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863110832,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863111962,"data":"       | (1-x)^{2}f(1-x)+f(x)=-(1-x)^{4}+2(1-x).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863113139,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863114232,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863115307,"data":"       | Thus for each \\(x\\) we have the linear system  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863116552,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863117619,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863118722,"data":"       | \\begin{cases}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863119873,"data":"       | x^{2}f(x)+f(1-x)= -x^{4}+2x,\\\\[2mm]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863121189,"data":"       | f(x)+(1-x)^{2}f(1-x)= -(1-x)^{4}+2(1-x).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863122466,"data":"       | \\end{cases}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863123609,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863124699,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.86312579,"data":"       | The coefficient matrix is  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863126957,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863128312,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863130202,"data":"       | A=\\begin{pmatrix}x^{2}\u00261\\\\[2mm]1\u0026(1-x)^{2}\\end{pmatrix},\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863132405,"data":"       | \\qquad\\det A =x^{2}(1-x)^{2}-1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863134304,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863136195,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863137842,"data":"       | When \\(\\det A\\neq0\\) the system has a unique solution, giving a unique value for\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863140036,"data":"       | \\(f(x)\\).  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863141819,"data":"       | The only points where the determinant vanishes satisfy  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863143662,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863145356,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863147292,"data":"       | x^{2}(1-x)^{2}=1\\iff \\bigl(x(1-x)\\bigr)^{2}=1\\iff x(1-x)=\\pm1 .\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863149415,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863151147,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863152956,"data":"       | * \\(x(1-x)=1\\) gives \\(x^{2}-x+1=0\\), which has no real roots.  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863155152,"data":"       | * \\(x(1-x)=-1\\) gives \\(x^{2}-x-1=0\\), whose real solutions are  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863157242,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863158945,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863160709,"data":"       | \\alpha=\\frac{1+\\sqrt5}{2},\\qquad \\beta=\\frac{1-\\sqrt5}{2}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863162215,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863163469,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863164572,"data":"       | At \\(x=\\alpha\\) or \\(x=\\beta\\) the two equations are linearly dependent, so\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863166007,"data":"       | \\(f(\\alpha)\\) and \\(f(\\beta)\\) cannot be uniquely determined.  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863167404,"data":"       | For every other real \\(x\\) the determinant is non‑zero and \\(f(x)\\) is uniquely\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863168879,"data":"       | determined (indeed, solving the system yields \\(f(x)=1-x^{2}\\)).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863171942,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863173074,"data":"       | Hence the required numbers are \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\) with  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863174439,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863175696,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863176799,"data":"       | \\alpha+\\beta =1,\\qquad \\alpha\\beta =-1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863182234,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863183392,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863184466,"data":"       | Now  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863185566,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863186803,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863187903,"data":"       | \\alpha^{2}+\\beta^{2}= (\\alpha+\\beta)^{2}-2\\alpha\\beta = 1^{2}-2(-1)=3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863189309,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4635.863190436,"data":"       | \n"}
