,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4713.311998966,"data":"       | analysisWe need to find (a+4)(b+4)(c+4) given three equations for (a+1)(b+1)(c+1)=1, (a+2)(b+2)(c+2)=2, (a+3)(b+3)(c+3)=3. a,b,c complex numbers, presumably distinct maybe not. We need product at shift 4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4713.312000421,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4713.312001734,"data":"       | We have three equations for three unknowns? Actually each equation is product of three terms (linear functions). So we have three equations in three unknowns, but they are symmetric (only product of shifted variables). There might be multiple solutions but product at shift 4 may be uniquely determined.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4713.312003391,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4713.312004493,"data":"       | Let’s denote function f(t) = (a+t)(b+t)(c+t). That's a cubic polynomial in t: f(t) = (t+a)(t+b)(t+c) = t^3 + (a+b+c) t^2 + (ab+ac+bc) t + abc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4713.312005905,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4713.31200701,"data":"       | We are given f(1)=1, f(2)=2, f(3)=3. Need f(4)=?.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4713.312008215,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4713.312009376,"data":"       | Thus we have cubic polynomial f(t) such that f(k)=k for k=1,2,3. Need f(4). So we can treat f(t)-t as a cubic minus linear: g(t) = f(t) - t. Then g(t) is cubic: g(t) = t^3 + (a+b+c) t^2 + (ab+ac+bc) t + abc - t = t^3 + (a+b+c) t^2 + (ab+ac+bc-1) t + abc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4713.312012783,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4713.312013926,"data":"       | We know g(1)=0, g(2)=0, g(3)=0 (since f(k)=k). So g(t) is a cubic with roots 1,2,3. Therefore g(t) = K (t-1)(t-2)(t-3) for some constant K (leading coefficient maybe?). Since leading coefficient of g(t) is 1 (coefficient of t^3 is 1). Let's compute leading coefficient of RHS: K times t^3 coefficient from (t-1)(t-2)(t-3) is K * 1 (since product expands to t^3 -6 t^2 + 11 t -6). So K must be 1. Therefore g(t) = (t-1)(t-2)(t-3).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4713.312015784,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4713.312016898,"data":"       | Thus f(t) = t + (t-1)(t-2)(t-3).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4713.312018075,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4713.312019293,"data":"       | Now compute f(4) = 4 + (4-1)(4-2)(4-3) = 4 + (3)(2)(1) = 4 + 6 = 10.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4713.312020524,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4713.312021601,"data":"       | Therefore (a+4)(b+4)(c+4) = 10.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4713.31202275,"data":"       | \n"}
